a stainless steel column 25 cm * 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsiiane with extra selectivity of a p enta fluoro \nphenyl bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such as ACE \nPFPC18), \n■■■■.■\n- column temperature: 40°, \n:\n- sample temperature: 5°, \nW\n- mobile phase: A. a buffer solution prepared by\ndissolving 1.36 g of potassium dihydrogen phosphate \nin 1000 ml of water, \n‘ \n■\nB. a mixture of 90 volumes of acetonitrile \nand 10 volumes of water,\n- a gradient programme Using the conditions given below;\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute, \n/ ; > :\n- spectrophotometer set at 245 nm, \n■ ’\n- injection volume: 5 pl. . \n,\nTime Mobile phase A Mobile phase B ' -\n. (inmin,). . (percent v/v).\n(per cent v/v).\n■■ 15 •\n0\n85\n60 \n•\n25\n40\n35 ■\ny. 44 .\n60\n40\n.80 \n. . /\n20\n80\n20\n48\n\"49\n85\n15\n15 \n/\n85\n55\nName\nRelative\nCorrection\nretention time \nfactor ,\nRerndesivir impurity A1\n0.17\n. . . 0.45 ■■ . .\nRerndesivir impurity B2*\n0.57\nRerndesivir (Retention time: \n:\nabout 21 minutes)\n1.0’\nRerndesivir impurity C3‘\n1.32\n—\nRerndesivir impurity D4’\n.1.8\n’Process impurity include for identification-only and-not included in the \ncalculation, of total'degradation products, .\n1(2RJ3R,4S,5R)?2-(4-aminopyrrolo[2,l-f|(.l,2,4]triazin-7-yl)-3,4- \ndihydroxy-5-(hydroxymethyl)tetrahydrofuran-2-carbonitrile (Fragment \nA), '\n2(3aR,4R,6R,6a7?)-4-(4-aminopyrrolo[2,l-f][l,2,4]triazin-7-yl)-6- \n(hydroxymethyl)-2,2-dimethyltetrahydrofufah[3,4-d][l,3]dioxole-4- \ncarbonitrile (Acetonide), \n; .■? \n' j '\n32-ethyIbutyl((S)'(((3aJ?)4I?,6J?,6aR:)-6-(4-aminopyrrolo[2,l-f] \n[1,2,4]triazinr7-yl)-6'Cyano-2,2-diinethyItetrahydrofuro[3,4-d] \n[l,3]dioxol'4-yl)methoxy)(phenbxy)phosphoryl)amino)-L-ataninate \n(Condensed),\nW-[(S)-(4-nitrophenoxy) piienoxyphdsphinyl]-l-AIame 2- ethylbutyl-\nester (Fragment'B).' J \n-;-\n3469\nwww.webofpharma.com\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3).Not more than 0.6 Endotoxin Unit \nper mg of remdesivir. \n;\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 40 volumes of acetonitrile and 60 volumes \nof water.\nTest solution. Reconstitute 5 vials with 20 ml each, with the \nsolvent mixture and pool the contents in 200-ml volumetric \nflask, dilute to volume with the solvent mixture. Dilute 2.0 ml \nof the solution to 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution.A0.01 percent w/v solution of remdesivir \nIPRS ih.the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm. x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane with extra selectivity of a penta fluoro \nphenyl bonded to porous silica (5, pm) (Such as ACE \nPFPC18), ; \n: : \n?\n- column temperature; 40°,\n- - sample temperature:. 10°, ; > - >\n- mobile phase: a mixture’ of 50 volumes of a buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 1 ;36 g o fflbtassium dihydrogen \n\" ' phosphate in 1000 ml of water and 50 volumes of\n, , acetonitrilo, ..\n, - flow rate: 1 ml per minute, ,,\n- spectrophotometer set at 245 nm,\n— injection volume: 10 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates, the; \ntailing factor is more than 2.0 and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent. \n.\nREMDESIVIR INJECTION\nIP 2022.\nInject the refcrence solution. The test is not valid unless the Inject the reference solution and the test;solution.\ncolumn \nthe Calculate the content of C27HJ5N6O J5. \n.. \n..\ntailingfactor is more than 2.0 and the relative standard deviation \n2 \n-• s ’ ■■ ■ ■' ; \n.. y\nfor replicate injections is not more than 10.0 per cent..,.; Storage. Store protected from light and moisture, at a\n>!s ‘■' \ntemperature not exceeding 30°. \n... J’.;:. \n■,\nInject the reference solution and: the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtainedwiththe test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to remdesivir impurity A is not more than \ntwice the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (2.0 per cent), the area \nany other secondary peak is not more, than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution (1.0 per cent) and, the sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks other than remdesivir impurity A is not more \nthan twice the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (2.0 percent). Ignore any \npeak with an area less than O .()5 tmies the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(0.05 per cent). - \n;\nRepaglinide\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 3.'0 per cent, determined on\n0.1g. ..... \nCjyHjftNbCh\nMol; Wt 452.6\nRepaglinide is (5)-2-Ethoxy-42[2-[3 -methyl- i-(2-(piperidin-\nl-yl)phenyl)buty)amino]-2-oxoethyI]benzoic acid.. \n7\nRepaglinide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more; \nthan 101.0 per cent of C27I k,(,N2Oi, calculated on the dried 7\nbasis.\nCategory.Antidiabetic. • , \n,.y \n. ■ <7-^ g.\nDescription. A white Or almost white powder. It shows \npolymorphism (2.5.11).\nJ / ■ .' < ■ 17 ■ \n{ .■ ' 7 : : - '■ ■ ’■ \n: d ; 1 : "}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/reproducibility","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Reproducibility"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Reproducibility\nReproducibility expresses the precision between laboratories \n(collaborative studies, usually applied to standardisation of \nmethodology).\nReproducibility is usually demonstrated by means of an inter­\nlaboratory trial.\nDetection Limit\nThe detection limit of an analytical procedure is the lowest \nconcentration of analyte in a sample that can be detected but \nnot necessarily quantitated as an exact value.\nThe detection limit is usually expressed as the concentration \nof analyte (e.g., percentage, parts per billion) in the sample.\nThe detection limit is usually demonstrated by measuring low \nconcentrations of the analyte and showing that a response is \nobtained.\nQuantitation Limit\nThe quantitation limit of an individual analytical procedure is \nthe lowest amount of analyte in a sample which can be \nquantitatively determined with suitable precision and accuracy. \nThe quantitation limit is a parameter of quantitative assays for\nlow levels of compounds in sample matrices, and is used \nparticularly for the determination of impurities and/or \ndegradation products.\n382\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP2022\n2.5; 1.1. POLYMORPHISM\n[t is usually expressed as. the concentration of analyte (e.g', \npercentage, parts per billion) in the sample. .\nThe quantitation limit is usually demonstrated by measuring \n|0v; concentrations of the analyte and showing that a \nrepeatable response is obtained."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Reproducibility\nReproducibility expresses the precision between laboratories \n(collaborative studies, usually applied to standardisation of \nmethodology).\nReproducibility is usually demonstrated by means of an inter­\nlaboratory trial.\nDetection Limit\nThe detection limit of an analytical procedure is the lowest \nconcentration of analyte in a sample that can be detected but \nnot necessarily quantitated as an exact value.\nThe detection limit is usually expressed as the concentration \nof analyte (e.g., percentage, parts per billion) in the sample.\nThe detection limit is usually demonstrated by measuring low \nconcentrations of the analyte and showing that a response is \nobtained.\nQuantitation Limit\nThe quantitation limit of an individual analytical procedure is \nthe lowest amount of analyte in a sample which can be \nquantitatively determined with suitable precision and accuracy. \nThe quantitation limit is a parameter of quantitative assays for\nlow levels of compounds in sample matrices, and is used \nparticularly for the determination of impurities and/or \ndegradation products.\n382\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP2022\n2.5; 1.1. POLYMORPHISM\n[t is usually expressed as. the concentration of analyte (e.g', \npercentage, parts per billion) in the sample. .\nThe quantitation limit is usually demonstrated by measuring \n|0v; concentrations of the analyte and showing that a \nrepeatable response is obtained."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/reserpine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Reserpine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Reserpine Injection\nReserpine Injection is a sterile solution of Reserpine in Water \nfor Injections prepared with the aid of a suitable acid. It may \ncontain suitable antioxidants.\nReserpine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of reserpine, \nww' : \n■\nUsual strengths. 1 mg per ml; 2.5 mg per ml.\nExtract a suitable volume of the injection containing 16 mg of \nReserpine with 10 ml of chloroform and evaporate the \nchloroform layer to. dryness. The residue complies with the \nfollowing tests..\nTest A may be omitted if tests B, C, D and E are carried out. \nTests B, C, D and E may be omitted if test A is carried out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with reserpine IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of reserpine.\nB. Dilute 1 ml of a 0.2 per cent w/v solution in chloroform to\n100 ml with ethanol (95per cent). When examined immediately \nafter preparation, in the range 230 nm tb 360 nm (2.4.7), the \nresulting solution shows an absorption maximum at about \n268 nm; absorbance at about 268 nm, about 0.55. Over the \nrange 288 nm to 295 nm, the spectrum exhibits a slight minimum \nand then a shoulder or a slight maximum; absorbance oyer \nthis range, about 0.34.\nC. To about 1 mg add 0.1 ml of a 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium molybdate in sulphuric acid; a yellow colour is \nproduced which changes to blue within 2 minutes.\nD. To 1 mg add 0.2 ml of a freshly prepared 1 per cent w/v \nsolution of vanillin in hydrochloric acid;-, a: pink- colour \ndevelops within 2 minutes.\nE. Mix about-0.5 mg with 5 mg of 4-dimethylaminobenzal- \ndehyde and 0;2 ml of glacial acetic acid and add 0.2 ml of \nsulphuric acid; a green, colour is produced; Add 1 ml of \nglacial acetic acid; the colour changes to red.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 71.5 Endotoxin \nUnits per mg of reserpine.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated .under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Protect the solutions from light throughout the assay.\n3477\nwww.webofpharma.com\nRESERPINE TABLETS\nMeasure a volume of the injection containing about 10 mg of \nReserpine and dilute with a 2 per cent w/v solution of citric \nacid to 100.0 ml: Extract 10:0 ml of the solution for \n2 minutes with three quantities, each of 15 ml; of chloroform. \nWash the combined extracts with 10 ml of a 1 per cent w/v \nsolution oisodium bicarbonate, add sufficient chloroform to \nproduce 50.0 ml, mix and evaporate 10.0 ml to dryness on a \nwater-bath. Dissolve the residue in 10 ml of ethanol (95 per \ncent), add 2 ml of 0.25 Msulphuric acid and 10 ml of ethanol \n(95 per cent) and warm gently to dissolve. Cool, dilute to\n100.0 ml with ethanol (95per cent) and dilute 5,6 ml to 50.0 ml \nwith the same solvent (solution A). Transfer 10.0 fnl to a bdiling \ntube, add 2 ml of 0.25 M sulphuric acid and 2 ml of a freshly \nprepared 0.3 per cent w/v solution of sodiumnitrite, mix and \nheat in a water-bath at 55° for 35 minutes. Cool, add 1 ini'of a \nfreshly prepared 5 per cent w/v solution of sulphamic acid \nand dilute to 25.0 ml with ethanol (95percent). Measure the \nabsorbance of the resulting solution at the maximum at about \n388 nm (2.4.7), using as the blank a solution prepared by \ntreating a further 10,0 ml of solution Ain the same manner and \nat the same time but omitting the sodium nitrite solution.\nCalculate the content of C33H40N2O9 from the , absorbance \nobtained by repeating the operation using reserpine IPRS in \nplace ofthe substance under examination.\nStorage. Store protected from light in single dose (or if \nstabilising agents are present, in multiple dose) containers.\nReserpine Tablets\nReserpine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of reserpine, \nC33H40N2O9.\nUsual strengths. 100 pg; 250 pg; 500 pg; 1 mg."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Reserpine Injection\nReserpine Injection is a sterile solution of Reserpine in Water \nfor Injections prepared with the aid of a suitable acid. It may \ncontain suitable antioxidants.\nReserpine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of reserpine, \nww' : \n■\nUsual strengths. 1 mg per ml; 2.5 mg per ml.\nExtract a suitable volume of the injection containing 16 mg of \nReserpine with 10 ml of chloroform and evaporate the \nchloroform layer to. dryness. The residue complies with the \nfollowing tests..\nTest A may be omitted if tests B, C, D and E are carried out. \nTests B, C, D and E may be omitted if test A is carried out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with reserpine IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of reserpine.\nB. Dilute 1 ml of a 0.2 per cent w/v solution in chloroform to\n100 ml with ethanol (95per cent). When examined immediately \nafter preparation, in the range 230 nm tb 360 nm (2.4.7), the \nresulting solution shows an absorption maximum at about \n268 nm; absorbance at about 268 nm, about 0.55. Over the \nrange 288 nm to 295 nm, the spectrum exhibits a slight minimum \nand then a shoulder or a slight maximum; absorbance oyer \nthis range, about 0.34.\nC. To about 1 mg add 0.1 ml of a 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium molybdate in sulphuric acid; a yellow colour is \nproduced which changes to blue within 2 minutes.\nD. To 1 mg add 0.2 ml of a freshly prepared 1 per cent w/v \nsolution of vanillin in hydrochloric acid;-, a: pink- colour \ndevelops within 2 minutes.\nE. Mix about-0.5 mg with 5 mg of 4-dimethylaminobenzal- \ndehyde and 0;2 ml of glacial acetic acid and add 0.2 ml of \nsulphuric acid; a green, colour is produced; Add 1 ml of \nglacial acetic acid; the colour changes to red.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 71.5 Endotoxin \nUnits per mg of reserpine.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated .under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Protect the solutions from light throughout the assay.\n3477\nwww.webofpharma.com\nRESERPINE TABLETS\nMeasure a volume of the injection containing about 10 mg of \nReserpine and dilute with a 2 per cent w/v solution of citric \nacid to 100.0 ml: Extract 10:0 ml of the solution for \n2 minutes with three quantities, each of 15 ml; of chloroform. \nWash the combined extracts with 10 ml of a 1 per cent w/v \nsolution oisodium bicarbonate, add sufficient chloroform to \nproduce 50.0 ml, mix and evaporate 10.0 ml to dryness on a \nwater-bath. Dissolve the residue in 10 ml of ethanol (95 per \ncent), add 2 ml of 0.25 Msulphuric acid and 10 ml of ethanol \n(95 per cent) and warm gently to dissolve. Cool, dilute to\n100.0 ml with ethanol (95per cent) and dilute 5,6 ml to 50.0 ml \nwith the same solvent (solution A). Transfer 10.0 fnl to a bdiling \ntube, add 2 ml of 0.25 M sulphuric acid and 2 ml of a freshly \nprepared 0.3 per cent w/v solution of sodiumnitrite, mix and \nheat in a water-bath at 55° for 35 minutes. Cool, add 1 ini'of a \nfreshly prepared 5 per cent w/v solution of sulphamic acid \nand dilute to 25.0 ml with ethanol (95percent). Measure the \nabsorbance of the resulting solution at the maximum at about \n388 nm (2.4.7), using as the blank a solution prepared by \ntreating a further 10,0 ml of solution Ain the same manner and \nat the same time but omitting the sodium nitrite solution.\nCalculate the content of C33H40N2O9 from the , absorbance \nobtained by repeating the operation using reserpine IPRS in \nplace ofthe substance under examination.\nStorage. Store protected from light in single dose (or if \nstabilising agents are present, in multiple dose) containers.\nReserpine Tablets\nReserpine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of reserpine, \nC33H40N2O9.\nUsual strengths. 100 pg; 250 pg; 500 pg; 1 mg."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Powder a few tablets and extract with chloroform. Evaporate \nthe extract to dryness, add 0.1 ml bf a 0.1 per cent w/v solution \nof sodium molybdate in sulphuric acid', a yellow colour is \nproduced which changes to blue within 2 minutes.\nB. Powder a few tablets and extract with chloroform. Evaporate \nthe extract to dryness, add 0.2 ml of a freshly prepared 1 per \ncent w/v solution of vanillin in hydrochloric acid', a pink \ncolour develops within 2 minutes.\nTests • : ' \n' \n' 'An J:: - r; ?•\nUniformity of content. Protect the solutions from, light \nthroughout the test. \n? b\nPowder one tablet, disperse in 10 ml of a 2 per cent w/v solution \nof citric acid and extract for 2 minutes with three quantities,\n3478\nIP 20225\neach of 5 ml, of chloroforms filter the extracts through a plUg \nof cotton moistened with chloroform. Wash the chlorofo^ \nextracts with 10 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of sodium ■ \nbicarbonate and evaporate the chlorofonn extracts to diyness \non a water-bath. For tablets containing upto 250 pg \nReserpine per tablet, dissolve the residue in 10.0ml of ethanol\n(95 per cent). For tablets containing more than 250 pg of \nReserpine per tablet, dissolve the residue in a suitable volume \nof ethanol (95 per cent) to give a concentration bf250 gg of \nReserpine per 10.0 ml; add 2 ml of 0.25 Msulphuric acid and \n2 ml of a freshly prepared 0.3 per cent w/v solution efi sodium \nnitrite, mix and heat in a water-bath at 55° for 35 minutes. Cool, \nadd 1 ml of a freshly prepared 5 per cent w/v solution of \nsulphamic acid and dilute to 25.0 ml, with ethanol (95 per \ncent). Measure the absorbance of the resulting solution at \nthe maximum at about 388 nm (2.4,7), using as the blank a \nsolution prepared by treating a further 10.0 ml of solution A in \nthe same manner and at the Same time but omitting the sodium \nnitrite solution.\nCalculate the content of C33H4oN209 in the tablet from the \nabsorbance obtained by repeating the operation using \nreserpine IPRS in place bf the substance under examination.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Protect the solutions from light throughoutthe dssay.\nWeigh a quantity of the powdered tablets containing about\n1 mg of Reserpine, add 10 ml of a 2 per cent w/v solution of \ncitric acid and extract for 2 minutes with three quantities, \neach of 15 ml, of chloroform. Wash the combined extracts \nwith 10 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of sodium bicarbonate, \nadd sufficient chloroform to produce 50.0 ml and evaporate\n10.0 ml to dryness on a water-bath. Dissolve the residue in \n10 mF of ethanol (95 percent) and add 2 ml of 0.25 A/sulphuric \nacid and 10 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and warm gently to \ndissolve. Cool, dilute to 100.0 ml with ethanol (95 per cent) \nand dilute 5.0 ml to 50.0 ml with the same solvent (solution A). \nTransfer 10.0 ml to a boiling tube, add 2 ml of 0.25 Msulphuric \nacid and 2 ml of a freshly prepared 0.3 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium nitrite, mix and heat in a water-bath at 55° for 35 minutes:\nCool, add 1 ml of a freshly prepared 5 per cent w/v solution of \nsulphamic acid and dilute to 25.0 ml with ethanol (95 per­\ncent). Measure the absorbance of the resulting solution at \nthe maximum at about 388 nm (2.4.7), using as the blank a \nsolution prepared by treating a further 16.0 ml of solution Ain \nthe same manner and at the same time but omitting the sodium \nnitrite solution.\nCalculate the content of C33II.30N2O9 from the absorbance \nobtained by repeating the operation using reserpine. IPRSif \nplace of the substance under examination. .\nStorage. Store protected from light. . .\nwww.webofpharma.com\nRIBAVIRIN\nIP 2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/reserpine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Reserpine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Reserpine\n1\nA. Mol. WL 608.7 v.|\nC33H40N2O9\nReserpine is methyl II, 17a-dimethoxy-18P-[(3,4;5-tri- \nmethoxybcnzoyl)oxy]-3p,20a-yohimbane-16p-carhoxylate i\nReserpine contains not less than 99.0 per cent and ndtihiore\nthan 101.0 per cent of total alkaloids andnot lessithan 98.0 per | \ncent and not more than 1,02.0 per cent of reserpine, \n1\nboth calculated on the dried basis. \n' ‘\nCategory.Antihypertensive. ....... ” ' ? 'J \nyg\nDescription. White to slightly yellow small crystals ora . | \ncrystalline powder which darkens slowly bn exposure tb light. . |"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Reserpine\n1\nA. Mol. WL 608.7 v.|\nC33H40N2O9\nReserpine is methyl II, 17a-dimethoxy-18P-[(3,4;5-tri- \nmethoxybcnzoyl)oxy]-3p,20a-yohimbane-16p-carhoxylate i\nReserpine contains not less than 99.0 per cent and ndtihiore\nthan 101.0 per cent of total alkaloids andnot lessithan 98.0 per | \ncent and not more than 1,02.0 per cent of reserpine, \n1\nboth calculated on the dried basis. \n' ‘\nCategory.Antihypertensive. ....... ” ' ? 'J \nyg\nDescription. White to slightly yellow small crystals ora . | \ncrystalline powder which darkens slowly bn exposure tb light. . |"},{"name":"Identification","text":"•.<..•■ \\ •Ayh'ivy\n',j • < 'y • ( 1 i \n; ■' f • ' , 7 '• s J s \n\\ ■ '• ’-’'i ' • G ■ i1\nTest A may be omitted if tests B, C, D and E are carried out. | \nTests B, C, D and E may be omitted if test A is carried out. ||\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). |\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with reseipmelPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of reserpine . \n' / \n|\nB. Dilute 1 mlof a 0.2 per cent w/v solution in chloroform^ |\n100.0 ml with ethanol (95 per cent). When examined | \nimmediately after preparation, in the range 230 nm to 360 nm |; \n(2.4:7),1 the resulting'solution shows an absorption.maximum g \nat about 268 nm; absorbance at about 268 nm. about 0.55.\nOver the range 288 nm to 295 nm; the spectrum'exhibits a J \nslight minimum and . then a shoulder or a: slight maximum; | \nabsorbance over this range, about 0.34. \nvym ••\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nRESERPINE INJECTION\nq To about 1 mg add 0.1 ml of a 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \n$0diurnmolybdaiein sulphuric acid', a yellow colour is\nproduced which changes to blue within 2 minutes.\nP To 1 mg add 0.2 ml of a freshly prepared 1 per cent w/v \nsolution of vanillin in hydrochloric acid', a pink colour \ndevelops within 2 minutes.\nE Mix about 0.5 mg with 5 mg of 4<limethylaminobenzal- \ndehyde ezodH.2 m\\ ol glacial acetic ac^ an4 add 0.2 ml of \nsulphuric acid; ,a green colour is produced. Add 1 ml of \nglacial acetic .acid; the colour changes to red."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Specific optical rotation (2.4.22). --128° to-116°, determined \nin a solution prepared immediately before use by dissolving \n0.25 gin sufficient chloroform to produce 25 ml.\nOxidation products. Absorbance of a 0.02 per cent w/v solution \nin glacial acetic acid at about 388 nm, measured immediately \nafter preparation, not more than 0.10 (2.4.7).\nSulphated ash (213.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss bn drying (2,4,19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 0.5 g by drying in an oven over phosphorus pentoxide at \n60° atapressure not exceeding 0.7 kPa for 3 hours.\nAssay. J^pr total, alkaloids — Weigh 0.5 g and dissolve in a \nmixture of 40 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic acid and 6 ml of \nacetic anhydride. Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric acid, \ndetermining the cnd-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry \nout a? blanktitration.\n1 ml of (1. I M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.06087 g of \ntotal alkaloids. '\nFor reserpirte, C3ilf9 — Carry out the following\nprocedure protected from light. Weigh 25.0 mg, moisten with \n2 nil of ethanol (95 per cen/J) ;add 2 ml of 0.25 M sulphuric \nacid and 10 ml of ethanol (9.5 per cent) and warm gently to \ndissolve. Cool, dilute to .100.0, ml with ethanol (95 per-cent)\ndilute 5.0 ml to 50.0 ml with the same solvent (solution A). \nTransfer 10.0 ml to a boiling tube, add 2 ml of 0.25 Msulphuric \n*?^apd2 ml of a freshly prepared 0.3 per cent w/v solution of \nsodiumnitrite,vci\\x and heat in a water^bath at 55° for 35 minutes.\nadd, 1 ml of a freshly prepared 5 per cent w/v solution of \n^Pbamlcacidcinddilute to 25.0 ml with ethanol (95 per\nMeasure the absorbance. of the resulting .solution at \nmaximuni at about 38,8 nm (2.4.7), using as.the blank a \n^Mbhprepared by. treating a further 10.0 ml of solution A in \nthe same manner and at the same time but omitting the sodium \nnitrite solution.\npH(2.4.24).3.0to4.0.\na^afe the content of CMI l40N2O9 from the absorbance \n0 ^ined by repeating the operation using reserpine IPRS in \nP ace of’the substance under examination.\nStorage. Store protected from light."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ribavirin-for-inhalation","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ribavirin for Inhalation"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ribavirin for Inhalation\nRibavirin for Inhalation is a sterile ribavirin consisting of \nRibavirin with or without excipients. It is filled in a sealed \ncontainer.\nThe inhalation solution is constituted by dissolving the \ncontents of the sealed container in the requisite amount of \nsterilewaterfor injection, immediately before use.\nThe'constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nCldri^ &f solution and particulate matter stated under \nInhalation Preparations. \n' j\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter: preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer. :\nRibavirin inhalation Solution contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nWWlr':\nThe content of the .sealed container complies with the \nrequirements stated under Inhalation Preparations and with \nthe following requirements."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Ribavirin for Inhalation\nRibavirin for Inhalation is a sterile ribavirin consisting of \nRibavirin with or without excipients. It is filled in a sealed \ncontainer.\nThe inhalation solution is constituted by dissolving the \ncontents of the sealed container in the requisite amount of \nsterilewaterfor injection, immediately before use.\nThe'constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nCldri^ &f solution and particulate matter stated under \nInhalation Preparations. \n' j\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter: preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer. :\nRibavirin inhalation Solution contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nWWlr':\nThe content of the .sealed container complies with the \nrequirements stated under Inhalation Preparations and with \nthe following requirements."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate'WithtszVicd gel G. \n'\nMobtie phase. A mixture of 20 volumes of 0,1 M ammonium \nf^df^and 90 volumes of acetonitrile.\n^stsolution. TTi^oIve ribavirin inhalation solution containing \nabqut 0.1 g of Ribavirin in 10 ml of water. :\nRIBAVIRIN INHALATION SOLUTION\nReference solution. A 1.0 per cent w/v solution of ribavirin \nIPRS in water. ■ c < .\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air for 15 minutes, and spray the plate with the \nmixture of 0.5 ml of anisaldehyde, 0.5 ml of sulphuric acid, \n0.1 ml of glacial acetic acid and 9 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) \nand heat at 105° for 40 minutes and allow to cool.The principal \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution is \nsimilar in position, colour and size to that in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution.\nB. In the Assay, the principal’peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\nTest solution. A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of ribavirin in the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution (a). A 0.000125 per cent w/v solution of\nribavirin in the mobile phase.\nReference solution (bf A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of \nribavirin IPRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 7.8 mm, packed with\nstrong cation-exchange resin of sulphonated, cross­\nlinked styrene-divinylbenzene co-polymer in the \nhydrogen form (7 to 11 pm) (Such as Aminex HPAH),\n- column temperature: 40°, \n. -v’\n- mobile phase: water, adjusted to pH 2.5 with sulphuric\nacid,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n■ spectrophotometer set at 207 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor for the principal peak is not less than 0.7 and not \nmore than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than the area of principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0.25 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 4 times the area of the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(1.0 percent).\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Reconstitute the contents of one container as \ninstructed on the label using a measured volume of diluent,\n3481\nwww.webofpharma.com\nRIBAVIRIN inhalation solution\ndilute a suitable volume of the solution to 200 volumes with \nthe mobile phase to produce a solution containing .0.1 per \ncent w/y of Ribavirin.mix and dilute 5 volumes ofthe resulting \nsolution to 20 volumes with the mobile phase. ;\nReference solution. A 0.025 per cent w/v solution of ribavirin \nIPRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n-- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 7.8 mm, packed with\n; ; strong cation-exchange resin of sulphonated, cross­\nlinked styrene-diviriylbenzerte co-polymer in the \nhydrogen form (7 to 1 lpm) (Such, as Aminex l I PAH),.\n- column temperature: 40°, \n> <\n- mobile phase: water, adjusted to pH 2.5 with sulphuric\nacid,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 207 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not less than 0.7 and not more than 1.5.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CaH]2N4O5 in inhalation solution. ,\nLabelling. The label of the sealed container states (1) the \nprocedure for preparing the solution, (2) the delivery system \nto be used for the constituted solution (3) the conditions \nunder which the constituted solution should be stored.\nStorage. Store procted from light and moisture,\nRifabutin\nH3C‘\nC46H62N4O,! \n■' \nMol.Wt.847.0\n3482\nIP 2022 :1\nRifabutin is (95, 12£, 145,-15/?, 165,17/?,18/?,19/?,205,21$ ? \n,22E’,24Z)-6,16,18,20-Tetrahydroxy-1 ’isobutyl-14-methoxy. ■ \n7,9,15,17,19,21,25-heptamethyIspirol[9,4-(epoxypentadeca f \n[1,1 l,13]trienimino)-27/-luro[22,32:7,8]naphthfl,2-t/jimidazo]e^ \n2,42 -pipcrid ine]-5,10,26-(3/7,9//)-trione-16-yl- acetate. ' ! 4\nRifabutin contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0percentofC46H62N4Oli calculated on the anhydrous ■\nbasis. \n■■ ■: c-y--, ■ ■ ■;\nCategory. Antituberculosis. \n-y ; y\nDescription. An amorphous red-violet powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rifabutin IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of rifabutin.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a); \n'\nA; Disperse; a quantity of capsule content in methanol to \nobtain absolution containing 0.002 per cent w/v, filter and\nexamine in the range 200 to 400 nm(2.4.7); The spectra obtained\nRIFABUTIN CAPSULES\nwith the test solution exhibits the maxima at the same \nwavelength with that ofthe reference solution.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.01 Mhydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume ofthe medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtered solution, suitably diluted with \nthe medium if necessary, at the maximum at about 280 nm \n(2.4.7). Calculate the content of C^H^N^n in the medium \nfrom the absorbance obtained by using a solution bf known \nconcentration of rifabutin IPRS.\nQ. Not less, than 75 per cent of the stated amount of \nC^H^^On.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), as described under Assay using the followi"},{"name":"Tests","text":"N-isobutylpiperidone. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silicagel G.\nSolvent mixture: Equal volumes of chloroform and methanol\nMobile phase. A mixture of 100 volumes of hexane and 30 \nvolumes of acetone. \n■ \n■\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml ofthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution. Prepare 0.0005,0.001,0.002,0.005 and 0.01 \nper cent w/v solutions of N-isobutylpiperidone IPRS in the \nsolvent mixture.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air and expose the plate to iodine vapour for \nabout 5 minutes, then spray with starph solution and allow to \nstand for 5 minutes. No spot in the chromatogram of the test \nsolution at Rf value corresponding to ,V-isobulylpipcridone is \nmore intense than that of the principal spot observed in the \nchromatogram obtained with die reference solution containing \n0.005 per cent solution (0.5 per cent);\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), as described under Assay using the following \nmodifications.\nInject the test solution. The area of any impurity detected-at a \nrelative retention time of about 0.5,0.6,0.8 or 1.4 with respect \nto rifabutin peak is not more than 1.0 per cent, the area of any \nother secondary peak is not more than 0.5 per cent and the \nsum of areas; of all the secondary peaks is not more than \n3.0 per cent, calculated by area normalisation. > A\nwww.webofpharma.com\n1P2022\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 2.5 per cent.\nAssay/petermine by liquid chn)mat(^raPhy (2-4.14).\nTest \nDissolve 25 mg of the substance under\nexamination in 5 ml of acetonitrile and dilute to 50.0 ml with \nthe mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 25 mg ci rifabutin IPRS in 5 \nnd ofaceibhlirile and dilute to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 10 mg of rifabutin IPRS \nand 2 ml of methanol, add 1 ml of 2 Msodium hydroxide and \nallow to stand for about 4 minutesi Add 1 ml of 2 M \nhydrochloric acid, and dilute to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n_ astainless steel column!2.5 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of equal volumes of acetonitrile\nand 0.1 M monobasic potassium phosphate, adjusted \npH6.5 with 2 Msodium hydroxide,\n- flow rate: I ml per minute, \n1spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nThe, relative, retention time with reference to rifabutin for a \nmajor peak for a degradant, two minor peaks for degradants \nareabout0.5,6.6arid 0.8 respectively.\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the rifabutin peak and the degradant \npeak eluting at d relative retention time of about 0.8 is not less \nthan1'3 in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b) and the column efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical \nplates and the relative standard deviation for replicate \ninjectidnkis not more than 2.0 per cent in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a).\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate.the content of C46H62N4O11.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nRifabutin Capsules\nRifabutin Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not\nmore than110.0 per cent of the stated amount of rifabutin,\nj.\nUsual strength. 1,50 mg."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ribavirin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ribavirin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ribavirin\nCiHaNM:\" ' ' . \nMol.Wt 244.2\nRibavirin is i-P-D-ribofuranosyl-l#-l,2,4-triazole-3- \ncarboxamide.\nRibavirin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CSH12N4O5, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiviral.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder. It \nshowspolymorph ism (2.5.11)."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Ribavirin\nCiHaNM:\" ' ' . \nMol.Wt 244.2\nRibavirin is i-P-D-ribofuranosyl-l#-l,2,4-triazole-3- \ncarboxamide.\nRibavirin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CSH12N4O5, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiviral.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder. It \nshowspolymorph ism (2.5.11)."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with ribavirin IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of ribavirin.\nTests’.';'//7.\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 6.5, determined in a 2.0 per cent w/v solution \nincarbondioxide-freewater.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). - 3 7° to - 33°, determined in \na 1.0 per cent w/v solution, use freshly prepared solution.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(24:14).\nTest solution.Dissolve 50 mg of substance under examination \nin70 ml o£ water and dilute to 100.0 ml with water.\nReference solution (a). In order to produce l-j3-D-rib- \nofiiranosyl-l/Z-l ,2,4-triazole-3-carboxylic acid (ribavirin \nimpurityA)insitu, mix; 5,0 ml of the test solution and 5.0 ml of \na A2 per cent w/v solution of sodium hydroxide and allow to \nstand for 90minutes. Neutralise with 5.6 ml of a 10.3 per cent \nw^v so^uribn of hydrochloric acid and mix well.\nReference solution (b) Dilute 1.0 ml ofthe test solution to\nIM-Oml with water. Dilute l.Omlofthe solution to 10.0ml with \nwater.\nReferencesolution (c) A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of \nribavirin IRRS in water.\nChromatographic system ;,\n- a stainless steel column? 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nend-capped octadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica \n(3 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. dissolve 1.0 g of anhydrous sodium\nsulphate in 950 ml of water, add 2.0 ml of a 5 per cent \nv/v solution of orthophosphoric acid, adjusted to pH\n2.8 with a 5 per cent v/v solution of orthophosphoric \nacid and dilute to 1000 ml with water,\nB. 5 volumes of acetonitrile and 95 \nvolumes of mobile phase A, \n< \n, / :\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 220 nm,\n- injection volume: 5 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent y/v)\n0\n100\n0\n15\n100\n0\n25\n0\n100\n35\n0\n100\n38\n100\n0\nThe relative retention time with reference to ribavirin (retention \ntime is about 6 minutes) for ribavirin impurity Ais about 0.8. \nThe correction factor for ribavirin impurity A is 2.3.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to ribavirin and ribavirin \nimpurity A is not less than 4.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak due to ribavirin, impurity A is not more than \ntwice the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.2 per cent). The area of \nany other, secondary peak is not more than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b) (0.1 per cent). The sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 3 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b),(0.3 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less \nthan 0.5 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.05 per cent))\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 4.0 g in 20 ml of water with \nheating if necessary and use 10 ml of the solution. The resulting \nsolution complies with the limit test for heavy metals, Method \nB(10ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 5 hour.\n3479\nwww.webofpharma.com\nRIBAVIRIN CAPSULES\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2'4.14) same as \ndescribed. underRelated substances with the= following \nmodification. ■ ■ bv.\nCalculate the content of C8Hi2N4O5. ,\nInject reference solution (c) and the test solution.\nStorage. Store protected from light.' \n''■ :;\nRibavirin Capsules\nRibavirin Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the; stated amount of ribavirin, \nC8HI2N4O5.\nUsual strengths. 100 mg; 200 mg. /\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket), \nMedium. 900 ml of water, . . . .\nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest. solution. ■. Use the filtrate, dilute if necessary, with the \ndissolution medium.\nReference solution. A 0.00225 per cent w/v solution of \nribavirin IPRS in the; dissolution medium.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 30 cm x 7.8 mm, packed with a\nstrong cation-exchange resin consisting of sulphonated\n. cross-linked styrene-divinylbenzene copolymer in the\nhydrogen form (9 pm),\n- \n- column temperature: 65°, \n.7\n- mobile phase: water, adjusted to pH 2.5 with sulphuric\nacid,. \\ ,.t ... ; \n/ \n' ' \n'.\n- \nflow rate: 1.5 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 207. nm,\n- injectionvolume:20ul. •' \n■\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. .\nCalculate the content of CgHjjN^Oj in the medium.\n3480 \nmBImIm\nI\nif 2022 ' I'\nQ. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of f H12N n 7/\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography li \n(2.4.14). \n;• j\nv.' \n' ■\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of the mixed content ? i \ncontaining 50 mg of Ribavirin in 50 ml of the mobile phase • \nwith the aid of ultrasound for 20 minutes with intermittent \nshaking and dilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase, filter. S\nReference solution. A 0.0025 per cent w/v solution of ribavirin -W - \nIPRS in the mobile phase. \n; f J\nChromatographic system \n: iff.\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 7.8 mm, packed with a iSf\nstrong cation-exchange resin consisting of di \nsulphonated cross-linked styrene-divinylbenzene \ncopolymer in the hydrogen form (7 gm), \n. .’W|\n- \nccdumn temperature: 65°, \nfg\n- mobile phase: water, adjusted to pH 2.5 wiiti sulphuric i t\nv ■■ acid, ' : \n.■' \n.- ■■ ■ f g\n- - flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \nI\n- spectrophotometer set at 207 nm, ..... \ny|\n- injection volume: 10 pl. \n” .jg\nName \nRelative .. Correction . y|\n_________________________ retention time factor .'■'■I\nRibavirin  \n1,0\nRibose triazololc carboxylic acid' 0.7 \n1.43 • c f |\n1 l-p-U-Ribofuranosyl-IH-1,2,4-triazole-3-carboxyIic acid.'* \n1 : \nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the ,1 \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any | \npeak corresponding to ribavirin impurity A is not more than | \n0.05 times the area of principal peak in the chromatogram | \nobtained with the reference solution (0.25 per cent), the area | \nof any other secondary peak is not more than 0.02 times the : | \narea ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with | \nthe reference solution (0.1 per cent) and the sum of the areas | \nof all the secondary, peaks is not more than 0.2 times the area | \nof the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the |\nreference solution (1.0 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area | \nless than 0.01 times the area of the principal peak in the | \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (0.05 per | \ncent). , \n... ,'.j j |\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). 1 .yjff |\nTest solution. Weigh and mix the content of 20. capsules, j \nDisperse a quantity ofthe mixed content containing 50 mgof | \nRibavirin in 50 ml of the mobile phase, with the aid of ultrasound g \nfor 20 minutes with intermittent shaking and dilute to 100.0 tri .. |f \nwith the mobile phase. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 100.0nn | \nwith the mobile phase. \ng\nReference solution; A 0.0025 per cent w/v solution of ribavirin \nIPRS in the mobile phase. \nda vmAb\nI\n \nV §\nA: g\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIf>2022\nUse chromatographic system as described under Related \nsubstances. \n'\nreference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 1.5 and the relative standard \ndeviation forreplicateinjections is not more than 2.0 per cent,\n[nject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C8H12N4O5 in the capsules.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature \nbetween 15° to 30°."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/riboflavin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Riboflavin"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Riboflavin\nLactoflavin; Vitamin B2\nMol. Wt. 376.4\nRiboflavin is 3,1O-dihydro-7,8-dimethyl-l0-[(2S,3S,4R)-\n2,3,4,5-tetrahydroxypentyl]benzo[g]pteridine-2,4-dione.\nRiboflavin contains not less than98.0 per cellt and not more\nthali 101.0 per celit of CI7H2oN406, calCulated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. B-group vitamin.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nDose. Prophylactic, 1 to 4 mg daily; therapeutic, 5 to 10 mg\ndaily.\nDescription. A yellow to orange-yellow, crystalline powder;\nodour, slight."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Riboflavin\nLactoflavin; Vitamin B2\nMol. Wt. 376.4\nRiboflavin is 3,1O-dihydro-7,8-dimethyl-l0-[(2S,3S,4R)-\n2,3,4,5-tetrahydroxypentyl]benzo[g]pteridine-2,4-dione.\nRiboflavin contains not less than98.0 per cellt and not more\nthali 101.0 per celit of CI7H2oN406, calCulated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. B-group vitamin.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nDose. Prophylactic, 1 to 4 mg daily; therapeutic, 5 to 10 mg\ndaily.\nDescription. A yellow to orange-yellow, crystalline powder;\nodour, slight."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with riboflavine RS\nor with the reference spectrum ofriboflavine.\nB. Dissolve about 1 mg in 100 ml ofwater. The solution has a\npale greenish yellow colour by transmitted light and an intense\nyellowish green fluorescence by reflected light, which\ndisappears on addition ofmineral acids or allcalis."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24).5.5 to 7.2, determined in a saturated solution.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). -115° to -135°, determined\nin a 0.5 percentw/v solution in carbonate-free 0.05 Msodium\nhydroxide. Measure the angle ofrotation within 30 minutes of\npreparing the solution.\nLight absorption (2.4.7). Dilute a suitable volume ofthe final\nsolution obtained in theAssay with an equal volume ofwater.\nWhen examined in the range 210 urn to 460 urn, the resulting\nsolution exhibits maxima at about 223 urn, 267 urn, 373 nm and\n444 nm; the ratio ofthe absorbance at the maximum at about\n373 urn to that at about 267 urn, 0.31 to 0.33 and the ratio ofthe\nabsorbance at the maximum at about 444 nm to that at about\n267 urn, 0.36 to 0.39.\nLumiflavine. Shake25 mgwith 10ml ofchlorofOrm for5minutes\nand filter. The filtrate is not more intensely coloured than\nreference solution BYS6 (2.4.1).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 1.5 percent, determined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay. Carry out the procedure in subdued light.\nWeigh accurately about 65 mg and transfer to an amber-glass\n500-ml volumetric flask, suspend in 5 ml of water, ensuring\nthat it is completely wetted. Dissolve in 5 ml of 2 M sodium\nhydroxide. As soon as dissolution is complete add 100 ml of\nwater and 2.5 ml ofglacial acetic acid and dilute to 500.0 ml\nwith water. To 20.0 ml ofthis solution add 3.5 ml ofa 1.4 per\ncent w/v solution of sodium acetate and dilute to 200.0 ml\nwith water. Measure the absorbance ofthe resulting solution\natthe maximum at about 444 nm (2.4.7).\nCalculate thec()ntetlt ()fC17H2oN406 taking 328 as the specific\nabsorbance at 444 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\n2052\nIP 2010"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rifampicin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rifampicin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rifampicin\nRifampin,\nC43H58N4Oi2 ' ’■ \n' ■■ Mol. Wt.823.0\nRifampicin is(1.2Z, l,4£,24£)-(25', 16.9,175,18/?, 197?,20/?, \n21 S,22R,2 3 5)-1,2-dihydro-5,6,9,17,19-pentahydroxy- \n23-meth oxy-2,4,12,16,18,20,22-heptamethyl-8-(4-methyl- \npiperazin-l-yl iminomethyl)-! ,1 l-dioxo-2,7-(epoxypcntadeca-\n1,11,13-trienimino)naphtho[2,l-fi]furan-21-yl acetate.\nRifampicin contains not less than 97.0 per cent arid not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C.rJl5SN.tO|2, calculated on the dried \nbasis. \n■ .w,.w \n7;'. ■’ \n■' 7 ■ w\nCategory. Antitubercular. ,\nDescription. A brick-red to reddish brown, crystalline powder.\n3484'\n2022"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rifampicin\nRifampin,\nC43H58N4Oi2 ' ’■ \n' ■■ Mol. Wt.823.0\nRifampicin is(1.2Z, l,4£,24£)-(25', 16.9,175,18/?, 197?,20/?, \n21 S,22R,2 3 5)-1,2-dihydro-5,6,9,17,19-pentahydroxy- \n23-meth oxy-2,4,12,16,18,20,22-heptamethyl-8-(4-methyl- \npiperazin-l-yl iminomethyl)-! ,1 l-dioxo-2,7-(epoxypcntadeca-\n1,11,13-trienimino)naphtho[2,l-fi]furan-21-yl acetate.\nRifampicin contains not less than 97.0 per cent arid not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C.rJl5SN.tO|2, calculated on the dried \nbasis. \n■ .w,.w \n7;'. ■’ \n■' 7 ■ w\nCategory. Antitubercular. ,\nDescription. A brick-red to reddish brown, crystalline powder.\n3484'\n2022"},{"name":"Identification","text":"|\nA. Determine by infraredabsorptiori spectrophotometry (2.4 5^ I\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rifampicin Ipgg 1 | \nor with the reference spectrum of rifampicin. \n;r;.,.. I\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram | \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the I \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution, ' I\nTests ■ ■ \n...........: \n\"I\n< if ■■■ - ■ ; ■ \n■ - 1 \n; ' ■ ' f : A ?■ ■■ ■ .■ \n’ • :-:?i =r.’ ; r- ; '\npH (2.4.24). 4.5 to 6.5, determined, in a 1.0 pet cent w/v | \nsuspension.., \n..... \n1\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography I \n(2.4.14). \n.m w ■ \n' I\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use. ■\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 10 volumes of a 21.01 per cent \nw/v solution of citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13,61 percent \nw/v solution potassium dihydrogenpAosp/mte, 77 volumes \nof a 17.42 per cent w/v solution of dipotassiym, hydrogen \nphosphate, 640 volumes of water and -250 volumes of\nacetonitrile.\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of powder containing 20 mg \nof Rifampicin, add 10 ml of acetonitrile^ shake and filter. Dilute \n5 ml of the filtrate to 50 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.02 per cent w/v of \nrifampicin quinone ilPRS in. acetonitrile, To 1 ml of the \nsolution, add 1 ml of the test solution and dilute to 100 ml with' \nthe solvcnt.mixture. \n...........,\nChromatographic system \n. ■> \n5\n— a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4,6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), \n; -\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution\ncontaining 0.1 per cent v/v of orthophosphoric acid, \n0.19 per cent w/v of sodium perchlorate, 0.59 per cent \nw/v of citric ufczt/ and 2,09. per cent .w/v of potassium: \ndihydrogen phosphate and 35 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flowrate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \n|\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \nB\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the J \nresolution between rifampicin arid1 rifampicin quinone is not | \nless than4. \n' ’'!i \n■ g\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area g \nof any peak due to rifampicin quinone is not more than |\n1.5 times the area of the peak due to rifampicin quinone in the g \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (1:5 per ’J \ncent); the area of any peak, other than thepeakdue to rifampicin. g. \nand rifampicin quinone, is not more than the areaof the peak | \ndue: to rifampicin in the chromatogram obtained :with: the g\nwww.webofpharma.com\nRIFAMPICIN CAPSULES\nIP 2022\nreference solution (1-0 per cent) and the sum of the areas of \naOy such peaks is not more than 3.5 times the area of the peak \ndue to rifampicin in the chromatogram obtained with the \nreference solution (3.5 percent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 1 -0 g by drying in an oven at 80° at a pressure not exceeding\n^• Shake a quantity .ofthe contents of the capsules containing\ng of Rifampicin with 5 ml of chloroform, filter and evaporate\nthe filtrate to dryness. The residue complies with the following \ntest. \n. . ■ -\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rifampicin IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of rifampicin.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the . chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (a).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium; 900 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter promptly \nthrough a membrane filter disc having an average pore diameter \nnot greater than 1.0 pm, rejecting the first 1 ml of the filtrate. \nDilute the filtrate, if necessary, with the same solvent. Measure \nthe absorbance (2.4.7) of the resulting solution at the maximum \nat about 475 nm. Calculate the content of C43HS8N40i2 in the \nmedium from the absorbance obtained from a solution of \nknown concentration of rifampicin IPRS.\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. 10volumesofa21.01 percent w/v solution \nof citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent w/v solution of \npotassium dihydrogen phosphate, 77 volumes of al 7.42 per\ncent w/v solution of dipotassium hydrogen phosphate, 640 \nvolumes of water and 250 volumes of acetonitrile.\nTest solution; Shake a quantity of the contents of the capsules \ncontaining 200 mg of Rifampicin, with 100 ml of acetonitrile \nand filter. Dilute 5 ml of the filtrate to 50 ml with the solvent \nmixture. \n■■ \n■\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.02 per cent \nw/v of rifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile. Dilute 1.0 ml of the \nsolution to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.01 per cent \nw/v each of rifampicin IPRS and rifampicin quinone IPRS in \nacetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture. ,\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n. - - column temperature: 30°,\n3485\nwww.webofpharma.com\n-mobile phase:, a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution\ncontaining 0.1 per cent v/v of orthophosphoric acid, \n0.19 per cent w/y of sodium perchlorate, 0.5 9 per cent \n.. w/v of citric acid and 2.09 per cent w/v of potassium\ndihydrogenphosphate and 35 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n-< spectrophotometer set at 254 nm, ‘\n; - injection volume: 20 pl.\nThe relative retention times are 1.0, 0.55, 1.25 and 3.56 for \nrifampicin, rifampicin quinone, rifampicin N-oxide and \n3-formylrifamycin SV respectively. The response factors are \n1.00, 1.19, 1"},{"name":"Tests","text":"0.7 kPa for 4 hours.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\n$OTE— Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 10 volumes of a 21.01 per cent\nw/v solution of citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent \nw/v solution of potassium dihydrogen phosphate, 77 volumes \nof a 17.42 per cent w/v solution of dipotassium hydrogen \nphosphate, 640 volumes of water and 250 volumes of\nacetonitrile.\nTest solution. Dissolve 20.0 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of acetonitrile and filter. Dilute 5.0 ml of \nthe filtrate to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.2 per cent w/v of\nrifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile: Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution \nto 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nC43H5JN4O12.\nUse the chromatographic system and the system suitability \nparameters described under the test for Related substances.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculatethecontent of\nStorage. Store protected from light, in an atmosphere of \nnitrogen;\nRifampicin Capsules\nRifampin Capsules\nRifampicin Capsules contain not less than 92.5 per cent and\nnot more than 1,07.5 per cent of the stated amount of rifampicin, \n^'4jH5gN4O12. ’\nUsualstrengths. 150 mg; 300mg; 450 mg; 600 mg.\npH (2.4.24). 4.2 to 4.8.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(24.14). \n‘ \n:\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use.....\nSolvent mixture, A mixture of 10 volumes of a 21.01 per cent ; \nw/v solution of citric acid, 23 volumes of a 13.61 per cent \nw/v solution ofpotassium dihydrogenphosphate, 77 volumes \nof a 17.42 per cent w/v solution'of dipotassium hydrogen \nphosphate, 640 volumes of water and 250 volumes,of\nacetonitrile. \n; 'f\nTest solution. Add 5 ml of water to a quantity of the'oral ?? \nsuspension containing 20 mg of Rifampicin and extract with <\nwww.webofpharma.com\nQuantities, each of 10 ml, of dichloromethane, filter the \nbined extracts and evaporate to dryness at a temperature \nC°texceeding40°. Dissolve the residue in 10 ml of acetonitrile.\nDilute 5-0 ml of the resulting solution to 50 ml with the solvent\nmixture\nneference sohdion (a). A solution containing 0.02 per cent\nof rifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile. Dilute 1.0 ml of the \nsolution to ail 00.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.01 per cent\n5y/v each of nfampicin IPRS and rifampicin quinone IPRS in \nacetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n; a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n~ column temperature: 30°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of a solution\ncontaining 0.1 per cent v/v of orthophosphoric acid,\n0.19 per cent w/v of sodium perchlorate, 0.59 per cent \nw/v of citric acid and 2.09 per cent w/v of potassium \nJ dihydrogen phosphate and 35 volumes of acetonitrile, \n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \n-injection volume: 20 pl.\nThe relative retention times are 1.0, 0.55, 1.25 and 2.61 for \nrifampicin, rifampicin quinone, rifampicin N-oxide and \n3-formylrifamycin SV respectively. Multiply the areas of each \nknown impurity by their response factor. The response factors \nare 1.00,1.19,1.03,1.22 and 1.25 for rifampicin, rifampicin \nquinone, rifampicin N-oxide, Isonicotinyl hydrazone and \n3-formylrifamycin SV respectively.\nRifampin Tablets\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between rifampicin and rifampicin quinone is not \nless than.4; the tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the \ncolumn efficiency is nbt less than 2000 theoretical plates.\nOther Tests; Comply with the tests stated under Oral \nSuspensions.\nRIFAMPICIN TABLETS\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of the oral suspension \ncontaining 150.mg,of rifampicin and dilute to 100.0 ml with \nacetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with \nacetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml ofthe resulting solution to 50.0 ml \nwith the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A 0.15 per cent w/v solution of\nrifampicin IPRS in acetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml of the solution \nto 50.0 ml with acetonitrile. Dilute 10.0 ml ofthe resulting\nsolution to 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.01 per cent\nw/v of each of rifampicin IPRS and rifampicin quinone IPRS \nin acetonitrile. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture.\nUse the chromatographic system and the system suitability \nparameters as described under the test for Related substances.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nDetermine the weight per ml of the suspension (2.4.29) and \ncalculate the content of C43H58N4OI2 weight in volume.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nRifampicin Tablets\nRifampicin Tablets contain not less than 92.5 per cent and not \nmore than 107.5 per cent bf the stated amount of rifampicin, \nc43h58n4o12.\nUsual strength. 150 mg.\nA. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 0.15g\nof Rifampicin with 5 ml of chloroform, filter and evaporate the \nfiltrate to dryness. The residue complies with the following \ntest..\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rifampicin IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of rifampicin.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (a).\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\n3487\nwww.webofpharma.com\nMedium. 900 ml "}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rilpivirine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rilpivirine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rilpivirine\nC22H18N6.HC1 \nMol Wt 402.9\nRilpivirine is 4^{[4-({4-[(£)-2-Cyanovinyl]-2,6-dimethylphehyl} \nami no)pyrimidin-2-yl] amino) benzonitrile. 5\nRilpivirine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and-not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C23H18N6.HCI, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral. \n..\nDescription. A white to pale brown colour powder; ■ m"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rilpivirine\nC22H18N6.HC1 \nMol Wt 402.9\nRilpivirine is 4^{[4-({4-[(£)-2-Cyanovinyl]-2,6-dimethylphehyl} \nami no)pyrimidin-2-yl] amino) benzonitrile. 5\nRilpivirine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and-not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C23H18N6.HCI, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral. \n..\nDescription. A white to pale brown colour powder; ■ m"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with rilpivirine IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of rilpivirine. ■’ u 1\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the'principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nTests . ; .. \n; \n, \n, . .., .ki mi Of-;\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). \n< ■ \n■■■ .t-;. \n'-.•Mvn'/T\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in methanol apA dilute to 100.0 mTwitii methanol\nReference solution. A0.003 per cent w/v solution of rilpivirfye \nIPRS, impurity A IPRS and impurity B IPRS \\r\\ methanol.\nDilute 0.5 ml of the solution td 20.0 ml with methanol.\"\nChromatographic system \n;\nr a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), ,\n- column temperature: 45°, \n- ; i j b\n. , - mobile phase: A. dissolve 3.08 g of ammonium acetate\n. in 1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH 6.0 withacetic acid,\nB. methanol, . \n■. ..o wcT -•\nwww.webofpharma.com\nfl*2022\nRISEDRONATE SODIUM\na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \nflow rate: 0.6 nil per minute,\nSpectrophotometer set at 295nm, \ninjection volume: 10 pl.\nTime\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent\n(in mini)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n40\nWo ■■■■>\n40\n60\n12\n20\n20\n80\n15\n20\n15\n20\n80\n18\n40\n40\n60\n20\n40.\n20\n40\n60\nInject the reference solution.\nInject reference solution. The test is not valid unless the the \nresoIution .'between rilpivirine impurity A (2E)-3-(4-amino- \n3 5dirnethyI phenyl) acrylonitrile hydrochlorid and rilpivirine \nimpurity B(4-(4-chloropyrimidin -2 yl-amino)- benzonitrile) is \nnot less than 5.0.\nInject reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of \npeak due to rilpivirine impurity A is not more than area of \nreference solution (0,3 per cent), the area of peak due to \nrilpivirinempurity B is not more than area of reference solution \n(0.3 per cent), area of any other secondary peak is not more \nthan area of rilpivirine peak in the chromatograpm obtained \nwith the reference solution (0.3 per cent) and the sum of area \nof all the secondary peaks is not more than 3.3 times the area \nof rilpivirine peak in the chromatograpm obtained with the \nreference solution (1.0 per cent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with limit test for heavy \nmetalSjMethodB (20 ppm).\nC7H10NNaO7P2\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nWater (2^3143). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined on\nAssay; Determineby liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\n^e^t solution. \n25 mg of the substance under\nexamination in methanol and dilute to 100.0 ml with methanol. \nDilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with methanol.\nReference solution. A 0.0025 per cent w/v solution of\nIPRS in methanol.\nChromatographic system\n' a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n-1; octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), \n' column temperature: 45°,\n. mobile phase: A. dissolve 3.08 g of ammonium acetate\nin 1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH 6.0 with acetic acid,\nB. a mixture of 50 volumes of acetonitrile\n-v and 50 volumes of methanol,\na gradient programme using the conditions given below,\n- flow rate: 0.6 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 295nm, \n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\nv/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n60\n80\n60\n60\nThe test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 3000 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution and.the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C22Hi8N6.HCl."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/risedronate-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Risedronate Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Risedronate Sodium\nOH\nH°\nH°4\nMoLWt 305.1\nMol. Wt. 323.1\nC7H10NNaO7P2,H2O(Monohydrate)\nMol. Wt 350.1\nC7Hi0NNaO7p2,2.5H2O (Hemi-pentahydrate)\nRisedronate Sodium is l-Hydroxy-2-(3-pyridyl)ethane-\nl,l-diylbis(phosphonic acid) monosodium salt.\nRisedronate Sodium contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C7H10NNaO7P2, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Calcium regulator.\nDescription. Awhite to off-white powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6), \ncompare, the spectrum with that obtained with risedronate \nsodium IPRS or with the reference spectrum of risedronate \nsodium.\nB. A 1.0 percent w/v solution gives, the reactions of sodium \nsalt (2.3.1).\n3497\nwww.webofpharma.com"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Risedronate Sodium\nOH\nH°\nH°4\nMoLWt 305.1\nMol. Wt. 323.1\nC7H10NNaO7P2,H2O(Monohydrate)\nMol. Wt 350.1\nC7Hi0NNaO7p2,2.5H2O (Hemi-pentahydrate)\nRisedronate Sodium is l-Hydroxy-2-(3-pyridyl)ethane-\nl,l-diylbis(phosphonic acid) monosodium salt.\nRisedronate Sodium contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C7H10NNaO7P2, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Calcium regulator.\nDescription. Awhite to off-white powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6), \ncompare, the spectrum with that obtained with risedronate \nsodium IPRS or with the reference spectrum of risedronate \nsodium.\nB. A 1.0 percent w/v solution gives, the reactions of sodium \nsalt (2.3.1).\n3497\nwww.webofpharma.com"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. A. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n_\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in mobile phase A and dilute to 10.0 ml with mobile \nphase A.\nReference solution (a). A 0.0001 per cent w/v solution of \nrisedronate sodium IPRS in mobile phase A.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.1 per cent \nw/v of risedronate sodium IPRS and 0.0001 per cent w/v of \nrisedronate impurity A in mobile phase A. ;\nChromatographic system ,\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (3.5 pm),\n■ ■ mobile phase: A. a mixture of 900 volumes of < buffer\nsolution prepared by dissolving 1.42 g of disodium \nhydrogen orthophosphate, 1.7 g of tetrabutyl \nammonium phosphate and 0.38 g of ethylenediamine \ntetraacetate in 950 ml of water, adjusting to pH 7.5 with \nammonium hydroxide solution .and 100 volumes of \nacetonitrile,\nB. a mixture of 800 volumes of buffer \nsolution and 200 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the condition given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 263 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl; 12\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n100\n0\n10\n100\n0 •: :\n40\n. \n0 ...\n100 : ■\n55 ...\n0\n100 .\n67\n100. -.\n0\n, 75 •\n100\n0\nName\nRelative \nretention time\n3-Pyridyl acetic acid\n0.3\n1.32\nRisedronate impurity A1\n0.51\n1.76\nRisedronate sodium\n1.0\n1 [l-hydroxy-2-(2-pyridinyl)ethylidene] bisphosphonic acid.\nEquilibrate the column with the mobile phase for at least\n2 hours. ■ • \n'•\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between; the peaks-due to risedronate and\nrisedronate impurity A is not less than 5.0. \n■\n3498\nRISEDRONATE SODIUM\nU>2()22\nInject'reference solption (a), and the test solution,.In \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than the area pf the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0.1 percent).\nB. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). . <;\nSolvent mixture. Dissolve 0.46 g of disodium edetate in\n500 ml of water, adjusted to pH 7.5 with 1 Msodium hydroxide.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 25.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution. A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of\nrisedronate sodium impurity B IPRS (3,6-bis[(3-pyridinylf \nmethyl]-2,5-dihydroxy-2,5-dioxido-l, 4,2,5 dioxadipho- \nsphorinane-3,6-diyl]bis[phosphonic acid] disbclium \ntetrahydrate IPRS) in the solvent mixture. J ;\nChromatographic system\n- \na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm packed with \noctadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: dissolve 16.15 g of dibasic potassium\nphosphate and 0.46 g oidisodium edetate in 400 ml of\nwater, add 5 ml of tetrabutyldmmonium- dihydrogen \nphosphate and 1 ml of hydrochloric acid, adjusted to\npH 7.5 With 1 Msodium hydroxide or 1 Mhydrochloric \nacid arid dilute to 480 ml with water, add 20 ml of \nmethanol, ' ' '\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n■ spectrophotometer set at 263 nm, \nwm/w; :\n- injection volume: 10 pl. \n: \n.. , 1\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 1.5. \n. >\nInject the test solution. The area of any peak corresponding \nto risedronate impurity B is not more than 0.1 per cent arid the \narea of any other secondary peak is not more than 0.1'per \ncent. The sum of areas of all the secondary peaks of tests A \nand B is not more than 0.5 per cent, calculated by area \nnormalization. Ignore any peak eluting before risedronate \nimpurity B.\nRelative \nresponse factor\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metal s, Method B (20 ppm).\nWater (2.3.43). 5.5 to 7.5 per cent for monohydrate; 11.9 to\n13.9 per cent for hemi-pentahydrate, determined on about \n0.25g.' \n......\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance, under \nexamination in mobile phase Aand dilute to 10.0 ml withmobile \nphase A.\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of risedronate \nsodium IPRS in mobile phase A.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nrisedronate sodium tablets\nIP 2022\nUse chromatographic system as described under Related \n$ubstances, test A.\ni * ct the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate inj ections, is not more\nthan 2.6 per cent. .;\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the'content of C7H i0NNa()7P2.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nLabelling. Label to indicate whether it is monohydrate or hemi- \npentahydrate.\nRisedronate Sodium Tablets\nRisedronate Sodium Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per \ncent and notmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nrisedronate sodium. C7HwNNaO7P2.\nUsual strengths. 35 mg; 75 mg; 150 mg.\nDissolution(2.5.2),\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of wa/er,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by. liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute the filtrate, if necessary, with the \ndissolution medium. \n.' = • .\nReference solution. Dissolve a quantity of risedronate sodium \nIPRS in the dissolution medium to obtain a solution of known \nconcentration similar to the expected concentration of the \ntest solution.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 5 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with a\nstrong anion-exchange microbeads consisting of \nsulphonated cross-linked polystyrene (10 pm),\n- mobile phase: a buffer solution prepared by dissolving\n1.8 g of disodium edetate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted \nto pH 9.5 with dilute sodium hydroxide,\n- flow rate: 0.8 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 263 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of \nC7H10NNaO7P2.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Disperse a quantity of the powdered tablets containing \n50 mg of risedronate sodium in 10 ml of water, shake and filter. \nAdd 10 ml-of 0.2M.cupric chloride solution, mix well arid \nallow the solution to stand.for about 10 minutes. Add 2 ml of \ndehydrated alcohol, mix well and allow the solution to stand \nfor a minimum of 1 hour, to form a blue precipitate of the \ncopper complex. Collect the precipitate using a nylon filter of \n0.45-pm pore size, wash it with 10 ml of dehydrated alcohol, \nand allow it to dry on the filter paper, determine by infrared \nabsorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum \nwith that obtained with risedronate sodium IPRS, treated in \nthe samemanner or with the reference spectrurti of risedronate \nsodium. -J\nNOTE — Dry the precipitate under ambient conditions, do \nnot heat'the precipitate. A modest change of colour (from \nblue to green) may be observed upon drying.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution ."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/risperidone-syrup","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Risperidone Syrup"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Risperidone Syrup\nRisperidone Oral Solution\nRisperidone Syrup contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nrisperidone, C23H27FN4O2. It may contain suitable preservative.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml.\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\n3501\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTests ■ ■\npH (2.4.24). 2.0 to 4.O., \n... .\nRelated substances. Determine . by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n......\nBuffer solution. AO.5 per cent w/v solution of ammonium \nacetate in water.\nTest solution. Transfer a suitable quantity ofthe syrup \ncontaining. 10 mg of Risperidone to a 50-ml volumetric flask, \nadd 25 ml of the buffer solution, fill with methanol almost to \nvolume and mix. Allow to cool to room temperature and dilute \nwith methanol to volume. \n,\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 2.5 mg of risperidone related \ncompounds mixture IPRS in 2 ml of methanol. Add 2 ml of \nwater and 5 ml of the buffer solution and allow to cool to room \ntemperature, dilute to 10.0 ml with methanol.\nReference solution (bf A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of \nrisperidone IPRS in methanol.\nReference solution (c). Transfer 10.0 ml of reference solution \n(b) to a 50-ml volumetric'iflask. Add 10 ml of water and 25 ml of \nthe buffer solution and allow to cool to room temperature, \ndilute, with wezAmm/.to volume.\nReference solution (^.Transfer 2.0 ml of reference solution\n(b) to a 50-ml volumetric flask. Add 10 ml of water, and 25 ml of \nthe buffer solution and allow to cool to room temperature, \ndilute with methanol to volume.\nChromatographic system \n.\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (3 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 78 volumes of the buffer\nsolution and 22 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 275 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nName\nRelative \nretention time\nfactor\nRisperidone m-A’-oxidc'\n0.33\n1.03\nB icyclori speridone2\n0.43\n1.49,\nRisperidone Z-oxirne3*\n0.53 ' ' \"\n—\nRisperidone\n; 1.0 ■ •\n—\n♦Process'impurity included for identification only. Process impurities \nare controlled in the-drug substance,\" and are. not to .be reported or \nincluded in the total impurities for the drug product,\n1 c is-3 - {2 -[4-(6-Fluoro-1,2-benzisoxazpl-3 -yl)-1 -piperidin yl] ethyl }-2- \nmethyI-6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-4H-pyrido[i,2-(2]pynmidih-4-drie TV-oxide,\n23-(4-Fluoro-2-hydroxypheny])-l-{2-(6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-2-methyl-4- \noxo-4/f-pyrido-[l ,2-a]pyrimidin-3-yI)ethyl}-2-az4'-l-azo'nia- \nbicyclo[2.2.2]oct-2-ene iodide,\n33-(2-{4-[(Z)'-(2,’4-Difluoroplienylj(hydroxyiminojmethyl]piperidin-l- \nyI}etliy])-2-methyl-6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-477-pyrido[l,2-alpyrimidin-4-\n3502 \nOllliilllMi\n„ \nRISPERIDONE TABLETS\nIP 202? ... .........   '....................................\nIP\nRISPERIDONE SYRUP\nStore protected from light, at a temperature not Inject the reference solution and the test solution,\nInject reference solution (a), (c) and (d). The test is not valj^ \nunless the resolution between the peaks due \nbicyclbrisperidone and risperidone Z-oxime is not less than\"\nexceeding 30°. Do not freeze. \nCalculate the content of C23H27FN4O2 in the medium.\n' \nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of\n1.5 in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n■\nthe relative standard deviation for replicate injections is not I \nmore than 5.0 per cent in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (c) and the signal-to-noise ratio for the \nprincipal peak is not less than 10.0 in the chromatogra^SlI \nobtained with reference solution (d). \n;\nC23H27FN4O2.\nRisperidone Tablets\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), as described under Assay with the following \nmodifications.\nRisperidoneTablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nDilute sodium hydroxide so"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Risperidone Syrup\nRisperidone Oral Solution\nRisperidone Syrup contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nrisperidone, C23H27FN4O2. It may contain suitable preservative.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml.\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\n3501\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTests ■ ■\npH (2.4.24). 2.0 to 4.O., \n... .\nRelated substances. Determine . by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n......\nBuffer solution. AO.5 per cent w/v solution of ammonium \nacetate in water.\nTest solution. Transfer a suitable quantity ofthe syrup \ncontaining. 10 mg of Risperidone to a 50-ml volumetric flask, \nadd 25 ml of the buffer solution, fill with methanol almost to \nvolume and mix. Allow to cool to room temperature and dilute \nwith methanol to volume. \n,\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 2.5 mg of risperidone related \ncompounds mixture IPRS in 2 ml of methanol. Add 2 ml of \nwater and 5 ml of the buffer solution and allow to cool to room \ntemperature, dilute to 10.0 ml with methanol.\nReference solution (bf A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of \nrisperidone IPRS in methanol.\nReference solution (c). Transfer 10.0 ml of reference solution \n(b) to a 50-ml volumetric'iflask. Add 10 ml of water and 25 ml of \nthe buffer solution and allow to cool to room temperature, \ndilute, with wezAmm/.to volume.\nReference solution (^.Transfer 2.0 ml of reference solution\n(b) to a 50-ml volumetric flask. Add 10 ml of water, and 25 ml of \nthe buffer solution and allow to cool to room temperature, \ndilute with methanol to volume.\nChromatographic system \n.\n- a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (3 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 78 volumes of the buffer\nsolution and 22 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 275 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nName\nRelative \nretention time\nfactor\nRisperidone m-A’-oxidc'\n0.33\n1.03\nB icyclori speridone2\n0.43\n1.49,\nRisperidone Z-oxirne3*\n0.53 ' ' \"\n—\nRisperidone\n; 1.0 ■ •\n—\n♦Process'impurity included for identification only. Process impurities \nare controlled in the-drug substance,\" and are. not to .be reported or \nincluded in the total impurities for the drug product,\n1 c is-3 - {2 -[4-(6-Fluoro-1,2-benzisoxazpl-3 -yl)-1 -piperidin yl] ethyl }-2- \nmethyI-6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-4H-pyrido[i,2-(2]pynmidih-4-drie TV-oxide,\n23-(4-Fluoro-2-hydroxypheny])-l-{2-(6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-2-methyl-4- \noxo-4/f-pyrido-[l ,2-a]pyrimidin-3-yI)ethyl}-2-az4'-l-azo'nia- \nbicyclo[2.2.2]oct-2-ene iodide,\n33-(2-{4-[(Z)'-(2,’4-Difluoroplienylj(hydroxyiminojmethyl]piperidin-l- \nyI}etliy])-2-methyl-6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-477-pyrido[l,2-alpyrimidin-4-\n3502 \nOllliilllMi\n„ \nRISPERIDONE TABLETS\nIP 202? ... .........   '....................................\nIP\nRISPERIDONE SYRUP\nStore protected from light, at a temperature not Inject the reference solution and the test solution,\nInject reference solution (a), (c) and (d). The test is not valj^ \nunless the resolution between the peaks due \nbicyclbrisperidone and risperidone Z-oxime is not less than\"\nexceeding 30°. Do not freeze. \nCalculate the content of C23H27FN4O2 in the medium.\n' \nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of\n1.5 in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n■\nthe relative standard deviation for replicate injections is not I \nmore than 5.0 per cent in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (c) and the signal-to-noise ratio for the \nprincipal peak is not less than 10.0 in the chromatogra^SlI \nobtained with reference solution (d). \n;\nC23H27FN4O2.\nRisperidone Tablets\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), as described under Assay with the following \nmodifications.\nRisperidoneTablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nDilute sodium hydroxide solution. To 1000 ml of water in a\nrisperidone, CojH? 7FN4O2.\nbeaker, add 0.1 Msodium hydroxide dropwise to obtain a pH \nof about 8.5.\nUsual strengths. 1 mg; 2 mg; 3 mg; 4 mg.\nInject reference solution (c) and the test, solution. In the 11 \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the. area of any f 1 \npeak corresponding to risperidone ciy-JV-pxide and \nbicyclorisperidone, each .of,, is not more than the area of the H \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (c) (0.5 per cent), the area bf any other secondary | \npeak is not more than 0.4 times the area ofthe principal peak in. \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) (0.2 /1 \nper cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary peaks \nis not more than twice the area of'the principal peak in the ? | \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) (1.0 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.2 times the area ■; | \nof the principalpeak in the\" chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (c) (0.1 per cent). ' \n\"\" J"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Dilute hydrogen peroxide solution. Dilute 1 ml of hydrogen \nperoxide solution (10 volume) with water to 500 ml.\nA. Powder an appropriate number of tablets to prepare about \n0.055 per cent w/v solution of risperidone in ethyl acetate, \nshake for30minutes and centrifuge for 20 minutes. Evaporate \n5 ml ofthe supernatant liquid with the aid of a stream of nitrogen \nto dryriess; the residue complies with the following test.\nReference solution.Drs>so\\ve 10 mg of risperidone IPRS in 10 \nml of dilute sodium hydroxide solution in a 100-ml volumetric \nflask. Store the flask at 90° for 24 hours. Cool the solution to \nroom temperature. Add 10 ml of dilute hydrogen peroxide \nsolution to the flask, and store at 90° for an additional two \nhours. Cool the mixture to room temperature, and dilute with \nmethanol to volume.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare’ the spectrum with that obtained with risperidone \nIPRS' or with the reference spectrum of risperidone.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nCorrection\nName\nRelative \nretention time\nfactor\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Total microbial count not more : | \nthan, 1.00 CFU per ml, total yeast and mould count not more t \nthan 1Q CFU per ml. 1 ml is free from Escherichia coli. / \n|\nBicyclorisperidone1\n0.68\n1.23\nRisperidone\n1.0 ■\n—"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\nRisperidone /ram-V-oxidc2\n1.65\n—\nOther tests: Comp ly with the tests stated under Oral Liquids! |\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 500 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid,\nRisperidone c/x-A-oxide3\n1.81\n1.05\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as : g \ndescribed under Related substances with the following g \nmodifications. . . \nj\nIndividual unidentified impurities —\n—\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nTotal impurities\n—\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 2.5 mg risperidone related | \ncompounds mixture IPRS in 2 ml of methanol. Add 2 ml of g \nwater and 5 ml of the buffer solution and allow to cool to room | \ntemperature, dilute to 10.0 ml with methanol. \nI\n'3“(4-fluoro-2-hydroxypheriyl)-l-[2-(6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-2-methyl-4- \noxo-4//-pyrido-[i,2-a]pyrimidin-3-yl)ethyl]-2-aza-l-azoniabicyclo \n[2.2.2]oct-2-ene iodide,\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute the filtrate, if necessary, with the \ndissolution medium.\n2/rnM5-3-[2-[4-(6^fluoro-l ,2-benzisoxazoi-3-yl)-l-piperidinyl]ethyl]- \n6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-2-methyl-4//-pyrido[l,2-a]pyrimidin-4-one, A7- \noxide monohydrate,\nReference solution. Dissolve a quantity of risperidone IPRS \nin the dissolution medium and dilute with the same solvent to \nobtain a solution having a known concentration similar to the \ntest solution.\nCorrection\nReference solution (bf A 0.1 per cent w/y. solution of \nrisperidone IPRS in methanol. Transfer 10.0 ml ofthe solution \nto a 50-ml volumetric flask. Add 10 ml of water and 25 ml ofthe \nbuffer solution and allow to cool to room temperature, dilute \nto volume with methanol.\n3c/s-3-[2-[4-(6-fluoro-1,2-benzisoxazol-3-yl)-1 -piperidinyl]ethyl]- \n6,7,8,9-tetrahydro-2-methyl-427-pyrido[l ,2-a]pyrimidin-4-one, N-\noxide monohydrate.\nChromatographic system\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to risperidone /ram-A-oxide \nand risperidone cA-7V-oxide is not less than 1.2.\n~ a stainless steel column 15 cm * 4.6 mm, packed with\n- octadecylsiiane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test ishot valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to bicyclorispqndoiie \nand risperidone 2-oxime is not less than 1.5 in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 1.0 per cent \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b). ,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of water, 35.\nInject the test solution. The area of any peak corresponding \nto bicyclorisperidone, and risperidone m-A'-oxide is not more \nthan 0.5 per cent, the area of any other secondary peak is not \nmore than 0.3 per cent and the sum of areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 1.0 per cent, calculated by area\nvolumes of acetonitrile and 0.1 volume of trifluoroacetic \nacid, adjust the pH to 3.0 with ammonia solution,\n~ flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n'• \" Spectrophotometer set at 237 nm,\ninjection volume: 50 pl.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. ;;-x, :\nnormalisation.\ninject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nRelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \n^2.0 per cent.\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \nTablets.\nDetermine the weight per ml ofthe oral solution (2.4:29) and \ncalculate the content of (^I^FNR)?.. \n- -;i*\n3503\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), using the \nchromatographic system as described under Dissolution, \nexcept using\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n.\nTest solution. Transfer 1 tablet to a 100-ml volumetric flask. \nAdd 50 ml of 0.7 ^hydrochloric acid and shake mechanical ly \nfor 30 minutes, dilute to the volume with 0.1 Mhydrochloric \nacid, filter.\nReference solution. Weigh accurately arid dissolve the \nrisperidone IPRS in I'M hydrochloric acid to obtain a \nsolution having a known concentration similar to the 'test \nsolution.' V •• \n. mW. ■'.y.z.r--, ••\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content bf C23H27FN4O2 in the tablet.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated tinder Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 80 volumes of methanol and 20 volumes of \nwater. '\nTest solution. Transfer not less than 10 tablets to a suitable \nvolumetric flask to obtain a 0.01 per cent w/v solution of \nrisperidone. Add an appropriate amount of water equivalent \nto 20 per cent of the total volume of the volumetric flask, and \nshake mechanically for about 30 minutes. Add a volume of \nmethanol equivalent to. 60 per cent of the total volume of the \nvolumetric flask, and shake mechanically for about 30 minutes, \ndilute with