of solution (2.4.1). A 2,0 per cent w/v solution in \ncarbon dioxide free water is' not more intensely coloured \nthan reference solution YS7 (2.4.1) and isnOt more opalescent \nthan standard OS 1 (2.4.1). \n:' r '\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). ; \n;\nTest solution, (a).. Dissolve 30 mg of the .substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of mobile phase. \n. ,v\nTest solution (&).■ Diluted. 0 ml of test solution (a) to 100.0 ml \nwith mobile phase. Further dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to 10.0 \nml with mobile phase. ...... \no -\nReference solution.,^..solution containing 0.001 per cent w/v\neach of ropivacaine hydrochloride IPRS and bupivacaine \nhydrochloride IPRS in the mobile phase/\nROPIVACAINE HYDROCHLORIDE\nIP 2022\nChromatographic system\n~ a stainless steel column 15 cm x 3 ;9 mm; packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (4 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 50 volumes of a buffer solution ^prepared by \ndissolving 0.18 g of monobasic'sodium hydrogen \nphosphate and 2,9 g of disodium hydrogen phosphate\n: dihydrate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted tq pH 8,0, \n- flow rale: 1 ml per minute, \n;s,.\n- spectrophotometer set at 240 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nThe relative retention times are about 1.0 for ropivacaine and\n1.6 for bupivacaine respectively.\nInject the reference solution and test solution (b). The test is \nnot valid unless the resolution between the peaks due to \nropivacaine and bupivacaine obtained with reference solution \nis not less than 6.0 and the signal to noise ratio for the principal \npeak obtained with test solution (b) is not less than 10.0.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution (a). The \narea of any peak due to bupivacaine is not more, than 0.2 per \ncent. The area of any other secondary peak is not more than \n0.1 per cent and the sum of the areas of'all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 0.5 per cent, calculatedrby area \nnormalisation.\nLimit of Ropivacaine related compound A (2,6-Dimethylaniline | \nhydrochloride). Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). |\nTest solution. Dissolve 100 mg of the substance under | \nexamination in 10. Oml.ofmobile phase. \n....... , |\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.0000T per cent | \nw/v of ropivacaine hydrochloride impurity A' IPRS in the . | \nmobile phase,'' \n• \nJ.\nUse chromatographic system as described under Related | \nsubstances. \n’ \n|\nInject the reference solution. In the chromatogram obtained . | \nwith the reference solution signal- to-noise ratio for the | \nprincipal peak is not less than 10. \n|\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the | \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any | \npeak due to ropivacaine impurity A is not more than the area | \nof the principal peak in the chromatogram, obtained with the | \nreference solution (0.001 percent). . \n. \n|\nEnantiomeric purity.Determine by liquid chromatography | \n(2.4.14). \n.......... ....... ■ \n|\nTest solution, Dissolve 75 mg of the substance under | \nexamination in water and dilute to 100.0 ml with water. Dilute |\n10.0. ml ofthis resulting solution to 100.0 ml with mobile phase g\nL \" '. \" ' , \n' \n... \n.. \n■m M\nReference solution.containing 0.0075 per cent w/v ..\nof ropivacaine hydrochloride IPRS arid 0.000075’per cent \nw/v of ropivacaine impurity B IPRS in the mobile phase. |\n3526\nwww.webofpharma.com\nROPIVACAINE INJECTION\nIP2022\nsystem\na stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with \nchiralpakAGP(5 pm),\nmobile phase: dilute 35.0 ml of isopropyl alcohol into a \n500-ml vdltinietric flask with buffer solution prepared \nby diluting 7-5. ml of 1M monobasic sodium phosphate \nsolution and28.5 ml of 0.5 Mdibasic sodium phosphate \n¿¡¡hydrate solution to a 1000 ml with water and mix.\n15.\nInject the test solution. The area of any peak corresponding \nto ropivacaine hydrochloride impurity B is not more than 0.5 \nper cent, calculated by area normalisation.\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 6fl.\npH (2.3.24). 4:5 to 6.0, determined on 1.0 per cent w/v solution.\nWater (2.3.43). 5.0 to 6.0 per cent, determined on 0.1 g.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 2.0 g complies with limit test for heavy \nmetals, method B (10 ppm).\nAssay. Dissolve 1.0 g of the substance in 10 ml of water and \n40 ml :of<ethanol. Add 1 nil of 1M hydrochloric acidtHfasfe \nwith IM sodium hydroxide, determining the end point \npotentiometrically (2.4.25). Read the volumes added between \nthe two points of inflection.\n1 ml of JAf sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.3109 g of \nCpH^O^Cl.\nRopivacaine hydrochloride intended for use in the \nmanufactureofparenteral preparations without a. further \nappropriate procedure for the removal of bacterial \nendotoxins complies with the following additional \nrequirement.\n■''1 1 P< / ■; yd -■: 1; ■. )L: i , \n■ ■ . ■ \n■ ■ \n, \n- ■■ ■\n(2.2.3). Not more than 60 Endotoxin Unit \nPer g of ropivacaine hydrochloride.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nStorage. Store in well-closed containers and at a temperature \nnotexceed30°\nRopivacaine Injection"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/rosemary-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Rosemary Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Rosemary Oil\nReflux 2.0 g of powdered substance under \nxanunihion with 100 ml of n-hexane on water-bath for \n30 ipinutes, cool and filter. Concentrate under vacuum to 5 ml.\nPuskara\nRosemary Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam distillation \nof the twigs and blossoming tips of Rosmarinus officinalis\nPushkarmula, Orris root; Inula racemosa\nsolution. Reflux 2.0 g ofpuskara IPBRS with 100 ml \nof on water-b4t11 for 30 minutes, cool and filter.\nLinn. (Fam. Lamiaceae).\nRosemary Oil (Indian variety) contains not less than 2.0 per \ncent and not more than 10.0 per cent of borneol, and not less \nthan 15.0 per cent and not more than 40.0 per cent of a-pinene.\n£oncéhtratc under vacuum to 5 ml.\nApplÿ to the plate 10 pl of each solutionas band length 8 mm.\n^{owthbmobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air and \nexamine in thd ultraviolet light at 254 nm and 366 nm. Spray the\nDescription. A clear, pale yellow to amber yellow liquid, free \nfrom sediment and suspended matter, spicy, characteristic and\nsulphuric acid reagent and heat the \nplate at 105° for 10 minutes and examine in day light. \nThe chromatographic profile of the test solution is similar to\nanise-like odour."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Rosemary Oil\nReflux 2.0 g of powdered substance under \nxanunihion with 100 ml of n-hexane on water-bath for \n30 ipinutes, cool and filter. Concentrate under vacuum to 5 ml.\nPuskara\nRosemary Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam distillation \nof the twigs and blossoming tips of Rosmarinus officinalis\nPushkarmula, Orris root; Inula racemosa\nsolution. Reflux 2.0 g ofpuskara IPBRS with 100 ml \nof on water-b4t11 for 30 minutes, cool and filter.\nLinn. (Fam. Lamiaceae).\nRosemary Oil (Indian variety) contains not less than 2.0 per \ncent and not more than 10.0 per cent of borneol, and not less \nthan 15.0 per cent and not more than 40.0 per cent of a-pinene.\n£oncéhtratc under vacuum to 5 ml.\nApplÿ to the plate 10 pl of each solutionas band length 8 mm.\n^{owthbmobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air and \nexamine in thd ultraviolet light at 254 nm and 366 nm. Spray the\nDescription. A clear, pale yellow to amber yellow liquid, free \nfrom sediment and suspended matter, spicy, characteristic and\nsulphuric acid reagent and heat the \nplate at 105° for 10 minutes and examine in day light. \nThe chromatographic profile of the test solution is similar to\nanise-like odour."},{"name":"Identification","text":"that of the refercnce solution.\nA. \nDetermine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\n■' A:7 \n'. \n. - \n..\nTest solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v 