a mixture of 37 volumes of acetonitrile\nPercentage of L.A - Serratiopeptidase Units/Tablet * jqq\n■ \n••• \n■■ L.A. \n■.\nand 63 volumes a buffer solution prepared by dissolving\nwhere, !76 = Conversion Co-efficient of Tyrosine to\n1.5 g of sodium dihydrogen phosphate in 1000 ml of \nwater.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with sertaconazole\nSerratiopeptidase,\n3570\n3571\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSERTACONAZOLE NITRATE\n. - flowrate: 1.6 ml per minute, .\n- spectrophotometer set at 220 nm,\n- \ninjectionvolume: 20pl. \n, :.........\nThe retention time of peaks due to nitrate ion is about 1 minute, \nmiconazole about 17 minutes and sertaconazole about 19\nminutes. ; \n; \n. •••\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to miconazole and \nsertaconazole is not less than 2,0.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 1.3 times the retention time of the principal \npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, \nthe area of any peak corresponding sertaconazole impurity A; \nB and C are hot more than the'areao f the principalpeak in the \nchromatogram obtained, with reference solution (a) (Q.25 per \ncent), the relative retention,time of impurity C, impurity B and \nimpurityAare about 0.12,0.18 and 0.3 respectively, thesum of \nthe areas of all the secondary peaks is not more than twice the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nréférencé'solution (aj (0.5 per cent); Ignore ânÿ peak which is \n0.2 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solutioh (a) (0.05 per ceht) arid düé to \niiitraté ion. ; ; \n\"■ - ■■ \n’• \n',:-\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 1.0 per,cent, determined, on\n0.5g.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.4 g in 50 ml mixture of equal volumes of \nanhydrous acetic acid and methyl .ethyl ketone. Titrate with \n0.Ï M perchloric acid, determining the end-point \npotentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry put a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.05008 g of\nStorage. Store protected from light."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related’substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). ? ■ ; < \n•\nSolvent mixture. 20 volumes of water and 80 volumes of \nacetonitrile.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 nig of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 25.0 ml with \nsolvent mixture. \n.\nReference solution. A 0.0003 per cent w/v solution of \nseratrodast IPRS ivPtxc solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 am),\n- mobile phase: A. a.0.1 per.ccntv/v solution of ortho­\nphosphoric acid in water,\nB. acetonitrile,\n- a gradientprogramme using the conditions given below,\n- flow rate: ,1ml per minute, .\n- spectrophotometer set at 215nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n:.....-A\nTime\n' Mobile phase A\n• Mobilephaseg\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n°\n’ 40\n20 .\n40\n■\n30 .-\n25\n40,\n. . 25\n, 75 \n,\n50\n40\n‘ J f'\"11 \"\n65\n40\n■ ' 60\nInject reference solution. The test is riot valid unless Ue \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 3000 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution;'Ih'U^ \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, thearea of any \nsecondary peak is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(0.3 per cent) and the sum of areas of all the peaksis not more \nthan 3.3 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (1.0 per cent); ' ’' ’? ■\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with limit testfor heavy \nmetals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.2 per cent;\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than5.0 percent, determined \non J .0 g by drying in an oven at 105°, for 3 hours. J’\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 20 volumes of water and 80 volumes of \nacetonitrile.\nTest solution: Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under examination \nin the solvent mixture and dilute to 100.0 ml with the solvent \nmixture. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the solved \nmixture.\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of seratrodast \nIPRS in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 35 volumes of 0.1 per cent\nv/v solution1; of orthophosphoric acid ' m water and\n65 volumes of acetonitrile, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute,: \n.\nspectrophotometer, set at 215nm,\n- injection volume: 20 jil.\nInject reference solution^ The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 3000 theoreticalplates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the .content of G22H26O4.\n3568\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP2022\nSeiratiopeptidase\n„ ijatiopeptidase is a proteolytic enzyme for oral use which is \np^duced by bacteria of genus Serratia. .\ng^lippeptidase contains, not less than 2Q00 units and not \nmore than 26.00 units of serratiopeptidase activity per mg.\nCaiegory. Proteolytic enzyme.\nwater.\nDescription- A grayish white to pale brownpowder.\nArsenic (2:3.10). Dissolve 2.0 g in 50 ml of water, and add\nstamiatedhydrochloric acid. The resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for arsenic,(5 ppm).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated Ash (2.3,18). Not more than 7.0 per cent.\nboss op drying (2.4,19). Not more than 7.0 per cent, determined\nòri 1 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 4 hours.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Total aerobic viable count is \nnot mòre than 103 CFU per g and total fongal count is not more \nthan 102CFU per g determined by plate count. 1 gis free from \nEscherichia coli and 10 g is free from Salmonella and \nShigella. -. ..\n■■ p\nAssay. Unit definition: one serratiopeptidase unit is defined \nas the amount of enzyme required to liberate 1 pg of free \ntyrosine per minute under the specified assay conditions.\nsodium borate hydrochloric acid buffer pH 9.0. Dissolve\ngsodiym borate jn 900 ml of water: Adjust the pH 9.0 with \n1 ^hydrochloric acid and dilute with water to 1000 ml.\nSubstrate: solution. Dissolve 1.2 g dried casein in 100 ml of \nsodium borate hydrochloric acid buffer pH 9.0. Keep it on\nSERRATIOPEPTIDASE\nboiling water-bath for 1-2 minutes to get clear solution. Cool \nand filter through cotton and dilute with sodium borate \nhydrochloric acid buffer pH 9.0 to 200 ml.\nProtein precipitating solution. To 18 g of trichloroacetic \nacid, add 30 g of sodium acetate and 20 ml of glacial acetic \nacid, and dilute with water to 1000 ml.\nDilute folin’s reagent. 1 ml of folin S' reagent, add 2 ml of\nReference tyrosine solution. Dissolve 10 mg of tyrosine in\n1 nd of 1Mhydrochloric acid and dilute to, 100 ml with sodium \nborate hydrochloric acid buffer pH 9.0.\nReference tyrosine curve. To 1, 2, 3 , 4 and 5 ml of reference\ntyrosine solution, add 5,:4, 3, 2 and 1 ml oí sodium borate \nhydrochloric acid buffer pH 9.0 respectively. Add 5 ml of \nprotein precipitating reagent in each tube. To 2 ml of these\nsolutions, add 5 ml of 6.0 per cent w/v sodium carbonate \nsolution. Add 1 ml of diluted folin’s reagentmix and allow to \nstand at 37° for 30 minutes, measure the absorbance at 660 nm \n(2.4.7). Plot a graph of pmol of tyrosine per system against the \nabsorbance.\nStock test solution. Weigh about 0.1 g of the substance under \nexamination, dissolve in 100 ml oí sodium borate hydrochloric \nacid buffer pH 9.0 (solution A). Mix and keep it for 5 minutes. \nTake 1 ml of solution A and dilute to 200 ml with ww/fo/n borate \nhydrochloric acid buffer pH 9.0 (solution B).\nTest solution. Pipet 1.0 ml of stock test solution, allow to \nstand in a water-bath at 37° for 5 minutes. Add 5 ml of substrate \nsolution shake immediately and allow to stand in water-bath \nat 37° for 20 mi"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/serratiopeptidase","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Serratiopeptidase"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Serratiopeptidase\nSerratiopeptidase is a proteolytic enzyme for oral use which is\nproduced by bacteria of genus Serratia.\nSerratiopeptidase contains not less than 2000 units and not\nmore than 2600 units ofserratiopeptidase activity per mg.\nCategory. Prolytic enzyme.\nDescription. A grayish white to pale brown pow4er with\ncharacteristic odour."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Serratiopeptidase\nSerratiopeptidase is a proteolytic enzyme for oral use which is\nproduced by bacteria of genus Serratia.\nSerratiopeptidase contains not less than 2000 units and not\nmore than 2600 units ofserratiopeptidase activity per mg.\nCategory. Prolytic enzyme.\nDescription. A grayish white to pale brown pow4er with\ncharacteristic odour."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Dissolve 0.4 g of serratiopeptidase in 100 ml of acetic acid\nsodium acetate buffersolutionpH5.0, transfer exactly 1mlof\nthis solution into 3 test tubes, refer them as A, Band C. Add 1\nml ofwater in tube A. In tube Band C add 1 ml of0.04 ml of\ndisodium edetate solution. Mix gently and allow them to stand\nat 4±10, for 1 hour. Add 2 ml ofwater in tube Aand C. Add 2 ml\nof 0.04 M zinc chloride solution in tube B. Mix gently and\nallow them to stand at4±1 0, for 1 hour. Pipette 1ml ofA and B\nand make up the volume to 200 ml with borate bufferpH9.0.\nPipette 1ml ofC and make up the volume to 50 ml with borate\nbufferpH9.O. Proceed with these solutions as sample solutions\nas directed in the assay. The activities ofsolution A and Bare\nalmost same and the activity ofC is not more than 5 per cent of\nthat of activity ofA."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Arsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 0.4 g in 50 ml ofwater, and add 10\nml of stannated hydrochloric acid. The resulting solution\ncomplies with the limit test for arsenic (5 ppm).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for\nheavy metals, MethodB (50 ppm).\nSulphatedAsh (2.3.18). Not more than 1.5 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 7.0 percent, determined\non 1 g by drying in an Qven at 105° for 4 hours.\nSERRATIOPEPTIDASE\nMicrobial contamination(2.2.9). Total viable aerobic count,\nnot more than 100micro-organisms perml,determined by plate\ncount. 1 ml is free from Escherichia coli.\nAssay. Unit defmition: one serratiopeptidase unit is defined\nas the amount of enzyme required to liberate 1 I!m of free\ntyrosine per minute under the specified assay conditions.\nsodium borate hydrochloric acid bufferpH 9. O. Dissolve 19\ngsodium borate in 900.ml ofwater. Adjust the pH 9.0 with 1\nM hydrochloric acid and dilute with water to 1000 ml.\nSubstrate solution. Dissolve 1.2 g dried casein in 100 ml of\nsodium borate hydrochloric acid buffer pH 9.0. Keep it on\nboiling water-bath for 1-2 minutes to get clear solution. Cool\nand filter through cotton and dilute with sodium borate\nhydrochloric acid bufferpH9.a to 200 ml.\nProtein precipitating solution. To 18 g oftrichloroacetic acid,\nadd 30 g ofsodium acetate and 20 ml ofglacial acetic acid,\nand dilute with water to 100 ml.\nReference tyrosine solution. Dissolve 10 mg oftyrosine in 1\nml of1 M hydrochloric ac:idand dilute to 100 ml with sodium\nborate hydrochloric acid buffer pH 9.0.\nReference tyrosine curve. To 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 ml of reference\ntyrosine solution, add 5, 4, 3, 2 and 1 ml of sodiul1~ borate\nhydrochloric acid buffer pH 9.0 respectively. Add 5 ml of\nprotein precipitating reagent in each tube. To 2 ml of these\nsolutions, add 5 ml ofsodium carbonate solution. Add 1 ml of\ndilutedfolins reagent mix and allow to stand at 37° for 30\nminutes, measure the absorbance at 660 nm (2.4.7). Plot a graph\nof I!m oftyrosine per system against the absorbance.\nStock test solution. Weigh about 0.1 g ofthe substance under\nexamination, dissolve in 100 ml ofsodium borate hydrochloric\nacidbufferpH9.a(solution A). Mix and keep it for 5 minutes.\nTake 1ml ofsolutionA and dilute to 200 ml withsodium borate\nhydrochloric acid bufferpH 9.0 (solution B).\nTest solution.\nPipet 1.0 ml of stock test solution, allow to\nstand in a water-bath at 37° for 5 minutes. Add 5 mlofsubstrate\nsolution shake immediately and allow to stand in water-bath\nat 37° for 20 minutes. Add 5 ml of protein precipitating\nsolution. Mix and allow to stand in water-bath at 37° for 30\nminutes and filter. Take 2 ml ofthe filtrate, add 5 ml of0.6 per\ncent w/v sodium carbonate solution. Add 1 ml of diluted\nfolin sreagent mix and allow to stand at 37° for 30 minutes.\nMeasure the absorbance at 660 nm (2.4.7).\nBlank solution. Pipet 1.0 ml of stock test solution, allow to\nstand in a 'Yater~bathat3rfor5 ll1inutes,add5 1l1l ofpr:ot~il1\nprecipitating solution, shake immediately and allow to stand\nin water-bath at 37° for 20 minutes. Add 5 ml of substrate\nsolution. Mix and allow to stand in water-bath at 37° for 30\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nminutes and filter. Take 2 ml ofthe filtrate, add 50 ml of0.6 per\ncent w/v sodium carbonate solution. Add 1 ml of diluted\nfolin s reagent mix and allow stand at 37° for 30 minutes.\nMeasure the absorbance at 660 mn (2.4.7).\nCalculate the serratiopeptidase IV/mg by using concentration\nof tyrosine from graph considering the reaction time and\ndilution.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture, attemperature\nnot exceeding 30°.\nSerratiopeptidase Tablets\nSerratiopeptidase Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent\nof the stated amount of Serratiopeptidase.\nUsual strengths. 5 mg; 10 mg.\nDilute folins reagent. 1 ml offolin s reagent, add 2 ml of\nwater.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. The tyrosine units are dissolved in sodium carbonate\nsolution, which is alkaline in nature. The Folin s ciocalteau\nreagent helps in colour development where the tyrosine units\nbind to the copper molecule in the reagent and causes the\nreduction of phosphomolybdate which is present in the\nreagent.\nThere is formation of tyrosine-copper molybdate\ncoloured complex. The intensity ofcolour depends upon the\ntyrosine units present which is read at 660 nm.\nDisodium tetraborate buffer. Dissolve 19.0 gm of disodium\ntetraborate in 900 ml of water, adjusted to pH 9.0 with 1 M\nhydrochloric acid and dilute to 1000 ml with water.\nCasein hammerstein substrate. Dissolve 1.2 gm of Casein\nHammerstein to 100 ml ofthe sodium tetraborate buffer. Allow\nthe casein to dissolve and form homogeneous solution. Boil\nthe solution in boiling water-bath for 2 minutes. After removing\nfrom boiling water-bath, cool the casein substrate immediately\nin ice cold water. Filter this solution through cotton plug to\nget clear solution and dilute to 200 ml with water.\nNOTE-Always add casein to the buffer while stirring. Do\nnot add buffer to casein as this will cause lumps and not go\ninto solution completely and giving incorrect values.\nTrichloroacetic acid reagent.\nDissolve 18 gm of\ntrichloroacetic Acid, 30 gm of anhydrous sodium acetate\nand 20 ml ofglacial acetic acid in 1000 ml ofwater.\nNOTE-TCA should be in the crystalform and not in liquid\nform as this will effect the quantity weighed and thereby\ninterfere with the complete precipitation of casein.\nUse\nSodium acetate anhydrous because if, sodium acetate\ntrihydrate should be used, the trihydrate forms gives lower\nresults.\n2098\nIP 2010\nCOLLOIDAL SILICON DIOXIDE\nDiluted Folin 's Ciocalteau Reagent. Dilute 1 ml of Fo1in's\nCiocalteau reagentwith 2 ml ofwaterto get 3 ml ofthe reagent.\nNOTE-Folin's Ciocalteau reagent should be ofthe protein\nestimation grade andnot the indicatorgrade. Folin 's reagent\n. should be diluted to just before addition to the tubes since it\nis sensitive to light.\nDo not prepare this solution at the\nbeginning ofthe assay.\nTyrosine re"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sertaconazole-nitrate-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sertaconazole Nitrate Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sertaconazole Nitrate Cream\nSertaconazole Nitrate Cream contains Sertaconazole Nitrate \nin a suitable cream base.\nSertaconazole Nitrate Cream.contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nsertaconazole nitrate, C20H15CbN2OS in a suitable cream base.\nUsual strength. 2 per cent w/w. ’ \\"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sertaconazole Nitrate Cream\nSertaconazole Nitrate Cream contains Sertaconazole Nitrate \nin a suitable cream base.\nSertaconazole Nitrate Cream.contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nsertaconazole nitrate, C20H15CbN2OS in a suitable cream base.\nUsual strength. 2 per cent w/w. ’ \\"},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the' peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.?,\nTests ■ \n■ \nj I\npH(2.4.24).3.0to4.5 ' ..'.'f. „ ... \n. ../ '•’!\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Crearos. \nI\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). \n. |\n..-'WV W... ‘ \n■a/.'v '''' ■\nTest solution. Disperse, a quantity of the cream .containing I \nabout 20 mg of Sertaconazole Nitrate in 7Q ml of the methane t \nand sonicate for 15 minutes with intermittent shaking. Cool to I \nroom temperature and dilute to • 100.0 ml with zhefAuho/ and I \nfilter. Further dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 25.0 fillAvith I\nmethanol. < \njííkíí-jj.v I\nReference solution. A 0.004 per cent w/v solution of ’ |\nsertaconazole nitrate IPRS in methanol. \nf\nChromatographic system \n; \n|\n- a stainless steel column 1-5 Cm x 4.6 mm, packed with |\nnitrile groups chemically-bonded to porous silica f \nparticles (5 pm), (Such as Zqrbax SB CN),: ' \n|\n- column temperature: 40°, \n|\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 40 volumes of a buffer solution I\nprepared by dissolving 1.5 g of sodium dihydrogen | \nphosphate monohydrate in 1000 mi of water and 60 I\nvolumes erf methanol, \n|\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, ................ |\n- spectrophotometer set at 220 nm, \n|\n- injection volume: 10 pl. \n|\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the | \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical-plates, the | \ntailing factor is not.more than 2.Q and the relative standard | \ndeviation for replicate injections is. not more than 2.0 per cent |\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. \n|\nCalculate the content of CjoHisCoNjOj inthe cream; |\nStorage. Store at a temperature not exceeding 25°. Do not | \nfreeze. \n|\n.... f \n< - ■ ■ ■■■■■ ■■ : |\nSertaconazole Nitrate and \n|\n. .......................■ \ng\nBeclomethasone Dipropionate Cream |\nSertaconazole Nitrate and Beclomethasone Dipropionate | \nCream in a suitable bases,-. \n..n:- i\nSertaconazole Nitrate and' Beclomethasone; Dipropionate \nCream contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not moro than\n1 IQ.p per cent of the stated amount of Sert aconazole NitraR \nC2oHi5Cl3N2OS and Beclomethasone Dipropionate,C^Hs/^\nUsual strength. 2,0 per cent w/w. Sertaconazole Nitrate t\n0.025 per cent w/wBeclomethasone Dipropionate. , . / ;:\n3572\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSERTACONAZOLE NITRATE AND BECLOMETHASONE DIPROPIONATE CREAM\n1P2022\nz obtained with the test solution corresponds to \npeaks in ^he chromatogram obtained with the' reference\nAssay, the retention time of the principal peaks in the\nsolution-\nTests'^ J' / - .\npH(2-4.24).3.0to4.5.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). ; ?\nDisperse a quantity of the cream containing \nabbat 50 mg i>f Sertaconazole Nitrate in 30.6 ml of methanol,\nvortex to disperse the cream completely, ultrasound for 15 \nniinuteswith intermittent swirling. Cool to room temperature \nand dilute to 50.0 ml with methanol and filter.\nReference spliition (a). A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of \nsertaconazole nitrate IPRS in methanol.\nReference solution (b). A 0.0125 per cent w/v solution of nitric\nacid'va. methanol.\nChromatographic system\n— a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n; octadecyisilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), \n-columntemperature: 40°,\n< - mobile phase: A. dissolve 1.5 g of sodium dihydrogen\n; phosphate monohydrate in 1000 ml of water and filter,\n....... , B. acetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given\n■ below, AW\n- flow rate: 1.6 ml per minute, \n:\n- spectrophotometer set at 220 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile Phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n60\n40\n10 ....\n. 60\n40\n40\n60\n: 35 .\nw \n40\n60\n42\n10\n90\n^■551T:'w\n10\n90\n60 '\n60\n40\n, 65\n60.\n40 .\nrelative retention time of Beclomethasone Dipropionate \niS about 0.9 with respect to Sertaconazole.\nreference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nC0 efficiency, is not less than 2000 .theoretical plates, the\ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative, standard \ndeviation for replicate injection is not more than 5.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution (a) and (b) and the test solution. In \nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of \nany secondary peak is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0.5 per cent) and the sum of areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than twice the area of the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (1.0 \nper cent) Ignore the peak corresponding to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) and any \npeak with an area less than 0. 1 times the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0.05 percent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Creams\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of the cream containing \nabout 50,0 mg. of sertaconazole nitrate and 0.625 mg of \nbeclomethasone dipropionate in 30 ml of the methanol, vortex \nto disperse the cream completely, ultrasound for 15 minutes \nwith intermittent shaking. Cool to room temperature and dilute \nto 50.0 ml with methanol and filter, (use it for the analysis of \nbeclomethasone dipropionate). Futher.dilute 5.0 ml to 50.0 ml \nwith methanol, (sertaconazole).\nReference solution. Asolution containing 0.01 per cent w/v of \nsertaconazole nitrate IPRS and 0.00125 per cent w/v of \nbeclomethasone dipropionate. IPRS vo methanol:\nChromatographic system \n;\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecyisilane bonded to porous silica (5 pin),\n- column temperature: 40°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 40 volumes of a buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 1.5 g of sodium dihydrogen \nphosphate dihydrate in 1000 ml of water,, and 60\nvolumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.6 ml per minute,\n- spectrop"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Apparatus No; 2 (Paddle), ;\nMedium. 900 ml of sodium acetate buffer solution pH 4.5,:\nSpeed and time. 75 rpm and 30 minutes. ; \n;\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Use the filtrate as given above.\nReference solution. A solution of sertraline hydrochloride\nIP RSof the concentration similar to the test solution in the \ndissolution medium. ’ ? •>. : : : \\ T\nIf 2022\nChromatographic system ■: \n1 - hLm-rr\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 nun,packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (4.prn),,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 15; volumes Of methanol\n; 40 volumes of a solution containing 0.286 percent v/v\nglacial acetic acid and 0.348 per cent vlvhffthylantine\nand 45 volumes of acetonitrile, \n.7’\n- flow rate: 1.8 ml per minute, ’\n- spectrophotometer set at 265 nm, \n?;\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nCalculate the content of Ci7Hi7C12N in the medium.\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of CJ7H17C12N.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). •’\nSolvent mixture. 30 volumes of acetonitrile and 70 volumes \nofw/er. \n’ \n'y.-.\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of whole tablets containing \n500 mg of Sertraline Hydrochloride with 150 ml of the solvent \nmixture with the aid of ultrasound and dilute to 200 ml with the \nsolvent mixture and filter. \n.. •; u ? • a,v: :\nReference solution (a): Dilute 1.0 ml of the test Solution to\n500.0 ml with the solvent mixture. \n' ’1 •.\nReference solution (b). A 0.001 per cent w/v solution of\n(fffmandelic acid (sertraline impurity, E) in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (c). A 0;25 per cent w/vj solution of \nsertraline hydrochloride impurity IPRS in solvent mixture..\nReference solution (d). Dilute 1.0 mlof reference ..solution (a) \nto 2.0 mlwith the solvent mixture.,,.\nChromatographic system \n’’\n' - a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 jufri),\n- mobile phase: A. a. 0.272 per cent w/v solution of\npotassium dihydrogeh orthophosphate, adjusted to pH\n3.0 with ortho phosphoric acid,\nB. acetonitrile, \n■.\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute.\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm, \n; : -\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n70 .\n30\n30\n70\n30\n40 \n■\n60\n40\n41\n.7°\n, ' 30\n51\n70 - ■\n. : ’ •• 30 ...;\n3576\nwww.webofpharma.com\n. t referencc solution (c). For peak identification of \n‘\"^linehydrochlomleimpunly. \n/\nI ect reference solution (a), (b), (d) and the. test solution. In \nthe chromatogram \nthe test solution, the area of\nvpà corresponding to sertraline impurity C ( lRS,4RS)-4- \nM-chlóf ophenyl)-N-tnethyb 1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalen-\n1 aniine) is not more than 4 times the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n.0g per cent)- The area of any peak corresponding to (//)- \njnandelic aòitì is not more than the area of any corresponding \npeak in tpe chrornatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b) (0.4 per cent), the arèa of any other secondary peak is not \nmore than ■the area :of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0,2 per cent) and the sum \nof areas of all the secondary peaks other than sertraline \nimpurity Cis notmore than 7.5 times the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (L5 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than that \nof the’principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (d) (0.1 percent).\nEnatiomeric purity. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2A14); m .............\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of whole tablets containing \n400 mg of Sertraline Hydrochloride with 140 ml of a mixture of\n1 volume cd acetonitrile and 2 volumes of water with the aid \nofiiltrasbund ahddilute to 200.0 ml with a mixture of 1 volume \nof acetonitrile and 2 volumes of water, filter.\nReference solution (a). A 0.003 per cent w/v solution of \nse^rpliQ^ydroch/oride cis-isomer IP RS in methanol\n(10.0 per cent).\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.0002 per cent \nw/y each of sertraline hydrochloride IPRS and sertraline \nhyd^chlpride cis-isomer ((IR,4R)-4-(3,4-dichloro-phenyl)- \nS-methyl-1,2,3',4-teirahydronaphthalen-l-amine) IPRS in \ntnethonof(10.0.per cent).\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nsilica gel QD for chiral sepearation(Such as ChromTech \nChiral-AGP), .\n7 mobile phase: amixtureof 10 volumes cdlpropan-2-ol \nand 90 volumes of a 0.23 per cent w/v solution of \nammonium dihydrogen orthophosphate,\n~ flow rate: 0.7 ml per minute,\n\"Spectrophotometer set at210nm, \n~ injection volume: 40 pl.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the\nc omatogrami obtained with the test solution, the area of any\nSILDENAFIL CITRATE\npeak corresponding to sertraline cis-isomer is not more than \nthe area of the principal peak in the; chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (1.5 per cent).\nOther tests.1 Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 'tablets! Disperse a quantity \nof the powder containing 0.2 g of Sertraline Hydrochloride \nwith 150 ml of the mobile phase with the aid of ultrasound for \n45 minutes, cool and dilute to 200 ml with water, filter. Dilute \n5 .0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution. A0.005 per cent w/v solution of sertraline \nhydrochloride IPRS in the mobile phase.:\nUse chromatographic system as described under Dissolution.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of Cl7H]7Cl2N, in the tablets."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/serum-albumin-nanocolloid-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Serum Albumin Nanocolloid Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Serum Albumin Nanocolloid Injection\n(99,’!Tc-IISA-NC Injection)\nCategory: Diagnostic.\nDescription. 99mTc-HSA-NC Injection is a clear, colourless\nsterile solution, containing not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated \"mTc-radioaCtivity at \nthe date and time stated on the label. Not less than\n90.'0 per cent of the total technetium-99m is present as\n99mTc“HSA-NC. 99mTc-HSA-NC injection should be used \nwithin 4 hours from the timc of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. 99tnTc-HSA-NC Injection \nis prepared aseptically following the manufacturer’s \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains human \nalbumin, stannous chloride, Tween 20, ascorbic acid and buffer.\n99mTc as sodium pertechnetate (fission or non-fission), is used \nwith the kit to make 99mTc-HSA-NC Injection."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Serum Albumin Nanocolloid Injection\n(99,’!Tc-IISA-NC Injection)\nCategory: Diagnostic.\nDescription. 99mTc-HSA-NC Injection is a clear, colourless\nsterile solution, containing not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated \"mTc-radioaCtivity at \nthe date and time stated on the label. Not less than\n90.'0 per cent of the total technetium-99m is present as\n99mTc“HSA-NC. 99mTc-HSA-NC injection should be used \nwithin 4 hours from the timc of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. 99tnTc-HSA-NC Injection \nis prepared aseptically following the manufacturer’s \ninstructions provided with the kit. The kit contains human \nalbumin, stannous chloride, Tween 20, ascorbic acid and buffer.\n99mTc as sodium pertechnetate (fission or non-fission), is used \nwith the kit to make 99mTc-HSA-NC Injection."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by gamma ray spectrometry using a gamma ray \nspectrometer. The prominent gamma photon of technetium- \n99m has energy of 0.140 MeV.\nB. In the test for radiochemical,purity, the chromatogram \nobtained contributes to the identification of 99mTc-HSA-NC."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 6.0.\nadequate shielding.\n4774\nRadioactivity assay. Radioactivity of an aliquot of test solution \nis assayed using a pre calibrated dose calibrator. It is expressed \nas MBq(mCi) in volume V ml at a particular time and date, \nV being the maximum recommended dose in ml.\nRadionuclidic purity. Not less than 99.9 per cent due to \nTechnctium-99m.\nComplies with the radionuclidic purity tests as described under \nmonograph on Sodium Pertechnetate (\"mTc) Injection \n(Fission) or Sodium Pertechnetate (\"mTc) Injection \n(Non Fission) used for preparation of 99mTc-HS A-NC Ihj ection.\nRadiochemical purity. Determine by paper chromatography \n(2.4.15) ..\nSupport. Chromatography paper Whatman paper No. 1 or \nequivalent. (3 cmx 15 cm)\nMobile phase. 0.9 per cent sodium chloride solution.\nApply 5 to 10 pl of the injection under examination 3 cm from \nbottom end of the chromatography strip. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 10 cm and dry the strip in air. Determine the \ndistribution ?of radioactivity using a suitable radioactivity \ncounting system. The free pertechnetate (Tc-99m) ion exhibits \nRf in the range 0.8 to 1.0 whereas 99mTc-HSA-NC remains at \nthe point of application (Rf 0.1). The percentage radioactivity\n(at Rf 0.8 to 1.0) should not more than 5 per cent.\nPhysiological distribution. To be done in laboratory animals \nas described in section 1.6 in General Chapter on \nRadiopharmaceutical preparations by using Wistar rats. The \nanimals are administered 50 ul volume of the 99mTc-HSA-NC \nInjection subcutaneously to the footpad of the animal under \nanaesthesia; At 2 hour post injection the 99l71Tc-activity in \nrelevant organs and tissues are measured using suitable \nradioactivity counter. 99mTc-activity in the sentinel node should \nnot be less than 1.5 per cent and 99mTc-activity in popliteal \nnode indicates sentinel lymph node accumulation and activity \nin upstream nodes (iliac, inguinal) and other regions indicates \nfurther spread of the preparation through the lymphatic stream^ \nNot more than 1.0 per cent of injected dose should be present\nin blood, not more than 1 per cent of injected dose should be \npresent in liver and not more than 1 per cent of injected dose \nshould be present in kidney in two out of three rate. ;\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility as describ,ed \nin general chapter on Radiopharmaceutical preparations. The \ninjection may be released for use before completion of the \ntest.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3): Less than 175 per V EU per ml,V \nbeing the maximum recommended dose in ml. Alternatively \nthe preparation should pass the pyrogen tests (2.2.8.), . :\nStorage: Injection may be stored at room temperature with\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nTECHNETIUM (99MTC) MACROSALB INJECTION\nLabelling. The label states, in addition to the information \nspecified under injection (1) the amount of 99raTc as labelled \nHSA-NC expressed as total megabecquerels (or millicuries) \nand the radioactiye concentration as megabecquerels per ml \n(or as millicuries per ml) on the date and time of reconstitution; \n(2) the expiration date and time; (3) the statement, “Caution 7 \nRadioactive Material,”\nTechnetium (99mTc) Macrosalb"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/serum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Serum"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Serum\nBrucella abortus Working Standard Serum is serum ofcattle\ninfected with Brucella abortus biotype I, or serum raised in\nrabbits against smooth cultures of B. abortus strain 99 or\nstrain 544 which is suitably diluted with healthy cattle serum\norrabbit serum as appropriate. It contains 0.01 per cent w/v of\nthiomersal as antibacterial preservative.\nThe serum is suitably standardised so that a 1 in 500 dilution\ngives 50 per cent agglutination in tube agglutination test in\ncomparison with the Brucella abortus standard serum."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Serum\nBrucella abortus Working Standard Serum is serum ofcattle\ninfected with Brucella abortus biotype I, or serum raised in\nrabbits against smooth cultures of B. abortus strain 99 or\nstrain 544 which is suitably diluted with healthy cattle serum\norrabbit serum as appropriate. It contains 0.01 per cent w/v of\nthiomersal as antibacterial preservative.\nThe serum is suitably standardised so that a 1 in 500 dilution\ngives 50 per cent agglutination in tube agglutination test in\ncomparison with the Brucella abortus standard serum."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Gives specific agglutination when mixed with a pure smooth\nculture ofBnlCelia abortus organisms."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Sterility (2.2.11). Complies with tests for sterility.\nOther tests. Complies with the tests stated under Veterinary\nDiagnostics.\nAssay. When tested with standardised Brucella abortus tube\ntest antigen, gives 50 per cent agglutination at 1 in 500 final\nserum dilution in tube agglutination test in comparison with\nthe Brucella abortus standard serum.\nStorage. As stated under Veterinary Diagnostics.\nLabelling. As stated under Veterinary Diagnostics."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/shankhpushpi","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Shankhpushpi"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Shankhpushpi\nShankhpushpi consists of whole plant of Convolvulus \npluricaulis Choisy (Fam. Convolvulaceae).\nShankhpushpi contains not less than 0.1 per cent to 0.3 per \ncent of Scopoletin, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiu Icerogenic, anti depressant, antiepileptic, \nhepatoprotective and anti-inflammatory.\nDescription. A light green yellowish, in colour and slightly \nbitter in taste with characteristic odour.\nA. Macroscopic — Root. Cylindrical, straight and branched, : \n7-12 cm long, 0.1-0.4 cm thick, the root is faintly longitudinally \nstriated and exhibits small circular rootlet scars, fractured\n•, \n' / • 1 \n, : . ’ : ‘ ‘ \n'J ! ? ; ; \n’lAt1 C:.! ’7:i'Q0\nsurface and is yellowish brown to light brown in colour.\nStem. Cylindrical and aerialstems densely covered with hairs. \nThe stemis 2 to 6nmiindiameter:and20.to.50cminlérigth, \nlight green in colour, taste is slightly bitter.. -,:r -. L\nLeaf. Shortly petiolate, linear-lanceolate, acute, hairy on both\nsurfaces; 0.5-2 cm long and 10-0.5 cm broad; light green.\nFlower. White or pinkish; solitaiy or in pairs sessile or sub \nsessile in the leaf axis; sepals narrow, linear lanceolate, sparsely \nhairy; corolla shortly discoid; stamen 5,free, epipetalous, \nalternate with the petals, inserted deep in the corolla tube; \novary superior and bicarpellary\nFruit. Capsule, oblong globose with coriaceous, pale brown \npericarp. ' ' ’-‘V'\nSeed. Brown, minutely puberulous. : \n: \n;\nB. Microscopic — TfódZi-T.S.-óf root shows outer cork \ncomposed of 10-15 years of tangentially elongated, thick-\n4295\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSHANKHPUSHPI\nwalled suberized cells, cortex many layered, parenchymatous, \ntraversed by yellowish-brown tanniniferbus secretory cells \nand starch grains, phloem composed of sieve elements, \nphloem parenchyma and phloem rays. Xylem vessel solitary \nor* in groups of 2-5; tracheids and parenchyma pitted; \nmedullary rays 1-3 seriate; starch grains, round to oval in \nshape,¡simple or compound’ measuring 3-8 p, in diaïïieter.\nStem. Phloem very narrow, xylem ring wider, vessels scattered \nthroughout but abundantly towards inner region, medullary \nraÿs uni seriate, central pith parenchymatous, encircled by \ngroups of perimedullary phloem. \nJ\nLeaf. T.S. of leaf shows convex midrib on the lower side andai \nthé upper side, a smallcentfàlly placed notch. A single layered \nepjderinis is present which is covered with thick striated cuticle \nand long unicellular trichomes. A two layered palisade region \nlies underneath the epidermis of the lamina. A layer of spongy \nparenchyma is seen in between the upper and lower palisade \ntissue.\nC. Determine by thin layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 4 volumes of toluene, 2.5 volumes \nof chloroform., and 3.5 volumes of acetone.\nTest solution. Reflux 2 g of coarsely powdered substance under \nexamination with 50 ml of methanol for 30 minutes, cool and \nfilter. Reflux the residue further with 50 ml bf methanol, cool \nand filter. Combine the filtrates and concentrate under vacuum \nto 25.0ml.\nReference solution. Reflux 2 g of shankhpushpi IPBRSpftâs \n50 ml of methanol for 30 minutes, cool and filter. Reflux the \nresidue further with 50 ml of methanol, cobl and filter. Combine \nthe filtrates and concentrate under vacuum to 25.0 ml.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of both test and referehce'sblution as \nbands bf 10 mm by 2 mm. Allow thé mobile phase to rise 8 cm.\nDry the plate in air and examine in ultraviolet light at 254 nm \narid 366 nm. Spray with anisaldehyde sulphuric acid reagent \nand heat the plate at 100° for 10 minutes. Examine the plate in \nday light. The chromatographic profile of the, test solution is \nsimilar to that of the reference solution.\nTests' \nr . .-.w ■.\nForeign organic matter (2,6.1). Not more than 1.0 per cent.\nEthanol- soluble extractive (2.6.2). Not less than 6:0 per cent\nWater soluble extractive (2.6.3). Not less than 13.0 per cent.\nTotal ash (2,3.19). Not more than 20.0 pe"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Shankhpushpi\nShankhpushpi consists of whole plant of Convolvulus \npluricaulis Choisy (Fam. Convolvulaceae).\nShankhpushpi contains not less than 0.1 per cent to 0.3 per \ncent of Scopoletin, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiu Icerogenic, anti depressant, antiepileptic, \nhepatoprotective and anti-inflammatory.\nDescription. A light green yellowish, in colour and slightly \nbitter in taste with characteristic odour.\nA. Macroscopic — Root. Cylindrical, straight and branched, : \n7-12 cm long, 0.1-0.4 cm thick, the root is faintly longitudinally \nstriated and exhibits small circular rootlet scars, fractured\n•, \n' / • 1 \n, : . ’ : ‘ ‘ \n'J ! ? ; ; \n’lAt1 C:.! ’7:i'Q0\nsurface and is yellowish brown to light brown in colour.\nStem. Cylindrical and aerialstems densely covered with hairs. \nThe stemis 2 to 6nmiindiameter:and20.to.50cminlérigth, \nlight green in colour, taste is slightly bitter.. -,:r -. L\nLeaf. Shortly petiolate, linear-lanceolate, acute, hairy on both\nsurfaces; 0.5-2 cm long and 10-0.5 cm broad; light green.\nFlower. White or pinkish; solitaiy or in pairs sessile or sub \nsessile in the leaf axis; sepals narrow, linear lanceolate, sparsely \nhairy; corolla shortly discoid; stamen 5,free, epipetalous, \nalternate with the petals, inserted deep in the corolla tube; \novary superior and bicarpellary\nFruit. Capsule, oblong globose with coriaceous, pale brown \npericarp. ' ' ’-‘V'\nSeed. Brown, minutely puberulous. : \n: \n;\nB. Microscopic — TfódZi-T.S.-óf root shows outer cork \ncomposed of 10-15 years of tangentially elongated, thick-\n4295\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSHANKHPUSHPI\nwalled suberized cells, cortex many layered, parenchymatous, \ntraversed by yellowish-brown tanniniferbus secretory cells \nand starch grains, phloem composed of sieve elements, \nphloem parenchyma and phloem rays. Xylem vessel solitary \nor* in groups of 2-5; tracheids and parenchyma pitted; \nmedullary rays 1-3 seriate; starch grains, round to oval in \nshape,¡simple or compound’ measuring 3-8 p, in diaïïieter.\nStem. Phloem very narrow, xylem ring wider, vessels scattered \nthroughout but abundantly towards inner region, medullary \nraÿs uni seriate, central pith parenchymatous, encircled by \ngroups of perimedullary phloem. \nJ\nLeaf. T.S. of leaf shows convex midrib on the lower side andai \nthé upper side, a smallcentfàlly placed notch. A single layered \nepjderinis is present which is covered with thick striated cuticle \nand long unicellular trichomes. A two layered palisade region \nlies underneath the epidermis of the lamina. A layer of spongy \nparenchyma is seen in between the upper and lower palisade \ntissue.\nC. Determine by thin layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 4 volumes of toluene, 2.5 volumes \nof chloroform., and 3.5 volumes of acetone.\nTest solution. Reflux 2 g of coarsely powdered substance under \nexamination with 50 ml of methanol for 30 minutes, cool and \nfilter. Reflux the residue further with 50 ml bf methanol, cool \nand filter. Combine the filtrates and concentrate under vacuum \nto 25.0ml.\nReference solution. Reflux 2 g of shankhpushpi IPBRSpftâs \n50 ml of methanol for 30 minutes, cool and filter. Reflux the \nresidue further with 50 ml of methanol, cobl and filter. Combine \nthe filtrates and concentrate under vacuum to 25.0 ml.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of both test and referehce'sblution as \nbands bf 10 mm by 2 mm. Allow thé mobile phase to rise 8 cm.\nDry the plate in air and examine in ultraviolet light at 254 nm \narid 366 nm. Spray with anisaldehyde sulphuric acid reagent \nand heat the plate at 100° for 10 minutes. Examine the plate in \nday light. The chromatographic profile of the, test solution is \nsimilar to that of the reference solution.\nTests' \nr . .-.w ■.\nForeign organic matter (2,6.1). Not more than 1.0 per cent.\nEthanol- soluble extractive (2.6.2). Not less than 6:0 per cent\nWater soluble extractive (2.6.3). Not less than 13.0 per cent.\nTotal ash (2,3.19). Not more than 20.0 per cent.\nAcid-insoluble ash (2.3.19), Not more than 6.0 per cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals. Method B (20 ppm). ; \n;\n4296\n1?2022\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 8,0 per cent determined \non 5.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Complies with the microbial \ncontamination test.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14 j.; \n\" f j ;\nTest solution. Reflux 20 g of the coarsely powdered substances \nunder examination with 100 ml of methanol cm water-bath for \n2 hours, cool and filter. Evaporate to dryness and dissolve in \n5 ml of methanol.\nReference solution. A 0.2 per cent w/v solution ofscopoletj^ \nIPRS 'va. methanol. . \n;\nChromatographic system\n- \na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 30 volumes of methanol and\n70 volumes of a buffer solution of 0.1 per cent v/v of \nformic acid, \n■\n- \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 365 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. Thé test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for the replicate injection is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent. \n/ .\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. \n, -\nCalculate the content of scopoletin.\nStorage. Store protected from heat, moisture and against attack \nby insects and rodents. \n■\nShatavari\nShatavariconsists of the tuberous roots of Aspdrdgus \nracemosus Willd. (Fam. Liliaceae). \n: ■ \"■\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSfeltavaricontains not less than 0.1 per cent w/w of shatavarin \n^calculated on the dried basis.\n' \n■ 'Antiinflammatory, immunomodulatory,\n^oprotcctive and antidysentery.\nngscription.-The tuberous root bits are dirty white in color, \n^gitudinally wrinkled with yellow hard central core. It is\nReference solution\nstarchy and slightly: bitter followed by sweet taste."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A/yfccravcppG - ~ The tuberous roots are borne in a compact \n¿untih ahd are fleshy, and spindle shaped. They are marketed \n¡^pieces 5-15 cm in length and 2 cm in thickness. They are \nIfvèiy white or ash-colored externally and white internally, \nMord or less smooth' when fresh, developing longitudinal \nvviinkles when dry.' They are adventitious that measure about \n¿rie Wter in length tapering at both ends with roughly à \nhundred on each plant.\nB. Microscopic — The inner parenchymatous zone of cortex \nis composed of 18-24 layers in upper and 42-47 layers in the \nmiddle tuberous portion of the roots. The cells are thin - walled \ncellulosic, with circular to oral outlines and distinct inter cellular \nspaces- In some roots 3 A layers of cortex immediately adjacent \nto the endodermis are modified into, a sheath of stone cells \nround the endodermis. The number of vascular bundles is \n30-35 in the upper levels and 35-45 in the middle tuberous \nportions of the roots.\nC. Deterrnine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 13 volumes of chloroform,\nlO volumcs of methanol and 2 volumes of -water.\nTest solution. Reflux I g of the coarsely powdered substance \ntinderexamination with 30 ml of methanol for 30 minutes, cool \nand filter. Reflux the residue further with 2 x 3 0 ml of methanol, \ncool and filter. Combine all the filtrates and concentrate under \nvacuum to 10ml.\nTest solution\nReference solution. Reflux 1 g of shatavari IPBRS with 30 ml\nof methanol for 3 Ó minutes, cool and filter. Reflux the residue \nfurther with 2 x 30 ml of methanol, cool and filter. Combine all \nthe filtrates and concentrate under vacuum to 10 ml.\nApply to the plate 5 pl of each solution as bands 10 mm by \n? inm. Allow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air \nand examine under ultraviolet light at 254 nm and 366 nm, \nspray the plate with vanillin sulphuric acid reagent. Heat the \nplate at: 120° for 10 minutes and examine the plate in day light. \nThe chromatographic profile of the test solution is similar to \nIhat of the reference solution. \n,\nP*;DNA-based Identification (2.2.17). Determine botanical \nidentity by DNA-based testing, in samples requiring such\n^ARI\n...........\" \n$ l\\~\ntesting due to suspected adulteration/subs \ndisputes this test shall be mandatory.\nGenomic DNA extraction\nRinse thoroughly the dried roots of a sample of shatavari \nIPRS in sterile distilled water and cut into pieces of \napproximately 1 mm using sterile blade. Grind 1 g of the sample \nin the presence of liquid nitrogen and 2 per cent w/w polyvinyl \npyrrolidone using pre-chilled mortar and pestle. Transfer the\nfrozen powder in 12 ml of extraction cetyltrimethylammonium \nbromide buffer containing 2 per cent mercaptoethanol to a 50 \nml centrifuge tube. Mix well and incubate at 60° for 12 h. Cool \nthe mixture to room temperature and add equal volume of 24:1 \nchlorqform-isoamylalcohol. Mix by inversion (20-30 times) \nto form an emulsion. Centrifuge the mixture at 10,000 rpm for\n10 minutes at 4°. Transfer the supernatant to a fresh tube and \nprecipitate the DNA by adding 0.6 volumes of chilled \nisopropanol and 1/10th volume , of 3M sodium acetate and \nstore at-20° for 5 hours. Centrifuge the precipitated DNA at\n10,000 rpm fori 0 minutes at 4°. Carefully decant the supernatant \nand wash the pellet with 70 per cent alcohol. Decant the \nsupernatant and dry the pellet at 37°. Dissolve the pellet in \ntris, buffer and store at -20°. Treat the DNA with 3 pl of I per \ncent RNase for 1 hour,at 37°. Add 3 pl of 1 per cent proteinase \nKand incubate at 37° for 15-20 minutes. Add 150 pl ofphenol \nand 150 pl of 24:1 chloroformdsoamylalcohol to each \nmicrofuge tube. Vortex briefly and centrifuge at 14,000 rpm for\n15 minutes. Transfer the supernatant to a fresh tube and re­\nprecipitate the DNA by adding double the volume of absolute \nalcohol and 1/10th volume of 3Msodium acetate and store at\n-80° for 3 hours. Precipitate DNA at 14,000 rpm for 12 minutes\nat 4° by centrifugation and wash the pellet with 70 per cent \nethanol. Dry the pellet at 37° and dissolve in 100 pl - 200 pl of \ntris buffer. \nf,-\nPerform DNA extraction adopting: the same procedure as for \nreference solution but using dried rootsfof shatavari sample \nunder examination in place'pf shdtfivarifPRS.\nPCR amplification\nprimers and conditions using the isdlated gcnomic DNA \nto examine efficiency ofaniplificatioh for the candidate\nDNA barcode. The universal primers/fForward primer: \n5 ’ - ArGC(}ATAC7fo(iGTGTGAAT-3’-ReyersCpr imer : \n5’ - GAC:GCr'rCT(:CAGACTACAM3’jforcandidatc DNA\nbarcode (ÏTS2) are designed. Carry but PCR fiir amplifying the \nDNA preparations in a 50 pl volume of reaction mixture \nconsisting of following components:\n4297\nwww.webofpharma.com\nComponents \n/ Volume\n50 ng Template genomic DNÀ \n2 pl\nlOOng Primer (Forward and Reverse) in , \nequal proportions \nipl'\n50 mM of Magnesium chloride solution , \nIpl\nPCR buffer (5 mMKCi, 0.15 mMMgClp,\n1 'mM Tris HClfpH 8.8; 0.1 per cent Triton X-l 00) 5 pl\n4 mM Deoxynucleotide triphosphate (dNTPs) in \nequal proportions \n: \n5pl\n0.5 UTaq DNA polymerase \n.... \n1 pl\n50 mMTétraméthylammonium chloride \nIpl,\nDouble distilled water \n34pl\nTotal \n. .\\ \n. \n... \n50pl\nCarry out amplifications by using a DNA thermal cycler with \nthe following cycling program: 94° for 5 minutes; 40 cycles at \n94° for 1 minute, 56° for 30 seconds, and 72° for 45 seconds; \nand; final extension at 72° for 10 minutes.\nVisualisation of PCR product 5 pl ofthe PCR product is mixed \nwith Ipl of 30 per cent glycerol and loaded on 1.5 per cent \nagarose gel mTris-borate-EDTA buffer along with 1 OObp DNA \nladder as size marker and 1 pl tracking dye (2.5 per cent Fico 11 \n400,11 mM EDTA, 3.3 mM Tris-HCl,0.017 percent SDS, 0.015 \npercent Bromophenol bide, pH 8.0 at 25°). Electrophoresis is \ncarried out at 100V for 45 minutes and photograph is taken \nunder UV light: Shatavari IPRS served as positive control in \nthe PCR reaction;\n"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/shatavari","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Shatavari"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Shatavari"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Shatavari"},{"name":"IPBRS TEST","text":"(Under UV light at 254 nm)\n3.2.2. THIN LAYER CHROMATOGRAMS OF HERBS AND HERBAL PRODUCTS\nIP 2022\n(At 366 nm after spraying \nwith anisaldehyde sulphuric\nanisaldehyde sulphuric\nacid reagent)\nacid reagent)\n(Under day light after spraying\n(Under UV light at 366 nm)\nwith vanillin sulphuric acid\nreagent)\nTLC Chromatogram of Shatavari\n920\nwww.webofpharma.com\n;?|p2022 \n3.2.2. THIN LAYER CHROMATOGRAMS OF HERBS AND HERBAL PRODUCTS"},{"name":"IPBRS TEST IPBRS TEST","text":"(Under UV light at 366 nm) (After spraying with"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/shati","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Shati"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Shati\nShati consists , of the dried rhizomes of Hedychium spicatum \nBuch.-Ham.ex Smith (Fam. Zingiberaceae).\nShati contains not less than 0.80 per cent w/w of p-methoxy \ncinnamic acid ethyl ester, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiasthmatic, antiseptic, indicated in skin diseases, \nanalgesic, kasa.\nDescription. A reddish-brown outer surface and white in side, \nshort and uneven fracture and camphoraceous odour with \naromatic and pungent taste. •••"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Shati\nShati consists , of the dried rhizomes of Hedychium spicatum \nBuch.-Ham.ex Smith (Fam. Zingiberaceae).\nShati contains not less than 0.80 per cent w/w of p-methoxy \ncinnamic acid ethyl ester, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiasthmatic, antiseptic, indicated in skin diseases, \nanalgesic, kasa.\nDescription. A reddish-brown outer surface and white in side, \nshort and uneven fracture and camphoraceous odour with \naromatic and pungent taste. •••"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Macroscopic —- Tuberous rhizome having reddish brown, \nrough outer surface with round root scars or rootlets which \nremain attached at some places. Transversely sliced pieces of \ndried rhizome are spherical, flat, 1 cm in thickness and 2-3 cm \nin diameter having white and starchy surface.\nB. Microscopic — Transverse section of rhizome shows \noutermost layer, of cork having 2-6 layers of isodiametic, \nnonlignified and suberised cells which are radially arranged. \nIn old rhizome^ the cork cells are exfoliated or crushed. ;Cortex \nis a broad zone with 20-25 layers of thin walled parenchymatous \ncells. Cortex region is filled with abundant starch grains and \nnumerous oleo-resin cells. Vascular bundles are closed and \ncollateral and scattered throughout the ground tissues. \nCambium has 4-5 rows of tangentially elongated cells. It forms \na complete ring between the xylem and phloem groups. Starch \ngrains tissue are simple, circular or oval in shape, found in \nground cells.\nC. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254. ;\nMobile phase; ; A mixture of 80; volumes of n-hexane and \n20 volumes of acetone. \n: \ni\n4300\n...................................................................................................... IP2022\nTest solution. Reflux l.-g of the coarsely powdered substan \nunder examination with 25 ml of methanol \\'or \\ 5minutes cool \nand filter. Reflux the residue further with 2 x 25 ml of methanol \ncool and filter. Combine all the filtrates and:concentrate under \nvacuumto25ml. : \nyiik.om\nReference solution. Reflux 0.5 g oi'thc .sAi?/z 7/3>ZJJ^5'Wjth25 ml \nof methanol for 15 minutes, cool and filter. Reflux the residue \nfurther with 2 x 25 ml of methanol, cooland filter. Combine all \nthe filtrates and concentrate under vacuum to 12.5 ml 5'\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution as bands 10 nun by\n2 mm. Allow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in ah \nand examine .under ultraviolet light at 254 nm and 366 nm, \nspray the plate with anisaldehyde sulphuric acid reagent \nHeat the plate at 110° for 10 minutes and examine the plate in \nday light. The chromatographic profile of the test solution i$ \nsimilar to that of the reference solution. ■ d"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Foreign organic matter (2.6.1). Not more than 2,0 percent.\nEthanol-soluble extractive (2.6.2). Not-less than 0.7 percent.\nWater-soluble extractive (2.6.3). Not less than 12.0 per cent\nby Method I.\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 8.0 per cent. . ;\nAcid-insoluble ash (2.3.19). Not more than 3.0 per cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limittest for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 14.0 per cent, \ndetermined on 5 g by drying in an oven at 105° \n;\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Complies with the microbial \ncontamination tests.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Reflux 2 g of the coarsely powdered substance \nunder examination with 50 ml of methanol oh a water-bath for\n15 minutes, cool and filter. Reflux the residue further with \nmethanol till the extract, turns colourless, cool and filter. \nCombine all the filtrates and concentrate to a volume slightly \nless than 100 ml. Dilute to 100.0 ml with\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solutionof \np-methoxy cinnamic acid ethyl ester IP RS m methanol..\nChromatographic system- \ni u ' r •• :\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), \nmobile phase: 40 volumes of water and 60 volumes of\nacetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n■\n- spectrophotometer set at 310 nm,\n- injectionvolume:20pl.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSHELLAC\nIP2022\nf ecf the reference s°luti°n- The test is not valid unless the \nVj^jve^taiidard deviation for the replicate injections is not \n^^2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nfalculate the content of p-methoxy cinnamic acid ethyl ester.\nStore protected from heat, moisture and against attack \nby insects and rodents."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/shellac","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Shellac"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Shellac\nLac ■\nShellacconsists of a purified substance prepared from the \nresinous secretion of the female insect, Laccifer lacça Kerr \n(Fairi. Coccoidae). Shellac is a polyester resin consisting of \ninter-and intra-esters of polyhydroxyl carboxylic acids formed \nfrom Certain hydroxyl acids and sesquiterpinic acids, and also \ncontains variable amounts of wax.\nThere arc four types of shellac depending on the nature of the"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Shellac\nLac ■\nShellacconsists of a purified substance prepared from the \nresinous secretion of the female insect, Laccifer lacça Kerr \n(Fairi. Coccoidae). Shellac is a polyester resin consisting of \ninter-and intra-esters of polyhydroxyl carboxylic acids formed \nfrom Certain hydroxyl acids and sesquiterpinic acids, and also \ncontains variable amounts of wax.\nThere arc four types of shellac depending on the nature of the"},{"name":"Tests","text":"treatment of crude secretion.\nOrange Shellac is produced by a process of filtration in the \nmolten state and/or by a process of solvent extraction. Orange \nshellac contains hot more than 5.5 per cent w/w of wax.\nRefined (dewaxed) orange shellac is produced by filtration of\nthe wax in a solvent process. It may also be declorized by \nactivated carbon. Refined orange shellac contains not more \nthan 0.2 percentw/w of wax..\nRegular bleached (white) shellac is prepared by dissolving \nthe lac in an alkaline solution and bleaching the solution with \nsodium hypochlorite. It is precipitated by dilute acid and dried. \nRegular bleached shellac contains not more than 5.5 per cent \nw/wofwax....\nRëfihéd bleached shellac is prepared by dissolving the lac in \nan alkaline solution and bleaching thé solution with sodium \nhypochlorite. During the process, wax is removed by filtration. \nIf is precipitated by dilute acid and dried. Refined bleached \nshellac contains not less than 0.2 per cent w/w of wax.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Brownish-orange or yellow, shining, translucent, \nhard or brittle, more or less thin flakes (wax-containing shellac\nand bleached shellac) or a creamy white or brownish-yellow \nPP^d^r. (bleached shellac and bleached, dewaxed shellac)."},{"name":"identification","text":"A-To 50 mg add a few drops of à mixture of 1 g of ammonium \nMolybdate and 3 ml of sulphuric acid; a green colour is \nProduced and it becomes lilac on standing for 5 minutes.\nB. Thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), using the plate coated \nwith silica gel GF254. \n;;\nMobile phase. Amixture of 1 volume of acetic acid, 8 volumes \nof methanol, 32 volumes of methylene chloride and60 volumes \nof ethyl acetate.\nTest solution. Heat 0.25 g of the coarsely powdered substance \nunder examination with 2 ml of dilute sodium hydroxide \nsolution for 5 minutes. Cool and add 5 ml of dilute acetic \nacid. Shake and filter the upper .layer.through;\nsodium sulphate. \n.w; n re- • •\nReference solution. Dissolve 6.0 mg of aleuriiic acid IPRS in\n1.0 ml of methanol, heat gently if required.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution as band length 8 mm \nby 2 mm. Allow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm Dry the plate' in \nair and examine in ultraviolet light at 254 nm and 366 nm. Spray \nthe plate with anisaldehyde sulphuric acid reagent. Heat the \nplate at 105° for 5 minutes and examine in day light. The \nchromatographic profile of the test solution is similar to that \nof the reference solution.\nAcid value (2.3.23). 65 to 95, determined by the following \nmethod. Weigh accurately 2.0 g and dissolve with the; aid, of \ngentle heat, in 50 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) previously \nneutralised to ethanolic thymol blue solution. Titrate with \n0.1 Methanolic potassium hydroxide using ethanolic thymol \nblue solution as an external indicator. Calculate the acid value \nfrom the expression\n5.61 x a/w\nwhere, a ~ number of ml'of 0.1 M ethanolic potassium\nhydroxide and \n, / \n; \n;\nw s= weight, in g, of thcsample. T ; J\nE th a nol-insol uble matter. Nbt; more than 2.0 per cent, \ndetermined by the following method. Weigh accurately 5.0 g \nin an extraction thimble. Cover with ethanol(95percent) and \nallow to stand, for 16 hours. Place in an apparatus for the \ncontinuous extraction of drugs, extract with er/z<7noZ (95 per \ncent) for 4 hours, dry the residueatl0pofor3hours and \nweigh. f T-: \"/xf \n.......\nColophony. Dissolve 2.0 gby shaking With10ml of ethanol, \nadd slowly with shaking 50 ml oflight petroleum (40°to 60°), \nwash with two successive portions, each of 50 ml of water, \nfilter the washed light petroleum solution, arid evaporate to \ndryness. To the residue, add 2 ml ofa mixture of 1 volume of \nbromine, 4 volumes of liquified phenol'arid. 2 volumes of \ncarbon tetrachloride, and cover both cavities with an inverted \nwatch-glass; no purple or deep indigo-blue colour is produced \nin the liquid containing the residue.\n4301\nwww.webofpharma.com\nArsenic (2.3.10). Heat gently 5.0 g with 2 ml of nitric acid and\n0.5 ml of sulphuric acid in a long-necked flask, until the first \nreaction has subsided, cool, add carefully and in small portions,\n15 ml of nitric acid and 6 ml of sulphuric acid taking care to \navoid excessive foaming, and continue heating, adding further \nsmall portion of nitric acid, if necessary, until white fumes are \nevolved and the solution becomes colourless or almost \ncolourless. Cool; add carefully 10 ml of water, evaporate until \nwhite fumes are evolved; and repeat the addition of water and \nevaporation until all the nitric acid has been removed, cool, \ndilute to 50 ml with water, and 10 ml of stannated hydrochloric \nacid /LT The resulting solution complies with the limit test \nfor arsenic (2 ppm), Use 0.5 ml of arsenic standard solution\n(10 ppm As) for the standard stain.\nHeavy metals, (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). ,\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 0.5 g.\nLoss on drying. Not more than 2.0 per cent for unbleached \nshellac and not more than 6.0 per cent for bleached shellac, \ndetermined on 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 40°- 45° for 24 \nhours.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture. Store \nbleached shellac arid bleached, dewaxed shellac at a \ntemperature not exceeding 15°."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sildenafil-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sildenafil Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sildenafil Citrate\no\nC^HjMOhS ; \n' \n/. Mol. Wt. 666.7\nSildenafil Citrate is 5-[2-ethoxy-5-(4-methylpiperazinyl- \nsulphonyl)phenyl]-l-methyl-3-«-propyl-1,6-dihydro- \n7#-pyrazolo[4,3-<7]pyrimidin-7-one citrate.\nSildenafil Citrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C28H3gN6O[]S, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Used in erectile dysfunction.\nDescription. A white to off white powder.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompiare the spectrum that obtained with sildenafil citrate \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of sildenafil citrate.\nCitricacid. 28.0 per centto 34.0 per cent.\nWeigh 0.2 g of the substance under examination and transfer \ninto a 250-ml conical flask, add 50 ml of methanol and sonicate\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSILDENAFIL. CITRATE\nlodissolve.then addSOml of wafen Titrate ydthfi EM sodium \nhydroxidesolutionrdragphenolphthalein indicator until the \ncolour changes from colourless topink. . \n-x, ;y sdri .<•\n1 ml- of 6:1 M'sodiumhydroxide is equivalent to 0.006403 g of\nC6H8O7. \nuh\nRelated.substance^; Determine, by liquid: chromatography\n(2.4.14)/ \n./. \n2 g\nNOTE—Use freshlyprepared solution.\nSolvent mixture.; 30 volumes of water, and. ,70 volumes; of \nacetonitrile.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50. mg of the substance under \nexamination in 100.0 ml of the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of \nsildenafil citrate IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (6). Dilute 10.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture. Further dilute 3.0 ml of the\nsolution to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system \n/ c? •:5?-c..c. •\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such \nas Hypersil ODS Cl 8), .\n- mobile phase: A, dissolve 3 ;85 g of ammonium acetate\nin 1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH 7.5 with ammon ia\n. . solution,\nB. acetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 240 nm,\n;. -• injection volume: 20 pl.\nNiobile phase A Mobile phase B\n. (percenv/v) ■ (percent v/v) ’\nTime \n(in min.)\n■ ■ ■ -: 65 ■ \n- \n35\n- 0\n: 15 : :\n30\n20 \n80\n20...... ' i, ; '' 80'\n40\n./• \n65 \na';:'-: \np - . -.< >\n45\n65 \n35 •\n50\nThe relative retention time with reference to sildenafil for citric \nacid is about 0.11, for N-oxide is about. 0.29 and for \nchlorosulphpnyl is about 1.6. \n...... /-••-. . : 1 ,\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0. \n. v:. -< c\nInject reference solution (b) ’and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution the areaof the peak\ndue to N-oxide impurity is not more than 1,67.. times the are^f \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with refereh^ \nsolution (b) (0,5 per pent), the area of peak due t0 \nchlorosulphonyl is not more than 0.67 times th^aréa.^^ \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with feÿéte^ç \nsolution (b)(0.2 per cent), the area of any othér secondai^ \npeak is riot more than the area of the principal peak ïa \nchromatogram obtained with 'reference solution (b j: (Ôi’d \ncent)I , and the ' sum of areas of all the secondary Jjeaks^^ \nmore than 3.3 times the area of the principal peak in g \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (LÔ jjd \ncent).' Ignore any peak corresponding to citric acid.\nHeavy meta