s/silver-sulphadiazine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Silver Sulphadiazine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Silver Sulphadiazine\nCioHLjAgNAS \nMol. Wt. 357.1\nSilver Sulphadiazine is benzcnesulphonamide, 4-amino-7V-2- \npyrimidinyl, rnonosilver(F) salt.\nSilver Sulphadiazine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of CioHçAgN^S, calculated \nonthe dried basis.\nCategory. Antiinfective.\nDescription. A white to creamy-white crystalline powder. \nStable in air, but turns yellow on exposure to light."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Silver Sulphadiazine\nCioHLjAgNAS \nMol. Wt. 357.1\nSilver Sulphadiazine is benzcnesulphonamide, 4-amino-7V-2- \npyrimidinyl, rnonosilver(F) salt.\nSilver Sulphadiazine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of CioHçAgN^S, calculated \nonthe dried basis.\nCategory. Antiinfective.\nDescription. A white to creamy-white crystalline powder. \nStable in air, but turns yellow on exposure to light."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A- Oetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with silver \nst^phadiazyneIPRS or with the. reference spectrum of silver \nsulphadiazene\nB- In-the Assay, the principal peak, in the chromatogram \no tained.with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nc °matogram obtained with the reference solution.\nC- Dissolve 1 g in 15 ml of ammonium hydroxide and 15 ml of \n^er‘ dilùté to 50 ml with water the solution complies with \n!he tests for silver (2.3.1).\nNitrate. Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nTest solution. Transfer2.0 g of the substance under examination \nto a beaker, add 30.0 ml of waterstir for 20 minutes, and filter \nthrough a suitable, nitrate-free filter.\nReference solution. A 0.033 per cent w/v solution of potassium\nPipet 3.0 ml of the test solution and of deionized water to \nprovide the blank into separate test tubes. Pip