nnpr held over the mixture does not become \nacetate pap^\n¿¡scoioured.\nhie (2.340)--0 g with 10ml pi bromine solution and \n^r-orate t0 dryness on a water-bath, ignite gently, dissolve\ncooled residue, ignoring any carbon, in 50 ml of -water and \n¡4 ml of brominated hydrochloric acid AsT and remove the \nexcess bromine with a few drops of stannous chloride solution \n2sT The resulting solution complies with the limit test for\nSodium PAS Tablets\narsenic (2 ppm). \n'\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 0.66 g complies with the limit test for \n¿aVy metals, Method B (30 ppm).\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 1.0 g in 10 ml of water, add 3 ml of \nacetic acid and filter, dilute the filtrate to 50 ml with water. \n50 ml of the solution complies withthe limit test for chlorides\northophosphate and 6.6 g of sodium dihydrogen \northophosphate dihydrate in 1600 ml with water, add\nDissolution (2.5.2).\n. 1.9 ml of tetra n-butyl ammonium hydroxide (20 per\ncent solution) and make the volume to 1700 ml with\n' water and add 300 ml of methanol,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \nS ~; ejection volume: 20 pi.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \n^Ws^otiS/not more than 2.0 and the column efficiency in \na°t less; than 3000 theoretical plates. The relative standard\n^¡ationfrirfeplfcate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent. \n^^fBference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the\nresolhfioii: between 3-aminophenol and sodium \nani®osalicyiate is not less than 5.0.\nSODIUM .AMINOSALICYLATE TABLETS\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C7H6NNaÓ3.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nSodium Aminosalicylate Tablets\nSodium aminosalicylate tablet contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent not more than 105.0 per cent of C7H6NNaO3,2H2O.\nUsual strengths. 500 mg; 1 g.\nDigest a quantity of powdered tablets containing about 3.0 g \nof sodium aminosalicylate, with 40 ml of water,'and filter. Add \nto the filtrate. 15.ml of IM acetic acid, and allow it to stand \nuntil precipitation has occurred. Collect the precipitate on a \nfilter, wash well with water and dry at 105°C for 30 min. The \nresidue complies with the following tests.\nA. Place about 1 g of the residue in a small, round-bottom \nflask, and add 10 ml of acetic anhydride. Heat the flask oh a \nsteam bath for 30minutes, and add 40 ml of water, mix, filter, \ncool, and allow to stand until diácetyl derivative crystallizes, \nwash well with water, and dry at 105° for 1 hour The diacetyl \nderivative so obtained melts at 191° to 197°. \n. v\nB. Shake 0.1 g of the residue with 10 ml of water and filter. To \n5 ml of the filtrate add 1 drop of ferric chloride-solution, A. \nviolet colour is produced. \n;\nC. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in.the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution;,\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml water, ., \n■ \n.\nSpeed and time. 100 ipm for 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. The filtrate diluted with the mobile phase to \nproduce a 0.055 per cent w/v solution.\nReference solution. A 0.055 per cent w/v solution of sodium \naminosalicylate 1PRS in the mobile phase. . : \n■\nChromatographic system \n''\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n3595\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSODIUM AMINOSALICYLATE TABLETS\n- mobile phase: dissolve. 6.0 g of disodium hydrogen\northophosphate and 6,6 ,g of sodium dihydrogen \northophosphate dihydrate in 1600 ml with water, add\n19 ml of tetran-butyl'ammonium hydroxide (20 per \ncent aqueous solution) and make the volume to 1700 ml \nwith water, add 300 ml of methanol, mix well and degas,\n- flow rate: "}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-ascorbate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Ascorbate","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Ascorbate\nI\nHCOHRO\nNad\nOH\nCHzOH\nMol. Wt. 198.1\nSodium Ascorbate is L-ascorbic acid monosodium salt.\nSodium Ascorbate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and\nnot more than 101.0 per cent ofC6H7Na06, calculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory. VitaminC.\nDose. The equivalent ofup to 1 g of ascorbic acid daily.\nDescription. White or faintly yellow crystals or a crystalline\npowder; odourless or almost odourless. It darkens gradually\non exposure to light.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSODIUM ASCORBATE"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Ascorbate\nI\nHCOHRO\nNad\nOH\nCHzOH\nMol. Wt. 198.1\nSodium Ascorbate is L-ascorbic acid monosodium salt.\nSodium Ascorbate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and\nnot more than 101.0 per cent ofC6H7Na06, calculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory. VitaminC.\nDose. The equivalent ofup to 1 g of ascorbic acid daily.\nDescription. White or faintly yellow crystals or a crystalline\npowder; odourless or almost odourless. It darkens gradually\non exposure to light.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSODIUM ASCORBATE"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test A may be omittediftests B, CandD are carriedout. Test\nBand C may be omitted iftests A and D are carried out.\nA. Detennine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry(2.4.6),\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with sodium\nascorbate RS.\nB. T04mlofa2percentw/vsolutionadd 1mlofO.1 Mhydro-\nchloric acid, add a few ml of2,6-dichlorophenol-indophenol\nsolution; the solution is decolorised.\nC. To 4mlofa2percentw/v solution add 1mlofO.1 Mhydro-\nchloric acid, add 1 drop ofa freshly prepared 5 per cent w/v\nsolution of sodium nitroprusside and 2 ml of dilute sodium\nhydroxide solution. Add 0.6 ml ofhydrochloric aciddropwise\nand stir; the yellow colour turns blue.\nD. A 2 per cent w/v solution gives the reactions of sodium\nsalts (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance ofsolution. A5.0 per cent w/v solution in carbon\ndioxide-free water is clear (2.4.1) and not more intensely\ncoloured than reference solution BYS7 (2.4.1).\npH (2.4.24). 7.0to 8.0, detennined in a 10.0percentw/vsolution.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). +103° to +108°, detennined\nin a 10.0 per cent w/v solution.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for\nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.25 per cent,\ndetennined on 1.0 g by drying overphosphorus pentoxide at\na pressure not exceeding 0.7 kPa for 24 hours.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.2 g, dissolve in a mixture of\n100 ml ofcarbon dioxide-free water and 25 rnl of1 M sulphuric\nacid. Titrate immediately with 0.05 M iodine, using 1 ml of\nstarch solution as indicator, until a persistent violet-blue\ncolour is obtained.\n1ml of0.05 M iodine is equivalentto 0.009905 g ofC6H7Na06.\nStorage. Store protected from light."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-benzoate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Benzoate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Benzoate\nCOONa\n6\nC7HsNaOz\nMol. Wt. 144.1\nSodium Benzoate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not\nmore than 100.5 per cent ofC7HsNaOz, calculatedon the dried\nbasis.\nIP 2010\nCategory. Phannaceutical aid(preservative).\nDescription. A white, crystalline or granularpowder or flakes;\nodourless or with a faint odour; hygroscopic."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Benzoate\nCOONa\n6\nC7HsNaOz\nMol. Wt. 144.1\nSodium Benzoate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not\nmore than 100.5 per cent ofC7HsNaOz, calculatedon the dried\nbasis.\nIP 2010\nCategory. Phannaceutical aid(preservative).\nDescription. A white, crystalline or granularpowder or flakes;\nodourless or with a faint odour; hygroscopic."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. To a 10 per cent w/v solution add ferric chloride test\nsolution; a buff coloured precipitate is fonned. Add dilute\nhydrochloric acid; a white crystalline precipitate is produced.\nB. Gives the reactions of sodium salts and reactions Band C\nofbenzoates (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance ofsolution. A 10.0per centw/v solution in carbon\ndioxide-free water is clear (2.4.1), and not more intensely\ncoloured than reference solution YS6 (2.4.1).\nAcidity or alkalinity. To 20 ml ofa 5.0 per cent w/v solution in\ncarbon dioxide-free water add 0.2 ml of phenolphthalein\nsolution. Not more than 0.2 ml of0.1 M hydrochloric acid or\n0.2 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide is required to change the\ncolour of the solution.\n~rsenic (2.3.10). Mix 5.0 gwith 10ml ofbromine solution and\nevaporate to dryness on a water-bath. Ignite gently, dissolve\nthe cooled residue, ignoring any carbon, in 50 ml ofwater and\n14 ml of brominated hydrochloric acidAsT, and remove the\nexcess of bromine with 2 ml of stannous Chloride solution\nAsT. The resulting solution complies with the limit test for\narsenic (2 ppm).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 2.0 g in 45 rnl ofwaterand 5ml\nofhydrochloric acid. 25 ml ofthe solution complies with the\nlimit test for heavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nChlorinated compounds. Dissolve 0.33 g in 5 ml of\n0.5 M sodium carbonate, evaporate to dryness and heat the\nresidue until completely charred, keeping the temperature\nbelow 400°. Extractthe residue with a mixture of10 ml ofwater\nand 12 ml ofdilute nitric acid and filter; the filtrate complies\nwith the limit test for chlorides (2.3.12).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 2.0 percent, detennined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.25 g, dissolve in 20 ml of\nanhydrous glacial acetic acid, wanning to 50° if necessary,\ncool. Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric acid, using 0.05 ml of\n1-naphtholbenzein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank\ntitration.\n1 ml·ofO.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.01441-g of\nC7HsNaOz.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\n2110\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nSODIUM BICARBONATE INJECTION"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-bicarbonate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Bicarbonate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Bicarbonate Injection \n|\nSodium Bicarbonate Intravenous Infusión1 \n; ■ L‘ ■ \n|\nSodium Bicarbonate Injection is a sterile solution of Sodium | \nB ¡carbonate in Water for 1 njections. \n'' / 1'ri' |\nriwri; ri . ri; ri.--./ ri .■ \nririMriQi-fj-ri-jriri;/ I\nSodium Bicarbonate Injection contains not less than 94.0 per f \ncent and not more than 106.0 per cent of the stated amount of | \nsodium bicarbonate, NaHCOj. \n..... ... ... \n|\nCategory. Electrolyte replenisher; systemic alkaliser ri |\nUsual strengths. 1.4,4.2,5.0,7.5 and 8.4 per cent w/v.. \n|\nDescription. Aclear, colourless solution. \nI\npo ;:.;7 ... . W.W. w w- ■ . -w;.. \n|"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Bicarbonate Injection \n|\nSodium Bicarbonate Intravenous Infusión1 \n; ■ L‘ ■ \n|\nSodium Bicarbonate Injection is a sterile solution of Sodium | \nB ¡carbonate in Water for 1 njections. \n'' / 1'ri' |\nriwri; ri . ri; ri.--./ ri .■ \nririMriQi-fj-ri-jriri;/ I\nSodium Bicarbonate Injection contains not less than 94.0 per f \ncent and not more than 106.0 per cent of the stated amount of | \nsodium bicarbonate, NaHCOj. \n..... ... ... \n|\nCategory. Electrolyte replenisher; systemic alkaliser ri |\nUsual strengths. 1.4,4.2,5.0,7.5 and 8.4 per cent w/v.. \n|\nDescription. Aclear, colourless solution. \nI\npo ;:.;7 ... . W.W. w w- ■ . -w;.. \n|"},{"name":"Identification","text":"I\n: \n. \n■ \n: \nri-- -•< \n: i ri, j- < w. 7\nA. The residue on evaporation, when moistened with |\nhydrochloric acid and introduced on a platinum wire into a |\nflame, imparts a yellow colour to the flame. ; i i ’ \n|\n■ ■ ’ \n■ ./ri\"-:'.ri.riri. ; ri ■■- riri/j-77:Q; riU ri> 7 \n’\nB. It gives reaction (A) of sodium salts and the reactions of | \nbicarbonates(2.3.1). \n! \n।\n: \nri-.; <\nA. Dissolve l g -in water and dilute to 10 ml with water, the \nsolution isstrongly alkaline.\nB. The solution prepared for test A gives reaction (A) of \ncarbonates and reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1)"},{"name":"Tests","text":"....... ■■ :■/ \n.... ri)riri ri- ‘\npH (2.4.24). 7.0to 8.5. ' ' \n’ \n' ” \n' ' ' \n|\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 1.7 Endotoxin Units | \nper ml. \n' \n7'; ri’ . \n|\nOther tests. Comply-with the., tests stated, under ..Parenteral. | \nPreparations (Injections). \n<. ... . . \n|\nAssay. Titrate a volume containing 1.5 g of Sodium g \nBicarbonate with 1Mhydrochloric add using meihyl drange g \nsolution as indicator. \nI\n1 ml of 1 Mhydrochloric acid is equivalent to 0.08401 g o( g \nNaHCO3. \n-'ri-ri .U'7 fri'T.ri. ■ i\nStorage. Store in single dose containers. \n|\nLabelling. The label states (1) the strength as the percentage |\nw/v of Sodium Bicarbonate; (2) the approximate concentration | \nin millimoles per litre, of the sodium ions and the bicarbonate | \nions; (3) that an injection containing visible particles in \nsolution should not be used. \ni\n3598\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSODIUM CHLORIDE\nIP 2022\nSodinfirCaTbonate\nNa CDs \nMol. Wt. 106.0 (anhydrous)\nNa>CQj Hz9 . \nMol.Wt. 124.0 (monohydrate)\ngoijjuhi Carbonate is anhydrous or contains one molecule of\nwater of hydration.\n. ..rA^i ■> f’: Rii) J i ' ; i ' \n: \n. \n■ -■ \n: •\nSodiuni Carbonate contains not less than 99.5 per cent and \nnot more than lOO .5 per cent of Na2CO3, calculated on the\ndriedba^v . V ■ ■ •••< ' ; \n; \n' <■ -■\nCategdry. Pharmacentical aid (alkalising agent)\nPescription./l \n— A white or almost white, slightly\ngranuiarpowdenhygroscopic.\nMonohydrate—- A .white, crystalline powder or colourless\ncrystals.:;^ / ; i-' \n■■■'■ • ■\n■ ■ •\nAppearance of solution. Dissolve 2.0 g in 10 ml of water. The \nresulting .solution is. clear and not more intensely coloured \nthan reference solution YS6 (2.4.1).\nAlkali hydroxides and bicarbonates. Dissolve 0.4 g in 20ml of \nwater, add 20 ml of barium chloride solution and filter. To\nTests ■ \"\n10 ml of the filtrate add 0. L ml of phenolphthalein, solution. \nThe solution does, not become red.- Heat the remainder, of the\nfiltrate to boiling for 2 minutes. The solution remainsclear.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 2.0 gin a mixture of 5 ml of \nhydrochloric acid and 25 ml of water. Heat the solution to \nboiling arid cbol. Add dilute sodium hydroxide solution until \nthe solution is neutral. Dilute to 50 ml with water (solution A).\n10 ml of the resulting solution complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method A (50 ppm). \n; . .\nfron (2.3 ■14). Dilute 20 ml of solution A to 40 ml with water. \nW^B^idn^complies with the limit test.for iron(50 ppm).\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 2:0 g in water, add 20 ml o£ dilute \nn,/r^aczi/ and dilute to 25 ml with water. The solution complies \n'^^he limit test for chlorides (125 ppm).\nSulphates (2.3.17). 15 ml of solution A complies with the limit\ntest for sulphates (250 ppm).\n^°ss drying (2.4.19). .Not more, than .1.0 per cent (for \nanhydrous form) or between 12.0 per cent and 15.0 per cent \n(ormonohydrate form), determined on 2.0 g by drying in an\nOvenat300°. ; \n. J ¡ .\nAssay. Dissolve 1.0 gin 25 ml of water and titrate with 1 M \nhydrochloric acid using methyl orange solution as indicator.\n1 ml of 1 M hydrochloric acid.is equivalent to 0.05299.g of\nNa2CO3. \n..... \n. \n■ -\nStorage. Store in tightly-closed, non-metallic containers.\nLabelling. The label states whether the material is anhydrous \nor monohydrate."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-bicarbonate-intravenous-infusion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Bicarbonate Intravenous Infusion"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Bicarbonate Intravenous Infusion\nSodium Bicarbonate Injection is a sterile solution ofSodium\nBicarbonate in Water for Injections.\nSodium Bicarbonate Injection contains not less than 94.0 per\ncent and not more than 106.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of\nsodium bicarbonate, NaHC03•\nCategory. Electrolyte replenisher; systemic allcaliser.\nUsualstrengths. 1.4,4.2,5.0,7.5 and 8.4 percentw/v.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Bicarbonate Intravenous Infusion\nSodium Bicarbonate Injection is a sterile solution ofSodium\nBicarbonate in Water for Injections.\nSodium Bicarbonate Injection contains not less than 94.0 per\ncent and not more than 106.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of\nsodium bicarbonate, NaHC03•\nCategory. Electrolyte replenisher; systemic allcaliser.\nUsualstrengths. 1.4,4.2,5.0,7.5 and 8.4 percentw/v.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. The residue on evaporation, when moistened with\nhydrochloric acid and introduced on a platinum wire into a\nflame, imparts a yellow colourto the flame.\nB. Gives reaction A of sodium salts and the reactions of\nbicarbonates (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 7.0 to 8.5.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.5 Endotoxin Unit\nperrnl.\nOther tests. Complies with the tests stated under Parenteral\nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Titrate avolume containing 1.5 g ofSodium Bicarbonate\nwith 1 M hydrochloric acid using methyl orange solution as\nindicator.\n1 ml of 1 M hydrochloric acid is equivalent to 0.08401 g of\nNaHC03•\nStorage. Store in single dose containers.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the strength as the percentage\nw/v of Sodium Bicarbonate; (2) the approximate\nconcentrations, in millimoles per litre, ofthe sodium ions and\nthe bicarbonate ions; (3) that an injection containing visible\nparticles in the solution should not be used.\nwww.webofpharma.com"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-bicarbonate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Bicarbonate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Bicarbonate\nSodium I lydrogen Carbonate\nAssay. Weigh 1.5 g, dissolve in 50 ml of carbon dioxide-free \nwater and titrate with 1 M hydrochloric acid using 0.2 ml of \nmethyl orange solution  as indicator.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (preservative). \t)01\nNaHCO3 \t\nMol. Wt. 84.0\nDescription. A white, crystalline or granular powder or flakes; \nodourless or with a faint odour; hygroscopic.\nSodium Bicarbonate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of NaHCO 3 .\n1 ml of 1 M hydrochloric acid is equivalent to 0.08401 g of \nNaHCO3."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Bicarbonate\nSodium I lydrogen Carbonate\nAssay. Weigh 1.5 g, dissolve in 50 ml of carbon dioxide-free \nwater and titrate with 1 M hydrochloric acid using 0.2 ml of \nmethyl orange solution  as indicator.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (preservative). \t)01\nNaHCO3 \t\nMol. Wt. 84.0\nDescription. A white, crystalline or granular powder or flakes; \nodourless or with a faint odour; hygroscopic.\nSodium Bicarbonate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of NaHCO 3 .\n1 ml of 1 M hydrochloric acid is equivalent to 0.08401 g of \nNaHCO3."},{"name":"identification","text":"Category. Electrolyte replenisher; systemic alkaliser.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nA. To a 10 per cent w/v solution add ferric chloride test \nsolution; a buff coloured precipitate is formed. Add  dilute\nDose. I to 4 g.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder or small, opaque, \nmonoclinic crystals. It gradually forms sodium carbonate on \nheating 111 the dry state or in solution.\nhydrochloric acid; a white crystalline precipitate is produced."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-carbonate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Carbonate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Carbonate\nNa2CO3 \t\nMol. Wt. 106.0 (anhydrous)\nNa2CO3, H2O \t\nMol.Wt.124.0 (monohydrate)\nSodium Carbonate is anhydrous or contains one molecule of \nwater of hydration.\nSodium Carbonate contains not less than 99.5 per cent and \nnot more than 100.5 per cent of Na 2CO3, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (alkalising agent)\nDescription. Anhydrous - A white or almost white, slightly \ngranular powder, hygroscopic.\nMonohydrate - A white, crystalline powder or colourless \ncrystals."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Carbonate\nNa2CO3 \t\nMol. Wt. 106.0 (anhydrous)\nNa2CO3, H2O \t\nMol.Wt.124.0 (monohydrate)\nSodium Carbonate is anhydrous or contains one molecule of \nwater of hydration.\nSodium Carbonate contains not less than 99.5 per cent and \nnot more than 100.5 per cent of Na 2CO3, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (alkalising agent)\nDescription. Anhydrous - A white or almost white, slightly \ngranular powder, hygroscopic.\nMonohydrate - A white, crystalline powder or colourless \ncrystals."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Dissolve 1 g in water and dilute to 10 ml with water; the \nsolution is strongly alkaline.\nB. The solution prepared for test A gives reaction (A) of \ncarbonates and reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1)"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. Dissolve 2.0 g in 10 KoTikiztgx.' The, \nresulting solution is clear and not more intensely co d \nthan reference solution YS6 (2.4.1).\nIP 201 8 \nSODIUM CHLORIDE \nSODIUM CARBONATE\nAlkali hydroxides and bicarbonates. Dissolve 0.4 g in 20 mi of \nwater, add 20 ml of barium chloride solution and filter. To\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours.\nAppearance of solution. Solution A is clear (2.4.1), and \ncolourless (2.4.1).\n10 ml of the filtrate add 0.1 ml of phenolphthalein solution. \nThe solution does not become red. Heat the remainder of th e\nAssay. Dissolve 0.1 g in 50 ml of water in a glass-stoppered \nflask. Add 50.0 ml of 0.1 Msilver nitrate, 5 ml of 2 M nitric \nacid and 2 ml ofdibutyl phthalate, shake well and titrate with \n0.1 M ammonium thiocyanate using 2 ml offerric ammonium \nsulphate solution as indicator, until the colour becomes reddish \nyellow.\n filtrate to boiling for 2 minutes. The solution remains clear.\nAcidity or alkalinity. To 20 ml of solution A add 0.1 ml of \nbrornothymol blue solution; not more than 0.5 ml of \n0.01 M hydrochloric acid or of 0.01 M sodium hydroxide is \nrequired to change the colour of the solution.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 2.0 g in a mixture of 5 ml of \nhydrochloric acid and 25 ml of water. Heat the solution to \nboiling and cool. Add dilute sodium hydroxide solution until \nthe solution is neutral. Dilute to 50 ml with water (solution A).\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 10.0 g in 50 ml of water and 12 ml of \nstannated hydrochloric acid AsT. The resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for arsenic (1 ppm).\n1 ml of 0.1 Msilver nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g of NaCI.\n10 ml of the resulting solution complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method A (50 ppm).\nSodium Chloride intended for use in the manufacture of \nparenteral preparations or in the manufacture of dialysis \nsolutions complies with the following additional requirement\nIron (2.3.14). Dilute 20 ml of solution A to 40 ml with water. \nThe solution complies with the limit test for iron (50 ppm).\nBarium. Dissolve 2 g in 10 ml of water, and add 2 ml of dilute \nsulphuric acid; no turbidity is produced within 2 hours.\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 2.0 g in water, add 20 ml of dilute \nnitric acid and dilute to 25 ml with water. The solution complies \nwith the limit test for chlorides (125 ppm).\nPotassium. Not more than 0.1 per cent, determined by flame \nphotometry (2.4.4), using a 1.0 per cent w/v solution and \nmeasuring at 768 nm. Use suitable dilutions in water of \npotassium solution FP for the standard solution.\nBromide. To 0.5 ml of solution A add 4.0 ml of water, 2.0 ml of \nphenol red reagent and 1.0 ml of 0.01 per cent w/v solution of\nchloramine T and mix immediately. After exactly 2 minutes,\nadd 0.15 ml of 0.1 M sodium thiosulphate, mix and dilute to\nSulphates (2.3.17). 15 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for sulphates (250 ppm).\n10.0 ml with water. The absorbance of the solution measured\nSodium Chloride intended for use in the preparation of \ndialysis solutions complies with the following additional \nrequirement.\nat about 590 nm (2.4.7), using water as the blank, is not more \nthan that of the standard solution prepared at the same time \nand in the same manner, starting with the addition of \" 2.0 ml \nof phenol red reagent \", using 5.0 ml of a 0.0003 per cent \nw/v solution ofpotassium bromide (100 ppm).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.0 per cent (for \nanhydrous form) or between 12.0 per cent and 15.0 per cent \n(for monohydrate form), determined on 2.0 g by drying in an \noven at 300°.\nAluminium. Not more than 0.2 ppm, determined by the \nfollowing method. Dissolve 20 g in 100 ml of water and add\nAssay. Dissolve 1.0 g in 25 ml of water and titrate with 1 M \nhydrochloric acid using methyl orange solution as indicator.\n10 ml of acetate bufftr pH 6.0. Extract the resulting solution \nwith successive quantities of 20, 20 and 10 ml of a 0.5 per cent \nw/v solution of 8-hydroxyquinoline in chlorofbrm and dilute \nthe combined extracts to 50 ml with chloroform. Use as the \nblank solution a mixture of 10 ml of acetate buffer pH 6.0 and\nCalcium and magnesium. Not more than 50 ppm, calculated \nas Ca, determined by the following method. Dissolve 20.0 g in \n200 ml of water, and add 0.1 ml of hydrochloric acid, 5 ml of \nstrong ammonia-ammonium chloride solution, 5 drops of \neriochrome black T solution and titrate with 0.005 Mdisodium \nedetate to a blue end-point.\n1 ml of 1 M hydrochloric acid is equivalent to 0.05299 g of \nNa2CO3.\nStorage. Store in tightly-closed, non-metallic containers.\n100 ml of water treated in the same manner and as the standard \nsolution a mixture of 2 ml of aluminium standard solution \n(2 ppm Al), 10 ml of acetate buffer  pH 6.0 and 90 ml of water \ntreated in the same manner. Measure the fluorescence of the \ntest solution and of the standard solution (2.4.5), using an \nexcitation wavelength of 392 nm and a secondary filter with a \ntransmission band centered at 518 nm, or a monochromator \nset to transmit at this wavelength, and setting the instrument \nto zero with the blank solution in each case. The fluorescence \nof the test solution is not more than that of the standard \nsolution.\nLabelling. The label states whether the material is anhydrous \nor monohydrate.\n1 ml of 0.005 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.0002004 g \nof Ca.\nFerrocyanide. Dissolve 2.0 g in 6 ml of water and add 0.5 ml of \na mixture of 5 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution offerric ammonium \nsulphate in a 0.25 per cent w/v solution of sulphuric acid, and \n95 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution offerrous sulphate; no blue \ncolour is produced within 10 minutes."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-chloride-hypertonic-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Chloride Hypertonic Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Chloride Hypertonic Injection\nHypertonic Saline\nSodium Chloride Hypertonic Injection is a sterile 1.6 per cent \nw\\?o|^tipn of Sodium Chloride in Water for Injections. It \ncontains ho antimicrobial agent.\nSodium Chloride Hypertonic Injection contains not less than"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Chloride Hypertonic Injection\nHypertonic Saline\nSodium Chloride Hypertonic Injection is a sterile 1.6 per cent \nw\\?o|^tipn of Sodium Chloride in Water for Injections. It \ncontains ho antimicrobial agent.\nSodium Chloride Hypertonic Injection contains not less than"},{"name":"Identification","text":"per cept w/v and not more than 1,68 per cent w/v of \n^diumchloride, NaCl.\n^escr^Pdon. A clear, colourless solution.\nh givesthe; reactions of sodium salts , and reaction (A) of \nchlorides(2,3.1); / .\npH (2.4.24). 4.5 to 7.0.\nTests', \"''\nPH(2.4i24).5.0to7.5.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Evaporate 40 ml to about 20 ml, add 2 ml \nof dilute acetic acid and dilute to 25 ml with water. The solution \ncomplies, with the limit test for heavy metals, Method A \n(0.5 ppm).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.5 Endotoxin Unit \nperml.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Intravenous Infusions).\nAssay. To a measured volume containing about 0.16 g of\nSodium Chloride in a glass-stoppered flask add50 ml of water \nand 50.0 ml of 0.1 Msilver nitrate, 5 ml of 2 Mnitric acid and \n2 ml of dibutyl phthalate. Shake well and titrate with 0.1 M \nammonium thiocyanate using 2 ml of ferric ammonium \nsulphate solution as indicator, until the .colour becomes reddish \nyellow. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g ofNaCl.\nStorage. Store in single dose containers of glass or plastic. \nOn keeping, small solid particles may separate from a glass \ncontainer.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the strength as the percentage \nw/v of Sodium Chloride; (2) that a solution containing visible \nsolid particles must not be used.\nWhen the preparation is intended for intravenous infusion, \nthe label states that the injection contains approximately \n270 millimoles each ofsodium and chloride ions per litre.."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-chloride-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Chloride Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Chloride Injection\nSodium Chloride Intravenous Infusion\nSodium Chloride Injection is a sterile 0.9 per cent w/v solution \nof Sodium Chloride in Water for Injections. It contains no \nantimicrobial agent.\nSodium Chloride Injection contains not less than 0.85 per cent \nw/v and not more than 0.95 per cent w/v of sodium Chloride, \nNaCl.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\nIt gives the reactions of sodium salts and reaction (A) of \nchlorides(2.3.1).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Evaporate 67 ml to about 20 ml, add 2 ml \nof dilute acetic acid and dilute to 25 ml with water. The solution \ncomplies with the limit test for heavy metals, Method A \n(0.3 ppm).\n3603\nwww.webofpharma.com"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Chloride Injection\nSodium Chloride Intravenous Infusion\nSodium Chloride Injection is a sterile 0.9 per cent w/v solution \nof Sodium Chloride in Water for Injections. It contains no \nantimicrobial agent.\nSodium Chloride Injection contains not less than 0.85 per cent \nw/v and not more than 0.95 per cent w/v of sodium Chloride, \nNaCl.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\nIt gives the reactions of sodium salts and reaction (A) of \nchlorides(2.3.1).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Evaporate 67 ml to about 20 ml, add 2 ml \nof dilute acetic acid and dilute to 25 ml with water. The solution \ncomplies with the limit test for heavy metals, Method A \n(0.3 ppm).\n3603\nwww.webofpharma.com"},{"name":"COMPOUND SODIUM CHLORIDE INJECTION","text":"Bacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.5 Endotoxin Unit \nper ml. ,.r!: . -y.?. ■; : ?.■■■■;:■• ■'.? ;■.///■''>■.. ■ \n•■■■.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Intravenous Infusions).\nAssay. Titrate: a measured volume containing about 0.2 g of \nsodium chloride with 0.1 M silver nitrate using potassium \nchromate solution as indicator. \n;\n1 ml of 0.1Msilver, nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g of NaCl.\nStorage. Store in single dose containers, of glass or plastic. \nOn keeping, small solid particles may separate from a glass \ncontainer. .\nLabelling;: The label states (T) the strength as the percentage \nw/v of Sodium Chloride; (2) that a-solution; containing visible \nsolid particles must not be used. ;\nWhen the preparation is intended for intravenous infusion, \nthe .label states that the. injection contains approximately\n.1.50 millimoles each of sodium and chloride ions per litre."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-chloride-irrigation-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Chloride Irrigation Solution"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Chloride Irrigation Solution\nReadily carbonisable substances. Heat 0.2 g, in powder, with\nSodium Chloride Irrigation Solution is  a sterile solution \ncontaining 0.9 per cent w/v of Sodium Chloride in Water for \nInjections.\n10 ml of sulphuric acid (96 per cent w/w) in a water-bath at \n90° ± 1° for 1 hour and cool rapidly. The solution is not more \nintensely coloured than reference solution YS2 or GYS2 (2.4.1).\nCategory. Systemic alkalinising agent.\nDose. 1 to 10 g.\nSodium Chloride Irrigation Solution contains not less than \n0.85 per cent w/v and not more than 0.95 per cent w/v of \nsodium Chloride, NaCl. It contains no antimicrobial agent.\nDescription. White, granular crystals or a white, crystalline \npowder; odourless; slightly deliquescent in moist air.\nWater (2.3.43). 11.0 to 13.0 per cent, determined on 0.3 g and \nafter stirring for 15 minutes before titrating.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.15 g in 20 ml of  anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid, warming to about 50\". Allow to cool. Titrate with  0.1 M \nperchloric acid, using 0.25 ml of I -naphtholbenzein solution"},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Sodium Chloride Irrigation Solution\nReadily carbonisable substances. Heat 0.2 g, in powder, with\nSodium Chloride Irrigation Solution is  a sterile solution \ncontaining 0.9 per cent w/v of Sodium Chloride in Water for \nInjections.\n10 ml of sulphuric acid (96 per cent w/w) in a water-bath at \n90° ± 1° for 1 hour and cool rapidly. The solution is not more \nintensely coloured than reference solution YS2 or GYS2 (2.4.1).\nCategory. Systemic alkalinising agent.\nDose. 1 to 10 g.\nSodium Chloride Irrigation Solution contains not less than \n0.85 per cent w/v and not more than 0.95 per cent w/v of \nsodium Chloride, NaCl. It contains no antimicrobial agent.\nDescription. White, granular crystals or a white, crystalline \npowder; odourless; slightly deliquescent in moist air.\nWater (2.3.43). 11.0 to 13.0 per cent, determined on 0.3 g and \nafter stirring for 15 minutes before titrating.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.15 g in 20 ml of  anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid, warming to about 50\". Allow to cool. Titrate with  0.1 M \nperchloric acid, using 0.25 ml of I -naphtholbenzein solution"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Chloride\nNaCl \n- \n■ Mol. Wt.58.4\nSodium Chloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent ofNaCl, calculated oh the dried Basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (tonicity agent); fluid arid \nelectrolyte replenisher.\nDescription. White or colourless crystals or a white crystalline \npowder.\nA. It gives the reactions of chlorides (2.3.1). \n<\nB. A 2Q per cent w/v. solution in.carbon dioxide-free water \nprepared from distilled water (solution A) gives the reactions \nof sodium salts (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. Solution A is clear (2.4.1), and \ncolourless(2.4:1). \n' ■■\nAcidity or alkalinity. To 20 rial of solution A add 0.1 ml of \nbromothymol blue solution', not more than 0.5 ml of \n0.01 M hydrochloric acid or of 0.01 M sodium hydroxide is \nrequired to change the colour of the solution.\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 10.0gin50ml of water and 12ml of \nstannated hydrochloric acid AsT, The resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for arsenic (1 ppm).\nBarium. Dissolve 2 g in 10 ml of water, and add 2 ml of dilute \nsulphuric acid', no turbidity is produced within 2 hours.\nBromide. To 0.5 nil of solution A add 4.0 ml of water, 2.0 ml of \nphenol red reagent and 1.0 ml of 0.01 per cent w/v solution of\nchloramine—and mix immediately. After exactly 2-minutes, \nadd 0.15 ml of 0.1- Msodium thiosulphate^ mix and dilute to\n10,0 ml with water. The absorbanceof the solution measured \nat about 590 nm (2.4.7), using water as the blank, is not more \nthan that of the standard solution prepared, at the same time \nand in the same manner, starting with the addition of“ 2.0 ml\n3599\nwww.webofpharma.com\nofphenol red reagent.^rising 5.0 ml of a 0.0003 per cent \nw/v solution of potassium bromide (100 ppm);\nCalcium arid magnesium Not more than 50 ppm, calculated \nas Ca, determined by the following method. Dissolve 20.0 g in \n200 ml of water, and add 0.1 ml of hydrochloric, acid, 5 ml of \nstrong ammonia-ammonium chloride solution, 5 drops of\neribchrome black T solution and titrate with 0.005 Mdisodiiim \nedetate to a blue end-point.\n1 ml of 0.005 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.0002004 g \nofCa.\nFerrocyanide. Dissolve 2.0 g in 6 ml of water and add 0.5 mlof \na mixture of 5 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of ferric ammonium \nsulphate in a 0.25 per cent w/v solution of sulphuric acid, and\n95 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of ferrous sulphate;, no blue \ncolour is produced within 10 minutes.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 4.0 g in 2 ml of diluteacetic acid and \nsufficient water to produce 25 ml. The solution complies with \nthe limit test for heavy metals, Method A (5 ppm).\nIodide. Moisten 5 g by adding dropwise, a solution freshly \nprepared by mixing 25 ml of iodide-free starch solution 2 ml of\n0.5 Msulphuric acid, 0.15 ml of sodium nitrite solution and \n25 ml of water and examine the mixture in daylight; the \nsubstance shows ho blue colour ;after 5 riiiriUtes.\nIron (2.3.14). 2:0 g dissolved in 20 ml oiwater complies with \nthe limit test for iron (20 ppm).\nSulphates (2.3.17). 2.5 ml of solution A diluted to 15 ml with \nwater complies with the limit test for sulphates (300 ppm), ;\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more thanl.Q percent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours. . . .\nAssay. Dissolve 0.1g in 50 ml of water in a glass-stoppered \nflask. Add 50.0 ml of 0.1 Msilver nitrate, 5 ml of 2 M.nitric \nacid and.2 ml of,dibutyl phthalate, shake well and titrate with \n0.1 Mammonium thiocyanate using 2 ml offerric ammonium \nsulphate solution as indicator, until the colour becomes reddish \nyellow. ' ' \n' ' ” \n- - \n'1\n1 ml of 0.1Msilver nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g of NaCl.\nSodium Chloride intended for use in:.the manufacture of \nparenteral preparations or in the manufacture of dialysis\nsolutions complies with the following. additional \nrequirement. ... \n. * .\nPotassium. Not more than 0.1 per cent; determined by flame \nphotometry (2:4.4) or by method A for atomic absorption\nspeefrophotometry (2.4.2), using a 1"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Chloride\nNaCl \n- \n■ Mol. Wt.58.4\nSodium Chloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent ofNaCl, calculated oh the dried Basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (tonicity agent); fluid arid \nelectrolyte replenisher.\nDescription. White or colourless crystals or a white crystalline \npowder.\nA. It gives the reactions of chlorides (2.3.1). \n<\nB. A 2Q per cent w/v. solution in.carbon dioxide-free water \nprepared from distilled water (solution A) gives the reactions \nof sodium salts (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. Solution A is clear (2.4.1), and \ncolourless(2.4:1). \n' ■■\nAcidity or alkalinity. To 20 rial of solution A add 0.1 ml of \nbromothymol blue solution', not more than 0.5 ml of \n0.01 M hydrochloric acid or of 0.01 M sodium hydroxide is \nrequired to change the colour of the solution.\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 10.0gin50ml of water and 12ml of \nstannated hydrochloric acid AsT, The resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for arsenic (1 ppm).\nBarium. Dissolve 2 g in 10 ml of water, and add 2 ml of dilute \nsulphuric acid', no turbidity is produced within 2 hours.\nBromide. To 0.5 nil of solution A add 4.0 ml of water, 2.0 ml of \nphenol red reagent and 1.0 ml of 0.01 per cent w/v solution of\nchloramine—and mix immediately. After exactly 2-minutes, \nadd 0.15 ml of 0.1- Msodium thiosulphate^ mix and dilute to\n10,0 ml with water. The absorbanceof the solution measured \nat about 590 nm (2.4.7), using water as the blank, is not more \nthan that of the standard solution prepared, at the same time \nand in the same manner, starting with the addition of“ 2.0 ml\n3599\nwww.webofpharma.com\nofphenol red reagent.^rising 5.0 ml of a 0.0003 per cent \nw/v solution of potassium bromide (100 ppm);\nCalcium arid magnesium Not more than 50 ppm, calculated \nas Ca, determined by the following method. Dissolve 20.0 g in \n200 ml of water, and add 0.1 ml of hydrochloric, acid, 5 ml of \nstrong ammonia-ammonium chloride solution, 5 drops of\neribchrome black T solution and titrate with 0.005 Mdisodiiim \nedetate to a blue end-point.\n1 ml of 0.005 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.0002004 g \nofCa.\nFerrocyanide. Dissolve 2.0 g in 6 ml of water and add 0.5 mlof \na mixture of 5 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of ferric ammonium \nsulphate in a 0.25 per cent w/v solution of sulphuric acid, and\n95 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of ferrous sulphate;, no blue \ncolour is produced within 10 minutes.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 4.0 g in 2 ml of diluteacetic acid and \nsufficient water to produce 25 ml. The solution complies with \nthe limit test for heavy metals, Method A (5 ppm).\nIodide. Moisten 5 g by adding dropwise, a solution freshly \nprepared by mixing 25 ml of iodide-free starch solution 2 ml of\n0.5 Msulphuric acid, 0.15 ml of sodium nitrite solution and \n25 ml of water and examine the mixture in daylight; the \nsubstance shows ho blue colour ;after 5 riiiriUtes.\nIron (2.3.14). 2:0 g dissolved in 20 ml oiwater complies with \nthe limit test for iron (20 ppm).\nSulphates (2.3.17). 2.5 ml of solution A diluted to 15 ml with \nwater complies with the limit test for sulphates (300 ppm), ;\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more thanl.Q percent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours. . . .\nAssay. Dissolve 0.1g in 50 ml of water in a glass-stoppered \nflask. Add 50.0 ml of 0.1 Msilver nitrate, 5 ml of 2 M.nitric \nacid and.2 ml of,dibutyl phthalate, shake well and titrate with \n0.1 Mammonium thiocyanate using 2 ml offerric ammonium \nsulphate solution as indicator, until the colour becomes reddish \nyellow. ' ' \n' ' ” \n- - \n'1\n1 ml of 0.1Msilver nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g of NaCl.\nSodium Chloride intended for use in:.the manufacture of \nparenteral preparations or in the manufacture of dialysis\nsolutions complies with the following. additional \nrequirement. ... \n. * .\nPotassium. Not more than 0.1 per cent; determined by flame \nphotometry (2:4.4) or by method A for atomic absorption\nspeefrophotometry (2.4.2), using a 1.0 per cent w/v solution \nand measuring at 767 nm. Use suitable dilutions in water of \npotassium solution FP or potassium solution A AS\nrespectively, for the standard solution; : <\n3600 \n• iljlllilg\nSODIUM CHLORIDE\nn>2&$ -W\nSodium Chloride intended for use in the preparation \"t I \ndialysis solutions complies with the following additio^ I \nrequirement. \nI\nAluminium. Not more than 0.2 ppm, determined by | \nfollowing method. Dissolve 20 g in 100 ml of water and add I\n10 ml of acetate buffer pH 6.0. Extract the resulting solution I \nwith successive quantities of20,20 and 10 ml of a 0.5 percent I \nw/v solution of 8-hydroxyquinoline in chloroform and dilute 1 \nthe combined extracts to 50 ¿11 with chloroform; Use as the I \nblank solution a mixture of 10 ml of acetate buffer pH \n| \n100 ml of water treated in the same manner and as the standard \nsolution a mixture of 2 ml of aluminium standard .solution \n(2ppm Al), TO ml of acetate bufferpH6.0efrfr$Q^'ofyQier \ntreated in the same manner. Measure the fluorescencei of the \ntest solution and of the standard solution (2.4.5), using an \nexcitation wavelength of392 nm and a secondary filter with a \ntransmission band centered at 518 nm, or a monochromator \nset to transmit at this wavelength, and setting the instrument\nI\nto zero with the blank solution in each case. Thefluorescence I\nof the test solution is not more than that of the standard f \nsolution. \nr \n|\nSodium Chloride intended for use in the manufacture of I \nparenteral preparations without a further appropriate | \nprocedure for the removal of bacterial endotoxins complies |\n. . ■ . ..................... ; .. ........ |\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3): Not more than 5 .0 Endotoxin Unit |\nper g of sodium chloride. \n|\nwith the following additional requirement \n... \n|\nStorage. Store protected from light. ' \n|\nLabelling. The label states whether or not the material is |\nsuitable for use in the manufacture of parenteral preparations | \nor for the preparation of dialysis solutions, u"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. To1 riiladd0.05 ml of potassium cupri-tartrate solution', \nthe solution remains blue and clear. Heat to boiling, a copious \nred precipitate is formed.\nB. It gives reaction (B) of sodium salts and reaction (A) of\nchlorides (23.1)\n1 mlofpj M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g of NaCl.\n^or dextrose — To a measured volume containing 2 to 5 g of\nai%^roiis dextrose, C^Hi^Oe, add 0.2 ml of 5 Mammonia and \n^««cient.W&ferto^prbduce 100.0 ml: Mix well, allow to stand\n®r30 minutes aridmeasure the optical rotation in a 2-dm tube\n-22). Theobserved rotation in degrees multiplied by 0.9477 \n^P^senis thewcight, in g, of dextrose, C(JI!2O6, in the volume\ne ejection taken for; assay."},{"name":"Tests","text":"x : •\n;• \nJ'S \n>7 ï.v - \n..... \n.’ e\npH(2.4.24).3.5io6.5.\n5-Hydroxymethylfurfural and related substances. Dilute a \nvolume containing 1.0 g of dextrose, C(,H12O6, to 500 ml. with \nwater and measure the absorbance .of the. resulting solution \natthemaxiihum at about 284 nm (2.4.7); absorbance at about \n284 nm, not more than 0.25.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 10 Endotoxin Units \nper g of dextrose\n^ther tesis. Cornply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Intravenous Infusions).\n^&y. For sodium chloride —= Titrate a measured volume \n^ntaining,0;;lig pfiSodium Chloride with 0. 1 Msilver nitrate\npotassiumchromate solution as indicator.\nStoje, ;in; single dose containers. On keeping, small \nPeeles may¿separate from a glass container.\npH (2.4.24). 3.0 to 6.0.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the strength as the percentages \nw/v of Sodium Chloride, and Dextrose; (2) that a solution \ncontaining visible particles must not be used. ;\nWhen the preparation is intended for intravenous infusion, \nthe label states the approximate concentrations, iri millimoles \nper litre, of the sodium and chloride ions and the number of\ngrams per litre of dextrose, C6H12O6.\nSodium Chloride and Fructose"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-citrate-irrigation-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Citrate Irrigation Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Citrate Irrigation Solution\nSodium Citrate Irrigation Solution is a sterile solution of sodium \ncitrate in water for injections containing anhydrous citric acid \nor citric acid monohydrate.\nSodium Citrate Irrigation Solution contains'not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of sodium citrate, C6H5Na3O7,2H2O.\nUsual strength. 3 per cent w/v.\nDescription A colourless solution."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Citrate Irrigation Solution\nSodium Citrate Irrigation Solution is a sterile solution of sodium \ncitrate in water for injections containing anhydrous citric acid \nor citric acid monohydrate.\nSodium Citrate Irrigation Solution contains'not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of sodium citrate, C6H5Na3O7,2H2O.\nUsual strength. 3 per cent w/v.\nDescription A colourless solution."},{"name":"Identification","text":"If gives reactions of sodium salt arid citrates (23.1)'. '\nTeStS ; \n............ \n■-.■■■- i\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections). ':.............- - ■\npH (2.4.24). 6.0to7.5. \n■\nAssay. Evaporate a volume containing 0.15 g of Sodium Citrate \nto dryness and dissolve the residue in anhydrous glacial \nacetic acid, warming to about 50° and allow to cool. Titrate \nwith 0.1 M perchloric acid, using 0.25 ml of 1-naphthlol- \nbenzein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml'of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent,to, 0.009803 g of \nC6H5Na3O7,2H2O."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-diatrizoate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Diatrizoate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Diatrizoate Injection\nDiatrizoate Sodium Injection ■\nSodium Diatrizoate Injection is a sterile solution of Sodium \nDiatrizoate in Water for Injections. It may contain small \namounts of suitable buffers, stabilisers and antimicrobial agents \nbut the preparation intended for intravenous administration \ncontains no antimicrobial preservative.\nSodium Diatrizoate Injection contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and hot more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nsodium diatrizoate, C11HsI3N2NaO4.\nUsual strengths. 25 per cent w/v; 40 per cent w/v.\nEvaporate a volume ofthe injection containing 1 g of Sodium \nDiatrizoate to dryness. The residue complies with following \ntests..\nA. Heat 0.5 g of residue in a crucible; violet vapours of iodine \nare evolved.\nB. To 20 mg of the residue add 5 ml of 7 Msodium hydroxide \nand boil gently under a reflux condenser for 10 minutes. Cool, \nadd 5 ml of 2 Mhydrochloric acid and cool in ice for 5 minutes. \nAdd 4 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of sodium nitrite, cool in \nice for 5 minutes, add 0.3 g oisulphamic acid, shake gently \nuntil effervescence ceases and add 2 ml of a 0.4 per cent w/v \nsolution oiN-(l-naphthyl) ethylenediamine dihydrochloride-, \nan orange-red colour is produced.\nC. Heat 0.5 g of residue with 1 ml of sulphuric acid on a water­\nbath until a pale violet solution is produced, add 2 ml of ethanol \n(95 per cent) arid heat again;' odour of ethyl acetate is \nproduced.\nFree amine. To a volume containing 1.0 g of Sodium Diatrizoate \nin a 50-ml glass-stoppered volumetric flask, add 5 ml of water,\n10 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide and 25 ml of dimethyl \nsulphoxide.. Stopper the flask, mix the contents gently and\ncool in ice, protected from light. After 5 minutes, slowly add \n2 ml of hydrochloric acid, mix and allow to stand for 5 minutes. \nAdd 2 ml of a 2 per cent w/v solution of sodium nitrite, mix \nand allow to stand for 5 minutes. Add 1 ml of a 8 per cent \nw/v solution of sulphamic acid, mix and allow to stand \nfor 5 minutes. Add 2 ml of a 0.1 per cent w/v solution'of \nN-(l-naphthyl)ethylenediamine dihydrochloride in a 70. per\ncent w/v. solution of 1,2-propanediol and mix. Remove the \nflask from the ice and allow to stand in water at 25° ± 2° for\n10 minutes, with occasional shaking. Add sufficient’ dimethyl \nsulphoxide to produce 50 ml and mix. Within 5 minutes, measure\n3607\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSODIUM DIATRIZOATE INJECTION\nthe absorbance of the resulting solution .at the maximum at \nabout 470 nm (2.4.7), using as the blank a solution prepared \nby treating 5 ml of water in flie same mánner;: absorbance, not \nmore than 0.40, calculated on the anhydrous basis. ,\nFree iodine and iodide. To.a volume containing 2.0 g of Sodium \nDiatrizoate in 24 ml of water taken in a 50-ml glass-stoppered \ncentrifuge tube add 5 ml oí toluene and 5 ml of 1M sulphuric \nacid, shake well and centrifuge; no red colour appears in the \ntoluene layer. To the mixture add 1 ml, of a 2 per cent w/v \nsolution of sodium nitrite, shake and centrifuge; any red colour \nin the toluene layer is not more intense than that produced in \na solution prepared in the same manner using 2.0 ml of a \n0.025 per cent w/v solution ofpotassium iodide and 22-ml of \nwater (220 ppm).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 5.6 Endotoxin Units \nper ml for injections containing 20.0 per cent of sodium\ndiatrizoate; not more than 1.3 Endotoxin Units per ml'for \ninjections containing 25.0 per cent of sodium diatrizoate; and \nnot more than 5.Q Endotoxin Units per ml for injections \ncontaining 50,0 per cent of sodium diatrizoate.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Weigh 0.5 g in a glass-stoppered conical flask, add\n12 ml of 5 M sodium hydroxide and 1 g oí zinc powdér and \nboil under a reflux condenser for 30 minutes. Cool, rinse the \ncondenser with 30 ml of water, filter through cotton and wash \nthe flask and filter with two quantities; each of 20 ml, of water: \nTo the combined "},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Diatrizoate Injection\nDiatrizoate Sodium Injection ■\nSodium Diatrizoate Injection is a sterile solution of Sodium \nDiatrizoate in Water for Injections. It may contain small \namounts of suitable buffers, stabilisers and antimicrobial agents \nbut the preparation intended for intravenous administration \ncontains no antimicrobial preservative.\nSodium Diatrizoate Injection contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and hot more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nsodium diatrizoate, C11HsI3N2NaO4.\nUsual strengths. 25 per cent w/v; 40 per cent w/v.\nEvaporate a volume ofthe injection containing 1 g of Sodium \nDiatrizoate to dryness. The residue complies with following \ntests..\nA. Heat 0.5 g of residue in a crucible; violet vapours of iodine \nare evolved.\nB. To 20 mg of the residue add 5 ml of 7 Msodium hydroxide \nand boil gently under a reflux condenser for 10 minutes. Cool, \nadd 5 ml of 2 Mhydrochloric acid and cool in ice for 5 minutes. \nAdd 4 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of sodium nitrite, cool in \nice for 5 minutes, add 0.3 g oisulphamic acid, shake gently \nuntil effervescence ceases and add 2 ml of a 0.4 per cent w/v \nsolution oiN-(l-naphthyl) ethylenediamine dihydrochloride-, \nan orange-red colour is produced.\nC. Heat 0.5 g of residue with 1 ml of sulphuric acid on a water­\nbath until a pale violet solution is produced, add 2 ml of ethanol \n(95 per cent) arid heat again;' odour of ethyl acetate is \nproduced.\nFree amine. To a volume containing 1.0 g of Sodium Diatrizoate \nin a 50-ml glass-stoppered volumetric flask, add 5 ml of water,\n10 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide and 25 ml of dimethyl \nsulphoxide.. Stopper the flask, mix the contents gently and\ncool in ice, protected from light. After 5 minutes, slowly add \n2 ml of hydrochloric acid, mix and allow to stand for 5 minutes. \nAdd 2 ml of a 2 per cent w/v solution of sodium nitrite, mix \nand allow to stand for 5 minutes. Add 1 ml of a 8 per cent \nw/v solution of sulphamic acid, mix and allow to stand \nfor 5 minutes. Add 2 ml of a 0.1 per cent w/v solution'of \nN-(l-naphthyl)ethylenediamine dihydrochloride in a 70. per\ncent w/v. solution of 1,2-propanediol and mix. Remove the \nflask from the ice and allow to stand in water at 25° ± 2° for\n10 minutes, with occasional shaking. Add sufficient’ dimethyl \nsulphoxide to produce 50 ml and mix. Within 5 minutes, measure\n3607\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSODIUM DIATRIZOATE INJECTION\nthe absorbance of the resulting solution .at the maximum at \nabout 470 nm (2.4.7), using as the blank a solution prepared \nby treating 5 ml of water in flie same mánner;: absorbance, not \nmore than 0.40, calculated on the anhydrous basis. ,\nFree iodine and iodide. To.a volume containing 2.0 g of Sodium \nDiatrizoate in 24 ml of water taken in a 50-ml glass-stoppered \ncentrifuge tube add 5 ml oí toluene and 5 ml of 1M sulphuric \nacid, shake well and centrifuge; no red colour appears in the \ntoluene layer. To the mixture add 1 ml, of a 2 per cent w/v \nsolution of sodium nitrite, shake and centrifuge; any red colour \nin the toluene layer is not more intense than that produced in \na solution prepared in the same manner using 2.0 ml of a \n0.025 per cent w/v solution ofpotassium iodide and 22-ml of \nwater (220 ppm).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 5.6 Endotoxin Units \nper ml for injections containing 20.0 per cent of sodium\ndiatrizoate; not more than 1.3 Endotoxin Units per ml'for \ninjections containing 25.0 per cent of sodium diatrizoate; and \nnot more than 5.Q Endotoxin Units per ml for injections \ncontaining 50,0 per cent of sodium diatrizoate.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Weigh 0.5 g in a glass-stoppered conical flask, add\n12 ml of 5 M sodium hydroxide and 1 g oí zinc powdér and \nboil under a reflux condenser for 30 minutes. Cool, rinse the \ncondenser with 30 ml of water, filter through cotton and wash \nthe flask and filter with two quantities; each of 20 ml, of water: \nTo the combined filtrate and washings add 80 ml of hydrochloric \nacid, cool and titrate with 0,05 M potassium iodate.until the \ndark brown colour becomes pale brown. Add 5 ml of chloroform \nand continue the titration, shaking well, after each addition, \nuntil the chloroform becomes colourless.\n1 ml of 0.05 Mpotassium iodate is equivalent to 0.02120 g of \nC11H8I3N2NaO4.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nLabelling. The label states (1 ) the concentration of the active \ningredient; (2) ■ whether The contents are intended for \nintravenous injection and, if so, that the unused -portion \nremaining in the container after use must be discarded.\nSodium Fluoride\nNaF , \n, \n; \nMoI.Wt.42.0\nSodiüm Fluoride contains not less than 98.5 per cent and hot \nmore than 100.5 per cent of NaF, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory . Preventive for dental caries.\nDescription. A white powder or colourless. Crystals. ;\n^2022"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Dissolve 2.5 g in sufficient carbon dioxide free\\vaier \nwithout heating to produce 100 ml (solution A). To 2 ml oj \nsolution A add 0.5 ml of calcium chloride solution', a gelatinous \nwhite precipitate is produced which dissolves on adding 5 \nof ferric chloride solution. i\nB. Add about 4 mg to a mixture of 0.1 ml of alizarin reds \nsolution and 0.1 ml of zirconyl nitrate solution and mix; the \ncolour changes to yellow.\nC. It gives reaction (A) of sodium salts (2.3. 1).\nTests' ; \n• \n'!‘\"7 -----\nAppearance of solution. Solution A is clear (2.4.1), and \ncolourless (2.4.1).\nAcidity or alkalinity. Dissolve 2.5 g oí potassium nitrate fa \n40 ml of solution A, dilute to 50 ml with carbon dioxide-free \nwater, cool to 0° and add 0.2 ml of dilute phenolphthalein \nsolution. Ifthe solution is colourless, not more than 1.0 ml of\n0.1 M sodium hydroxide is required to produce a red colour \nthat persists for not less than 15 seconds. If the ¡ solution is \nred, not more than 0.25. ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid is \nrequired to change the colour of the solution.-.Reserve the \nneutralised solution for the test for Fluorosilicate.\nChlorides (2.3.12). 40 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for chlorides (250 ppm).\nFluorosilicate. Heat to boiling the solution reserved in the \ntest' for Acidity of alkalinity and titrate while hot with\n0.1 M sodium hydroxide until a red colour is produced. Mot \nmore than 1J5 ml of 0.1 Msbdiwn hydroxide is required.\nSulphates (2.3.17). Dissolve 0.25 g in 10 ml of a saturated \nsolution of boric acid in distilled water and add 5. ml of \ndistilled water and 0.6 ml of .7 M hydrochloric acid. The\nsolution complies with the limit test for sulphates (200 ppm)- \nPrepare the standard by mixing together p.6 ml of 7 Mhydrfr \nchloric acid, 5 ml of sulphate standard solution\n(10 ppm SOy and 10 ml of a saturated solution of boric acid, \nin distilled water. \n.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent,determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 130° for 3 hours.\nAssay. Weigh 80 mg, add a mixture of 5 ml of acetic anhydride \nand; 2Q; ml of anhydrous glacial acetic acid and heat to \ndissolve.. Cool,; add 20 mF of dioxan. Titrate with 0.1 \nperchloric; acid, using crystal violet, solution as indicator,\nuntil a green colour is produced. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0,1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.004199 g.of\nNaF.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture. \n::\n3608\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nSODIUM FUSIDATE"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-diatrizoate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Diatrizoate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Diatrizoate\nDiatrizoate Sodium\nCOONa\nCnH8I3N2NaO4 \nMok Wt 635.9\nIB 2022\nSodium Diatrizoate is sodium 3 ,5-diacelamido-2,4,6^0^ \nbenzoate. \n-. \n.-.w\nSodium Diatrizoate contains not less than 98.0,.per cppt and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C( iHgI3N2NaO4, calculatedOn \nthe anhydrous basis. .\nCategory. Urographic radio-opaque substance.\nDescription. A white powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Diatrizoate\nDiatrizoate Sodium\nCOONa\nCnH8I3N2NaO4 \nMok Wt 635.9\nIB 2022\nSodium Diatrizoate is sodium 3 ,5-diacelamido-2,4,6^0^ \nbenzoate. \n-. \n.-.w\nSodium Diatrizoate contains not less than 98.0,.per cppt and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C( iHgI3N2NaO4, calculatedOn \nthe anhydrous basis. .\nCategory. Urographic radio-opaque substance.\nDescription. A white powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Tests A and D may be omitted iftests B, C;E and Fare, carried \nout. Tests B, C.andF may be omittedif tests A, Dancl E.qre \ncarried out. \n;\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2,4,6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with sodium \ndiatrizoate IPRS or with the reference spectrum of sodium \ndiatrizoate. \n. .■ ;I ... ...\nB. Determine by thin-layer chromatography, (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 20 volumes of chloroform,\n10 volumes of methanol and 2 volumes of strong ammonia \nsolution. \n■■■■ ' m . \n- ' ■ ■■ ■ r ■ - V V-: ■■\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under \nexamination in 100 ml of 0.08 per cent w/v solution of sodium \nhydroxide, in methanol. ■ \n, ; \n■\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v of sodium diatrizoate \nIPRS in 0.08 per cent w/v solution of sodium 'hydrdxide in\nmethanol. ■ - \" ■ \n; \n'<\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution After development, \ndry the plate, in air; and-examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm: The principal spot in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe test solution corresponds to that in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution.\nC. Heat 0.5 g in a crucible; violet vapours of iodine are evolved\nD. To 20 mg add 5 ml of 7 Msodium hydroxide and boil gently \nunder a reflux condenser for 10 minutes. Cool, add .5 nil of \n2 Mhydrochloric acid and cool in ice for 5 minutes. Add 4 ml \nof a 1 per cent w/v solution of sodium nitrite, cool in ice for \n5 minutes, add 0.3 g of sulphamic acid, shake gently until \neffervescence ceases and add 2 ml of a 0.4 per cent w/v solution \nof N-(l-naphthyl) ethylenediamine dihydrodhloride', 8® \norange-red colour is produced. \n.\nE. Heat 0.5 g with 1 ml of sulphuric acid on a water-bath until\na pale violet solution is produced, add 2 ml of ethanol (9)Per \ncent) and heat again; odour of ethyl acetate is produced. ..\nF. It gives the reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nTests,\npH (2.4.24). 7.5 to 9.5, determined in a 50 per cent w/vsolution*\n3606\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSODIUM DIATRIZOATE INJECTION\nIP 2022\nc amine. Place 1.0 g in a 50-ml glass-stoppered volumetric \nflask, add 5 ml of water, 10 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide and \n- ml of dimethyl sulphoxide. Stopper the flask, mix the \ncontents gently and cool in ice, protected from light. After \n5 minutes, slowly add 2 ml cd hydrochloric acid, mix and allow \nto stand for 5 minutes. Add 2 ml of a 2 per cent w/v solution of \nsodium nitrite, mix and allow to stand for 5 minutes. Add 1 ml \nof an 8 per cent w/v solution ofsulphamic acid, mix and allow \nto stand fors 5 minutes. Add 2 ml of a 0.1 per cent w/v solution\nN-(l-naphthyi)ethylenediamine ■ dihydrochloride in a\n70 per cent w/v solution of 1,2-propanediol and mix. Remove \nthe flask from the ice and allow to stand in water at 25° ± 2° for\n10 minutes, with occasional shaking. Add sufficient dimethyl \nsulphoxide to produce 50 ml and mix. Within 5 minutes, measure \nthe absorbahrie of the resulting solution at the maximum at \nabout 470 nm (2.4.7), using as the blank a solution prepared \nby treating 5 ml of water in the same manner; absorbance, not \nmore than 0.40, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nFree iodine aridiodide. Dissolve 2.0 g in 24 ml of water taken \nin a 50-ml glass-stoppered centrifuge tube. Add 5 ml of toluene \nand 5 nil of 7 'M sulphuric acid, shake well and centrifuge; no \nred colour appears in the toluene layer. To the mixture add 1 ml \nof a 2 per cent w/v solution of sodium nitrite, shake and \ncentrifuge; any red 'colour in the toluene layer is not more \nintense than that produced in a solution prepared in the same \nmanner using .2.0 nil of a 0,025 per cent w/v solution of \npotassium iodide and 22 ml of water (220 ppm).\nHeavy metals. Dissolve 1.0 g in 20.0 ml of water and 5 ml of \nIMsodiiyn hydroxide, transfer,the solution to.a 50-ml Nessler \ncylinder, , dilute with water to 40 ml and mix. Add 10 ml of \nsodium sulphide solution, shake and allow to stand for\n5 minutes (20 ppm), the colour of the solution when viewed \ndownward over a white surface is not more intense than that \nproduced by treating 2.0 ml of lead standard solution \n(10 ppm Pb) in the same maimer in place of the substance \nunder examination. ■>"},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 6.6 to 7.6.\nWater (2.3^43). 4.0 to 7.0 per cent, determined on 0.4 g.\nAssayWeigh0.4 g in a glass-stoppered conical flask, add\n12 ml of 5 Id sodium hydroxide and 1 g of zinc powder and\na reflux condenser for 30 minutes. Cool, rinse the \ncondenser with 30 ml of water, filter through cotton and wash \ndie flask and filter with two quantities, each of 20 ml, of water. \nT° fre combined filtrate and washings add 80 ml oihydrochtdric\ncool and titrate with 0.05 Mpotassium iodate until the \nbrown colour becomes pale brown: Add 5 ml of chloroform \n^d continue the titration, shaking well after each addition,\nchloroform becomes colourless.\n1 ^0:05 Mpotassium iodate is equivalent to 0.02120 g of \nWjNiNaO«.\nStorage. Store protected from light."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-dihydrogen-phosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Dihydrogen Phosphate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Dihydrogen Phosphate \n(Dihydrate) \n0.251 g\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nFrom the volume of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide required subtract \na volume, in ml, equal to 1.28 times the number of mg of \nNaH2PO4,2H20 present, as determined in the Assay for sodium \nacid phosphate.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nWater for Injection to \n100 \t\nml\nNOTE - 14 ml are to be used for 100 ml of whole blood.\nAssay. For sodium citrate  - Pipette 50.0 ml into a beaker and \ntitrate with 1.3 M hydrochloric acid to a pH of 1.98 ± 0.02, \ndetermining the end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry \nout a blank titration with 50 ml of water.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured liquid; \nodourless.\n1 ml ofthe remainder is equivalent to 0.007005 g ofC 6H8O7,H2O.\nFor sodium dihydrogen phosphate dihydrate - Dilute 5.0 ml \nto 100.0 ml with water. Transfer 5.0 ml to a 25-m1 graduated \nflask and add 10.0 ml of a 2.8 per cent w/v solution of sulphuric \nacid followed by 2.0 ml of a 2.5 per cent w/v solution of \nammonium molybdate, mixing after each addition. Add 1.0 ml \nof aminohydroxynaphthalenesulphonic acid solution and \nsufficient water to produce 25.0 ml, mix and keep aside at 25° \nfor 10 minutes. Measure the absorbance (A 1) of the resulting \nsolution at the maximum at about 660 nm (2.4.7) using as the \nblank 5 ml of water treated in the same manner. Calculate the \ncontent of NaH,PO4,2H20 in each ml of the solution under \nexamination from the absorbance (A,) obtained by \nsimultaneously carrying out the operation using 5.0 ml of a \nsolution of potassium dihydrogen phosphate containing \n0.11 mg of KH 2PO4 per ml (C) and from the expression"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Dihydrogen Phosphate \n(Dihydrate) \n0.251 g\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nFrom the volume of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide required subtract \na volume, in ml, equal to 1.28 times the number of mg of \nNaH2PO4,2H20 present, as determined in the Assay for sodium \nacid phosphate.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nWater for Injection to \n100 \t\nml\nNOTE - 14 ml are to be used for 100 ml of whole blood.\nAssay. For sodium citrate  - Pipette 50.0 ml into a beaker and \ntitrate with 1.3 M hydrochloric acid to a pH of 1.98 ± 0.02, \ndetermining the end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry \nout a blank titration with 50 ml of water.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured liquid; \nodourless.\n1 ml ofthe remainder is equivalent to 0.007005 g ofC 6H8O7,H2O.\nFor sodium dihydrogen phosphate dihydrate - Dilute 5.0 ml \nto 100.0 ml with water. Transfer 5.0 ml to a 25-m1 graduated \nflask and add 10.0 ml of a 2.8 per cent w/v solution of sulphuric \nacid followed by 2.0 ml of a 2.5 per cent w/v solution of \nammonium molybdate, mixing after each addition. Add 1.0 ml \nof aminohydroxynaphthalenesulphonic acid solution and \nsufficient water to produce 25.0 ml, mix and keep aside at 25° \nfor 10 minutes. Measure the absorbance (A 1) of the resulting \nsolution at the maximum at about 660 nm (2.4.7) using as the \nblank 5 ml of water treated in the same manner. Calculate the \ncontent of NaH,PO4,2H20 in each ml of the solution under \nexamination from the absorbance (A,) obtained by \nsimultaneously carrying out the operation using 5.0 ml of a \nsolution of potassium dihydrogen phosphate containing \n0.11 mg of KH 2PO4 per ml (C) and from the expression"},{"name":"Identification","text":"1 ml of 1.3 M hydrochloric acid is equivalent to 0.1274 g of \nC6H5Na307,2H20.\nA. To 1 ml add 0.05 ml ofpotassium cupri-tartrate solution; \nthe solution remains blue and clear. Heat to boiling, a copious \nred precipitate is formed.\nFor free citric acid - Pipette 20.0 ml into a conical flask and \ntitrate with 0.1 M sodium hydroxide using phenolphthalein \nsolution as indicator.\nB. It gives the reactions of sodium salts and reaction (B) of \nphosphates (2.3.1).\nC. To 2 ml add 3 ml of water. The resulting solution gives \nreaction (A) of citrates (2.3.1).\n1 ml of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.006404 g of \nC6H807 or 0.007005 g of C61-1807,H20."},{"name":"Tests","text":"For dextrose - Determine the optical rotation in a 2-dm tube \n(2.4.22). The observed rotation multiplied by 1.0425, represents \nthe weight of C611, 206,H20 in 100 ml of the solution.\npH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 6.0.\nBacterial Endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 5.56 Endotoxin \nUnits per ml.\nStorage. Store protected from light in a single dose, tamper-\nevident container of colourless, transparent glass or of a \nsuitable plastic material.\n22.92 C (AI/A2).\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nFor dextrose - Weigh a clean, medium- porosity sintered-\nglass crucible containing a few glass beads. To 50 ml of \npotassium cupri-tartrate solution add the glass beads from\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nLabelling. The label states (1) whether the contents are \nSolution A or Solution B; (2) volume of the solution required \nper 100 ml of whole blood or the volume of the solution \nrequired pervolbme of whole blood to be collected; (3) where \napplicable, the maximum amount of blood to be collected in \nthe container.\nthe weighed crucible, 45 ml of water and 5.0 ml of the solution \nunder examination. Heat the solution at such a rate that it \nbegins to bpilin 3.5 to 4 minutes, boil the solution for exactly \n2 minutes and filter immediately through the weighed crucible, \ntaking care to transfer all the glass beads to the crucible, along\nAssay. For sodium citrate- Dilute 25.0 ml to 100.0 ml with \nwater and mix. Dilute 5.0 ml of the resultiolutioti• to\n100.0 ml with water and mix. Transfer 1.0 ml ofthis solution to \na test-tube, add 1.3 ml ofpvridine, swirl to nti add 5.7 ml of\n\"\nwith the precipitate. Wash the precipitate with hot water and \nthen with 10 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and dry it to constant \nweight at 110°. Carry out a blank determination.\n1 mg of the precipitate is equivalent to 0.000496 g of \nC6111206,1120.\nStorage. Store in a single dose, tamper-evident container of \ncolourless, transparent glass or of a suitable plastic material, \nprotected from light.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the composition and volume of \nthe solution; (2) volume of the solution required per 100 ml of \nwhole blood or the volume of the solution required per volume \nof whole blood to be collected; (3) where applicable, the \nmaximum amount of blood to be collected in the container.\nAnticoagulant Citrate Phosphate"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-fluoride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Fluoride"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Fluoride\nNa1. \t\nMol. Wt. 42.0\nSodium Fluoride contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent of NaF, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Preventive for dental caries.\n- \t\nF:'14-- N- \n..fr-- \t\nT. \n.  \nDescription. A white powder or colourless crystal. ' --- -'-c-  , Storage. Store wprotected from moisture. \n..,-7---V1:-,__.-7_ z.\n- I\nSODIUM DIATRIZOATE INJECTION \nIP 2018 \nSODIUM FUSIDATE \n11)201 8"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Fluoride\nNa1. \t\nMol. Wt. 42.0\nSodium Fluoride contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent of NaF, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Preventive for dental caries.\n- \t\nF:'14-- N- \n..fr-- \t\nT. \n.  \nDescription. A white powder or colourless crystal. ' --- -'-c-  , Storage. Store wprotected from moisture. \n..,-7---V1:-,__.-7_ z.\n- I\nSODIUM DIATRIZOATE INJECTION \nIP 2018 \nSODIUM FUSIDATE \n11)201 8"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Sodium Formaldehyde Sulphoxylate\nSodium sulphite. Not more than 5.0 per cent, calculated as \nNa2SO3, determined by the following method. Transfer 4.0 ml \nof the solution obtained in the Assay to a flask, add 2 ml of \nformaldehyde solution and titrate with 0.05 M iodine that is\nA. Dissolve 2.5 g in sufficient carbon dioxide-free water \nwithout heating to produce 100 ml (solution A). To 2 ml of \nsolution A add 0.5 ml of calcium  chloride solution; a gelatinous\n4'14, \t\n0 \n4114 \t\nNa\n-  0 \t\nOH 2H 20\nused for the Assay, adding starch solution towards the end \nof the titration as indicator. \nCalculate the percentage of Na 2SO3 from the expression \n78.775(V2 - V I) (MIW),\n white precipitate is produced which disolves on ading 5 ml \nof ferric chloride solution.\nC1-1 1Na03S,2H20 \t\nMol Wt. 154.1\nB. Add about 4 mg to a mixture of 0.1 ml of alizarin red S \nsolution and 0.1 ml of zirconyl nitrate solution and mix; the \ncolour changes to yellow.\nSodium Formaldehyde Sulphoxylate is monosodium \nhydroxymethane sulphinate dihydrate. It may contain a \nsuitable stabilising agent such as sodium carbonate.\nwhere V I and V2 are the volumes, in ml, of 0.05 M iodine \nconsumed in this test and in the Assay respectively, M is the \nexact molarity of 0.05 M iodine solution and W is the weight, \nin g, of the substance under examination taken for the Assay.\nC. It gives reaction (A) of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nSodium Formaldehyde Sulphoxylate contains an amount of \n0-1,Na03S equivalent to not less than 45.0 per cent and \nnot more than 55.0 per cent of SO 2, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nA.Dissolve about 4 g in 10 ml of water in a test-tube and add\n1 ml of ammoniacal silver nitrate solution;  metallic silver is \nproduced either as a finely divided grey precipitate or as a \nbright metallic mirror on the inner surface of the tube.\nSodium Fusidate\nChlorides (2.3.12). 40 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for chlorides (250 ppm).\nB.Add about 50 mg to a solution of 40 mg of salicylic acid in \n5 ml of sulphuric acid and warm very gently; a permanent \ndeep red colour develops.\nFluorosilicate. Heat to boiling the solution reserved in the \ntest for Acidity or alkalinity and titrate while hot with \n0.1 M sodium hydroxide until a red colour is produced. Not \nmore than 1.5 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide is required.\nA. Dissolve 0.1 g in 5 ml of water, add 5 ml of chloroform and \n0.1 ml of a 10 per cent w/w solution of phosphoric acid, shake \nvigorously for 1 minute, allow to separate and filter the lower \nlayer through absorbent cotton covered with anhydrous \nsodium sulphate. Repeat the extraction with two quantities, \neach of 5 ml, of chloroform, evaporate the combined extracts \nat a pressure of 2 kPa, dry the residue over phosphorus \npentoxide at a pressure not exceeding 0.7 kPa for 2 hours and\ndissolve in 1 ml of chlorofbrm IR.\nOn the residue determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with filsidic acid RS or with the reference spectrum \nof fusidic acid.\nB. Ignite 1 g. The residue gives the reactions of sodium salts \n(2.3.1).\n(2.3.13). Dissolve 1.0 g in 5 ml of water and 10 ml\nolifea37,initai\nhyedroschloric acid, heat to boiling, cool and dilute to\nA. When warmed with potassium permanganate, gives \nacetaldehyde, recognisable by its odour.\n times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (4.0 per cent). Ignore any \npeak with an area less than the area of the principal p eak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) \n(0.02 per cent).\n25 mi with water. The solution complies with the limit test for \nppm).\n plies with the limit test for \nhfroanvy(2m3elta4l)s.,2M0 m\netlhoofdsAoiu(\nB. The residue on evaporation, when moistened with \nhydrochloric acid and introduced on a platinum wire into a \nflame, imparts a yellow colour to the flame.\nsolution\niron (20 ppm). \nCarbonates. Not more than 2.0 per cent, calculated as Na2CO3,\nC. Carry out reaction C of calcium salts (2.3.1); no white \nprecipitate is produced.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined on \n0.5 g.\n o \t\n.\no\n2 e ) A\n8 m \nel(d 2 0i f \n. in 3.t\ndetermined\nDissolve 2.0 g in 5 ml of water, acidify with \nb\n.  Dissolve"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Loss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 27.0 per cent, \ndetermined on 0.5 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours.\n'11%* \nAppearance of solution. Solution A is clear (2.4.1), and \ncolourless (2.4.1).\nAssay. Dissolve 1.0 g in 25 ml of water, add sufficient water to \nproduce 50.0 ml and mix. To 4.0 ml of this solution add 100 ml \nof water and titrate with 0.05 M iodine using 3 ml of starch \nsolution, added towards the end of the titration, as indicator.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (antioxidant).\nAcidity or alkalinity. Dissolve 2.5 g ofpotassium nitrate in \n40 ml of solution A, dilute to 50 ml with  carbon dioxide-free \nwater, cool to 0° and add 0.2 ml of dilute phenolphthalein \nsolution. If the solution is colourless, not more than 1.0 ml of\nDescription. White crystals or hard white masses; odour, \ncharacteristic and garlic-like.\n1 ml of 0.05 M iodine is equivalent to 0.001602 g of SO 2. \nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\n0.1 M sodium hydroxide is required to produce a red colour \nthat persists for not less than 15 seconds. If the solution is \nred, not more than 0.25 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid is \nrequired to change the colour of the solution. Reserve the \nneutralised solution for the test for Fluorosilicate.\nAppearance of solution. A 5.0 per cent w/v solution in carbon \ndioxide-free water is clear (2.4.1), and colourless (2.4.1).\nSulphates (2.3.17). Dissolve 0.25 g in 10 ml of a saturated \nsolution of boric acid in distilled water and add 5 ml of \ndistilled water and 0.6 ml of 7 M hydrochloric acid. The \nsolution complies with the limit test for sulphates (200 ppm). \nPrepare the standard by mixing together 0.6 ml of 7  M hydro-\nchloric acid, 5 ml of sulphate standard solution \n(10 ppm SO 4) and 10 ml of a saturated solution of boric acid \nin distilled water. \n-\n1\nAlkalinity. Dissolve 1.0 g in 50 ml of water and add 0.15 ml of \ndilute phenolphthalein solution; not more than 3.5 ml of \n0.05 Msulphuric acid is required to change the colour of the \nsolution.\npH (2.4.24). 9.5 to 10.5, determined in a 2.0 per cent w/v solution \nin carbon dioxide-free water.\nC31 H47Na06 \t\nMol. Wt. 538.7\nSodium Fusidate is sodium (17Z)-1613-acetoxy-3a,1loc-di-\nhydroxyfusida-17(20),24-diene-21-oate, produced by the \ngrowth of certain strains of Fusidium coccineum or by any \nother means.\nIron (2.3.14). Ignite 1.0 g, initially at a low temperature until \nthoroughly charred and finally at about 600°, preferably in a \nmuffle furnace, until all the carbon has been burnt off. Cool, \ndissolve the residue in 2 ml of  hydrochloric acid and dilute to \n50 ml with  water. Add about 50 mg of ammonium  persulphate \nand 5 ml of ammonium thiocyanate solution, mix and transfer \nto a Nessler cylinder. The red colour of the solution is not \nmore intense than that of 1.0 ml of iron standard solution \n(10 ppm) treated in the same manner.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, detenninj\ntR \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 130° for 3 hours. \t\n,48\nAssay. Weigh 80 mg, add a mixture of 5 ml of acetic  anhydridi \nand 20 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic acid  and heat  If \ndissolve. Cool, add 20 ml of dioxan. Titrate with 0.1 M \nperchloric acid, using crystal violet solution as indicator,\nSodium Fusidate contains not less than 97.5 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C311 -147Na06, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nuntil a green colour is produced. Carry out a blank titration.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\n1 ml \n.0.1 rchloric acid is equivalent to 0.004199 g of\n_\nDose..-1,5 .g daily, in divided doses.\nSulphides. Dissolve 6 g in 14 ml of water itlest-lube anti,  \nWet a strip of lead acetate paper in the deal. .solution; no \ndiscolouration is evident within 5 minutes. ?\"'\nDescription. - A-white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nslightly hygroscopic.\n7\", \nt .\n.11\nAppearance of solution. A 15.0 per cent w/v solution in carbon \ndioxide free water is not more intensely coloured than \nreference solution BS6 (2.4.1).\npH (2.4.24). 7.5 to 9.0, determined in a 1.25 per cent w/v solution.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). +5.0° to +8.0°, determined at \n20\" by dissolving 1.5 g in 25 ml of water, adding 0.1 ml of \n5 M ammonia and diluting to 50 ml with water.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of the mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 5 mg of 3-ketofusidic acid \nRS in 5 ml of the mobile phase. To 1.0 ml of this solution add \n0.2 ml of the test solution and dilute to 20.0 ml with the mobile \nphase.\nReftrence solution (h). Dilute 20 gl of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 12.5 cm x 5 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), \nmobile phase: a mixture of 10 volumes of methanol, 20 \nvolumes of 1.0 per cent w/v solution of orthophosphoric \nacid, 20 volumes of water and 50 volumes of \nacetonitrile,\nflow rate: 2 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 235 nm, \ninjection volume: 20 gl.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not -valid unlesS 'the \nresolution between the peaks due to 3-ketofusidic acid and \nsodium fusidate is not less than 2.5.\nCOMPOUND SODIUM LACTATE AND DEXTROSE INJECTION \nSODIUM FUSIDATE \nIP 20 - \t0)2018\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\nThe resulting solution complies with the limit test for arsenic\nInject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution. The \nsignal-to-noise ratio of the principal peak is not less than 3 \nRun the chromatogram 3.5 times the retention time of prioeipai \npeak. The sum of all the secondary peaks is not mo re than 4\n(4 ppm).\nAssay. Dissolve 0.2 g in a mixture of 15 ml of water and 20 rill \nof ethanol (95 per cent). Titrate with 0.1 M hydrochloric \nacid to pH 4.1, stirring continuously, determining the end-\npoint potentiometrically (2.4.25).\nand dilute to 20 ml with water. The \nabout\nacid\nnitric\nsolution, without the addition of dilute nitric acid"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/i