solution', not more than 3.5 ml of \n0.05 M sulphuric acid is required to change the colour of the \nsolution.\nPH (2.4.24). 9.5 to 10.5, determined ina2.0 per cent w/v solution \nfcarbondioxidefree water.\nIron (2.L14); Ignite J .0 g, initiallyat a low temperature until \n^omughly chaned and finally at about 600°, preferably in a \nmuffle furnace, until all the carbon has been burnt off. Cool, \ndissolve the residue in 2 ml oí hydrochloric acid.and dilute to \n50 ml with water. Add about 50 mg of ammonium persulphate \n^d 5 ml of'ammonium thiocyanate solution, mix and transfer \nt0 a ^ess*er cylinder. The red colour of the solution is not \nmore intensé; than that of 1.0 ml of iron standard solution \n(10 ppm) treated in the same mariner. \nj;\nSulphides. Dissolve 6 g in 14 ml of water in a test-tube and\nWefea^stripjof dead acetate paper in the clear solution; no\nsc°i°uration is evident within 5 minutes..: \n; .\nSodium sulphite. Not more than 5.0 per cent, calculated as \nNa2SO3, determined by the following method. Transfer 4.0 ml \nof the solution obtained in the Assay to a flask, add 2 ml of \nformaldehyde solution and titrritc with 0.05 M iodine that is\nused Tor the Assay, adding starch solution towards the end \nof the titration as indicator. \n' ;‘ ‘ ;\nCalculate the percentage of Na2SO3 from the expression \n78.775(V2-V!)(M/W), \nJ.,.../.”\nwhere V: and V2 are the volumes, in ml, of 0.05 M iodine \nconsumed in this test and in the Assay respectively, M is the \nexact molarity of 0.05 M iodine solution and W is the weight, \nin g, of the substance under examination taken for the Assay.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 27.0 per cent, \ndetermined on 0.5 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours.\nAssay. Dissolve 1.0 g in 25 ml of water, add sufficient water to \nproduce 50.0 ml and mix. To 4.0 ml of the solution add 100 ml of \nwater and titrate with 0.05 M iodine using 3 ml of starch \nsolution, added towards the end of the titration, as indicator.\n1 ml of 0.05 Miodine is equivalent to 0.001602 g of SO2.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-fusidate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Fusidate","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Fusidate\nC3ln4/NaO6 \n. Mol. Wt. 538.7\nSodium Fusidate is sodium (17Z)-16p-acetoxy-3a,lla-di- \nhydroxyfusida-17(20),24-dierie-2'l-oatei, produced by the \ngrowth of certain strains di'Fusidium coccineum or by any \nother means. 1\nSodium Fusidate contains not less than 97.5 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C^H^NaO/calculatcd on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nslightly hygroscopic.\n3609\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSODIUM FUSIDATE"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Fusidate\nC3ln4/NaO6 \n. Mol. Wt. 538.7\nSodium Fusidate is sodium (17Z)-16p-acetoxy-3a,lla-di- \nhydroxyfusida-17(20),24-dierie-2'l-oatei, produced by the \ngrowth of certain strains di'Fusidium coccineum or by any \nother means. 1\nSodium Fusidate contains not less than 97.5 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C^H^NaO/calculatcd on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nslightly hygroscopic.\n3609\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSODIUM FUSIDATE"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Dissolve Ó.1 gin5 ml of wa/er, add 5 ml of chloroform and\n0.1 ml of a 10 per cent w7w solution pfphosphoric acid, shake \nvigorously for 1 minute, allow to separate and filter the lower \nlayer through absorbent cotton, covered with anhydrous \nsodium sulphate. Repeat the extraction with two quantities,\neach of 5 ml, of chloroform, evaporate the Combined extracts \nat a pressure of 2 kPa, dry the residue over phosphorus \npentoxide at a pressure not exceeding 0.7 kPa for 2 hours and\ndissolve in \\ yn\\ of chloroform IR.. ; \n, \n;\nOn the residue: determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained withfusidic acid IP RS or with the reference spectrum \nof fusi die acid>.; : < .;\nB. Ignited g. The residue gives the reactions of sodi urn. salts\nTests _ \" \n' d\n(2-3.T).\nAppearance of solution. A; 15.0 per cent w/v solution in carbon \ndioxide-free water is not more intensely coloured than \nreference solution BS6 (2.4.1). \n:\npH (2.4.24). 7.5 to 9.0, determined in a 1.25 per cent w/v solution.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). + 5.0° to+ 8.0°, determined at \n20° by dissolving 1.5 g in 25 ml of water, adding'O.lyml of \n5 Mammonia and diluting to 50 ml with water.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). \n' \n'\nTest solution./Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of the mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 5’mg of 3-ketofusidic acid \nIPRS in 5 ml of the mobile phase. To 1.0 ml of the solution add\n0.2 ml of the test solution and diluteto 20.0 ml with the mobile \nphase. \n- .,\nReference solution (b). Dilute 20 pl of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 12.5 cm x 5 mm, packed with\nqctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), :\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 10 volumes of methanol, 20\n: , volumes of 1.0 per cent w/vsolution of orthpphosphoric\nacid, 20 volumes of water and 50 volumes of \nacetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 2 ml per minute, \"!!\n- spectrophotometer sét al 235 rim, \n'\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n;\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the. peaks due to 3-ketofusidic ;acid and \nsodium fusidate is not less than 2.5. \nD / .4 ./-\nÍP2022 í\nInject reference.solutipn (a), (b)- and the test, solution. \nsignal-to-noise ratio of the principal peak is not less than 3 \nRun the chromatogram 3.5 times the retention time of principi \npeak and the sum of all the secondary peaks is not more than \n4 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (4.0 per cent). Ignore any \npeak with an area less than the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with refcrencc solution (fy \n(0.02 per cent). \nr ' :\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined on \n0.5g. \ni \n.''y.\nAssay. Dissolve 0;2 g in a mixture of 15 ml ofwater and 20 nil \nof ethanol (95 per .cent). Titrate with 0.1 Mchydmchlòrk \nacid to pH 4.1; stirring continuously, determining the; end­\npoint potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration. :\n1 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid is equivalent to Ó.05387 g of |\nC3lH47NaO6. \n|I\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture. , \n|\n. ■■ ■ ' I\nSodium Hydroxide' \n.. \n|\n- '\nSodium Hydroxide contains not less-than 97.0 per cenfand | \nnot more than 100.5 per cent of total alkali, calculated as NaOH. |\nCaustic Soda \n; \n|\nNaOH ' \n\\ \n. \nMpl.Wt.40.0 |\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (alkalising agent). \n|\nDescription. White, crystalline masses supplied (às sticks, | \npellets ór slabs ; deliquescent. Readily absorbs carbon dioxide.\nCAUTION ~ Great care should be exercised'in-handling | \nSodium Hydroxide as it rapidly destroys tissues. \n|\n' ' \n' \nJ \"C .V : : ' I\n) 1 \n|\nA. Carefully dissòlve 5.0 g in 12 ml of distilled'water, add g\n17 ml of 7 M hydrochloric acù?,'adjusted to pH 7.0 with |\n1 ■ M hydrochloric acid and add. sufficient distilled\n water to | \nproduce 50 ml (solution A). 2 ml of solution A gives reaction । \n(A) of sodium salts (2.3.1). \n4 •■rAi..-;;\" |\n*\nB. pl I of a 0.01 per cent w/v solution, hot less than 11.0 (2-4.24). |\ntX. A-.:-/ I\nTests' • ” ' \" \n' \n1\nAppearance of solution. Al 0-0 per cent w/v solutions clear | \n(2.4.1), and colourless (2.4.1). \n:■ 'í-cedu \n|\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 2.5 g in 5 0 ml of water, add? 16 ml of I \nbrominated hydrochloric acid AsT, and remove the excess of 4 \nbromine with a few drops of stannous chloride solution Asi- I\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCOMPOUND SODIUM LACTATE AND DEXTROSE INJECTION\n11*2022\nThe resulting solution complies with the limit test for arsenic\n(4ppm)-\nu w metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 1.0 gin 5 ml of water and 10 ml\nMHydrochloric acid, heat to boiling, cool and dilute to \n°5 ml with water. The solution complies with the limit test for \n¿vy metals, Method A (20 ppm).\njrbn (2 3 \n^0 ml of solution A complies with the limit test for\niron(20ppm). \n_\nCarbonates Not more than 2.0 per cent, calculated as Na2CO3, \ndetermined'in the: Assay. \n;\nTests -\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 2.0g in 5 ml of water; acidify with \nabout 8 ml of w/tete acic/ and dilute to 20 ml with water. The \ns0|utibri, without the addition of dilute nitric acid, complies \nwith the limit test for chlorides (125 ppm).\npH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 7.0.\nSulphates^Btl 7). Dissolve 2.0 g in 6 ml of distilled water, \nadjusted to pH 7 with hydrochloric acid and dilute to 20 ml \nwith distilled water. The resulting solution complies with the \nlimit test for sulphates (75 ppm).\nPotassium. Acidify 2.5 ml of solution A with acetic acid and \nadd 0.15 ml of sodium cobaltinitrite solution-, no precipitate \nisformed.\nAssay. Dissolve 2.0 g in about 80 ml of carbon dioxide-free \nwater, add 0.3 ml ofphenolphthalein solution and titrate with\nI Id hydrochloric acid. Add 0.3 ml of methyl orange solution \nand continue, the titration with 1 M hydrochloric acid.\nI nil. of ’ I ' M hydrochloric acid used in the second part of the \nt i (ration is equ ¡valent to "},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.424). 4.0.to 6.5. ,\n5-Hydroxymethylfurfural and Related substances; Dilute a \nvolume containing 1.0 g of Dextrose to 500.0 ml fvith water\nand measure the absorbance of the resulting solution at the \nmaximum at about 284 nm; absorbance at about 284 nm, not \nmore than 0.25 (2.4.7). .\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Evaporate a volume containing 4 g of \ndextrose to 10 ml and add 2 nil “¡of ’diliite\n*acetic\n acid and \nsufficient water to produce 25 rpl. The solution complies with \nthe limit test for heavy metals. Method A (5 ppm).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.5 Endotoxin Unit \nperml. \nr. ■\nQther tests. Comply with.the .tests, stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections). ... \n........... \n...\nAssay. For sodium -IDilute suitably with water and determine \nbyMethodA for flame photometry(2.4.4), or by Method Afor \natomic absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.2). measuring at \n589 nm and using sodium solution, FPor sodium solution\n3612\n20$\n4/I ^respectively, suitably diluted with water for the: stands \nsolutions. \n.. \n...\nFor potassium t-t-I Dilute suitably with water anddeternu^ \nby Method A for flamephotometry (2.4.4), or byMethodAf^ \natomic absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.2), measuring2i \n767 nm and using potassium solution FP/Qt. potassium \nsolution A AS respectively, suitably diluted with water for the \nstandard solutions. \nj'.'..’.’\nFor total chlorides—To 20.0 ml add 30 ml of water, 50.0 ml of\n0.1 M silver nitrate arid 2' ml of nitric acidi Filter,\" wash the \nprecipitate with water slightly acidified with' nitric acid and \ntitrate: the excess of silver-nitrate^with 0:R Mi ammoniti^ \nthiocyanate ; using ferric : ammonium sulpha te i solution as \nindicator¡until.a reddish yellow colour is produced. Carryout \na blank titration. \n; A nuT ■.<\n1 ml of 0.1 M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.003545,g of total \nchloride, calculated as Cl.\nFor calcium chloride — To 50.0 ml add 5.0 ml of\n0.01 M magnesium sulphate and 5 ml tìf ammonia buffer \npH 10.9 and titrate with 0.01 M disodium edetate. using\ner iochrome black.T, mixture as indicator. Carry outa blank \ntitration.\n1 ml of Q. 01M disodium edetate, is equivalent to,0,00147 g of \nCaCl2,2H2O. .. \n-./\nFot lactate — Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4 J 4);\nTest solution. The preparation under examination:\nReference solution.f. 0.28 per cent w/v solution of /item \nlactate IPRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system.\" \ny'y.\n- \nà stainless steel column 20 crii x 4.'6 rrim, packed with\nv octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 phi), A 1 y\n- \nmobile phase: a mixture of 90 volumes of water and \n10 volumes of a 2 per cent v/v solution of octylamine \nacetonitrile, the pH of which is adjusted to 7.0 with a\n10 per cent v/v solution of phosphoric acid,\n- \nflow rate: 2 ml per minute,  : j A MM Mr> ;\n*\n- \nspectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n- \ninjection volume: 10 pl. \n;•\nInject separately the reference solution and the test solution \narid measure the responses for the major peak.7 \n(\nCalculate the content of C3H6O3, in the injection. tv \nFor dextrose—To a measured volume containing'2 g to 5 g$ \nDextrose, add 0,2 ml of 5 M ammonia and sufficient water® \nproduce 100.0; ml. .Mix well, allow to. stand \n30 minutes and measure the optical rotation in a 2-din^ \n(2.4.22). The observed rotation in degrees multipli^\n0.9477 represents the weight, in g, of dextrose, CgHnOfe® \nvolume taken for assay. :\nwww.webofpharma.com\nde ’Store in single dose containers of glass or plastic-at \nSt°mperature not exceeding 30°. On keeping, small particles \n^ay separate from the solution in glass containers.\nbelling- The l^el states (0 l'iat inJ action contains, in- \n'Uimolespef litre, the following approximate amounts of the\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 6.5.\n^ôdiürriJ; 1 ’ potassium, 5, calcium, 2; bicarbonate \nlactate), 29 and Chloride, 111; (2) the tdtal osmolar \nconcentration in mOsmol per litre; (3) that the inj ection should \nnot be used if it contains visible particles. .\nHalf Strength Compound Sodium \nLactate and Dextrose Injection\nHalf Strength Compound Sodium Lactate with Dextrose \nIntravenous Infusion; Half Strength Ringer-Lactate\nSolution with Dextrose Injection\nHalf Strength Compound Sodium Lactate and Dextrose \nInjection is a sterile solution containing 0.12 per cent v/v of \nLactic Acid (equivalent to 0.16 per cent w/y of sodium lactate) \nwith 6 3 percent w/y of Sodium Chloride, 0.02 per cent w/v of \nPotassium Chloride, 0.0135 per cent w/v of Calcium Chloride \nand 5 per cent w/v of Dextrose in Water for Injections.\nsolutions.\nCompound Sodium Lactate and Dextrose Injection contains \nnot less than. 0.135 per cent and not more than 0.16 per cent \nw/v of sodium,Na, not less than 0.0095 per cent and not more \nthan 0.011: per cent w/v of potassium, K, not less than\n0.185 per cent and not more than 0.210 per cent w/v of total \nchloride, Cl, not less than 0.0125 per bent and hot more than \n0.0145 per cent w/v of calcium chloride, CaCl2;2H2O, and not \nless than O.¡14 5 per cent and not more than 0.140 per cent w/v \nof lactate; calculated as CJLO,, and not less than 4.5 per cent \nandnot more than 5.25 per cent w/v of dextrose, C6H]2O6. It \ncontains no antimicrobial agent.\nCategory. Fluid and electrolyte replenisher.\n$wcription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured \nsolution. ,\nIdentification7\nA. To 1 mi add 0.05 ml ofpotassium cupri-tgrtrate solution', \n^solution remains blue and clear. Heat to boiling, a copious \nred precipitate is formed.\nh- When warmed with;potassium permanganate gives\nacetaldehyde, recognisable by its odour.\nL*3^ residueon evaporation, when moistened with\nro^°ric bczi/ airid introduced on a platinum wire into a\nJ*** 16 imparts a yellow colour to the flame. When viewed\nough: a suitable < blue glass, the flame is tinged reddish\nPurple.\nHALF STRENGTH COMPOUND SODIUM LACTATE AND DEXTROSE INJECTION\nD. It gives reaction (C) of calcium salts and reaction (A) of \nchlorides(2.3.1). \n‘o .:\n5-Hydroxymethylfurfural and Related substances. Dilute a \nvolum"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-hydrogen-carbonate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Hydrogen Carbonate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Hydrogen Carbonate\nSodium Bicarbonate contains ñót less than 99.Ó pér cent arid \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of NaHCO3.\nCategory. Electrolyte replenisher; systemic alkaliser. < ;; ;\nDescription. A white? crystalline powder or small, opaque, \nmonoclinic crystals. It gradually forms sodium carbonate on \nheating in the dry state or in solution."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Hydrogen Carbonate\nSodium Bicarbonate contains ñót less than 99.Ó pér cent arid \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of NaHCO3.\nCategory. Electrolyte replenisher; systemic alkaliser. < ;; ;\nDescription. A white? crystalline powder or small, opaque, \nmonoclinic crystals. It gradually forms sodium carbonate on \nheating in the dry state or in solution."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. To 5 ml of a 5.0 per cent w/v solution in carbon dioxide­\nfree water (solution A) add 0.1 ml ofphenolphthalein solution^\na pale pink colour is produced. On heating, a gas is evolved \nand the?solution becomes red: ■ ■ '■ •! : ; \"•’■1 •z ' ' ' ■ •! ? \n.\nB. ït gives reaction (A) of bicarbonates (2.3.1).\nC. S olution A gives the reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nTests 7 ' ' / ' 1 ri./' \n.... '■ \"ri.\nAppearance1 of solution. Solution A is clêar (2.4.1), and \ncolourless (2.4.1). \ni,:\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 5.0 gin 50 ml oiWater, add 15 ml of \nbrômiriated hydrochloric acid AsT and remove the excess of \nbromine with- a few drops- of stannous chloride solution AsT. \nThe resulting solution complies with the limit test for arsenic \n(2ppm). \nri;, ri:: Ï ;ri7./ ■-■■■■■ ri'\"-'' \n■■■:ri;;<;\nCalcium. À2.0 per cent w/v solution, when boiled for 5 minutes, \nis clear.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Mix4.0 g with 5 ml ofwnterand 18 mlóf \ndilute hydrochloric acid, heat to boiling and maintain that \ntemperature for I minute. Add 0.05 ml of phenolphthalein \nsolution and sufficient 5 M ammonia dropwise to give the\nsolution a faint pink colour, cool and dilute to 25 ml with water. \nThe solution complies with the limit test for heavy metals, \nMethod A (5 ppm). - ' --ri: \n;• wwm ■ ,\nIron (2.3.14). Dissolve 2.0 g in 20 ml of water arid 4 rnl of \nhydrochloric acid and dilute to 40 ml'with water; the resulting \nsolution complies with the limit test fbriron (20. ppm).,\nCarbonate. pH of solution A, when freshly prepared^ not more \nthan 8.6. \no?\nChlorides (2.3.12). 1.25 g dissolved in 15 nil of water arid 2 ml \nof nitric acid complies with the limit test for chlorides \n(200 ppm). ; W:.,:-/.:. .j'J ■ : 'riw \n-ri \\ \n: A\nSulphates (2.3.17). Suspend 1.0 g in 10 ml oí distilled water, \nneutralise with hydrochloric, acid and dilute; to-15 ml with\ndistilled water. The resulting solution complies with the limit I \ntest for sulphates (150 ppm). . \n, .- ..ri ■ I\nAssay. Weigh j. 5 g, dissolve in 50 ml of carbon dioxidefree | \nwater and titrate with 1 M hydrochloric acid using 0,2ml of | \nmethyl orange solution as indicator. \n‘ \n|\n1 ml of 1 Mhydrochloric acid is equivalent to 0.08401 g of | \nNaHCO3. \nC:- \n■■ ;‘ ' — ri '■\nStorage. Store protected from irioisture. ' ' \" \n|\nW. ri;w ;w.w -ri-,:.:7 \n'■ ...........ri ri:<<ri,^ ri |"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-hydroxide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Hydroxide"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Hydroxide\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). +5.0° to +8.0°, determined at\nCaustic Soda\n20° by dissolving 1.5 g in 25 ml of water, adding 0.1 ml of\n5 M ammonia and diluting to 50 ml with water.\nNaOH\nMol. Wt. 40.0\nSodium Hydroxide contains not less than 97.0 per cent and\nnot more than 100.5 per cent oftotal alkali, calculated as NaOH.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (allcalising agent).\nDescription. White, crystalline masses supplied as sticks,\npellets or slabs; deliquescent. Readily absorbs carbon dioxide.\nCAUTION -\nGreat care should be exercised in handling\nSodium Hydroxide as it rapidly destroys tissues."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Hydroxide\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). +5.0° to +8.0°, determined at\nCaustic Soda\n20° by dissolving 1.5 g in 25 ml of water, adding 0.1 ml of\n5 M ammonia and diluting to 50 ml with water.\nNaOH\nMol. Wt. 40.0\nSodium Hydroxide contains not less than 97.0 per cent and\nnot more than 100.5 per cent oftotal alkali, calculated as NaOH.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (allcalising agent).\nDescription. White, crystalline masses supplied as sticks,\npellets or slabs; deliquescent. Readily absorbs carbon dioxide.\nCAUTION -\nGreat care should be exercised in handling\nSodium Hydroxide as it rapidly destroys tissues."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Carefully dissolve 5.0 g in 12 ml of distilled water, add\n17 ml of 7 M hydrochloric acid, adjust the pH to 7.0 with\n1 M hydrochloric acid and add sufficient distilled water to\nproduce 50 ml (solutionA). 2 ml ofsolutionA gives reaction A\nofsodium salts (2.3.1).\nB. pH ofa 0.01 percentw/v solution, not less than 11.0 (2.4.24)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance ofsolution. A 10.0 per cent w/v solution is clear\n(2.4.1), and colourless (2.4.1).\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 2.5 gin 50 ml ofwater, add 16ml of\nbrominated hydrochloric acidAsT, and remove the excess of\nbromine with a few drops ofstannous chloride solution AsT.\nThe resulting solution complies with the limit test for arsenic\n(4 ppm).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 1.0 ginS ml ofwaterand 10ml\nof 3 M hydrochloric acid, heat to boiling, cool and dilute to\n25 ml with water. The solution complies with the lirriit test f6r\n, heavy metals, Method A ( 20 ppm).\nIron (2.3.14). 20 ml ofsolutionA complies with the limittest for\niron (20 ppm).\n2124\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nCOMPOUND SODIUM LACTATE AND DEXTROSE INJECTION\nCarbonates. Notmore than 2.0 per cent, calculated as Na2C03,\ndetermined in the Assay.\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 2.0 g in 5 ml ofwater, acidify with\nabout 8 ml ofnitric acid and dilute to 20 ml with water. The\nsolution, without the addition of dilute nitric acid, complies\nwith the limit test for chlorides (125 ppm).\nSulphates (2.3.17). Dissolve 2.0 gin 6 ml ofdistilled water,\nadjust the pH to 7 with hydrochloric acid and dilute to 20 ml\nwith distilled water. The resulting solution complies with the\nlimit test for sulphates (75 ppm).\nPotassium. Acidify 2.5 ml ofsolution A with acetic acid and\nadd 0.15 ml of sodium cobaltinitrite solution; no precipitate\nis formed.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 2.0 g, dissolve in about 80 ml\nof carbon dioxide-free water, add '0.3 ml ofphenolphthalein\nsolution and titrate with 1 M hydrochloric acid. Add 0.3 ml\nof methyl orange solution and continue the titration with\n1 M hydrochloric acid.\n1 ml of 1 M hydrochloric acid used in the second part ofthe\ntitration is equivalent to 0.1060 g ofNa2C03'\n1 ml of1 M hydrochloric acidused in the combined titrations\nis equivalent to 0.0400 g oftotal alkali, calculated as NaOH.\n. Storage. Store protected from moisture, in non-metallic\ncontainers."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-hyposulphite","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Hyposulphite"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Hyposulphite\nTest solution (a).  Dissolve 0.5 g of the substance under \nexamination in water and dilute to 10.0 ml with water. -4410\nNa2S203,5H20\nMol. Wt. 248.2\nSodium Stibogluconate Injection contains not less than \n9.5 per cent w/v and not more than 10.5 per cent w/v of \npentavalent antimony, Sb.\nTest solution (h). Add 5 ml of a 0.2 per cent v/v solution of \nethanol (internal standard) to 0.5 g of the substance under \nexamination and dilute to 10.0 ml with water.\nSodium Thiosulphate contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of Na2S 203,5H20.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 100 mg of pentavalent \nantimony per ml.\nCategory. Antidote to cyanide poisoning.\nReference solution. Add 1 ml of a 1.0 per cent v/v solution of \nmethanol to 5 ml of a 0.2 per cent v/v solution of the internal \nstandard and dilute to 10.0 ml with water. \nRt4\nDose. By intravenous or intramuscular injection, 300 mg to 1 g.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured \nsolution.\nDescription. Colourless large crystals or a coarse, crystalline \npowder; odourless; deliquescent in moist air and effloresces \nin dry air at temperature above 33°. It dissolves in its water of \ncrystallisation at about 49°.\nChromatographic system"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Hyposulphite\nTest solution (a).  Dissolve 0.5 g of the substance under \nexamination in water and dilute to 10.0 ml with water. -4410\nNa2S203,5H20\nMol. Wt. 248.2\nSodium Stibogluconate Injection contains not less than \n9.5 per cent w/v and not more than 10.5 per cent w/v of \npentavalent antimony, Sb.\nTest solution (h). Add 5 ml of a 0.2 per cent v/v solution of \nethanol (internal standard) to 0.5 g of the substance under \nexamination and dilute to 10.0 ml with water.\nSodium Thiosulphate contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of Na2S 203,5H20.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 100 mg of pentavalent \nantimony per ml.\nCategory. Antidote to cyanide poisoning.\nReference solution. Add 1 ml of a 1.0 per cent v/v solution of \nmethanol to 5 ml of a 0.2 per cent v/v solution of the internal \nstandard and dilute to 10.0 ml with water. \nRt4\nDose. By intravenous or intramuscular injection, 300 mg to 1 g.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured \nsolution.\nDescription. Colourless large crystals or a coarse, crystalline \npowder; odourless; deliquescent in moist air and effloresces \nin dry air at temperature above 33°. It dissolves in its water of \ncrystallisation at about 49°.\nChromatographic system"},{"name":"Identification","text":"-\na glass column 1.5 m x 4 mm, packed with porous pot}\nbeads (80 to 100 mesh) (Such as Porapak Q), \n-\ntemperature: \ncolumn: 130°,\nA.It is dextro-rotatory.\nB.Dilute 2 ml to 10 ml with water and pass hydrogen sulphide \nthrough the solution; no immediate precipitate is produced. \nContinue to pass hydrogen sulphide through the solution for \nseveral minutes; an orange precipitate is produced. \nC.The residue obtained by evaporation to dryness, after \nincineration, gives the reactions of antimony compounds and \nthe reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\ninlet port 180° and detector at 180°, \nflame ionization detector, \t\nt, \nflow rate: 40 ml per minute using nitrogen as carrier gas. \n-\ninjection volume: 20. \n)‘4 \nInject the reference solution, test solutions (a) and (b). \t\n• _\nA.To 0.5 ml of a 10.0 per cent w/v solution in carbon dioxide-\nfree water (solution A) add 0.5 ml of water and 2 ml of\n0.1 M silver nitrate; a white precipitate is produced which \nquickly becomes yellowish and finally black.\nB. To a portion of solution A add a few drops of  iodine \nsolution; the colour is discharged.\nCalculate the content of methanol taking 0.792 g as its weight \nper ml (2.4.29) at 20°."},{"name":"Tests","text":"C. Dilute 2.5 ml of solution A to 5 ml with water and add 1 ml of \nhydrochloric acid; a gas is evolved which turns starch-iodate \npaper blue and a precipitate of sulphur is produced.\nUndue toxicity. Dissolve a suitable quantity of the substance \nunder examination in water.* injections to give a solution \ncontaining 28 mg of pentavalent antimony per ml. Inject \nintravenously 0.3 ml of the solution into each of 10 mice that \nhave been deprived of food for not less than 17 hours. After \ninjections allow the mice access to food and water. None of\nPH (2.4.24). 5.0 to 5.6.\nUndue toxicity. Dilute a suitable volume of the injection under \nexamination with sufficient water for injections to give a \nsolution containing the equivalent of 28 mg of pentavalent \nantimony per ml. Inject intravenously 0.3 ml of the solution \ninto each of 10 mice that have been deprived pfrood for 'not \nless than 17 hours. After injections allow the n*e.access to \nfood and water. None of the mice dies within24- hours. If one.\nD. 1 ml of solution A gives reaction (A) of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nthe. icediesliiithin 24 hours. If one of the mice dies within\nAppearance of solution.  Solution A is clear (2.4.1), and\n- _c.?lourless\n24 h0,its, repeat the test. None of the second group of mice \n.\npit (2.4.241 6.0 to 8.4, determined in solution A.\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 5.0 g in 50 ml of water and add 10 ml \nof stannated hydrochloric acid AsT. The resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for arsenic (2 ppm).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 1.0 g in 10 ml of water. Slowly \nadd 5 ml of dilute hydrochloric acid and evaporate the mixture \nto dryness on a water-bath. Gently boil the residue with 15 ml \nof water for 2 minutes and filter. Heat the filtrate to boiling, \nadd sufficient bromine solution to the hot filtrate to produce \na clear solution and add a slight excess of bromine solution.\nBoil the solution to expel the bromine completely, cool to room \ntemperature and add a drop of phenolphthalein  solution and \nsodium hydroxide solution until a slight pink colour is \nproduced. Add 2 ml of dilute acetic acid and dilute with water \nto 25 ml. The solution complies with the limit test for heavy \nmetals, Method A ( 20 ppm).\nChlorides (2.3.12). To 12.5 ml of solution A add 15 ml of \n2 Mnitric acid, boil gently for 3 to 4 minutes, cool and filter. \nThe filtrate complies with the limit test for chlorides (200 ppm).\nSulphides. To 10 ml of solution A add 0.05 ml of a freshly \nprepared 5 per cent w/v solution of sodium nitroprusside; the \nsolution does not become violet.\nSulphates and su 1phit es (2.3.17). Dilute 2.5 ml of solution A to\n10 ml with distilled water. To 3 ml of this solution add 2 ml of \niodine solution and gradually add more  iodine solution and \ndilute to 15 ml with distilled water. The resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for sulphates (0.2 per cent).\nAssay. Dissolve 0.5 g in 20 ml of water and titrate with 0.05 M \niodine using starch solution, added towards the end of the \ntitration, as indicator.\n1 ml of 0.05 M iodine is equivalent to 0.02482 g of \nNa2S203,5H2O.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-lactate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Lactate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Lactate Injection\nA. Carefully dissolve 5.0 g in 12 ml of distilled water, add\nSodium Lactate Intravenous Infusion\n17 ml of 7 M hydrochloric acid, adjust the pH to 7.0 with \n1 M hydrochloric acid and add sufficient distilled water to \nproduce 50 ml (solution A). 2 ml of solution A gives reaction\nSodium Lactate Injection is a sterile solution containing\nCompound Sodium Lactate and \nDextrose Injection\n1.85 per cent w/v of sodium lactate in Water for Injections. It is \nprepared from Lactic Acid with the aid of Sodium Hydroxide \nand sufficient Dilute Hydrochloric Acid to adjust the pH of \nthe solution.\n(A) of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nB.pH of a 0.01 per cent w/v solution, not less than 11.0 (2.4.24).\nCompound Sodium Lactate with Dextrose Intravenous \nInfusion; Ringer-Lactate Solution with Dextrose for \nInjection; Hartmann's Solution with Dextrose for \nInjection."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Lactate Injection\nA. Carefully dissolve 5.0 g in 12 ml of distilled water, add\nSodium Lactate Intravenous Infusion\n17 ml of 7 M hydrochloric acid, adjust the pH to 7.0 with \n1 M hydrochloric acid and add sufficient distilled water to \nproduce 50 ml (solution A). 2 ml of solution A gives reaction\nSodium Lactate Injection is a sterile solution containing\nCompound Sodium Lactate and \nDextrose Injection\n1.85 per cent w/v of sodium lactate in Water for Injections. It is \nprepared from Lactic Acid with the aid of Sodium Hydroxide \nand sufficient Dilute Hydrochloric Acid to adjust the pH of \nthe solution.\n(A) of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nB.pH of a 0.01 per cent w/v solution, not less than 11.0 (2.4.24).\nCompound Sodium Lactate with Dextrose Intravenous \nInfusion; Ringer-Lactate Solution with Dextrose for \nInjection; Hartmann's Solution with Dextrose for \nInjection."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Sodium Lactate Injection contains not less than 1.75 per cent \nand not more than 1.95 per cent w/v of sodium lactate, \nC31-1,Na03.\n4111, \nAppearance of solution. A 10.0 per cent w/v solution is clear \n(2.4.1), and colourless (2.4.1).\nCompound Sodium Lactate and Dextrose Injection is a sterile\nCategory. Fluid and electrolyte replenisher.\nArsenio ( \t\n). Dissolve 2.5 g in 50 ml of water, add 16 ml of\ng 0.24 per cent v/v of Lactic Acid (equivalent \n/v of sodium lactate) with 0.6 per cent w/v of \ne, 0.04 per cent w/v of Potassium Chloride,\nbromine with a few drops of stannous chloride solution AST. \nbromiputiid \t\n-ochloric acid As T, and remove the excess of\nDose. By intravenous infusion at the rate o \nminute.\n0.027 per cent w/v of Calcium Chloride and 5 per cent w/v of \nDextrose in Water for Injections.\nCompound Sodium Lactate and Dextrose Injection contains \nnot less than 0.27 per cent and not more than 0.32 per cent \nw/v of sodium, Na, not less than 0.019 per cent and not more \nthan 0.022 per cent w/v of potassium, K, not less than 0.37 per \ncent and not more than 0.42 per cent w/v of total chloride, Cl, \nnot less than 0.025 per cent and not more than 0.029 per cent \nw/v of calcium chloride, CaC1 2,2H20, and not less than \n0.23 per cent and not more than 0.28 per cent w/v of lactate, \ncalculated as C 3H603, and not less than 4.50 per cent and not \nmore than 5.25 per cent w/v of dextrose, C 6H 1206. It contains \nno antimicrobial agent.\nCategory. Fluid and electrolyte replenisher.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured \nsolution.\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 6.5.\n5-Hydroxymethylfurfural and Related substances. Dilute a \nvolume containing 1.0 g of Dextrose to 500.0 ml with water \nand measure the absorbance of the resulting solution at the \nmaximum at about 284 nm; absorbance at about 284 nm, not \nmore than 0.25 (2.4.7).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Evaporate a volume containing 4 g of \ndextrose to 10 ml and add 2 ml of dilute acetic acid and \nsufficient water to produce 25 ml. The solution complies with \nthe limit test for heavy metals, Method A ( 5 ppm).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.5 Endotoxin Unit \nper ml.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. For sodium - Dilute suitably with water and determine \nby Method A for flame photometry (2.4.4), or by Method A for\n-,t1=\nIP 200 \nCOMPOUND SODIUM LACTATE AND DEXTROSE INJECTION\nI) 2011 \nHALF STRENGTH COMPOUND SODIUM LACTATE AND DEXTROSE INJECTION\n0.9477 represents the weight, in g, of dextrose, C 6H 1206, in the\natomic absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.2), measuring at\nflame imparts a yellow colour to the flame. When viewed \nthrough a suitable blue glass, the flame is tinged reddish \npurple.\n 589 nm and using \nsodium solution FP or sodium solution \nAAS respectively, suitably diluted with water for the standard\nvolume taskteonrefoinr assay.\nsingle\ndose containers of glass or plastic at\nsolutions.\nSattemrapg\ne temperature not exceeding 30°. On keeping, small particles \nmay separate from the solution in glass containers.\nD. It gives reaction (C) of calcium salts and reaction (A) of \nchlorides (2.3.1).\nFor potassium - Dilute suitably with water and determint \nby Method A for flame photometry (2.4.4), or by Method A for \natomic absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.2), measuring at \n767 nm and using potassium solution FP or potassium \nsolution AAS respectively, suitably diluted with water for the\nLabelling. The label states (1) that the injection contains, in\nmillimoles per litre, the following approximate amounts of the \nions. sodium, 131, potassium, 5, calcium, 2; bicarbonate \n(as lactate), 29 and Chloride, 111; (2) the total osmolar\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 6.5.\n5-Hydroxymethylfurfural and Related substances. Dilute a \nvolume containing 1.0 g of Dextrose to 500.0 ml with water \nand measure the absorbance of the resulting solution at the \nmaximum at about 284 nm; absorbance at about 284 nm, not \nmore than 0.25 (2.4.7).\nstandard solutions.\nconcentration in mOsmol per litre; (3) that the injection should \nnot be used if it contains visible particles.\nFor total chlorides-To 20.0 ml add 30 ml of water, 50.0 ml of \n0.1 M silver nitrate and 2 ml of nitric acid. Filter, wash the \nprecipitate with water slightly acidified with nitric acid and \ntitrate the excess of silver nitrate with 0.1 M ammonium \nthiocyanate using ferric ammonium sulphate solution as \nindicator until a reddish yellow colour is produced. Carry out \na blank titration.\nHalf Strength Compound Sodium \nLactate and Dextrose Injection\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.5 Endotoxin Unit \nper ml.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nHalf Strength Compound Sodium Lactate with Dextrose \nIntravenous Infusion; Half Strength Ringer-Lactate \nSolution with Dextrose Injection\n1 ml of 0. I M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.003545 g of total \nchloride, calculated as Cl.\nAssay. For sodium - Dilute suitably with water and determine \nby Method A for flame photometry (2.4.4), or by Method A for \natomic absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.2), measuring at \n589 nm and using sodium solution FP or sodium solution \nAAS respectively, suitably diluted with water for the standard \nsolutions.\nFor calcium chloride - To 50.0 ml add 5.0 ml of \n0.01 M magnesium sulphate and 5 ml of ammonia buffer \npH 10.9 and titrate with 0.01 M disodium edetate using\nHalf Strength Compound Sodium Lactate and Dextrose \nInjection is a sterile solution containing 0.12 per cent v/v of \nLactic Acid (equivalent to 0.16 per cent w/v of sodium lactate) \nwith 0.3 per cent w/v of Sodium Chloride, 0.02 per cent w/v of \nPotassium Chloride, 0.0135 per cent w/v of Calcium Chloride \nand 5 per cent w/v of Dextrose in Water for Injections.\neriochrome black T mixture as indicator. Carry out a blank \ntitration.\nFor potassium - Dilute suitably with water and determine \nby Method A for flame photometry (2.4.4), or by Method A for \natom"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. To 1 ml add 0.05 ml ofpotassium cupri-tartrate solution; \nthe solution remains blue and clear. Heat to boiling, a copious\nred precipitate is formed.\nB. When warmed with potassium permanganate gives \nacetaldehyde, recognisable by its odour.\nC. The residue on evaporation, when moistened with \nhydrochloric acid and introduced on a platinum wire into a \nflame imparts a yellow colour to the flame. When viewed \nthrough a suitable blue glass, the flame is tinged reddish \npurple.\nD. It gives reaction (C) of calcium salts and reaction (A) of \nchlorides (2.3.1).\nInject separately the reference solution and the test solution \nand measure the responses for the major peak.\nA.To 1 ml add 0.05 ml ofpotassium cupri-tartrate solution; \nthe solution remains blue and clear. Heat to boiling, a copious \nred precipitate is formed.\n1 ml of 0.01 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.00147 g of \nCaCl2, 2H20. \nFor lactate - Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nCalculate the content of C 3F1603, in the injection.\nFor dextrose -  To a measured volume containing 2 g to 5 g of\nB. When warmed with potassium permanganate gives \nacetaldehyde, recognisable by its odour.\nDextrose, add 0.2 ml of 5 M ammonia and sufficient waterto\n.:> Test s9lut4on.,The preparation under examination.\n-•  piso,tifice ml. Mix well allow to stand for\n30 minutes and -measure the optical rotation in a 2-din tube \n(2.4.22). The observed rotation in degrees multiplied by\n:ReferOce.thlytion. A 0.28 per cent w/v solution of lithium\nenhe residue on evaporation, when rri;i4eried'with \nhydrochloric acid and introduced on a platintiin wire into a\nIcioiitte RS lit the mobile phase.\n52-22 \n32-23\n61k\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 20 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 [tin), \n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 90 volumes of water and\n10 volumes of a 2 per cent v/v solution of octylamine in \nacetonitrile, the pH of which is adjusted to 7.0 with a\n10 per cent v/v solution of phosphoric acid, \n-\nflow rate: 2 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 210 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 10 IA\nInject separately the reference solution and the test solution \nand measure the responses for the major peak.\nCalculate the content of C 3H(;03 in the injection.\nFor dextrose-To a measured volume containing 2 g to 5 g of\nDextrose, add 0.2 ml of  5 Mammonia and sufficient water to \nproduce 100.0 ml. Mix well, allow to stand for\n30 minutes and measure the optical rotation in a 2-dm tube \n(2.4.22). The observed rotation in degrees multiplied by 0.9477 \nrepresents the weight, in g, of dextrose, C 6H 1206, in the volume \ntaken for assay.\nStorage. Store in single dose containers of glass or plastic at \na temperature not exceeding 30°. On keeping, small particles \nmay separate from the solution in glass containers.\nLabelling. The label states (1) that the injection contains, in \nmillimoles per litre, the following approximate amounts of the\nions. sodium, 65.5, potassium, 2.5, calcium, 1, bicarbonate \n(as lactate), 14.5, and chloride, 55.5; (2) the total osmolar \nconcentration in mOsmol per litre; (3) that the injection should \nnot be used if it contains visible particles.\nModified Compound Sodium Lactate \nand Dextrose Injection\nModified Compound Sodium Lactate with Dextrose \nIntravenous Infusion; Modified Lactated Ringer's and \nDextrose Injection.\nModified Compound Sodium Lactate and Dextrose Injection \nis a sterile solution containing 0.048 per cent v/v of Lactic \nAcid (equivalent to 0.064 per cent w/v of sodium lactate) with \n0.12 per cent w/v of Sodium Chloride, 0.008 per cent w/v of \nPotassium Chloride, 0.0054 per cent w/v of Calcium Chloride \nand 5 per cent w/v of Dextrose in Water for Injections.\nModified Compound Sodium Lactate and Dextrose Injection \ncontains not less than 0.054 per cent and not more than \n0.064 per cent w/v of sodium, Na, not less than 0.0038 per cent \nand not more than 0.0044 per cent w/v of potgggiiiti,.K; not \nless than 0.074 per cent and not more than 0.084;fiericeritwiv \nof total chloride, Cl, not less than 0.005 per cent and-kit more\n1P 20 \nCOMPOUND SODIUM LACTATE AND DEXTROSE INJECTION \nCOMPOUND SODIUM LACTATE INJECTION\nthan 0.0058 per cent w/v of calcium chloride, CaC12,2H20, and\nprecipitate with water slightly acidified with  nitric acid and \ndote the excess of silver nitrate with  0.1 114 ammonium \nthiocyanate using  ferric ammonium sulphate solution as \nindicator until a reddish yellow colour is produced. Carry out\nCompound Sodium Lactate Injection\n not les than 0.046 per cent and \nnot more than 0.056 per cent\n w/v of lactate, calculated as C\n3H603, and not less than 4.5 \ncent and not more than 5.25 per cent w/v of dextrose, C,,H, 2\nCompound Sodium Lactate Intravenous Infusion; Ringer- \nLactate Solution for Injection; Hartmann's Solution for \nInjection\nIt contains no antimicrobial agent.\nCategory. Fluid and electrolyte replenisher.\nilver nitrate is equivalent to 0.003545 g of total\nCompound Sodium Lactate Injection is a sterile solution \ncontaining 0.24 per cent v/v of Lactic Acid (equivalent to\nhobmlrol a n\ni\nfle: ut rcl maaA s\na\ncicouhl\nre iddaesCI.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-colo \nsolution.\nchloride\ncalcium t\n-Evaporate 100.0 ml on a water-bath \nto 50 ml, add to this solution 5.0 ml of 0.01 M magnesium \nsulphate  and 5 ml of ammonia buffer pH 10.9  and titrate with \n0.01 M disodium edetate  using  eriochrome black T mixture \nas indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n0.32 per cent w/v of sodium lactate) with 0.6 per cent w/v of \nSodium Chloride, 0.04 per cent w/v of Potassium Chloride and \n0.027 per cent w/v of Calciwn Chloride in Water for Injections.\n2\nFor\nto\nA. To 1 ml add 0.05 ml of potassium cupri-tartrate solution; \nthe solution remains blue and clear. Heat to boiling, a copious \nred precipitate is formed.\nCompound Sodium Lactate Injection contains not less than \n0.27 per cent and not more than 0.32 per cent w/v of sodium, \nNa, not less than 0.019 per cent and n"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-lauryl-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Lauryl Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Lauryl Sulphate\nSodium Lauryl Sulphate is a mixture of sodium alkyl sulphates \nconsisting .mainly of sodium dodecyl - sulphate^ \nC^[CH2])0ÇH2OSO3Na. _\n$odium Lauryl Sulphate contains not less than 85.0 per cent \n«sodium alkyl sulphates, calculated as CTlfyNaChS.\nDescription. A white or pale yellow powder or crystals.\nA. A 1 per cent w/v solution, when shaken, produces plenty \nof foam.\nB. Mix 0.1 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution with 0.1 ml of a 0.1 per \ncent w/v solution of methylene blue and 2 ml of 1M sulphuric \nacid, add 2 ml of dichloromethane and shake; the dichloro- \nmethane layer is intensely blue.\nC. Mix about 10 mg with 10 ml of ethanol and heat to boiling \non a water-bath, shaking frequently. Filter immediately and \nevaporate the ethanol. Dissolve the residue in 8 ml ofwater, \nadd 3 ml of 2 M hydrochloric acid, evaporate the solution to \nhalf its volume and cool. Filter and to the filtrate add 1 ml of\nbarium chloride solutions awhile, crystalline precipitate is \nproduced.\nD. It gives reaction (B) of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nAlkalinity. Dissolve 1.0 g in 100 ml of carbon dioxide-free \nwater and add 0.1 ml of phenol red solution. Not more than \n0.5, ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric.acid is required to change the \ncolour of the solution.\nNon-esterified alcohols. Not more than 4 per cent, determined \nby the following method. Dissolve 10.0 g in 100 ml of water, \nadd 100 mF of ethanol (95 per cent) and extract the solution \nwith three quantities, each of 50 ml, of n-pentane, adding \nsodium chloride, if necessary, to promote separation of the\ntwo layers. Wash the combined organic layers with three \nquantities, each of 50 ml, of water. Dry the organic solution \nover anhydrous sodium sulphate, filter and evaporate on a \nwater-bath until the odour of pentane is no longer detectable. \nHeat the residue at 105° for 15 minutes, cool and weigh.\nSodium Chloride and Sodium Sulphate. Not more than a total \nof 8.0 per cent, determined by the following methods.\nFor sodium chloride — Dissolve 5.0 g in 50 ml of water, add\n2 M nitric acid drop wise until the solution is neutral to litmus \npaper, add 2 ml of potassium chromate solution and titrate\nwith 0.1 M silver nitrate.\n1 ml of 0.1 Msilver nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g of NaCl.\nFor sodium sulphate — Dissolve Ó.5 g in 20 ml of water, \nwarming gently if necessary, and add 1 ml of a 0.05 per cent \nw/v solution of dithizone in acetóne. If the solution is red, \nadd 1 Mnitric acid, dropwise, until it becomes bluish green. \nAdd 2 ml of dichloroacetic acid solution and 80 ml of acetone\nand titrate with 0.01 M lead nitrate until a permanent orange- \nred colour is obtained.\n3617\nwww.webofpharma.com\n1 ml; of 0.01 M lead nUrate is equivalent to 0.001420 g of \nNa2SO4. ...........; \n.... ;;......■....... . ■■ ; ...... .....\nAssay. Weigh 1.15 g, dissolve in sufficient water to produce\n1000.0 ml, warming if necessary. To 20.0 ml add 15 ml of \nchloroform, 10 ml. of dilute sulphuric acid and\n1 nil of dimethyl yellow-oracet blue B solution and titrale \nwith 0.004 M benzethonium chloride, shaking vigorously\nand allowing the: layers to separate after each addition, until \nthe chloroform layer acquires a permanent clear green colour.\n1 ml of 0.004 M benzethonium.chloride is equivalent to \n0.001154 g of sodium alkyl sulphates, calculated as \nC!2Il25NaO4S. • \n: f ■\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Sodium Lauryl Sulphate\nSodium Lauryl Sulphate is a mixture of sodium alkyl sulphates \nconsisting .mainly of sodium dodecyl - sulphate^ \nC^[CH2])0ÇH2OSO3Na. _\n$odium Lauryl Sulphate contains not less than 85.0 per cent \n«sodium alkyl sulphates, calculated as CTlfyNaChS.\nDescription. A white or pale yellow powder or crystals.\nA. A 1 per cent w/v solution, when shaken, produces plenty \nof foam.\nB. Mix 0.1 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution with 0.1 ml of a 0.1 per \ncent w/v solution of methylene blue and 2 ml of 1M sulphuric \nacid, add 2 ml of dichloromethane and shake; the dichloro- \nmethane layer is intensely blue.\nC. Mix about 10 mg with 10 ml of ethanol and heat to boiling \non a water-bath, shaking frequently. Filter immediately and \nevaporate the ethanol. Dissolve the residue in 8 ml ofwater, \nadd 3 ml of 2 M hydrochloric acid, evaporate the solution to \nhalf its volume and cool. Filter and to the filtrate add 1 ml of\nbarium chloride solutions awhile, crystalline precipitate is \nproduced.\nD. It gives reaction (B) of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nAlkalinity. Dissolve 1.0 g in 100 ml of carbon dioxide-free \nwater and add 0.1 ml of phenol red solution. Not more than \n0.5, ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric.acid is required to change the \ncolour of the solution.\nNon-esterified alcohols. Not more than 4 per cent, determined \nby the following method. Dissolve 10.0 g in 100 ml of water, \nadd 100 mF of ethanol (95 per cent) and extract the solution \nwith three quantities, each of 50 ml, of n-pentane, adding \nsodium chloride, if necessary, to promote separation of the\ntwo layers. Wash the combined organic layers with three \nquantities, each of 50 ml, of water. Dry the organic solution \nover anhydrous sodium sulphate, filter and evaporate on a \nwater-bath until the odour of pentane is no longer detectable. \nHeat the residue at 105° for 15 minutes, cool and weigh.\nSodium Chloride and Sodium Sulphate. Not more than a total \nof 8.0 per cent, determined by the following methods.\nFor sodium chloride — Dissolve 5.0 g in 50 ml of water, add\n2 M nitric acid drop wise until the solution is neutral to litmus \npaper, add 2 ml of potassium chromate solution and titrate\nwith 0.1 M silver nitrate.\n1 ml of 0.1 Msilver nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g of NaCl.\nFor sodium sulphate — Dissolve Ó.5 g in 20 ml of water, \nwarming gently if necessary, and add 1 ml of a 0.05 per cent \nw/v solution of dithizone in acetóne. If the solution is red, \nadd 1 Mnitric acid, dropwise, until it becomes bluish green. \nAdd 2 ml of dichloroacetic acid solution and 80 ml of acetone\nand titrate with 0.01 M lead nitrate until a permanent orange- \nred colour is obtained.\n3617\nwww.webofpharma.com\n1 ml; of 0.01 M lead nUrate is equivalent to 0.001420 g of \nNa2SO4. ...........; \n.... ;;......■....... . ■■ ; ...... .....\nAssay. Weigh 1.15 g, dissolve in sufficient water to produce\n1000.0 ml, warming if necessary. To 20.0 ml add 15 ml of \nchloroform, 10 ml. of dilute sulphuric acid and\n1 nil of dimethyl yellow-oracet blue B solution and titrale \nwith 0.004 M benzethonium chloride, shaking vigorously\nand allowing the: layers to separate after each addition, until \nthe chloroform layer acquires a permanent clear green colour.\n1 ml of 0.004 M benzethonium.chloride is equivalent to \n0.001154 g of sodium alkyl sulphates, calculated as \nC!2Il25NaO4S. • \n: f ■\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-metabisulphite","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Metabisulphite"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Metabisulphite\nSodium Pyrosulphite; Sodium Disulphite\nNa2S2O5 \nMol. Wt. 190.1\nSodium Metabisulphite may be prepared by saturating a \nsolution of Sodium Hydroxide with sulphur dioxide and \nallowing crystallisation to occur.\nSodium Metabisulphite contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 100.5 per cent of Na2S2O5.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (antioxidant).\nDescription. Colourless, prismatic crystals or a white or creamy \nwhite powder. ... . . .; \n; \n. ."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Metabisulphite\nSodium Pyrosulphite; Sodium Disulphite\nNa2S2O5 \nMol. Wt. 190.1\nSodium Metabisulphite may be prepared by saturating a \nsolution of Sodium Hydroxide with sulphur dioxide and \nallowing crystallisation to occur.\nSodium Metabisulphite contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 100.5 per cent of Na2S2O5.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (antioxidant).\nDescription. Colourless, prismatic crystals or a white or creamy \nwhite powder. ... . . .; \n; \n. ."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A'. A 5 per cent w/v solution in' carbon dioxide free-water \n(solution A)gives reaction (A) bf sodium salts (2.3.1).\nB. To 0.4 ml of iodinatedpotassium iodide solution, add 8 ml \nof water and 1 ml of solution A, diluted 1 to 1.6 in water. The \nsolution is colourless and gives reaction (A) of sulphates \n(2.3.1). \n•.\nTests ■ \nir. .... ■.\npH (2.4.24). 3.5 to 5.0, determined in solution A.\nArsenic (2.3.10). Mix 2.5 g in a porcelain dish with 10 ml of \nwater, 1.25 g ofpotassium chlorate and 16 ml of hydrochloric \nacid and heat to expel chlorine, remove the last traces, of \nchlorine with a few drops of stannous chloride' solution AsT \nand add 35 ml of water. The resulting solution complies with \nthe limit test for arsenic (4 ppm). ........... \n. ■■\nHeavy metals (2.3; 13).Dissolve 1.0 g in 10 ml of water, add 5 ml \nof hydrochloric acid arid evaporate to' dryness on a water­\nbath. Dissolve the residue in 25 ml of water containing 2 ml of\n3618\nIP'20$\nSODIUM METABISULPHITE\ndilute acetic acid. The solution complies with the:limiti t\nfor heavy metals, Method A ( 20 ppm). DJ jjy 5 I\nIron; To 0.5 gadd2 ml of hydrochloric acid arid evaporate ■ I\na water-bath to dryness. Dissolve the residue in 2 ml of I\nhydrochloric acid and 20 ml of water and add a few drops of 1\nbromine solution, cool, dilute with water to 25 ml,-then add I\n50 mg Of ammonium persulphate and 5 ml of \n|\nthiocyanate solution. Any colour produced is not more intense I\nthan that obtained by adding 5 ml of ammonium thiocyanate \nsolution to a mixture of 1 ml of iron standard solution\n(20 ppm Fe), 2 ml of hydrochloric acid, 50 mg, of ammonium \npersulphate and sufficient water to. produce 25 ml (40 ppm).\nThiosulphate. Dissolve 1.0 g in 10 ml of 2 M hydrochloric \nacid and heat on a water-bath for 16 minutes; not more than a \nfaint opalescence is produced. \n’ . w , , \n,\nAssay. Dissolve 0.1 g in 50.0 ml 0.05 M iodine, add 1 ml of \nhydrochloric acid and titrate the excess of iodine with 0./ \nsodium thiosulphate using starch solution, added towards \nthe end of the titration, as indicator. \n\" ~ \n■ '\nI\n1 ml of 0.05 Miodine is.equivalent to 0.004753.gofNa2S20<.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture; On exposure \nto air and moisture it is slowly oxidised fo sulphate with \ndisintegration of the crystals. \n'\nSodium Methylparaben\nSodium Methyl Hydroxybenzoate\nO .\n, ^ch3\n■ &\nNaO\nC8H7NaO3 !\n' ' Mol. Wt 174.1\nSodium Methylparaben is the sodium salt of methyl . .. |\n4-hydroxybenzoate. \n1 |\nSodium Methylparaben contains not less than 95.0 per cent |\nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C8H7NaO3, calculated oil J\nthe anhydrous basis. \n: |\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial preservative). |\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; hygroscopic. |\n; j\nA. Dissolve 0.5 g in 5 ml of water and acidify to litmus pap^ ; j \nwith hydrochloric acid', a white precipitate is produced. W ; | \nthe precipitate with water and dry. ■■ \n'■■■. ■■■■' ■''' \nJ\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\n-g by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCmpare the spectrum with that obtained with methylparaben\nIPRS. \n'r..\na < ner cent w/v solution gives the reactions of sodium\nsalts(2.3-l)- \n: \n■\nThe relative retention time with reference to methyparaben for \n^ydroxybénzoic acid is about 0.6."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. A10.0 per cent w/v solution in water\nis clear (2 .4 .1),\npH P4 24)- 95 to J 0.5, determined in a 0.1 per cent w/v solution.\nRelated substances.. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). w Jr> \n'\nDissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination hi 2.5 ml oi methanol and dilute to 50.0 ml with the \nmobile phase. Dilute 10.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with\nthe mobile phase.\n1.0g.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 5 mg of 4-hydroxybenzoic \nacid and 5 mg of the substance under examination in the \nmobile phase and dilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase. \nDilute 1.0 ml of the solution to 10.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 50 mg of methylparaben \nÌPRS in 2.5 m\\ oi methanol and. dilute to 50.0 ml with the \nmobile phase. Dilute 1Ó.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with\nthe mobile phase.\nReference solution (c). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to \n20.0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml ofthe solution to \n10.0 nil with thè mòbile phase.\nChromatographic system\n. - a. stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed-with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm),\n~ mòbile phase: a mixture of 35 volumes of 0.68 per cent\nw/v solution of potassium dihydrogen phosphate, and \n65 volumes of methanol,\n- flow rate: 1:3 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer sèt at’272 nm;\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not.valid unless_the \nresolution between the peaks due to methylparaben and \n^ydroxybenzoic acid is not less than 2.0.\ninject reference solution (c) and the test solution. Run the\nc omatogram five times the retention time of the principal\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the \nareanfjany peak corresponding to 4-hydroxybenzoic acid\n, ’Pl^d by 1,4is not more than the six times the area of the \nP^riciphl peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference\non (c) (3.0 per cent), the area of any other secondary\nSODIUM NITRITE\npeak is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) (0.5 per \ncent) and the sum of areas of alLthe secondary peaks other \nthan 4-hydroxybenzoic acid is not more than twice the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (c) (1.0 per cent). Ignore the peak with an area less \nthan 0.2 times the area ofthe principal peak in thechromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) (0.1 per cent).\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 1.0 g in 20 ml of water, add 0.4 ml \nof nitric acid and filter. 15 ml of the filtrate complies with the \nlimittest for chlorides (330 ppm).\nSulphates (2.3.17). Dissolve 0.5 g in 40 ml of water, add 3.5 ml \nof 2 Mhydrochloric acid, dilute to 60 ml with water and filter.\n15 ml of the filtrate complies with the limit test for sulphates \n(0.12 per cent).\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 5.0 per cent, determined on\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as \ndescribed under Related substances.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of CgH7NaO3, multiplying the content of \nmethylparaben by a correction factor of 1.145. \n■ ■\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-methyl-hydroxybenzoate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Methyl Hydroxybenzoate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Methyl Hydroxybenzoate\nCsH7Na03\nMol. Wt. 174.1\nSodium Methylparaben is the sodium salt ofmethyl\n4-hydroxybenzoate.\nSodium Methylparaben contains not less than 99.0 per cent\nand not more than 102.0 per cent ofCsH7Na03' calculated on\nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial preservative).\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; odourless or almost\nodourless; hygroscopic."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Methyl Hydroxybenzoate\nCsH7Na03\nMol. Wt. 174.1\nSodium Methylparaben is the sodium salt ofmethyl\n4-hydroxybenzoate.\nSodium Methylparaben contains not less than 99.0 per cent\nand not more than 102.0 per cent ofCsH7Na03' calculated on\nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial preservative).\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; odourless or almost\nodourless; hygroscopic."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Dissolve 0.5 g in 5 ml ofwater and acidify to litmus paper\nwith hydrochloric acid; a white precipitate is produced. Wash\nthe precipitate with water and dry.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with methylparaben\nRS.\n2132\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nB. The residue on ignition gives the reactions ofsodium salts\n(2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance ofsolution.Al0.0 per cent w/v solution in water\nis clear (2.4.1).\npH (2.4.24). 9.5 to 1O.5,determinedin a 0.1 percentw/v solution.\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography\n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobilephase. A mixture of1 volume ofglacial acetic acid, 30\nvolumes ofwater and 70 volumes of methanol.\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under\nexamination in 10 ml of water. Immediately add 2 ml of\nhydrochloric acid and shake with 50 illl of ether. Evaporate\nthe upper layer to dryness and take up the residue with 10 ml\nof acetone.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 34.3 mg of4-hydroxybenzoic\nacid (sodium propylparaben impurity A) in 100.0 ofacetone.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 0.5 ml of test solution (a) to\n100.0 ml with acetone.\nReference solution\n(c).\nDissolve\n10 mg of ethyl\nparahydroxybenzoateRSin 1 ml oftest solution (a) and dilute\nto 10.0 ml with acetone.\nApply 5 IJ.I ofeach solution. Allow the mobile phase to rise 15\ncm. Dry the plate in air and examine in ultraviolet light at 254\nnm. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, any\nspot due to 4- hydroxybenzoic acid is not more intense than\nthe corresponding spot in the chromatogram obtained with\nreference solution(a) (4.0 per cent) and any other secondary\nspot is not more intense than the spot in the chromatogram\nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.5 per cent). The test is\nnot valid unless the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (c) shows two clearly separated principal spots.\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 1.0 g in 20 ml ofwater, add 0.2 ml\nofnitric acid and filter. 15 ml ofthe filtrate complies with the\nlimit test for chlorides (330 ppm).\nSulphates (2.3.17). Dissolve 0.5 gin 40 ml ofwater, add 3.5 ml\nof2 M hydrochloric acid, dilute to 60 ml with water and filter.\n15 ml ofthe filtrate complies with the limit test for sulphates\n(0.12 per cent).\nWater (2.3.43). Notmore tban 5.0 percent, determined on 1.0 g.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.15 gin 50 ml of anhydrous acetic acid.\nTitrate with 0.1 Mperchloric acid, determining the end-point\npotentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.01741 g of\nCgH7Na03'\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\n2133\nSODIUM PHOSPHATE"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-nitrite-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Nitrite Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Nitrite Injection\nSodium Nitrite Injection is a sterile solution of Sodium Nitrite \nin Water for Injections. \n-\nSodium Nitrite Injection contains riot less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nNaNO2. ’ \n.\nUsual strength. 30 mg per ml."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Nitrite Injection\nSodium Nitrite Injection is a sterile solution of Sodium Nitrite \nin Water for Injections. \n-\nSodium Nitrite Injection contains riot less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nNaNO2. ’ \n.\nUsual strength. 30 mg per ml."},{"name":"Identification","text":"The injection complies with the tests of sodium salts (2.3.1) \nand nitrite (2.3.1). \n1 f"},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 7.0 to 9.0.' \n■ ;i\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2:3), Not more than 0.33 Endotoxin \nUnit per ing of sodiuhi nitrite.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Injections.\nAssay. Pipette a volume of injection containing 90 mg of \nSodium Nitrite into a mixture of 5 0.0 ml of 0.02 -.Mpotassium \npermanganate, 50 ml of water and 20;ml o£; dilute sulphuric\nacid, immersing the tip of the pipette beneath the surface of \nthe permanganate mixture during the addition. Stopper the\nflask, allow it to stand for 1$ minutes with occasional shaking \nand add 3 go£potassium iodide. and titrate the liberated iodine \nwith 0.1 M sodium thiosulphate using starch solution added \ntowards the end of titration as an indicator. Cany out a blank \ntitration. . .'.1 \nfl\n1 ml of 0.02 M potassium permanganate isequ iva lent to \n0.00345 g of NaNO2. \n.7 :¡f;burl '\nStorage., Preserve in single-dose containers^ preferably of \nType 1 glass. \n, \n- \n>>. E\n3620\nSODIUM NITRITE INJECTION\nn*2022"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-nitrite","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Nitrite"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Nitrite\nNaNO2 \nMol. Wt. 69.0\nSodium Nitrite is nitrous acid, sodium salt.\nSodium Nitrite contains not less, than 97.0 per .cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent ofNaNO2, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nA 10 per cent w/v solution complies with the tests of sodium \nsalts (2.3.1) and nitrite (2.3.1).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 1 g in 6 ml of 3 Mhydrochloric \nacid, and evaporate on a steam-bath to dryness. Reduce the \nresidue to a coarse powder, and continue heating on the steam­\nbath until the odor of hydrochloric acid no longer is perceptible. \nDissolve the residue in 23 ml of water, and add 2 ml of 1 M \nacetic acid. The solution complies with the limit test for heavy \nmetals, method A (20 ppm).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.25 per cent, drying it \nover silica gel for 4 hours.\n3619\nwww.webofpharma.com\nAssay. Dissolve, 1; gin 250:0 ml of waler. Pipette 25 ml of the \nsolution into: a - mixture of 50.0I ml of; 0.02 :M potassium \npermanganate, 50 ml of w/er and 20 ml of dilute sulphuric\n«ezf/. When adding the sodium nitrite solution, immerse the \ntip of the pipctte beneath the surface of thfe. permanganate \nmixture. Stopper lhe flask, allow it to stand for 15 minutes with \noccasional shaking and add 3 g o£potassium iodide and titrate \nthe liberated iodine withe 0.1 M. sodium thiosulphate using\nstarch solution, added, towards the end of titration as an \nindicator. Carry out a blank titration. '\n1ml of 0.02 Mpotassium permanganate is equivalent to \n0.00345 g of NaNO2.\nStorage,. Store protected ■ from moisture, at a temperature \nbetween 15° to30°. \n.<■ \n-u ,."},"sections":[{"name":"CONTENT","text":"Sodium Nitrite\nNaNO2 \nMol. Wt. 69.0\nSodium Nitrite is nitrous acid, sodium salt.\nSodium Nitrite contains not less, than 97.0 per .cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent ofNaNO2, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nA 10 per cent w/v solution complies with the tests of sodium \nsalts (2.3.1) and nitrite (2.3.1).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 1 g in 6 ml of 3 Mhydrochloric \nacid, and evaporate on a steam-bath to dryness. Reduce the \nresidue to a coarse powder, and continue heating on the steam­\nbath until the odor of hydrochloric acid no longer is perceptible. \nDissolve the residue in 23 ml of water, and add 2 ml of 1 M \nacetic acid. The solution complies with the limit test for heavy \nmetals, method A (20 ppm).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.25 per cent, drying it \nover silica gel for 4 hours.\n3619\nwww.webofpharma.com\nAssay. Dissolve, 1; gin 250:0 ml of waler. Pipette 25 ml of the \nsolution into: a - mixture of 50.0I ml of; 0.02 :M potassium \npermanganate, 50 ml of w/er and 20 ml of dilute sulphuric\n«ezf/. When adding the sodium nitrite solution, immerse the \ntip of the pipctte beneath the surface of thfe. permanganate \nmixture. Stopper lhe flask, allow it to stand for 15 minutes with \noccasional shaking and add 3 g o£potassium iodide and titrate \nthe liberated iodine withe 0.1 M. sodium thiosulphate using\nstarch solution, added, towards the end of titration as an \nindicator. Carry out a blank titration. '\n1ml of 0.02 Mpotassium permanganate is equivalent to \n0.00345 g of NaNO2.\nStorage,. Store protected ■ from moisture, at a temperature \nbetween 15° to30°. \n.<■ \n-u ,."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-nitroprusside-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Nitroprusside Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Nitroprusside Injection\nsolution pH 4.6. Use three 50-m1 volumetric flasks (A, B, C).\nTo flask B, add 1.0 ml offerricyanide standard solution\nSodium Nitroprusside Injection is a sterile material obtained \nfrom a solution of Sodium Nitroprusside by freeze drying. It \nmay contain excipients. It is filled in a sealed container. \nThe injection is constituted immediately before use by \ndissolving Sodium Nitroprusside Injection in the requisite \namount of Glucose Intravenous Infusion (50 g per litre) and \ndiluting the resulting solution with 250 to 500 times its volume \nof Glucose Intravenous Infusion (50 g per litre). \nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer. \nSodium Nitroprusside Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of sodium nitroprusside dihydrate, Na 2Fe(CN)5NO, \n2H20.\n(50 ppm Fe(CN)6) : To flasks A and B, add 1 ml of a 0.5 per cent\nON \n/CN \nNC - Fe- ON\nw/v solution of ferrous ammonium sulphate. To the three \nflasks, add 10.0 ml of the solution of the substance under\nC N \nNat\nNC \\\nexamination. Dilute the contents of each flask to 50.0 ml\nwith water. Allow to stand for 30 minutes. The absorbance of \nthe solution  in flask A measured at 720 nm (2.4.7) using the \nsolution in flask C as the compensation liquid is not more \nthan the absorbance of the solution in flask B measured at \n720 nm using the solution in flask A as the compensation\nNa2[Fe(CN)5N01,2H20 \t\nMol. Wt. 298.0\nSodium Nitroprusside is sodium pentacyanonitrosylferrat e.\nSodium Nitroprusside contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 100.5 per cent of  sodium \npentacyanonitrosylferrate (111)-Na 2[Fe(CN)5N0], calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nFliciid\neurroc(2y0a0nPidPem). \ns.Dissolve 4.0 g in 100.0 ml of water. Use three \n50-m1  volumetric flasks (A, B, C). To flask B, add 2.0 ml \nofferrocyanide standard solution (100 ppm Fe(CN) 6). To \nflasks A and B, add 1 ml of  ferric chloride solution.  To the \nthree flasks, add 25.0 ml of the solution of the substance under \nexamination. Dilute the contents of each flask to 50.0 ml with \nwater. Allow to stand for 30 minutes. The absorbance of the \nsolution in flask A measured at 695 nm (2.4.7) using the \nsolution in flask C as the compensation liquid is not more \nthan the absorbance of the solution in flask B measured at \n695 nm using the solution in flask A as the compensation \nliquid (200 ppm).\nCategory. Vasodilator.\nDescription.  A reddish-brown powder or crystals.\nUsual strength. 50 mg per vial. \nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Nitroprusside Injection\nsolution pH 4.6. Use three 50-m1 volumetric flasks (A, B, C).\nTo flask B, add 1.0 ml offerricyanide standard solution\nSodium Nitroprusside Injection is a sterile material obtained \nfrom a solution of Sodium Nitroprusside by freeze drying. It \nmay contain excipients. It is filled in a sealed container. \nThe injection is constituted immediately before use by \ndissolving Sodium Nitroprusside Injection in the requisite \namount of Glucose Intravenous Infusion (50 g per litre) and \ndiluting the resulting solution with 250 to 500 times its volume \nof Glucose Intravenous Infusion (50 g per litre). \nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer. \nSodium Nitroprusside Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of sodium nitroprusside dihydrate, Na 2Fe(CN)5NO, \n2H20.\n(50 ppm Fe(CN)6) : To flasks A and B, add 1 ml of a 0.5 per cent\nON \n/CN \nNC - Fe- ON\nw/v solution of ferrous ammonium sulphate. To the three \nflasks, add 10.0 ml of the solution of the substance under\nC N \nNat\nNC \\\nexamination. Dilute the contents of each flask to 50.0 ml\nwith water. Allow to stand for 30 minutes. The absorbance of \nthe solution  in flask A measured at 720 nm (2.4.7) using the \nsolution in flask C as the compensation liquid is not more \nthan the absorbance of the solution in flask B measured at \n720 nm using the solution in flask A as the compensation\nNa2[Fe(CN)5N01,2H20 \t\nMol. Wt. 298.0\nSodium Nitroprusside is sodium pentacyanonitrosylferrat e.\nSodium Nitroprusside contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 100.5 per cent of  sodium \npentacyanonitrosylferrate (111)-Na 2[Fe(CN)5N0], calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nFliciid\neurroc(2y0a0nPidPem). \ns.Dissolve 4.0 g in 100.0 ml of water. Use three \n50-m1  volumetric flasks (A, B, C). To flask B, add 2.0 ml \nofferrocyanide standard solution (100 ppm Fe(CN) 6). To \nflasks A and B, add 1 ml of  ferric chloride solution.  To the \nthree flasks, add 25.0 ml of the solution of the substance under \nexamination. Dilute the contents of each flask to 50.0 ml with \nwater. Allow to stand for 30 minutes. The absorbance of the \nsolution in flask A measured at 695 nm (2.4.7) using the \nsolution in flask C as the compensation liquid is not more \nthan the absorbance of the solution in flask B measured at \n695 nm using the solution in flask A as the compensation \nliquid (200 ppm).\nCategory. Vasodilator.\nDescription.  A reddish-brown powder or crystals.\nUsual strength. 50 mg per vial. \nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. When examined in the range 350 nm to 600 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.7 per cent w/v solution in water shows an absorption \nmaximum at 395 nm, a shoulder at 510 nm and a minima at \n370 nm. The specific absorbance at the maximum is 0.65 to 0.80.\nA. When examined in the range 350 nm to 600 nm (2.4.7), a\nB. Dissolve 20 mg in 2 ml of water and add 0.1 ml of sodium \nsulphide solution;  a deep violet-red colour is produced.\n1.6 per cent w/v solution exhibits a maximum at 395 nm and the \nabsorbance at 395 nm is about 1.1. \nB. Dissolve 20 mg in 2 ml of water and add 0.1 ml of sodium \nsulphide solution; a deep purple colour is produced. \nC. It gives the reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nSulphates (2.3.17). Not more than 100 ppm.\nTest solution. Dissolve 3.6 g in 120 ml of water, add with \nmixing 4 ml of sulphate standard solution (10 ppm 50 4) and \n20 ml of a 25 per cent w/v solution of cupric chloride and \ndilute to 150.0 ml with water. Allow to stand for 16 hours and \nfilter or centrifuge until a clear light-blue solution is obtained.\nC. Dissolve 50 mg in 1 ml of water and acidify the solution by \nthe addition of hydrochloric acid. Place a drop of the solution \nin an oxidising flame; a persistent yellow colour is produced."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Reference solution.  To 40 ml of sulphate standard solution \n(10 ppm SO4), add 80 ml of water and 12 ml to 13 ml of a 25 per \ncent w/v solution of cupric chloride, dilute to 150.0 ml with \nWater. The volume of cupric chloride solution added is such\nBacterial endotoxins  (2.2.3). Not more than 0.05 Endotoxin \nUnit per 1.ig of sodium nitropruside. \nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nInsoluble matter. Dissolve 10 g without heating in 50 ml \nof water. Allow to stand for 30 minutes and filter through a \nsintered-glass filter. Wash the filter with cold water until the \nfiltrate is colourless. Dry the residue on the filter at 105°. The \nresidue weighs not more than 1 mg (100 ppm).\nsolution.\ntha t the ol\nour of the final solution matches that of the test\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). \nNOTE- Protect the solutions from light.\nChlorides (2.3.12). In a metallic crucible (nickel) mix 1.0 g with \n8 ml of a 20 per cent w/v solution of sodium hydroxide. Heat\nAllow the solutions to stand. Filter both solutions, discarding \nthe first 25 ml of filtrate. To 100 ml of each filtrate, add 0.5 ml of \nacetic acid. Mix and add 2 ml of a 25 per cent w/v solution \nof barium chloride  and mix again. The test solution is not \nmore opalescent than the reference solution.\nTest solution. Dissolve the contents of a sealed container in \n5 ml of water and dilute the resulting solution with water to \nproduce a  solution containing the equivalent of 0.15 per cent \nw/v of Sodium nitroprusside dihydrate. \nReference solution (a). A 0.15 per cent w/v solution of sodium \nnitroprusside RS in water. \nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.015 per cent \nw/v each of sodium nitroprusside RS and potassium \nhexacyangferrate (III) in water. \nChromatographic system\nslowly and evaporate carefully to dryness over a small flame,\nthen heat to a dull red colour for 30 minutes. Allow to cool and \ntransfer the solid residue with three successive portions, each \nof 8 ml of dilute sulphuric acid.  Filter the sulphuric acid \nextracts on a filter paper and collect the filtrates. Render the \nfiltrate acid to litmus paper by adding, if necessary, a few drops \nof dilute sulphuric acid. Wash the crucible and the filter paper \nwith three successive portions of 10 ml of water , add the\nWater (2.3.43). 9.0 to 15.0 per cent, determined on 0.25 g.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.25 g in 100 ml of water and add 0.1 ml of  dilute \nsulphuric acid. Titrate with 0.1 M silver nitrate, determining \nthe end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25), using silver-\nmercurous sulphate electrode system.\nwashings to the main sulphuric acid solution and dilute to \n60 ml--With wetter.  . 15 ml of the solution complies with the limit \ntest for chlorides (200 ppm), using 2 ml of  chloride standard \nsolution (25 ppm CO.\n1 ml of O./ M silver nitrate is equivalent .t9...0.0 \nNa2[Fe( \t\n5N0]\n7711itain165 steel column 30 cm x 3.9 mm packed with \n:;phenyl- :groups chemically bonded to porous silica\nCN).\nStorage. Store protected from light.\n(10 gm) (Such as pBondapak),\n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 6 volumes of methanol and \n9 volumes of a solution containing 0.34 per cent w/v of \ntetrabutylammonium hydrogen sulphate  and 0.284 per \ncent w/v of anhydrous disodium hydrogen ortho-\nphosphate, adjusted to pH 6.0 with orthophosphoric\nacid,\nflow rate: 1.2 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks corresponding to ferricyanide \nand nitroprusside is not less than 3.0.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content ofNa 2Fe(CN)5N0,2H20 in the injection.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the content of Sodium \nNitroprusside in terms of the equivalent of sodium \nnitroprusside dihydrate contained in it; (2) that the contents \nof the sealed container should be dissolved in, and immediately \ndiluted with, the stated volume of Glucose Intravenous \nInfusion (50 g per litre); (3) that Sodium Nitroprusside \nIntravenous Infusion must be used immediately after \npreparation; (4) that highly coloured solutions must not be \nused; (5) that the diluted solution must be protected from \nlight during infusion.\nMonobasic Sodium Phosphate\nSodium Dihydrogen Phosphate\nOH \nHO-p-0 Na +\n 0\nNaH2PO4,xH2O\nMonosodium phosphate anhydrous \t\nMol. Wt.120.0\nMonosodium phosphate monohydrate \t\nMol. Wt.138.0\nMonosodium phosphate dihydrate \tMol. Wt.156.0\nMonobasic Sodium Phosphate contains (one or two molecules \nof water of hydration or it is anhydrous) not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 103.0 per cent ofNaH 2PO4, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white powder or colourless crystals."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sodium-nitroprusside","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sodium Nitroprusside"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sodium Nitroprusside\nONX /CN\nNC—Fé—CN\nNCZ XCN\nNa2\nNa2[Fe(CN)5NO],2H2O \nMol. Wt. 298.0\nSodium Nitroprusside is sodium pcntacyanon itrosylferrate '■\nSodium Nitroprusside contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 100.5 per cent of sodium \npentaçyanonitrosylferrate (l[I)-Na2[Fc(CN)sNO], calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Vasodilator. \n/ .\nDescription. A reddish-brown powder or crystals."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sodium Nitroprusside\nONX /CN\nNC—Fé—CN\nNCZ XCN\nNa2\nNa2[Fe(CN)5NO],2H2O \nMol. Wt. 298.0\nSodium Nitroprusside is sodium pcntacyanon itrosylferrate '■\nSodium Nitroprusside contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 100.5 per cent of sodium \npentaçyanonitrosylferrate (l[I)-Na2[Fc(CN)sNO], calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Vasodilator. \n/ .\nDescription. A reddish-brown powder or crystals."},{"name":"Identification","text":"j\nA, . When examined:in the range 350 nm to 600 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.7 per cent w/v solution in water shows an absorption \nmaximum at 395 nm, a shoulder at 510 rim and a minima at \n370 nm. The specific absorbance at the maximum is 0:65 to 0.80.\nB. Dissolve 20 mg in 2 ml of water and add 0,1 ml of sodium \nsulphide solution', a deep violet-red colour is produced.\nC. Dissolve 50 mg in 1 ml of water and acidify, the solution by \nthe addition of hydrochloric acid. Place a drop of the solution \nin an oxidising flame; a persistent yellow colour is produced.\nTests...... ■-<••• ■ \n1 ■ \n::\nInsoluble matter. Dissolve 10 g without heating-in 50 ml \nof water, Allow to stand for 30 minutes and filter through a \nsintered-glass filter. Wash the filter with cold, water until the \nfiltrate is colourless. Dry the residue on the filter at 105°. The \nresidue weighs not more, than 1 mg(lOOppm).\nChlorides (2.3.12). In a metallic crucible (nickel) mix 1.0 g with \n8 ml of, a 20 per cent w/v solution of sodium hydroxide. Heat \nslowly and evaporate carefully to dryness over a small flame, \nthen heat to a dull red colour for 30 minutes. Allowto cool and \ntransfer the solid residue with three successive portions, each \nof 8 inL o£ dilute Sulphuric acid. Filter the sulphuric add \nextracts on a filter paper arid collect the filtrates. Render the \nfiltrate acid to, litmus paper by adding, if necessary, a few 