 Ammonium Acetate Butfer - \n» \nL062\nAcebutolol Hydrochloride: \n.-...;;+- \n“9 21265,486,1365 \nAcetic Acid, Anhydrous \n: 1066\nAcebutolol-Hydrochloride Tablets \n, \n1366 \nAcetic Acid, Dilute \nop \n1066\nAcebutolol Tablets \nAcetic Acid, Glacial \n265; 1066, 1374 \n   \n  \n    \nVolume 1: i to xxxiv and 1 to 1276; I-1 to [-fGSEVG \nVolume 4: xliii to xlvi and 4785 to 5024; I-\n190, Volume 3: Xxxix to xHi and 2991 to 4784;\nI-11\nwww.webofpharma.com\n_INDIAN’*PHARMACOPOEIA 2022 \n  \nINDEX\nAcetic Acid: Glacial, Anhydrous \n: 1066\nAcetic Acid Ear Drops \nABTA\nAcetic Acid Sp \n“1066\nAcetic Acid Sp., Dilute \n1066\nAcetic Acid, x M \n“1066\nAcetic Acid Otic Solution \n\"1374\nAceti¢ Acid-in Peptides \npl \n. 333 \nAcétic-Ammonia Buffer pH3.7,, ‘Bthanolic \nPP sa tet \n1062 \nAcetic Anhydride \na \nPave \n‘1066\nAcetic Anhydride Solution \n1066 \nAceti¢ Anhydride-Dioxan Solution \n©\" \n\"21066\nAcetic Bromine Solution \n- \n1076 \n5 \nAcetic Potassium Iodobismut"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/somatropin-for-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Somatropin for Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Somatropin for Injection\nSomatropin for Injection is a freeze-dried, sterile preparation \nofa protein having the structure (191 amino-acid residues) of \nthe major component of growth hormone produced by the \nhuman pituitary.\nSomatropin for Injection contains not less than 89 per cent \nand not more than 105 per cent of the stated potency. By \nconvention, for the purpose of labelling of somatropin \npreparations 1 mg of anhydrous somatropin is equivalent to \n3.0 IU of biological activity.\nSomatropin for Injection is prepared either from somatropin or \nfrom somatropin concentrated solution, or by a method based \non recombinant DNA (rDNA) technology in which the \ninjectable preparation is produced without the isolation of an \nintermediate solid or liquid bulk. In the later case, during the \ncourse of product development, it must be demonstrated that \nthe manufacturing process produces a product having a \nbiological activity of not less than 2.5 IU per mg, using a \nvalidated bioassay based on growth promotion and approved \nby the competent authority. The purified preparation, to which \nbuffers and stabilizers may be added, is filtered through a \nbacteria-retentive filter, aseptically distributed in sterile \ncontainers of glass type I (6.2.1) and freeze-dried. The \ncontainers are immediately sealed so as to exclude microbial \ncontamination and moisture.\nUsual strength. 5 mg (15 IU), 6 mg (18 IU), 10 mg (30 IU) and\n12 mg (36 IU).\nHost cell derived proteins (HCP). Not more than 100 ppm.\nHost cell or vector derived DNA. Not more than 10 ng per \ndose.\nDescription. White or almost white powder"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Somatropin for Injection\nSomatropin for Injection is a freeze-dried, sterile preparation \nofa protein having the structure (191 amino-acid residues) of \nthe major component of growth hormone produced by the \nhuman pituitary.\nSomatropin for Injection contains not less than 89 per cent \nand not more than 105 per cent of the stated potency. By \nconvention, for the purpose of labelling of somatropin \npreparations 1 mg of anhydrous somatropin is equivalent to \n3.0 IU of biological activity.\nSomatropin for Injection is prepared either from somatropin or \nfrom somatropin concentrated solution, or by a method based \non recombinant DNA (rDNA) technology in which the \ninjectable preparation is produced without the isolation of an \nintermediate solid or liquid bulk. In the later case, during the \ncourse of product development, it must be demonstrated that \nthe manufacturing process produces a product having a \nbiological activity of not less than 2.5 IU per mg, using a \nvalidated bioassay based on growth promotion and approved \nby the competent authority. The purified preparation, to which \nbuffers and stabilizers may be added, is filtered through a \nbacteria-retentive filter, aseptically distributed in sterile \ncontainers of glass type I (6.2.1) and freeze-dried. The \ncontainers are immediately sealed so as to exclude microbial \ncontamination and moisture.\nUsual strength. 5 mg (15 IU), 6 mg (18 IU), 10 mg (30 IU) and\n12 mg (36 IU).\nHost cell derived proteins (HCP). Not more than 100 ppm.\nHost cell or vector derived DNA. Not more than 10 ng per \ndose.\nDescription. White or almost white powder"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Where Somatropin for injection is prepared from Somatropin \nor from Somatropin concentrated solution compliance with \nthe requirements for host cell derived proteins, host cell and \nvector-derived DNA, identification test A, identification test \nC, and charged variants need not be reconfirmed by the \nmanufacturer during subsequent production of Somatropin \nfor injection.\nA. Determine by capillary electrophoresis (capillary zone \nelectrophoresis) (2.4.32) as described in the test for charged \nvariants with the following modifications.\nInject test solution (b) under pressure or-vacuum, in the \nfollowing sequence: sample injection for at least 3 seconds \nthen CZE buffer injection for 1 second.\nIn the electropherogram obtained with the test solution (b) \nonly one principal peak corresponding to somatropin, is \ndetected and no doubling of this peak is observed.\n4687\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSOMATROPIN FOR INJECTION\nIP 2022\nB. Determine by Liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as described \nin the test for related proteins.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the \nretention time of principal peak is similar to that of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution.\nC. Determine by Peptide mapping (2.3.47),\nTest solution. Prepare a solution of the substance under \nexamination in 0.05M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution pH\n7.5. to obtain a solution containing 2 mg per ml of somatropin \nand transfer about 1.0 ml to a tube made from a suitable material \nsuch as polypropylene. Prepare a 1 mg per ml solution of \ntrypsin in 0.05M .tris-hydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5 \nand add 30 pl to the solution of the preparation under \nexamination. Cap the tube and place in a water-bath at 37° for \n4 hours. Remove sample horn the water-bath and stop the \nreaction immediately, for example by freezing. If analyzed \nimmediately using an automatic injector, maintain at 2° to 8°.\nReference solution. Prepare reference solution as per the \nprocedure, given for the test solution, ensuring that all \nprocedures are carried out simultaneously, and under identical \nconditions but using somatropin IPRS instead of the \nsubstance under examination.\nChromatographic system\n- stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilyl silica gel (5-10 pm) with a pore size of 30 nm, \n- column temperature: 30°,\n- mobile phase: A, dilute 1 ml of trifluoroacetic acid to\n1000 ml with water,\nB. to 100 ml of water, add 1 ml of \ntrifluoroacetic acid and dilute to 1000 ml with \nacetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 214 nm,\n- inj ection volume: 100 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase\n(in min)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v\n0\n100 ‘\n0\n20\n' \n80\n20\n40\n75 '\n25\n65\n. 50\n50\n■ 70 .\n20\n80\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. The \nchromatogram obtained with each solution is qualitatively \nsimilar to the reference chromatogram of somatropin IPRS \ndigest.\nThe profile of the chromatogram-obtained with the test \nsolution corresponds to that of the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution.\nD. Determine by Size-exclusion chromatography (2.4.16) as \ndescribed in assay.\nThe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution is similar in retention time to the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related Proteins. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), using the normalization procedure.\nMaintain the solutions at 2° to 8° and use within 24 hours. If \nan automatic injector is used, maintain it at 2° to 8°,\nTest solution. Prepare the solution of the preparation under \nexamination in 0.05M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution \npH 7.5 to obtain a concentration of 2 mg per ml of somatropin.\nReference solution (a). Prepare a solution of somatropin IPRS\nin 0.05M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5 to obtain \na concentration of 2 mg per ml of somatropin.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve the contents of a vial of \nsomatropin/desamidosomatropin IPRS in 0.05M tris- \nhydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.5 to obtain a \nconcentration of 2 mg per ml of somatropin. \n:<••••\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, singly end-\ncapped butylsilylsilica gel (5 pm) and with pore size of \n30 nm; a silica saturation column is placed between me \npump and the injector valve,\n- column temperature: 45°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 29 volumes ofpropanol and\n71 volumes of 0.05M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution \npH7.5 \n' '\n- flow rate: 0.5 ml per minute,\n- \nspectrophotometer set at 220 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n,,\nPrecondition the column with 200mlto500mlofa0.1 per cent \nv/v solution of trifluoroacetic acid in a 50 per cent v/v solution \nof acetonitrile', repeat as necessary, to improve column \nperformance. \n. - 0\nInject'reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks corresponding tb \ndesamidosomatropin and somatropin is at least 1.0. ;' ■\nInject reference solution (a) and test solution, the sum of the \nareas of all the peaks other than the principal peak is not more \nthan 13.0 per cent of the total area of all the peaks. Relative \nretention with reference to somatropin: ; for \ndesamidosomatropin is about 0.85.\nDimer and related substances of higher molecular mass! \nDetermine by size-exclusion chromatography (2.4.16). usetfi^ \nnormalization procedure.\nw\n4688\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nSOMATROPIN ['bR-iftjECTidN-\nTest solution. Dilute the preparation under examination in \n0.025Mphosphate Buffer solution pH 7.0 to obtain a \nconcentration of 1.0 mg per ml of somatropin^ 1\nReference solution. Dissolve the contents of a vial of \nsomatropin IPRS in 0.025M phosphate buffer solution pH\n7.0 and dilute with the same solution to obtain a concentration \nof1 mgper ml, ' \n:-\nReference solution (b). Place 1 vial of somatropin IPRS in an \noven at 50° for a period sufficient to generate 1 to 2 per cent of \ndimer (typically 12 to 24 hours). Dissolve nts contents in \n0. 025M'phosphate buffer solution'pH 7.0 and dilute with the \nsame solution to obtain a concentration of 1 mg per mL\nChromatographic system...........\n- \"a sfainless'steel column 30 cm x 7.8 mm, packed, with\n’ hydrophi 1 ic silica gel, of a grade suitable for fractionation\nof globular proteins in the relative molecular mass range\n■' \nof5000to 150000, ' '.J \n:\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 3 volumes of2-propanol and\n97 volumes of 0.063M tris-hydrochloride buffer \nsolution pH 7.0,\n- flow rate: 0.6 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 214 nm,\n. injection volume: 20 pl, \n.\nInject reference solution (a) and test solution.'Relative \nretention time vyith reference tp somatropin monomer: related \nsubstances of higher molecular mass is about 0.65; somatropin \ndimer is about 0.9.\nInject reference solution (b), the test is not valid unless the \nratio of the peak height of the dimer, to the height above the \nbaseline.of the lowest point of the curve separating the .dimer\nfrom the monomer peak is not less than 2.5.\nCalculate the content of dimers, oligomers and aggregates. \nThe sum of the peaks with retention times less than that ofthe \nprincipal peak is not more than 6.0 per cent.\nCharged variants. Determine by Capillary electrophoresis \n(2.4.32). . \n'\nAll,the solutions should be filtered through 0.45 urn membrane \nfilter before use, . \n. .¿.-a, \nm,.\nCZE buffer. Dissolve 13,2 gram c>i ammonium phosphate in\n1000 ml of water and adjust the p.H tp 6.0 using, \northophosphoric acid. ,\nTest'solution (a).'Di lute-the preparation under examination to \nobtain a concentration of 1 mg per ml of somatropin.\nTest .solution (b).,Mix equal volumes of test solution (a) and \nthe reference, solution. r ..... . \n.\nReference solution. Dissolve the contents ¿of a vial of \nsomatropin IPRS in water and dilute with the same solvent to\nobtain a concentration of I mgper ml ■ A;\nCapillary System \nvn&sa\n- material: Uncoated fused silica, ... \n...................\n- size: effective length is at least 70 cm, internal dianteter’\nis 50 pm, '\n- temperature: 30°.\n- spectrophotometer set at 200 nm,- m 3NiPiiftrS-\nSet the autosampler to store the samples at# during analysis?\nPreconditioning of the capillary. Rinse with\" IM sbdiimf \nhydroxide for 20 minutes, with waler for20miriufo§:and'Withs \n\\hcC7.T buffer for 20 minutes at 20 psi. mto\nBetween-run rinsing. Rinse using 0.1M sodium hydroxide \nfor 2 minutes and with the CZE buffer for 6 minutes.\nRinsing times may be adapted according to .the lepgth, ofthe.' \ncapillary and the equipment used. .. \n,\nInject, test solution (a) and the reference solution .under \npressure or vacuum, using the following sequence:: sample? \ninjection for at least 3 seconds then CZE buffer injection for\n1 second. The injection time and pressure may be adapted in \norder to meet the system suitability criteria.\n■ g i''? ?r''r ? - -Z j ■’$ f¿5 \nMigration. Apply a field strength of 217 V/cm (20 kV for \ncapillaries of 92 cm total length) for <80 minutes using-the CZE \nbuffer as the electrolyte in both buffer reservoirs. It is \nrecommended to use fresh buffer for each separation to \ndecrease the risk of ion depletion'. \nz;:?'z: \nR\nThe electropherogram obtained with test solutionRa)'; and)\nreference solution are similar to the electropherogram of \nsomatropin supplied with somatropin IPRS. Twb peaks (ft,ft) \neluti"},{"name":"Assay","text":"Determine by Size-exclusion chromatography (2.4.16) as \ndescribed in the test for dimer and related substances of higher \nmolecular mass.\nCalculate the content of somatropin from the. declared content \nof somatropin IP RS.\nStorage. Store in a sterile, airtight, tamper-proof container, at a \ntemperature of 2° to 8°.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the content of Somatropin \nin the container,, in milligrams; (2) the . composition and \nvolume of the liquid to be added for reconstitution; \n(3) the time within which the reconstituted solution shall be \nused and the storage conditions during this period; (4) the \nname and quantity of any excipient; (5) the storage temperature; \n(6) that the preparation shall not be shaken during \nreconstitution."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sorbic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sorbic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sorbic Acid\nh3c^^cooh\nC6H8O2 \nMol. Wt 112.1\nSorbic Acid is (2/?,4A)-hexa-2,4-dienoic acid. ) t; .y\nSorbic Acid contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C(;H8O2, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial preservative).\nIdentification-' •••• \n'■-\n*\nDescription. Awhite or almost white, crystalline powder.\nTest A may be omitted if tests B and C are carried out. Tests 5\nand C may be omitted if test A is carriedout. ! 1 '■ 1 :\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4-O- \nCompare the spectrum with:-that obtained - with’ sorbic \nIPRS or With the reference spectrum of sorbic acid: ’ “ ;, ; /\nB. Dissolve 50 mg in sufficient water to producc 250 nil ^rd \ndilute 2 ml of the solution to 200 ml with 0.1 M hydrochloric\nacid. \n■■ \n'.rodp\n3646\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSORBITAN. OLEATE\nIP2Q22................. _...................................................................\nexamined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), the\n1 * e solution shows an absorption maximum at about \n^^fedbancoatabout 264 nm,-.0.43.to 0.51.\nDissOIve0.2g in 2 ini of ethanol (95 per cent) and add \n0 7 mlj \nwater, the solution is decolorised."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sorbic Acid\nh3c^^cooh\nC6H8O2 \nMol. Wt 112.1\nSorbic Acid is (2/?,4A)-hexa-2,4-dienoic acid. ) t; .y\nSorbic Acid contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C(;H8O2, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial preservative).\nIdentification-' •••• \n'■-\n*\nDescription. Awhite or almost white, crystalline powder.\nTest A may be omitted if tests B and C are carried out. Tests 5\nand C may be omitted if test A is carriedout. ! 1 '■ 1 :\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4-O- \nCompare the spectrum with:-that obtained - with’ sorbic \nIPRS or With the reference spectrum of sorbic acid: ’ “ ;, ; /\nB. Dissolve 50 mg in sufficient water to producc 250 nil ^rd \ndilute 2 ml of the solution to 200 ml with 0.1 M hydrochloric\nacid. \n■■ \n'.rodp\n3646\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSORBITAN. OLEATE\nIP2Q22................. _...................................................................\nexamined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), the\n1 * e solution shows an absorption maximum at about \n^^fedbancoatabout 264 nm,-.0.43.to 0.51.\nDissOIve0.2g in 2 ini of ethanol (95 per cent) and add \n0 7 mlj \nwater, the solution is decolorised."},{"name":"Tests","text":"nearance of solution. A 5.0 per cent w/v solution in ethanol\nis clear (2.4.1), and colourless (2.4.1).\nHeavy metals. 12 ml of 5 per cent w/v solution in ethanol (95\ncomplies with the test for heavy metals, Method D\n(10 ppin), usiilg 5 rnl of lead standard solution (1ppm Pb) and \n5 mlof^buo/(95per cent). \n’\nAldehydes. Not more than ,0.15 per cent, determined by the \nfollowing mefliod. Dissolve 1.0 g in a mixture, of 50 ml of \n2-propanol and 3 0 ml of water, adj ust the. pH of the solution \nto 4.0with 0.1M hydrochloric acid or ,0.1 M sodium \nhydroxide and dilute to 100 ml with water. To 10. ml of the \nresulting solution add 1 ml of decolorised juchsin solution \nand allow to stand for 30 minutes. Any colour produced is not \nmore intense than that obtained in a solution prepared \nsimultaneously t»y adding 1 ml of decolorised fuchsin solution \nto a mixture of 1.5L ml of acetaldehyde standard solution \n(l0dppmC2HhO), 4 nil of 2-propanol and 4.5 ml of water.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.2 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined on\n2.0g.J' \n■\nAssay. Weigh 6.2 g, dissolve jn 20. ml of ethanol (95 per cent)\nand titrate with 0.1 M sodium hydroxide, using 0.2 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution as indicator, until a pink colour is\nproduced. Carry out a blank titration.\nI nil of 0d M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.01121 gof \nw. ■\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nMol. Wt. 428-6 \ncro/.\"\nS°rbitanQleate is /mixture usually obtained by esterification \nof 1 mole of sorbitol and its niono-and di- anhydrides per mole\nof oleic (c/s-9-octadecenoiç) acid. A suitable antioxidant may \nbe added.\nN()TE- -- Whe/i sorbitan mono-oleaté is'demanded, Sorbitan \nOleate shall be supplied. 1 \n■\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A brownish-yellow, viscous liquid.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent,\n^ter (2.3.43). Not more than 1.5 per cent, determined on\nUg. : : \n■ \nr .. \n, ;;\nAssay.De ter mi ne by liquid chromatography (2.4,14) as \nScribed under test for Related substances with the following\nModification.\nSORBITOL SOLUTION (70 PER CENT) (CRYSTALLISING)\nCalculate the content of C6H14O6. iu ; \n*. :\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. ...\nSorbitol intended for usé in the mcinufactureofparenteral \npreparations without a further appropriate procedure for\nremoval of bacterial endotoxins càmplies with thefollowing \nadditional requirement. \n' . \n\\ ;\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2,3). Not more than 4.0 Endotoxin Units \nper g for. parenteral preparations having a concentration of \nless than 10 per cent w/v of sorbitol and not more than 2.5 \nEndotoxin Units, per g for parenteral preparations containing\n10 per cent w/v or more of sorbitol. ,■\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nLabelling. The label states whether or hot the contents arc \nintended for use in: the manufacture of parenteral preparations.\nSorbitol Solution (70 Per cent) \n(Crystallising)\nSorbitol (70 per cent) (Crystallising)\nSorbitol Solution (70 per cent) (Crystallising) is an aqueous \nsolution of hydrogenated, partly hydrolysed starch,.\nSorbitol Solution (70 per cent) (Crystallising) contains not \nless than 68,0 per cent w/w and not more than 72.0 per cent \nw/w of anhydrous substances and not less than 92.0 per cent \nw/w and not more than 101.0 per cent w/w of D-glucitol, \nC6H14O6, calculated on the anhydrous basis. >'\nCategory, Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A clear, colourless, syrupy liquid, miscible with \nwater. .\nA. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nB. To 7.0 g, add 40 ml of water and 6.4 g of disodium \ntetraborate; Allow to stand for 1 hour, shake occasionally \nand dilute to 50.0 ml with water: The angle of rotation (2A22) \nis 0° to 4-1.5°. \n; \n: \n-\nC. It is clear syrupy liquid at 25°.\nAppearance of solution. A 14.0 per cent w/v solution in water\nis clear (2.4.1) and colourless (2.4.1). \n,\nConductivity (2,4.9). Not more than 10 pS cm !, measured on \nundiluted liquid sprbitol (crystallising), while gently stirring \nwith a magnetic stirrer.\n3649\nwww.webofpharma.com\nReducing sugars; Not more than 0.2 per cent, calculated as \nglucose equivalent c .p\nTo 5.0 g, add 6.0 ml of water, 20,0 ml o f cupri-citric solution \nand a few glass beads, Heat so that boiling begins after \n4 minutes and maintain boiling for 3 minutes. Cool rapidly and \nadd 1 ÒÓ.Ó ml of a 2.4 per cent v/v solution of glacial acetic \nacid and 20.0 ml of0.025 M iodine. With continuous shaking, \nadd 25.0 ml of à mixture of 6 volumesof hydrochloric acid and \n94 volumes of water and when the precipitate has 'dissolved, \ntitrate thé excess ofiodine with 0.05 Msodiüni thiosulphate \nusing1 nil of starch solution, added' towards the end of the \ntitration, as indicator. Not less than i 2:8 ml of 0.05 :Msodium \nthiosulphate is required. \nn. \nr.w • ■'r\nLead.Not more than 0.5 ppm. \n..............\nDetermine by ; atomic .absorption: spectrometry (2.4.2), \nMethod A.\nSolvent mixture. Equal volumes of dilute acetic acid and \nwater. ...... s ...... -,\nTest solution. Dissolve 100 g of the substance under \nexamination in 500 ml of the solvent mixture. To 100 ml of thé \nsolution add 2.0 ml of 1 per cent .w/y solution of ammonium \npyrrolidinedithio-carbamate and 10.0 ml of methyl isobutyl\nketone. sh'âkc for few seconds’protected from bright 'light. \nAllow the layers tó separate arid üsè The methyl isobutyl \nketone layer. . ,!: \n,, .■ \n■ ■ .¡..\nReference solution, (a): Dissolve 0.5 ml of lead standard \nsolution (10 ppm Pb) in T OO ml of the test solution. Add\n2.0 ml:of a.clear 1 per cent w/v solution of ammonium \npyrrolidinedithiocarbamateremd. 10.0 ml dimethyl isbbtityl\nketone, shake for few seconds and protected from .bright light. \nAllow the layers to separate and use the methyl isobutyl\nketone layer. '' i/; ■ \n: 1 ■ ■ ■ - \nw.:<w w-r/\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 1.0 ml of lead staridürd \nsolut"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Complies with the test for hydroxyl value (2.3.27).\nB. Complies with the test for iodine value (2.3.28).\nC. Complies with the test for composition of fatty acids. ■\nD. Margaric acidit^ot more than 0.2 per cent for oleic acid of \nvegetable origin and not more than 4.0 per cent for oleic acid \nof animal origin.\nRelative density (2.4.29). About 0.99.\nAcid value (2.3.23). Not more than 8.0, determined ori 5.0 g.\nHydroxyl value (2.3.27). 190 to 210.\nSaponification value (2.3.37). 145 to 160. Carry out the \nsaponification for 1 hour..\nIodine value (2.3.28). 62 to 76.\nPeroxide value (2.3.35). Not more than 10.0.\nComposition of fatty acids. Determine Fatty Acid Composition \nby gas chromatography (2.3.50, Method C).\nComposition of the fatty acid fraction of the substance:\n—- 'myristic acid: Not more than 5.0 per cent; \n■:\n— palmitic acid: Not more than 16.0 per cent; . .. \n— palmitoleic acid: Not more than 8.0 per cent; :; \n— stearic acid: Not more than 6.0 per cent; : \n— oleic acid: 65.0 per cent to 88.0 per cent;\n— linoleic acid: Not more than 18.0 per cent; : ; ; ; ■ \n— linolenic acid: Not more than 4.0 per cent; -? ■ \n— fatty acids with chain length greater than 675: Not\nmore than 4.0 percent. \n; \n....\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). In a silica crucible, mix thoroughly 2.0 g \nof the substance under examination with 0.5 g of magnesium \noxide. Ignite to dull redness until a homogeneous white or \ngreyish-white mass is obtained. - \n■\nIf after 30 min of ignition the mixture remains coloured,allow \nto cool, mix using a fine glass rod arid repeat thé ignition. !f \nnecessary repeat the operation. Heat at 800° for abdrif 1 hour. \nTake up the residue in 2 quantities, each of 5 ml, of a mixture of \nequal volumes of hydrochloric acid and. water. Add 0;l ml of\n3647\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSORBITOL : ;\nphenolphthalein solution and then concentrated ammonia\nuntil a pink colour is obtained. Cool, add glacial acetic acid \nuntil .the solution is decolourised and add 0.5 ml in excess. \nFilter if necessary and wash, the filter. Dilute to .20 ml with \nwater. 12 ml of resultant solution complies with the limit test \nfor heavy metals, Method D (10 ppm). Prepare thc standard \nusing 10.0 ml of lead standard solution (1 ppm Pb). .\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined on\nl-5g.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 1.5 per cent, determined on\n1.0g. \n■■■ \nA. ; \n•• ■ \n-\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nLabelling. . The label, states the. origin of the oleic acid used \n(animal or vegetable).\nSorbitol\nD-Glucitol .\n'• : \nr \nCH2OH - ■ .\nH-C-OH\nHO-C-H .\nH-C-OH\nH-C-OH \nrs ,\n........ . \n.... ' ch2oh .\nC6H14O6 \n■ :-y - P - u ■ ■ Mol. Wt. 182.2\nSorbitol is D-glucitol, ahexahydric alcoholrelated to glucose.\nSorbitol contains hot less than 98^0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C6HI4O6, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis. \n; •. . .\nCategory. Nutrient., (for parenteral administration); \npharmaceutical aid (sweetening agent; excipient).\nDescription. Awhitc, crystalline powder.\nA. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds tQ the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a). ' \n.,z..\nB. Dissolve 50 mg in 3 ml of water, add3ml of catechol solution \nand pour the.mixture into 6 ml of sulphuric acid; a pink colour \nis produced. \n......A.,.. \n;\nC. Dissolve 5 g in 3 ml of water with the aid=of gentle heat, \ncool, add 7 ml of methanol, 1 mlof benzaldehyde \n1 ml of\nIP 2022\nhydrochloric acid, mix and shake continuously fot 2 hours \nFliter, ■ dissolve the crystals in 20 ml of boilingsodium \nbicarbonate solution- and allow to crystallise. The résidu^ \nafter washing rapidly with 5 ml of a mixture pf equal volumes \nof methanol and water and drying in a current of air, melts at \nabout 175°(2.4.21). \n' ’■■■:UÎ y:\nTests . \n■\nAppearance of solution. Solution A is clear (2.4.1), \ncolourless(2.4.1).\nAcidity or alkalinity. Dissolve 5.0 g in sufficient carbon \ndioxide-free water prepared from distilled water to produce \n50 ml (solution A). To 10 ml of solution A add 10mlofcarbon \ndioxide-free water. To 10 ml of the resulting solution add \n0.05 ml ofphenolphthalein solution; hot more than 0.2 mlof \n0.01 M sodium hydroxide is required to change the colour of \nthe solution to pink. To a further 10 ml of the solution add \n0.05 ml of methyl red solution; Not more than 0:3 mlof 0.01 M \nhydrochloric acid is required to change thé colour of the \nsolution to red; \n; \n--f \n-\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). + 4.0° to T 7.0°, determined in \na solution prepared in the following manner. Dissolve a mixture \nof 5.0 g of the substance under examination and 6.4 g ofborax \nin 40 ml of water, allow to stand for 1 hour, shaking occasionally, \ndilute to 50 ml with water and filter, if necessary.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). w‘\nTest solution. Dissolve 5.0 g of the substance under \nexamination in 100.0 ml of water.\nReferencesoludon. (a). Dissolve 0,5 g of sorbitol IPRS in\n10.0mlofwater. \n. \n:\nReference solution ,(b). Dilute 2.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with water.\nReference solution (cf Dilute 5.0 ml of reference solution (b) \nto 100.0 ml with water.\nReference solution (d). Dissolve 0.5 g each of sorbitol and \nmannitol (sorbitol impurity A) in 10.0 ml of water. : .\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 30 cm x 7.8 mm, packed with\nstrong cation exchange resin (calcium form) (9 pm),\n- column temperature: 85°,\n- mobile phase: water,\n- flow rate: 0.5 ml per minute, ;\n- refractometer at a constant temperature, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nThé relative retention time with reference to sorbitol for ihaltitol\n(sorbitol impurity C) is about 0.6, for mannitol (sorbitol impunty \nA) is aboutO.8 andfor iditol (sorbitol impurity B) isabout 1.1\n*\n3648\nwww.webofpharma.com\nÎP.2Q22\n'ftct reference solution (d). The test is not valid unless the\n* iution between the peaks due to sorbitol and sorbitol \ntap°uriiyAi’'>etlessthan2(l'\nreference solut"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/sorbitan-oleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Sorbitan Oleate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Sorbitan Oleate\nH 0 \t\nOH\nC 1 7 Fl 33 )\nOH\nC24H4406 \t\nMol. Wt. 428.6\nSorbitan Oleate is mixture usually obtained by esterification \nof 1 mole of sorbitol and its mono-and di- anhydrides per mole \nof oleic (cis-9-octadecenoic) acid. A suitable antioxidant may \nbe added.\nNOTE-When sorbitan mono-oleate is demanded, Sorbitan\nOleate shall be supplied.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A brownish-yellow, viscous liquid."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Sorbitan Oleate\nH 0 \t\nOH\nC 1 7 Fl 33 )\nOH\nC24H4406 \t\nMol. Wt. 428.6\nSorbitan Oleate is mixture usually obtained by esterification \nof 1 mole of sorbitol and its mono-and di- anhydrides per mole \nof oleic (cis-9-octadecenoic) acid. A suitable antioxidant may \nbe added.\nNOTE-When sorbitan mono-oleate is demanded, Sorbitan\nOleate shall be supplied.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A brownish-yellow, viscous liquid."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Complies with the test for hydroxyl value (2.3.27).\nB. Complies with the test for iodine value (2.3.28).\nC. Complies with the test for composition of fatty acids.\nD. Margaric acid. Not more than 0.2 per cent for oleic acid of \nvegetable origin and not more than 4.0 per cent for oleic acid \nof animal origin.\n-\na stainless steel column 30 cm x 7.8 mm, packed with \nstrong cation exchange resin (calcium form) (9 um), \n-\ncolumn temperature. 85°, \n-\nmobile phase: water, \nflow rate: 0.5 ml per minute, \n-\nrefractometer at a constant temperature, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nA.  In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nof the substance under examination with 0.5 g of \nHeavy metals (2.3.13). In a silica crucible, mix thoroughl y 2 0g\n \nobtained 