.19). Not more than 0.5 percent, detennined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°. \n; ! < ; ;\nAssay. Dissolve 1.0g in 150 mlpianhydrous glacial acetic \nacid. Titrate with 0. / Mperchloric acid, using !-naphthol­\nhenzein solution as indicator. Carry put a blank titration,\n1 mlof 0.1 Mperchloric acid, is equivalent to 0.05636 g of\nC26H29NO,C6H8O7. \nX.- > 'j X \"\nStorage ; Store protected from light and moisture.\nTamoxifen Tablets\nTamoxifen Citrate Tablets \n,\nTamoxifen Tablets contain hot less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of tamoxifen, \nC26ll29NO. . . \n... \n... \n.\nUsual strengths: The equivalent of 10 mg, 20 mg and 40 mg of \ntamoxifen.1 ■. \n•.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket), a-.-.A ■\nMedium. 1000 ml of0.02M.hydrochloric acid,;; ;\nSpeed and time. 4 50 rpm and 45 minutes. . , ; q ; .\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter, dilute if \nneeessary.withthedissolulionm.edium.Measurc.the \nabsorbance ofthe resulting solution at the maximum at about \n275 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content of C^H^NO in the medium \ntaking 305 as the specific absorbance at 275 rim:: \n.\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of thè stated amount bf CasH^NO;\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2:4.44). wmvmfwo Mòj \nAMA M' ?\na5.;• aA.y V-.j \n¡- : ; A •\nNOTE - Prepare the'solutions immediately before use, \nprotectfromlighf.'' \"‘f f \"f ...\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of powdered tablets, \ncontaining 50 mg of tamoxifen in the mobile phase, with the \naid of ultrasound for 5 minutes and dilute to 50.0 ml with the \nmobile phase, centrifuge and filter. \nt\nReference.solution i(a).. A 0.0015 per cent w/v solution of\ntamoxifen citrate IPRS in the mobile phase. ■ : r j ; > ;\nReference solution '(b). A (1.15 per; cent w/v solution of\ntamoxifen citrate for performance test IPRS in the mobile \nphase. AtAxi \nrx} AMA\nReference solution (df. Dilute IÌ0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 20.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nChrornatographic system \n:;1; v' ' ' \n' ''''J '\n- a stainless steel column 25 dm x 4.6 him,’ packed with\noctadecylsilanebondedtoporoussilica (5 pm), A\n■. ; mobile phase: a mixture of 60 volumes of abuffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 0.9 g of sodium dihydrogen\n■ orthophosphate and 4:8 g of N,N~dimethyloctyj^ S\nin 1000 ml of water, adjusted to-pH 3;o w^\n.. ortho phosphoric acid, and 40 volumes of acetoniMt\n- flow rate: 1.2 ml per minute,.\n- spectrophotometer set at 240 nm, ■ . .. ’. '• V:.j' A\n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n.... i\nName . \n; \nRelative^\n___________ / ■, • J. ; ■■■■■: \n■. . retention ^ '\nTampxifen.E-isomer1 . \n.. . \n0.8. \n;\nTamoxifen impurity F* 2- , ; , ? \n/ \n,y. :0.9 ; , /■\nTamoxifen (retention time: about 20 minutes)^- ' liO-f -rr.:.;\n‘2-[4-[(E)-l,2-diphenylbut-l-en-l -yI]phenoky]-N,N-dimethylethanq) \namine((e)-isomcr), , ■ > - ; - \n. ... ' \n, \n;\n22-[4-[(Z)-l,2-diphen.ylbut-l,-en-.lLyl]phenoxy]-N-.methyIethan-f.\namine. \n.■...■.■■A/. ■ .-\nInject reference solution "}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tamsulosin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tamsulosin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tamsulosin Hydrochloride\n, Hel\nCzOHZ8NzOsS,HCL_..\n_ .._._\n_MoLWt.A45.0.\nTamsulosin Hydrochloride is (R)..5..(2 ([2\"(0\"ethoxyphenoxy)\nethyl]aminopropyl)-2-methoxybenzenesulfonamide\nhydrochloride.\nIP 2010\nTamsulosin Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per\ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of CzoHz8NzOsS,HCI,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. Awhite to offwhite powder.\nDose. 400 to 800 Jlg daily."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tamsulosin Hydrochloride\n, Hel\nCzOHZ8NzOsS,HCL_..\n_ .._._\n_MoLWt.A45.0.\nTamsulosin Hydrochloride is (R)..5..(2 ([2\"(0\"ethoxyphenoxy)\nethyl]aminopropyl)-2-methoxybenzenesulfonamide\nhydrochloride.\nIP 2010\nTamsulosin Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per\ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of CzoHz8NzOsS,HCI,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. Awhite to offwhite powder.\nDose. 400 to 800 Jlg daily."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tamsulosin\nhydrochloride RS or with the reference spectrum of\ntamsulosin hydrochloride.\nB. When examined in the range 200 nm to 350 urn (2.4.7), a\n0.001 per cent w/v solution in methanol shows absorption\nmaxima at about 279 and 225 urn.\nC. Dissolve with heating 0.75 g in water and dilute to 100 ml\nwith water. To 5 ml ofthe solution, cooled in an ice-bath add\n3 ml of dilute nitric acid and shake. Allow to stand at room\ntemperature for 30 minutes and filter. The filtrate gives reaction\nA ofchlorides (2.3.1).\nA. Ignite a few mg; it gradually decomposes giving off an\nodour resembling that of burnt sugar (distinction from citric\nacid).\n.\nB. A 10 per centw/v solution in distilledwater (solution A) is\nstrongly acidic.\nC. Gives reactions A and B oftartrates (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Specific optical rotation (2.4.22). -17.0° to-21.0°, determined\nin a 0.75 per cent w/v solution.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nA. Test solution. Dissolve 50.0 mg of the substance under\nexamination in 10 ml ofthe mobile phase.\nReference solution(a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml ofthis solution to\n10.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 4 mg of2-methoxy-5-[(2R)-\n2-[(2-(2-methoxyphenoxy)ethyljaminoJpropyljbenzenesul-\nphonamide RS (tamsulosin impurity A RS) and 4 mg of the\nsubstance under examination in the mobile phase and dilute\nto 20.0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 2.0 ml ofthis solution\nto 20.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve 4 mg of (2R)-N- [2-(2-\nethoxyphenoxy)ethyIJ-l-(4-methoxyphenyl)propan-2-amine\nRS (tamsulosin impurity B RS) and 4 mg of the substance\nunder examination in the mobile phase and dilute to 20.0 ml\nwith the mobile phase. Dilute 2.0 ml ofthis solution to 20.0 ml\nwith the mobile phase\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 rum, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 !!m),\n(il'liiinn\"temp'eiilfiiTe'.···40d~·\n..-.-.-.--.-.,----\"--,-\",','-,-',',-~,,',',\"-,,---,-'_ ._._-\nmobile· phase: a mixture of 600 ml of acetiiizifrile arid\n1400 ml of a solution prepared by dissolving 3.0 g of\nsodium hydroxide in a mixture of 8.7 ml ofperchloric\n2184\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nacid and 1900 ml ofwater, adjusting the pH to 2.0 with\n0.5 M sodium hydroxide and diluting to 2000 ml with\nwater,\n-\nflow rate. 1.3 ml per minute,\n-\nspectrophotometer set at 225 nm, .\n-\ninjection volume. 10 Ill.\nInject the test solution and reference solutions (a) and (b) and\nrecord the chromatograms for 1.5 times the retention time of\ntamsulosin (retention time = about 6 minutes). In the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b), the\nresolution between the peaks due to tamsu10sin impurity A\nand tamsulosin is not less than 6.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, for each\nunspecified impurity eluting before tamsuIosin, the area of\nthe peak is not more than the area ofthe principal peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per\ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 times the area\nof the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with\nreference solution (a) (0.05 per cent).\nB. Repeat the chromatographic procedure with the following\nmodifications.\n-\nmobile phase: dissolve 3.0 g ofsodium hydroxide in a\nmixture of8.7 ml ofperch10ric acidand 900 ml ofwater,\nadjust the pH to 2.0 with 0.5 M sodium hydroxide and\ndilute to 2000 ml with water; add 2000 ml ofacetonitrile,\n-\nflow rate. 1mlper minute,\nInject the test solution and reference solutions (a) and (c).\nRun the chromatograms for 5 times the retention time of\ntamsulosin (retention time = 2.5 minutes). In the chromatogram\nobtained with reference solution (c), the resolution between\nthe peaks due to tamsulosin and tamsuIosin impurity B is not\nless than 2.\n.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, for each\nunspecified impurity eluting after tamsulosin, the area ofany\npeak is not more than the area of the principal peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per\ncent). The sum of the areas of the impurities eluting before\ntamsulosin in test A and after tamsulosin in test B is not more\nthan twice the area ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent). Ignore any\npeak with an area less than 0.5 times the area ofthe principal\npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution\n(a) (0.05 per cent).\nS-isomer. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.05 g of the substance under\nexamination in methanol and add sufficient methanol to\nproduce 25.0 ml.\nReference solution (aj. Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with methanol. Dilute 1.0 m1 ofthis solution to 10.0 ml\nwith methanol.\n2185\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTARTARIC ACID\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 5.0 mg of tamsulosin\nracemate RS in methanol and dilute to 25.0 ml with methanol.\nDilute 2.0 ml ofthis solution to 10.0 ml with methanol.\nChromatographic system\nastainless steel column 25 cm x.4.6 mm packed with\nsilica gel OD for chiral separations (Such as Chiralpak\nAD-H) (5 Ilm),\n-\ncolumn temperature. 40°,\n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 1 volume of diethylamine,\n150 volumes of methanol, 200 volumes of anhydrous\nethanol and 650 volumes of hexane,\n-\nflow rate. 0.5 mlperminute,\n-\nspectrophotometer set at 225 nm,\n-\ninjection volume. 10 Ill.\nInject reference solution (b). Record the chromatogram for\nabout 20 minutes. The resolution between tamsulosin and\nimpurity C (5-[(2S)-2-[[2-(2-ethoxyphenoxy) ethyl]amino]\npropyl]-2-methoxybenzenesulphonamide) is not less than 2.\nThe relative retention with reference to tamsulosin (retention\ntime = about 14 minutes) oftamsulosin impurity C is about 0.8.\nInject the test solution and reference solution (a). In the\nchromatogram obtained, the area ofthe peak obtained due to\ntamsulosin impurity C is not more than the area ofthe principal\npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution\n(a) (0.1 percent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for\nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 0.5 per cent, determined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 2 hours.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.35 g and dissolve in 5.0 ml of\nanhydrousformic acid, add 75 ml ofa mixture of2vo1umes of\nacetic anhydride and 3 volumes ofglacial acetic acid Titrate\nimmediately with 0.1 Mperchloric acid, determining the end-\npointpotentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.0445 g of\nCzoHzsNzOsS,HCl.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nTartaric Acid\nL-Tartaric Acid\nH\nOH\n,,'\nHOOC/\\ /COOH.\nH;<'OH\nC4H60 6"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tartaric-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tartaric Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tartaric Acid\nC ¿.Tartaric Acid\n- H OH ,\n: h 0H\nHOOC-V000«\nC,HA \nMol. Wt. 150.1\nTartaric Acid is (27?,37?)-2,3-dihydroxybutanedioic acid.\nTartaric Acid contains not less than 99.5 per cent and not \nfliore than 1'01.0 per cent of C4H6O6, calculated on the dried \nbasis>-:; \n.:••••. \n■\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (buffering agent).\nDescription, Colourless crystals or a white or almost white \n'crystalline powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tartaric Acid\nC ¿.Tartaric Acid\n- H OH ,\n: h 0H\nHOOC-V000«\nC,HA \nMol. Wt. 150.1\nTartaric Acid is (27?,37?)-2,3-dihydroxybutanedioic acid.\nTartaric Acid contains not less than 99.5 per cent and not \nfliore than 1'01.0 per cent of C4H6O6, calculated on the dried \nbasis>-:; \n.:••••. \n■\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (buffering agent).\nDescription, Colourless crystals or a white or almost white \n'crystalline powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A.lgniteafewmg; it gradually decomposes giving off an \nodour resembling that of burnt sugar (distinction from citric\nacid).?\nB. A10 per cent w/v solution in distilled water (solution A) is \nstrongly acidic (2.4.46).\nC. If gives reactions (A) and (B) of tartrates (2.3.1).\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). +12.0° to +12.8°, determined \nin a 20.0 per cent w/v solution.\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 5.0 g in 50 ml of water and add 10 ml \nof stannated hydrochloric acid AsT. The resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for arsenic (2 ppm).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 2.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy-metals, Method B (10 ppm).\nChlorides (2.3.12). 20 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for chlorides (125 ppm)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. Solution A is clear (2.4.1), and not \nmore intensely coloured than reference solution YS6 (2.4.1).\nSulphates (2.3.17). 10 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for sulphates (150 ppm).\nOxalate. ^Neutralise 10 ml of solution A with dilute ammonia \nsolution, add 0.1 ml of dilute acetic acid and 10 ml of calcium \nsulphate solution', no opalescence is produced within \n20minutes.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent.\nCoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more thari0.2 per cent, determined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nTAUROURSODEOXYCHOLIC ACID\nAssay. Dissolve 1.0 g in 25 ml of water and titrate with 1 M \nsodium hydroxide using 0.5 ml of phenolphthalein solution \nas indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.07505 g of \nC4H6O6.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTauroursodeoxycholic Acid\nC26H45NO6.S \nMol Wt. 499.7\nTauroursodeoxycholic Acid is 3a, 7 p-dihydroxy-5 P - \ncholanoyltaurine.\nTauroursodeoxycholic Acid contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C26H45 NO6S, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Use to dissolve cholesterol gallstones.\nDescription. A white to off white powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with \ntauroursodeoxycholic acid IPRS or with the reference \nspectrum of tauroursodeoxycholic acid.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). + 42° to + 48°, determined \non 1.0 per cent w/v solution in ethanol at 20°. \n;\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n- y 'r../. \n.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 25.0 ml with the \nmobilephase. \n; \n;\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \ntauroursodeoxycholic acid IPRS in the mobile phase.\n3719\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTAUROURSODEQXYCHQLIC ACID\nChromatographic system \n!\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded? to. porous silica (5 pm),\n~ column temperature: 40°, \n;\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 30 volumes of a solution\nprepared by dissolving 0.69 g of sodium dihydrogen \northophosphate into 1000 ml of water, 40 volumes of \nmethanol, 30 volumes of acetonitrile and 0.1 volume \nof triethylamine, adjusted to pH 3.0 with ortho - \nphosphoric acid,\n- flowrate: 1.2 ml per minute,\n- reflective index detector set at a constant temperature,\n- injection volume: 100 pl.\nInject the reference solution: The test, is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates n \nand the tailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nInject the test solution. The sum of areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 3.0 per cent, calculated by area \nnormalization. \n’ v, .' \n.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy rpetals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2,3.18). Not more than 0.5 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 8.0 per cent,; determined on \n0-5g. \n.....: ;\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). : \n. . .\nTest solution. Dissolved mg of the substance under \nexamination, in the mobile phase and.diluteto 25.0 ml with: the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of\ntauroursodeoxycholic acid IPRS in the mobile phase.; ?\nChromatographic system , .\nL,. - a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n; octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- column temperature: 40°, \n; ;\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 30 volumes of a solution\nprepared by dissolving 0.69 g frf sodium' dihydrogen \northophosphate into 1000 ini of water, 40 volumes of \nmethanol, 30 voluihesacetonitrile and' 0.1 volume \nof triethylamine, adjusted to pH 3.0 with ortho- \nphosphoric acid,\n“■flowrate: 1.2 mlper minute,\n- refractive index detector set at a constant temperature,\n. injection volume: 100 pl;.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unlessthe \ncolumn efficiency is not less, than 1500 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor :is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per pent.\nInject .the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculalc the content of CUU NO6iS. . .. m \n■ 2 ,\n3720"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tazobactam-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tazobactam Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tazobactam Sodium\nCkjHj 1N4NaOsS \nMol. Wt. 322.3\nTazobactam Sodium is Sodium (2S,3 S,5R)-3-methyl-7-oxo-3T \n(1H- 1,2,3-triazol-1 -ylmethyl)-4-thia-1 -azabicyclp[3.2.0] \nheptane-2-carboxylicacid,4,4-dioxide. \n. \n.\nTazobactam Sodium contains not less than 98.0, per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of .CioHnN4Na05S, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis. ’\nCategory. Beta-lactamase inhibitor.\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline hygroscopic \npowder. \n,"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tazobactam Sodium\nCkjHj 1N4NaOsS \nMol. Wt. 322.3\nTazobactam Sodium is Sodium (2S,3 S,5R)-3-methyl-7-oxo-3T \n(1H- 1,2,3-triazol-1 -ylmethyl)-4-thia-1 -azabicyclp[3.2.0] \nheptane-2-carboxylicacid,4,4-dioxide. \n. \n.\nTazobactam Sodium contains not less than 98.0, per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of .CioHnN4Na05S, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis. ’\nCategory. Beta-lactamase inhibitor.\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline hygroscopic \npowder. \n,"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tazobactam \nsodium IPRS or with the reference spectrum of tazobactam \nsodium;\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nC. Gives the reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\n3721\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTAZOBACTAM SODIUM\nwhenexamined in the range 200 nm to 400 nm (2.4.7), a \n0 005 per cent w/v solution in water shows an absorption \njjjaximum at about 278 nm.\ng In the composition and related substances, The principal \npeaks (teicoplanins A3.t, A2.i, A2.2, A2.3, A2.4 and A2.5) in the \n¿hrhnritogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthepnncip3^ peak .in the chromatogram obtained with the \nreference solution. \n■'\nf\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6) B \nCompare: the spectrum with that obtained with telmisartan \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of telmisartan. ; |\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of phosphate buffer pH 7.5, prepared by \ndissolving 13.61 g of potassium dihydrogen phosphate in \nabout 800 ml of water, adjusted to pH 7.5 with 2M sodium \nhydroxide, and diluted with water to 1000 ml, ;\nSpeed and time. 75 rpm for 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable voltime of the medium and filter. Reject \nthe first few ml of the filtrate and dilute a suitable volume of\nthe filtrate with dissolution medium. Measure the absorbance \nof the resulting solution at the maximum at about296 nm (2.4.7). \nCalculate the content of C33H30N4O2 in the medium from the \nabsorbance obtained from a solution prepared by dissolving \n44 mg of teimisartan IPRS in 1 ml of 0. IM sodium hydroxide, \nand dilute to 100 ml with methanol. Further dilute the solution \nwith dissolution medium to obtain a solution with a final \nconcentration of 0.0011 per cent of teimisartan.\nQ. Not. less, than 75 per cent of the stated amount of\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 80 volumes of buffer solution prepared by \ndiluting 5.0 ml Of triethylamine to 2000 ml with water and 20 \nvolumes of methanol.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof powder containing 100 mg of Teimisartan in 100:0 ml of \nsolvent mixture with the aid of ultrasound for 45 minutes and \nfilter.\nReference solution. A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of\nteimisartan IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5-pm) (Such \nas Inertsil ODS-3), \n. <\n- mobile phase: A. dissolve 0.5 g of potassium\ndihydrogen phosphate in 1000 ml of water, add 2 ml of \ntriethylamine, adjusted to pH 3 ;2 with orthophosphoric\n.. acid, • • J '<'</■■\nB: acetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1.8 ml per minute;\n- spectrophotometer set at 298 nm,\n- injectionvolume:20pl.\n3727\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n■ 6 . ■,\n80. ■ . ,\n.• -/ \n20 \n.\n7.\n70\n30\n15\n60\n40\n25 .\n60\n40\n26\n40\n60\n35. .\n'■ ’ï æ '\n^40\n.78'.\n22 \n> .\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntheoretical plates is not less than 3000, tailing' factor is not \nmore than 2.0 and relative standard; deviation for. replicate\ninjections is not morc than 5.0 per cent. \n- .\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(0.5 per cent), the sum of the areas of all the secondary peaks \nis hot more than 4 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (2.0 per cent). \nIgnore any peak with atiaf ea less than 0.1 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution(0.05 percent). \n:-\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets;\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture, 80 volumes of buffer solution prepared by \ndiluting 5.0 ml of triethylamine to 2000 ml with water, and 20 \nvolumes of methanol.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof powder containing 40 mg of Telmisartan in 100 ml of the \nsolvent mixture and sonicate for 45 minutes. Dilute 5.0 ml of \nthe solution to 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution..A; Q.004 per cent w/v solution of \ntelmisartan IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- \n¡a stainless;steel.column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed