he test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peak due to teicoplanin A2.4 and \nteicoplanin A2.5 is not less than 1.0.\nInject the test solution.\nCalculate the percentage content ratio of the different \ncomponents using the following equations\nS \nTeicoplanin A? group ~ \n:5-------- x 100\nSa +0.83xSb +SC ..\n0 83 x S \nTeicoplanin A, group = .......-...‘...........   x 100\nr 3 \nSa+0.83xSb+SC\nS \nImpurities = ------------- 5------------ x 100\n. \n- \nSa + 0.83 xSb + Sc\nWhere,.Sa = sum of the. areas of the. peaks due to\nteicoplanin A2 group in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution;\nSb = sum of the areas of the peaks due to\nteicoplanin A3 group in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution;\n= sum of the areas Of the peaks with a relative\nretention more than L25; \n,\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTELMISARTAN '\nThe area of peaks corresponding to teicoplanin A2 group is \nnot less than 75.0 per cent, teicoplanin A3 group is not more \nthan 20.0 per cent and the sum of the areas of all other \nsecondary peaks with a relative retention time more than 1.25 \nis hot more than 5.0 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 5.0 per cent, determined on \no.5g. ■ \n’\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.30 Endotoxin \nunit per mg of teicoplanin.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complie"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tazobactam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tazobactam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tazobactam\nC10H!2N4O5S . \nMol. Wt. 300.3\nClrtH12N;O5S,‘/2H2O ’ \nMoI.Wt.309T\nTazobactam is (2S,35,5/U-3-mcthyh7-oxo-3-(1//-l,2,3-triazok\nl-ylmethyl)-4“thia-l-azabicyclo[3.2.0]heptane-2-carboxylic \nacid,4,4-dioxide hemihydrate. \n. \n...\nTazobactam contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C|0Hi2N4OsS, calculated?bh\"the \nanhydrous basis. \n''•V.”.\";’. :\nCategory. Beta-lactamase inhibitor. \n■ ■F ' 4\nDescription. A white to pale;yellow, non-hygroscopic, \ncrystalline powder. ."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tazobactam\nC10H!2N4O5S . \nMol. Wt. 300.3\nClrtH12N;O5S,‘/2H2O ’ \nMoI.Wt.309T\nTazobactam is (2S,35,5/U-3-mcthyh7-oxo-3-(1//-l,2,3-triazok\nl-ylmethyl)-4“thia-l-azabicyclo[3.2.0]heptane-2-carboxylic \nacid,4,4-dioxide hemihydrate. \n. \n...\nTazobactam contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C|0Hi2N4OsS, calculated?bh\"the \nanhydrous basis. \n''•V.”.\";’. :\nCategory. Beta-lactamase inhibitor. \n■ ■F ' 4\nDescription. A white to pale;yellow, non-hygroscopic, \ncrystalline powder. ."},{"name":"Identification","text":"y\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry' (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum; with that .obtained with, tazobactam \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of tazobactam.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds, to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with, the reference; solution. . . \n;\nTests ■ ■■'■■■■ ■ ■ ' \n■' C'A.i-.? ■■ ruk y ;\npH, (2.4.24). 1.8 to 2.8, determined in a 0.25 per cent w/v solution\nin water. ’ , \n' \n\"\"f\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22).-+160.0° to+167®, determined \nat 20° in a 1.0 per cent w/v solution in dimethylformamide.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid- chromatography \n(2.4.14). . r . , .. \n. ; \n. , . .\nNOTE—Use freshly prepared solutions^ store anddnject al \n3°. \n.. \n.. ■ .. \n: \n; ■;\nTest solution. Use the test solution as given under Assay.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.0016-per cent w/v \nof L-phenylalaninei, 0.0050 per cent w/v of tazobactam 'IPRS \nand 0.0008 per cent w/v of tazobactam- related compound A \nIPRS [(2S, 3S)-2-amino-3-methyl-3-sul[)hino-4-(iII-l,2f-\ntriazol-l-yl)butyric acid]yn mobile phase. ; ... r\nUse the chromatographic conditions as described under\nAssay. \ni:;,?....-. ■.? vc y\nwww.webofpharma.com\nfl>2022\nTAZOBACTAM SODIUM\n„ tect the reference solution. The relative retention time with Sterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility,\nreference to tazobactam for tazobactam- related compound A \nis about 0.3 and for /.-phenylalanine is about 0.7.\nThetest is not valid unless the resolution between tazobactam \nand ¿-phenylalanine peaks is not less than 6.0.\nthe test solution. The area of any peak corresponding \nto tazobactam- related compound A is not more than 1.0 per \ncent.The area of any other, secondary peak is not more than \n01 ij^iìèent and the sum areas of .ali the secondary peaks \nother than tazobactam- related compound A is hot more than \n0/£per cent, calculated by area normalization.\nflehyy ntetals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy métals, Method B(20ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.6 per cent for anhydrous form \nand between 2.2 per cent to 3.8 per cent for hemihydrate form.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). The total viable aerobic count \ndoes notexceed TO3 cfu per g and the total combined moulds \nand yeasts count does not exceed 102 cfu per g.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nNOTE— iUse freshly prepared solutions, store and inject at \n3°, using a cooled autosampler.\nTési solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 100.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution. A 0.05 per cent w/v solution of tazobactam \nIPRS'm the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n¡r; a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadccylsiiane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n. A . sample temperature: 2° to 4°,\n~: mobile phase: a mixture of a buffer solution prepared by\n- dissolving E32 g of dibasic ammonium phosphate in\nabout 750 ml of water, adjusted to pH 2.5 with 5 per cent\n' y/v. solution of orthophosphoric acid, dilute to 1000 ml\nwith water and 3 0 ml of acetonitrile,\n- .flowrate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n— r .injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 1,8 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\n^niecf th,e reference solution and the test solution. ..\nCalculate the content of.CioH^N^S. \n\\ \n;\nTazobactam intended for use in the manufacture ofparenteral\nPreparations without a further appropriate sterilisation \nP^acedurewcomplies with, the following additional\nrequirement,\nTazobactam intended for use in the manufacture ofparenteral \npreparations without a further appropriate procedure for\nthe removal of bacterial endotoxins complies with the \nfollowing additional requirement.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.08 Endotoxin \nUnit per mg of tazobactam.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, preferably at \ntemperature below 30°.\nLabelling. The label states whether the material is anhydrous \nor hemihydrates form."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/teicoplanin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Teicoplanin Injection","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Teicoplanin Injection\nThe area of any secondary peak corresponding to teicoplanin\nTeicoplanin Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nTeicoplanin with or without auxiliary substances. It is filled in \na sealed container.\nA2 group is not less than 80.0 per cent; teicoplanin A2.2 group \nis not less than 35.0 per cent and not more than 55.0 per cent. \nTeicoplanin A2.1 group is not more than 20.0 per cent, \nteicoplanin teicoplanin A2.3 group is not more than 20.0 per \ncent, teicoplanin A24 group is not more than 20.0 per cent, \nteicoplanin teicoplanin A2.5 group is not more than 20.0 per \ncent and teicoplanin A3 group is not more than 15.0 per cent. \nThe sum of the areas of all other secondary peak other than \nmesityl oxide with a relative retention time 1.25 times is not \nmore than 5.0 per cent. Ignore the peak due to teicoplanin\n1.25. \nCalculate the content of the different components using the \nfollowing equations.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of Sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nteicoplanin A 2 group = \t\nx 100 \nS. \nS. + 0.83 x S„ + S,\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nthe Clarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nS. + 0.83 x Si, + S, \n\t\nx100 \nS 2\nteicoplanin A 2_,\nA2-2 with an area less than 0.5 times of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (b) (0.25 \nper cent).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nSi \n\tx 100 \nteicoplanin A,_, group = S. + 0.83 \nS b\nImpurity A. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTeicoplanin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nTeicoplanin.\nUse the Chromatographic system as described under test for \nComposition and related substances.\nteicoplanin A 23 group = \nS3 \n\tX 100\nS a + 0.83 x S, + S c\nName\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations (Powders\nRelative \nretention time\nS4 \n\t\nX 100 \nteicoplanin A 2_4\nSa + 0.83 x St, + Sc\nfor Injection) and with the following requirements.\nTeicoplann impurity A' \t\n.0.6 \nTeicoplann A2_2 (Retention time: about 18 minutes) 1.0-\nU.suil..Strerigths. 200 mg; 400 mg.\nteicoplanin A 2_, group = \t\n \nS5 \t\nx 100 \nS. + 0.83 x St, + Sc\nDesCription. A white or off white to yellowish powder.\n'4-methylpent-3-en-2-one  (mesityl oxide).\n- \t\n\"\n33-17\nTEICOPLANIN INJECTION"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Teicoplanin Injection\nThe area of any secondary peak corresponding to teicoplanin\nTeicoplanin Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nTeicoplanin with or without auxiliary substances. It is filled in \na sealed container.\nA2 group is not less than 80.0 per cent; teicoplanin A2.2 group \nis not less than 35.0 per cent and not more than 55.0 per cent. \nTeicoplanin A2.1 group is not more than 20.0 per cent, \nteicoplanin teicoplanin A2.3 group is not more than 20.0 per \ncent, teicoplanin A24 group is not more than 20.0 per cent, \nteicoplanin teicoplanin A2.5 group is not more than 20.0 per \ncent and teicoplanin A3 group is not more than 15.0 per cent. \nThe sum of the areas of all other secondary peak other than \nmesityl oxide with a relative retention time 1.25 times is not \nmore than 5.0 per cent. Ignore the peak due to teicoplanin\n1.25. \nCalculate the content of the different components using the \nfollowing equations.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of Sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nteicoplanin A 2 group = \t\nx 100 \nS. \nS. + 0.83 x S„ + S,\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nthe Clarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nS. + 0.83 x Si, + S, \n\t\nx100 \nS 2\nteicoplanin A 2_,\nA2-2 with an area less than 0.5 times of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (b) (0.25 \nper cent).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nSi \n\tx 100 \nteicoplanin A,_, group = S. + 0.83 \nS b\nImpurity A. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTeicoplanin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nTeicoplanin.\nUse the Chromatographic system as described under test for \nComposition and related substances.\nteicoplanin A 23 group = \nS3 \n\tX 100\nS a + 0.83 x S, + S c\nName\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations (Powders\nRelative \nretention time\nS4 \n\t\nX 100 \nteicoplanin A 2_4\nSa + 0.83 x St, + Sc\nfor Injection) and with the following requirements.\nTeicoplann impurity A' \t\n.0.6 \nTeicoplann A2_2 (Retention time: about 18 minutes) 1.0-\nU.suil..Strerigths. 200 mg; 400 mg.\nteicoplanin A 2_, group = \t\n \nS5 \t\nx 100 \nS. + 0.83 x St, + Sc\nDesCription. A white or off white to yellowish powder.\n'4-methylpent-3-en-2-one  (mesityl oxide).\n- \t\n\"\n33-17\nTEICOPLANIN INJECTION"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. When examined in the range 200 nm to 400 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.005 per cent w/v solution in water shows an absorption \nmaximum at about 278 nm.\nB. In the composition and related substances, The principal \npeaks (teicoplanins A3.1, A2.1, A2.2, A2.3, A2_4 and A2.5) in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the \nreference solution.\nsecondary peaks with a relative retention time more than 1.25 \nis not more than 5.0 per cent.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with  telmisartan RS \nor with the reference spectrum of telmisartan.\n<0.70\nTeicoplanin A3 group\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 5.0 per cent, determined on\n>  0.70 and \n1.25 and \nwithin this group \n< 1.00\nTeicoplanin A2\n0.5 g. \nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.30 Endotoxin\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntheoretical plates is not less than 3000, the tailing factor is not \nmore than 2.0 and the relative standard deviation for replicate \ninjections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v) \n78 \n22\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtai n\n0\n with the test solution coresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.  tAglig\n80 \n20 \n70 \n30 \n60 \n40 \n60 \n40 \n40 \n60 \n20 \n80\n6\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\n7\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c).\nD. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amom i.\nIn the Assay, the principal peaks in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the principal peaks in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (c).\nReference solution (a).  A 0.008 per cent w/v solution of \ntelmisartan RS in solvent mixture.\nTime \n(in min.)\nMobile phase A\nhydrochlorothiazide peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (c) (2.0 per cent).\nMobile phase B\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with temozolomide \nRS or with the reference spectrum of temozolomide. \nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n° \n85 \n15 \n3.5 \n85 \n15 \n3.51 \n45 \n55 \n7.7 \n45 \n55 \n7.71 \n20 \n80 \n12.0 \n20 \n80 \n12.1 \n85 \n15 \n15.5 \n85 \n15 \nInject reference solution (c). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to hydrochlorothiazide and \nbenzothiadiazine impurity A is not less than 2.0 and the relative \nstandard deviation for replicate injections for both peaks of \ntelmisartan and hydrochlorothiazide is not more than 2.0 per \ncent.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tab4s..\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 0.005 M methanolic solution of sodium\nhydroxide."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 6.0 to 8.0, determined in 5.0 per cent w/v solution \nin the carbon dioxide free  water.\nComposition and Related Substances. Determine by liquid \nchromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution.  Weigh a quantity of injection powder containing \n400 mg of Teicoplanin, disperse in sufficient water with the \naid of ultrasound and dilute to 200.0 ml with water.\nReference solution. A 0.2 per cent w/v solution of teicoplanin \nRS in water.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed  with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 p.m) (Such \nas Inertsil ODS 3V), \n-\nmobile phase: A. a mixture of 850 volumes of a solution \nprepared by dissolving 3.9 g of  sodium dihydrogen \northophosphate in 850 ml of water and 150 volumes of \nacetonitrile, adjusted to pH 6.0 with sodium hydroxide\nsolution.\nB. a mixture of 300 volumes of a solution \nprepared by dissolving 3.9 g of sodium dihydrogen \northophosphate in 300 ml of water and 700 volumes of \nacetonitrile, adjusted to pH 6.0 with sodium hydroxide \nsolution. \n-\na gradient programme using the conditions given below,\nflow rate: 1.8 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nMobile phase B\nMobile phase A\nTime \n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\nl00\n0\n30\n70\n19\n50\n50\n22\n0\n100\n24\n30 \t 100 \t\n0\nRelative retention of groups and impurities witliYefereitce to 7: -The_ifea of Oaks corresponding to teicoplanin A, group is \n. \nteicoplanin A2.2 is. \nnot less than 75.0 per cent, teicoplanin  A3 group is not more\n0; 201 8 \nTELMISARTAN\nName \t\nRelative -- \nretention time\nthan 20.0 per cent and the sum of the areas of all other\nTeicoplanin A2.1 group\nunit per mg of teicoplan in.\n1.00 \n> 1.00 and<  1.12 \nabout 1.12\nTeicoplanin  A2.2 \nTeicoplanin A2.3 group\nAppearance of solution. A 5 per cent w/v solution in I M \nsodium hydroxide  is not more intensely coloured than \nreference solution YS4 (2.4.1).\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nTeicoplanin A2-4 \nTeicoplanin A2.5 group\nAssay. Determine by the microbiological assay of antibiotics, \nMethod A (2.2.10) on a solution prepared in the following\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\n>1.12and<1.25\n> 1.25\nCalculate the content of C 33H30N402 in the tablets.\n15\n-I 9 t \nDissolution (2.5.2).\n25\nApparatus No. 1, \t\n,•;! \nMedium. 900 ml of phosphate huger pH 7.5, prepared by \ndissolving 13.61 g of potassium dihydrogen phosphate in \nabout 800 ml of water, adjusted to pH 7.5 with 2M sodium \nhydroxide, and diluted with water to 1000 ml, \nSpeed and time. 75 rpm for 30 minutes.\n26\nTelmisartan and Amlodipine Tablets\n35\nc\n78 \n22\n40\nTelmisartan and Amlodipine Besylate Tablets; \nTelmisartan and Amlodipine Besilate Tablets\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntheoretical plates is not less than 3000, tailing factor is not \nmore than 2.0 and relative standard deviation for replicate \ninjections is not more than 5.0 per cent.\nTelmisartan and Amlodipine Tablets contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of telmisartan, C 33H 30N402  and amlodipine, \nC 20 H2 5 C1N 2 05 .\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Reject \nthe first few ml of the filtrate and dilute a suitable volume of \nthe filtrate with dissolution medium. Measure the absorbance \nof the resulting solution at the maximum at about 296 nm (2.4.7). \nCalculate the content of C 33H30N402 in the medium from the \nabsorbance obtained from a solution prepared by dissolving \n44 mg of telmisartan RS in 1 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide, \nand dilute to 100 ml with methanol. Further dilute this solution \nwith dissolution medium to obtain a solution with a final \nconcentration of 0.0011 per cent of telmisartan.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(0.5 per cent), the sum of the areas of all the secondary peaks \nis not more than 4 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (2.0 per cent). \nIgnore any peak with an area less than 0.1 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution (0.05 per cent).\nUsual strengths. Telmisartan, 40 mg and amlodipine, 5 mg ., \nTelmisartan, 80 mg and amlodipine, 10 mg.\nC3H30N402•\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nDissolution (2.5.2). \nApparatus No. 1, \nMedium. 500 ml of 0.01 M hydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 75 rpm and 45 minutes.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 80 volumes of buffer solution prepared by \ndiluting 5.0 ml of triethylamine to 2000 ml with water and 20 \nvolumes of methanol. \n4 \nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof powder containing 100 mg of Telmisartan in 100.0 ml of \nsolvent mixture with the aid of ultrasound for 45 minutes and \nfilter.\nSolvent mixture. 80 volumes of buffer solution prepared by \ndiluting 5.0 ml of triethylamine to 2000 ml with water and 20 \nvolumes of methanol.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter, rejecting \nthe first few ml of the filtrate.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof powder containing 40 mg of Telmisartan in 100 ml of the \nsolvent mixture and sonicate for 45 minutes. Dilute 5.0 ml of \nthis solution to 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Use the filtrate, dilute if necessary,with the \ndissolution medium.\nReference solution. A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of \ntelmisartan RS in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution. A 0.004 per cent w/v solution of \ntelmisartan RS in the solvent mixture.\nReferenc