lution (a) and the test solution. Run. the \nchromatogram 3.2 times the retention time of the principal \npeak. The area of any péàk due to 2-azahypoxanthine is not \nmore than twice the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (à) (0.2 per \ncent), the area of any peak due to temozolomide related \ncompound A is not more than 5 times the area of principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0.5 per cent), the area of any peak corresponding to \ntemozolomide acid and aminoimidazolecârboxàmidë, each of, \nis not more than the area of the principal peak, in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solutipn.(a),(0.1 per \ncent), the area of any peak corresponding to cyanptemozol \nomide is not more than 1.5 times the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.15 \nper cent), the area of any other secondary peak is hot more \nthan the area of the principal peak;in. the = chromatogram\nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 pencent) and the \nsum of the areas . of all the secondary peaks is not more than 8 \ntimes the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.8 per cent)..\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 0.67 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (30 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.4 per cent, using method 3.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as\ndescribed under Related substances using following \nmodifications.\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \ntemozolomidè IPRS in dimethylsulphoxide.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 1.9 and the relative standard \ndeviation of replicate injections is not more than 1.5.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C6H6N6O2.\nStorage. Store protectedfrom light and moisture.\nTemozolomide Capsules\nTemozolomide Capsules/contain not less Than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent bf the stated amount of \ntemozolomide, C6H6N6O2.\nCAUTION—Temozolomide iscytotoxic; extra care required \nto prevent inhaling particles and exposing the skin to it.\nUsual strengths. 20 mg; 100 mg; 250 mg. \n< :\n3733\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTEMOZOLOMIDE CAPSULES\nIn the Assay, .the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test, solution corresponds to. the, peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution.\nTests ,, .. \n. \n.?\nOther tests. Comply with tests stated under capsules.\nAssay. Determine by liquid; chromatography (2.4.14).- ,\nTest solution.: Weigh a quantity of mixed contents of 20 \ncapsules containing 20 mg of Temozolomide in 100.0 mI of \nmobilephàse. \n-- ’ d \n■-1\nReference solution. A 0,02 per cent w/v solution of\ntemozolomide IPRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system ; \n, - ■ ; \nn\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5' pm) (Such \nas Hypersil BDS), ; ■ ’ < : \n,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 90 volumes of 0.5 per cent\n, v/v acetic acid and 10 ml volumes of acetonitrile,\n■ flowrate: 1 mlperminute, \n'\n- spectrophotometer set at 329 nm,\n- \ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The; test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0, the column efficiency is not \nless than 2000 theoretical plates and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is hot more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C6H6N6O2"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tenofovir-disoproxil-fumarate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate\nHXGaaH\n,\nI\nHaaG\nH\nMol. Wt. 635.5\nTenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate salt ofbis(isopropyloxy-\ncarbonyloxymethyl ester of(R)-9-(2-phosphonomethoxy-\npropyl)adenine with fumaric acid.\nTenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate contain not less than 97.0 per\ncent and not more than 102. 0 per cent ofCI9H30N501OP,C4~04,\nc!ll~lll_at~~ on tl1eanb:ydr()us basis.\nCategory.Antiviral.\nDose. 300 mg daily.\nDescription. A white to off- white crystalline powder.\n2188\nIP 2010\nTENOFOVIR DISOPROXIL FUMARATE"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate\nHXGaaH\n,\nI\nHaaG\nH\nMol. Wt. 635.5\nTenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate salt ofbis(isopropyloxy-\ncarbonyloxymethyl ester of(R)-9-(2-phosphonomethoxy-\npropyl)adenine with fumaric acid.\nTenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate contain not less than 97.0 per\ncent and not more than 102. 0 per cent ofCI9H30N501OP,C4~04,\nc!ll~lll_at~~ on tl1eanb:ydr()us basis.\nCategory.Antiviral.\nDose. 300 mg daily.\nDescription. A white to off- white crystalline powder.\n2188\nIP 2010\nTENOFOVIR DISOPROXIL FUMARATE"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Detennine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tenofovir\ndisoproxil jitmarate. RS or with the reference spectrum of\ntenofovir disoproxil fumarate.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nA. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nB. When examined in the range 200 nm to 400 nm (2.4.7), a\n0.001 per cent w/v solution in methanol shows an absorption\nmaximum at the same wavelength as the reference solution.\nIn theAssay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\n2191\nTENOFOVIR AND EMTRICITABINE TABLETS"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. Detennine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nNOTE -\nPrepare the solutions immediately before use.\nTest solution. Dissolve 100 mg of the substance under\nexamination in 50 ml ofmethanol.\nReferencesolution (a). A 0.2 per cent w/v solution oftenofovir\ndisoproxil jitmarate RS in methanol.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml ofreference solution (a)\nto 100.0 ml with methanol.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve 10 mg ofthejUmaric acid in\n50 ml ofmethanol.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 Ilm) (Such\nasODS3V),\ncolumn temperature. 30°,\nmobile phase: A. D.1 M ammonium acetate solution\nwith the pH adjusted to 3.8 with glacial acetic acid,\nB. a mixture of 70 volumes of methanol\nand 30 volumes of acetonitrile,\nflow rate. 1.5 ml per minute,\na linear gradient programme using the conditions given\nbelow,\nspectrophotometer set at 260 nm,\ninjection volume. 10 Ill.\nTime\nmobile phase A\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\no\n95\nmobile phase B\n(per cent v/v)\n5\n50\n10\n50\n25\n50\n50\n50\n20\n80\n60\n95\n5\n65\n95\n5\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the\ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the column efficiency in\nnot less than 2000 theoretical plates.\n2189\nwww.webofpharma.com\nInject the test solution, reference solution (b) and reference\nsolution (c). In the chromatogram obtained with the test\nsolution, the area of any secondary peak is not more than\nthe area of the peak in the chromatogram obtained with the\nreference solution (b) (1.0 per cent) and the sum of all the\nsecondary peaks is not more than 2.5 times the' area of the\npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution\n(b) (2.5 per cent). Ignore any peak corresponding to the peak\nobtained in the chromatogram in the reference solution (c)\nand any peak having area less than 0.02 times the area ofthe\nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (b) (0.02 per cent).\nFumaricAcid. 17.5 per cent to 19.0 percent.\nDetennine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under\nexamination in 100 ml ofthe mobile phase.\nReference solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the jitmaric acid in\n50 ml of the mobile phase. Dilute 10 ml of the solution to\n100 ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 Ilm),\n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 40 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 60 volumes ofa buffer solution prepared by adding\n0.1 per cent v/v of triethylamine in D.D5M sodium\ndihydrogen phosphate with the pH adjusted to 2.3 with\northophosphoric acid imd filtered,\nflow rate. 1ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\ninjection volume. 10 Ill.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\ntailing factor is not more than 2.0, the column efficiency in not\nless than 2000 theoretical plates and the relative standard\ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the test solution and the reference solution.\nCalculate the content offumaric acid.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 1.0 per cent, detennined on\n1.0g.\nAssay. Detennine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nNOTE -\nPrepare the solutions immediately before use and\ncarry out the test protectedfrom light.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under\nexamination in 50.0 ml ofthe mobile phase. Dilute 5.0 mlofthe\nsolution to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/vsolution of tenofovir\ndisoproxiljillnarate RS in the mobile phase. Dilute 5.0 ml of\nthe solution to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nTENOFOVIR DISOPROXIL FUMARATE TABLETS\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 /lm),\nmobile phase: a mixture of 40 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 60 volumes ofa buffer solution prepared by adding\n1 ml trieti1ylamine to 0.05M sodium dihydrogen\nphosphate with the pH adjusted to 2.3 with ortho-\nphosphoric acid and :filtered,\n-\nflow rate. 1 ml perminute,\nspectrophotometer set at 260 nm,\ninjection volume. 20 /ll.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\ntailing factor is not more than 2.0, the column efficiency in not\nless than 2000 theoretical plates and the relative standard\ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the test solution and the reference solution.\nCalculate the content ofCI9H30NsOIOP,C4H404.\nStorage. Store protected from light in a refrigerator (2° to 8°).\nTenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate Tablets\nTenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate Tablets contain not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated\namount oftenofovir disoproxil fumarate, CI9H30NsOIOP,C4H404.\nUsualstrength. 300 mg.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No.1,\nMedium. 900 ml of0.1 M hydrochloric acid,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes\nWithdraw a suitable volume ofthe medium and :filterpromptly.\nDilute the filtrate, ifnecessary, with the same solvent. Measure\nthe absorbance (2.4.7) ofthe resulting solution at the maximum\nat about260.nm. Calculate the content ofC19H30NsOIOP,C4:I-404\nin the mediiim from the absorbance obtained fromasoliition\noflmown concentration oftefzofovir dis6pt6xilfutJ1atateRS.\nD. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of\nCI9H30NsOIOP,C4:I-404'\nIF 2010\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nNOTE -\nPrepare the solutions immediately before use.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh accurately\na quantity of the powder containing 100 mg of Tenofovir\nDisoproxil Fumarate and disperse in 50 ml.of methanol and\nfilter.\nReference solution (a). A solution of tenofovir disoproxil\nfumarate RS containing 0.2 per cent oftenofovir disoproxil in\nmethanol.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1ml ofreference solution (a) to\n100 ml with methanol.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve 10 mg offumaric acid in\n50 ml ofmethanol.\nChromatographic system\na stainless ste"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tenoxicam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tenoxicam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tenoxicam\nTenoxicam is 4-Hydroxy-2-methyl-N-(pyridin-2-yl)-2H-\nIhieno [2,3 -e] 1,2-thiazine-3-carboxamide 1,1 -dioxide.\nTenoxicam contains not less than 9.9.0 per cent and not more \nthan 10Lb per cent of C^HuNat^Si, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory.Analgesic; anti inflammatory.\nDescription. A yellow crystalline powder. It shows \npolymorphism(2.5.11)."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tenoxicam\nTenoxicam is 4-Hydroxy-2-methyl-N-(pyridin-2-yl)-2H-\nIhieno [2,3 -e] 1,2-thiazine-3-carboxamide 1,1 -dioxide.\nTenoxicam contains not less than 9.9.0 per cent and not more \nthan 10Lb per cent of C^HuNat^Si, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory.Analgesic; anti inflammatory.\nDescription. A yellow crystalline powder. It shows \npolymorphism(2.5.11)."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tenoxicam IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of tenoxicam.\nIf the spectra obtained in solid state show difference, dissolve \nthe substance under examination and reference substance\nseparately inminimum volume of methylene chloride: \nEvaporate to dryness and record new spectra using the \nresidues? \n> • > v ‘ \n•\nA. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution. ;\n3752\nIP2°22\nB. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.1.7), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254. ■■■■■\nMobile phase. A mixture of 4 volumes of anhydrouffbdtifc '■% \nacid, 30 volumes of acetone and 70 volumes '¿1 ? \ndichloromethane. \"\" \n:\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of the powdered tables ■ \ncontaining 20 mg of Tenoxicam in 20 ml of dichloromethane ; \nwith the aid of ultrasound for 15 minutes, centrifuge andUse \nthe supernatant liquid. \n:■ a.\nReference solution; A 6.1 per cent w/v solution oftenoxicam \nIPRS in dichloromethane. J\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. .After development, \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at 254 \nnm„ The principal spot in the chromatogram obtained with the \ntest solution corresponds to the spot in the chromatograins \nobtained with the reference solution.\n.¡ÎlirQjnatogçam obtained with reference solution (a) (2.0 per\ncent) at 254 nm. \n' \n,\nOther tests. Comply with tests stated under Tablets.\nÂssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as stated\n^iderRelated substances with the following modifications.\nTest:solution. Disperse 10 intact tablets in 200.0 ml of the \nsolvent mixture; with the aid of ultrasound for 70 minutes with \nintermittent shaking. Allow to stand for 10 minutes, dilute a \nvolume of clear supernatant liquid’with the mobile phase to \nobtain a solution containing 0.025 per-cent w/v of Tenoxicam \nân'dfilter. --1 ■ \n>-■■■■ •' ■ ’ ' ' -"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution in \nnettyene chloride is clear (2.4.1).\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). “ •\nNOTE — Carry out the test protect from light.\nSolvent mixture. Equal volumes of acètohitrilë and water, \nadjusted to pH 3.2 with dilute orthophosphoric acid!\nTest solution. Dissolve 35 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture with the aid of ultrasound \nand dilute to 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with the solvent mixture. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution \nto 1 Q.O ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution fb). Dissolve the content of a vial of\ntenoxicam impurity mixturelPRS (containingimpurities B, G \nand H) in 1.0 ml of the test solution. ;\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steelcolumn 15 cm * 4.6 mm, packed with\ndiisopropylcyanosilane bonded to porous silica \n(3.5 pm),\n- column temperature: 35°,\nmobile phase: A. a mixture of 25 volumes of methanol, \n75 volumes of water, adjusted to pH 3.2 with dilute \northophosphoric acid,\nB. a mixture of 25 volumes of water and\n75 volumes of methanol,. adjusted to pH 3.2 with dilute \northophosphoric acid,\n- - a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute’\n- spectrophotometer set at 230 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase\nnmin.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v\no\n96\n4\n5\n96\n4\n16\n76\n•24\n’25\n76\n24\n26\n96\n4\n30’\n96\n4\nCorrection\nName\nRelative \nretention time\nfactor\nTenoxicam impurity A1\n0.10\n. 0.2\nTenoxicam impurity G?\n0.85\n—\nTenoxicam impurity H3\n0.90\n—\nTenoxicam (Retention time:\nabout 12 minutes)\n1.0\n—\nTenoxicam impurity B4\n1.30\n2.0\n'Pyridm^-amine, ;\n24-hydroxy-2-methyl-2Z/-thieno[2,3-e] 1,2-thiazine-3-carboxamide 1,1 - \ndioxide, ’ ’r ';\n3751\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTENOXICAM\n[(methylajginp)sulfgiiyl]thiophene-2-!C,ai;boxylic •aqi.d,.; \n: ■; ;■\n“methyl 4-hydroxy-2-methyI-2if-thieno[2,3-e] 1,2-thiazine-3-carbp- \nxylate 1,1-dioxide, ...\nInject reference solution,(b). The test is. not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to tenoxicam and tenoxicam\nimpurity II (or G if peaks are inverted) is not less than!.3 and \nthe resolutibn'betweeii the peaks due to tenoxicam iihpurity G \nandterioxicam impurify H isnot less than 1.3. \n-\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with thetest solution the area of any \npeak correspondirig to tenoxicam impurity A multiplied with \ncorrection factor 0.2 and tenoxicam impurity B multiplied with \ncorrection; factor 2.0, each of,. is ?riot. more than . 1.5. times the \narea of principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (0.15 per cent) the area iof any other\nsecondary peak is not more than the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0,1 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 3 times the area of the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a)\n(0.3 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 times \nthe, area, of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (0.05 per cent). ...\nHeavy metals (2.3-.13): 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more thah6.1 per cent.\nWater (2.4.43). Not more than 0,5 per cent, determined on\n1.0 g. \n' \nY\nAssay! Dissolve 0.25 g of the substance under examination in \n5 ml Qipnhydrous formic add and add lO ml of anhydrous \nacetic acid.. Titrate with 0,1 M perchloric acid, determining \nthe end-point potentiometrically ,.(2.4.25). Carry out a blank \ntitration.\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is' equivalent to 0,03374 g of \n/ •\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nTenoxicam Tablets\nTenoxicam Tablets contain not less than 92.5 per cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of tenoxicam, \nC^Hh^O.^. \n■\nUsual strength. 20 mg \n;\nDissolution (2.5.2)\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of phosphate buffer pH 6.8 prepared by \ndissolving 6.8 g of potassium dihydrogen orthophosphate in \n500 ml of water, add 2 ml of IM sodium hydroxide and dilute \nwith water to 1000 ml. Adjusted to pH 6.8 with IM sodium \nhydroxide or 10 per cent w/v solution of orthophosphoric \nacid as required.\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes. \n'\nWithdraw a suitable volume of medium and filter. Reject the\nfirst few nil of the filtrate and dilute a suitable volume of filtrate \nwith dissolution medium if necessary. Measure the absorbance\nof filtered solution at maximum at about 368 nm (2.4!7). Calculate \nthe content of tenoxicam, C13H} JN3O4S2 in the medium from \nthe absorbance obtained from a solution of known \nconcentration of tenoxicam IPRS in dissolution medium.\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of \nC13H11N3O4S2.\nRelated substances? Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). •• \n■■ \n: \n' •'< ■\" \n■■ \n7\" ■■\nSolvent mixture. Equal volumes of acetonitrile and water.\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 100 mg of Tenoxicam, disperse, in 50 ml of the \nsolvent mixture with the aid of ultrasound for 70 minutes with \nintermittent shaking and dilute to 100.0 ml with the solvent \nmixture. Allow to stand for 10 minutes, dilute 5.0 ml of the clear \nsupernatant liquid to 20.0 ml with the mobile phase and filter.\nReference solution faf Dilute 1.0 ml of the lest.solution4p;\n200.0 ml with the mobile phase. \n>\nwww.webofpharma.com\njpi2Ö22\nTERAZOSIN HYDROCHLORIDE\nftéferencesolution (b). A 0.0625 per cent w/v solution of 2-\nthe solvent mixture. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution\ntofOO-Onil Mth the solvent mixture. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution \n^ôWriû;with the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n¿s;® tâ’stainless stéel column 25 cm * 4.0 mm, packed with \nÏAtToctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5s urn) (Such as\nNucleosilC8),\nIgBi'ihidbile phase: a mixture of 7 volumes of 0.012 per cent\ns"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terazosin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terazosin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Terazosin Hydrochloride\nTerazosin Hydrochloride Dihydrate\nCl9H25N5O4,HCl,2H2O ; e , Mol. Wt. 459.9\nTerazosin. Hydrochloride is (7?5')-6,7-dimethoxy-2-[4- \n(tetrahydrofpran-2-carbonyl)piperazin“ 1-yl) quinazolin-4- \nylamine hydrochloride dihydrate.\nTerazosin Hydrochloride contains not less than’99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C19H25N5O4,HC1, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellow, crystalline powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with terazosin \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of terazosin \nhydrochloride.\nB. It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. Al. 0 per cent w/v solution in carbon­\ndioxide free water is clear (2.4.1) and not more intensely \ncoloured thanreference solution YS7 (2;4.1). - ' ■ ’ ■\npH (2.4.24). 3.0 to 5.0, determined in 2.0 per cent w/v solution \nin carbon-dioxide free water. \n.\nImpurities N and O. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n; \n? \n'\"'7 \n•'\nSolvent mixture. 20 volumes of acetonitrile, and 80-volumes \noiyvater.,.. . .. ,.=:. rc..,, =\nTest solution. - Dissolve\" 501 mgof the - substance under \nexamination in 50 ml of the solvent mixture. ■ ■ . '\nReference solution (a). Dissolve'5 mg each 'of2-chloro-6,7- \ndimethoxyquinazolin-4-amine (terazosin impurity A iP'RS)\nand-. A-[[(2RS)~tetrahydr,ofuran-2-yl] carbonyl]piperazine \n1PRS (terazosin impurity NJIPRS in acetonitrile, add 5 ml of \nthe test solution and dilute to 50 ml with acetonitrile. Dilute\n10.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\n3753\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTERAZOSIN HYDROCHLORIDE\nReference solution (b). Dilutei 0.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: dissolve 2.8 g of sodium laurylsulphate\nin 1000 ml ofwater and add 11 ini ofa solution containing \n20.24 per cent w/v of triethylàminè and 23.0 per cent \nw/v of orthophosphoric acid and adjusted to pH 2.5 \nwith orthophosphoric acid', mix 60 volumes of this \nsolution with 40 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.0 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference soldi ion (b). The relative retention time \nwith reference to terazosin for l,4-bis[(tctrahydrofuran- \n2-yl)carbonyl]piperazine (terazosin impurity O) is about 0.2, \nfor terazosin impurity N. is about 0.3 and for terazosin impurity \nA is about 0.4.;The test is not valid unless the resolution \nbetween the peaks due to terazosin impurity A and N is not \nless than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (b) and thè test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 4 times the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area \nof each peak corresponding to terazosin impurity N and \nterazosin impurity O is not more than the area of the peak due \nto terazosin in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution(b)(0.1 percent). ■. m «\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n. . • . i- ; :■■■■'■ \n,-\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 100 ml of the mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 2.0 ml of the test solution to\n100 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 5,0 ml of the solution to \n100 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve the contents of a vial of\nterazosin, far system suitability IPRS (containing, terazosin \nimpurity À, terazosin impurity B (l-(4-hydroxy-6,7- \ndimethoxyquinazolin-2-yl)-4-[[(2R5)-tetrahydrofuran-2-yl] \ncafbonÿl]piperazirie)', téràzosin impurity C (6,7-dimethoxy-2- \n(piperazin-1 -yl)quinazolin-4-amine), terazosin impurity I f D(4- \namino-6,7-dimcthoxyquinazolin-2-yl)-4-[(2/riS')-2-hydroxy- \npentanoyl]piperazine), terazosin impurity K (prazosin) and \nterazosin impurity M (1,4-bis(f’"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Terazosin Hydrochloride\nTerazosin Hydrochloride Dihydrate\nCl9H25N5O4,HCl,2H2O ; e , Mol. Wt. 459.9\nTerazosin. Hydrochloride is (7?5')-6,7-dimethoxy-2-[4- \n(tetrahydrofpran-2-carbonyl)piperazin“ 1-yl) quinazolin-4- \nylamine hydrochloride dihydrate.\nTerazosin Hydrochloride contains not less than’99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C19H25N5O4,HC1, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellow, crystalline powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with terazosin \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of terazosin \nhydrochloride.\nB. It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. Al. 0 per cent w/v solution in carbon­\ndioxide free water is clear (2.4.1) and not more intensely \ncoloured thanreference solution YS7 (2;4.1). - ' ■ ’ ■\npH (2.4.24). 3.0 to 5.0, determined in 2.0 per cent w/v solution \nin carbon-dioxide free water. \n.\nImpurities N and O. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n; \n? \n'\"'7 \n•'\nSolvent mixture. 20 volumes of acetonitrile, and 80-volumes \noiyvater.,.. . .. ,.=:. rc..,, =\nTest solution. - Dissolve\" 501 mgof the - substance under \nexamination in 50 ml of the solvent mixture. ■ ■ . '\nReference solution (a). Dissolve'5 mg each 'of2-chloro-6,7- \ndimethoxyquinazolin-4-amine (terazosin impurity A iP'RS)\nand-. A-[[(2RS)~tetrahydr,ofuran-2-yl] carbonyl]piperazine \n1PRS (terazosin impurity NJIPRS in acetonitrile, add 5 ml of \nthe test solution and dilute to 50 ml with acetonitrile. Dilute\n10.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\n3753\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTERAZOSIN HYDROCHLORIDE\nReference solution (b). Dilutei 0.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: dissolve 2.8 g of sodium laurylsulphate\nin 1000 ml ofwater and add 11 ini ofa solution containing \n20.24 per cent w/v of triethylàminè and 23.0 per cent \nw/v of orthophosphoric acid and adjusted to pH 2.5 \nwith orthophosphoric acid', mix 60 volumes of this \nsolution with 40 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.0 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference soldi ion (b). The relative retention time \nwith reference to terazosin for l,4-bis[(tctrahydrofuran- \n2-yl)carbonyl]piperazine (terazosin impurity O) is about 0.2, \nfor terazosin impurity N. is about 0.3 and for terazosin impurity \nA is about 0.4.;The test is not valid unless the resolution \nbetween the peaks due to terazosin impurity A and N is not \nless than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (b) and thè test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 4 times the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area \nof each peak corresponding to terazosin impurity N and \nterazosin impurity O is not more than the area of the peak due \nto terazosin in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution(b)(0.1 percent). ■. m «\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n. . • . i- ; :■■■■'■ \n,-\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 100 ml of the mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 2.0 ml of the test solution to\n100 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 5,0 ml of the solution to \n100 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve the contents of a vial of\nterazosin, far system suitability IPRS (containing, terazosin \nimpurity À, terazosin impurity B (l-(4-hydroxy-6,7- \ndimethoxyquinazolin-2-yl)-4-[[(2R5)-tetrahydrofuran-2-yl] \ncafbonÿl]piperazirie)', téràzosin impurity C (6,7-dimethoxy-2- \n(piperazin-1 -yl)quinazolin-4-amine), terazosin impurity I f D(4- \namino-6,7-dimcthoxyquinazolin-2-yl)-4-[(2/riS')-2-hydroxy- \npentanoyl]piperazine), terazosin impurity K (prazosin) and \nterazosin impurity M (1,4-bis(f’uran-2-ylcarbonyl)piperazine) \nin 10 ml ofthe mobile phase.\nReference so hit ion (c). Dissolve 5 mg of l-(furan~2- \nylcarbònytypiperazine IPRS (terazosin > impurity L■; IPRS) in\n100 ml of the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to \n100ml with the mobilephase. \n. m m\nIp2022\nReference solution (d)._ To 5 mg of 2,2-(piperazine-} 4\ndiyl)bis(6i 7-dimethoxyquinazolin-4-amine) IPRS (terazo^ \nimpurity E IPRS ) add 70 ml of methanol and 30 ml of water \nAllow to stand for at least ,1 hour to dissolve the substance*\nChrmatographic system\n... - a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- column temperature: 30°, \n: -\n- mobile phase: mix 2 volumes of triethylamine, 35Q\nvolumes of acetonitrile, and 1650. volumes of a solution \ncontaining 0.6 per cent w/v of sodium citrate and 1425 \nper cent w/v of anhydrous citric acid, . ;\n- flow rate: I ml per minute, \n; ?\n- spectrophotometer set at 245 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl. .\nInject reference solution (b). The test is hot valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to terazosin impurity B and \nterazosin impurity J is not less than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (a), (c) and the test solution. Run thè \nchromatogram 4 times the retention time ofthe principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area \nof secondary peak corresponding to terazOsm impurity A, C; \nE and K, each of, is not more than 5 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.5 per cent), the area of secondary peak \ncorresponding to terazosin impurity L is not more than the \narea of the corresponding peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (c) (0.1 per cent), the area of secondary \npeak corresponding to terazosin impurity B, J, M, each of, is \nnót more than the area of the principal peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per \ncent). Multiply the peak areas ofthe impuiritesby the"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2.(Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml ofwater, \nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the resulting solution at the maximum at \nabout 245 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content of C^H^N'sCL in \nthe medium from the absorbance obtained from a solution of \nknown concentration of terazosin, hydrochloride IPRS, \nprepared by dissolving in the dissolution medium to get similar \nconcentration as that of the test solution.\nQi Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). ' ' \n\" . .. \n;\nsolution. Disperse a quantity of powdered tablets \ncontaining 15 mg of terazosin in 25 ml of the mobile phase with \nAte aid of ultrasound for 10 minutes and with mechanical\nshaking for about 20. minutes and dilute to 50.0 ml with the \nmobile phase, filter through 0.45 pm nylon or. teflon filter.\nReference solution (a). A solution of terazosin hydrochloride. \nIPRS in the mòbile phase to obtain 0.0003 per cent w/v of \nterazosin.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 5.Ó ml óf reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with the mobile phase; ; ;; N\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), ..■■\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 6 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 19 volumes of buffer solution prepared by dissolving \n4.1. g of monobasic potassium phosphate and 1.1 g of \nsodium. 1 -heptanesulphonate monohydrate in 950 ml\nof water, adjusted to pH 3.0 with orthophosphoric acid. \nand dilute to 1000 ml with water,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, : \n>\n- spectrophotometer set at 246 nm, \n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nName \nRelative \nRelative\nretention time response factor\nPiperazinyl-ADMQ1 \n0.52 \n1.1\nTerazosin Hydrochloride \n1.0 \n—\nChloro ADMQ* \n2 \n1.37 \n1.2\nBis-ADMQ-piperazine3 \n3.85 \n1.0\nW-(4-Amino-6,7 diniethoxy-2-quinazoiinyl)piperazine, '\n22-Chloror4-amino-6,7 dimethoxy-2rquinazoline,\n3AIJV-Bis-(4-aniino-6,7 diinetlioxy-2-quihazolinyl)piperazine.\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0 in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) and signal-to-noise ratio \nis not less than 10.0 in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b).\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. Run,the test \nsolution 4.5 times the retention time of the principal peak. The \narea of any peak corresponding to piperazinyl-ADMQ, chloro \nADMQ and bis-ADMQ piperazine, each of, is not more than \n0.4 times the area of the principal peakinthe chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.4 per cent), the area of \nany other secondary peak is not more than 0.2 times the area \nof the principalpeak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a) (0.2 pier cent) and the sum of the areas of \nall the secondary peaks is not more than 1.2 times the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (1.2 per cent).\nUniformity of content. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14); as described tinder Assay using following \nmodification. \n'\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTERAZOSIN: TABLETS\nTestsolution: Transfer one tablet to 20 ml of solvent mixture, \ndisperse withthe aid of ultrasound for about 20 minutes; cool \nand dilute, with sufficient of the solvent mixture, to produce a \nsolution containing 0'005 percent w/v tcarzosim mix and shake \nwell,filter. : \n' \n............ ....... .......\"\nOther, tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.,\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). o f ■ :\nNOTE— Use an all-glass syringe. ;P ■.< :\nSolvent mixture. Ato volumes of 0:01 M:methanolic \nhydrochloric acid and 60 volumes of water. x -,\nTest solution. Weighand powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof powder containing 10 ihg of terazosin in 100 ml of the \nsolvent mixture with the aid of ultrasound for 1Ó minutes and \nmechanical shaking for further 10 minutes and dilute to 200.0 \nnil with tile sol vent mixture and filter. \n? \n: .\nReference solution, A0.0055 percent w/v solution of terazosin \nhydrochloride IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system :;\n— a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm; packed with\no’ctadecylsilane boiided to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 70 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 30 volumes of water, add 1 volume of glacial acetic \nacid, filter and pipet 0.2 volume, of diethylamine into \nthe solution,\n- flow rate: 2.5 ml per minute, \n:\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm, ! ■ '\n~ injection volume: 25 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The- test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not moire: than 2.0 andrihe relative.standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0\nInject the,reference, solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogramtwice the retention time of the principai peak.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture, at a \ntempérature not exceeding 30°. 'j .\nCalculate thé content of C19H25NSO4 in the tablets. \n: :\nTerbinafine Hydrochloride\n9H3\nCJ^HHCl , ,.... ........ \nMol. Wt. 327.9\nTerbinafine Hydrochloride is- A?(6, 6-dimethyL2-hepten-4-\nynyl)-N-methyl-,(E)-,hydrochloride. \nan , ;.:• u ? ; \n1\nww.webofpharma.com\n_________________________________________________ ___ Wj\nTerbinafine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C2lH2SN,HCl, calculate \non the dried basis. ;\nCategory. Antifungal.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; hygroscopic.\n■ . : \n'■ \n-, ;■ \n, .7\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatograph\n(2.4.14). \n■\nNOTE - Carty out the test protectedfrom light. : \n'■\nSolvent mixture,. Equal volumes of acetonitrile .and wate^-\nSolution A 300 Volumes of methanol A and .200 volumes:®\nacetonitrile, o b b--., -• ./ ■■ \n, \n? : ¿|||\nTest solution. Dissòlve 50 mg of the substance und^f\nexamination in 100;0 ml ofsò/vehi tritare. \n; : ; ;\nReference solution (a). A solution"},{"name":"Identification","text":": \nil\n■\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4,$\nCompare,the. spectrum .with that , obtained with terbinqfag \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of \nterbinafine hydrochloride. ■; ; \n: \n; ‘\nB. It gives reaction (A) of chloride (2.3.1). \n?"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terbinafine-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terbinafine Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Terbinafine Cream\nTerbinafine Hydrochloride Cream\nTerbinafine Cream contains ribt less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot morethan 110.0 pet cent of the stated amount of terbinafine \nhydrochloride, C21H25N,HC1 in a suitable cream base:\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/w."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Terbinafine Cream\nTerbinafine Hydrochloride Cream\nTerbinafine Cream contains ribt less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot morethan 110.0 pet cent of the stated amount of terbinafine \nhydrochloride, C21H25N,HC1 in a suitable cream base:\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/w."},{"name":"Identification","text":"In the Assay, theprincipabpeak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the, peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nTests- \n: \n'' '•\nr < ’• • , ') f i \nf \n■ ■ . j.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14); ■ \n■\n3757\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTERBINAFINE CREAM\nTest solution: Shake a quantity of the cream'Containing about \n25 mg of Terbinafine Hydrochloride with 70.0 ml ol'the mobile \nphase, warm oh; a water-bath at-55°;for 10,minutes. Cool to \nroom temperature. Sonicate with intermittent swirling toobtain \nuniform dispersion and dilute to. 100.0 ml with mobile phase\nandfilter. - \n; .\nReference solution. A 0.000125 per cent wZv solution of \nterbinafine hydrochloride IPRS in the mobile phase. :\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x ,4.6 mm, packed with\n, octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), L\n- mobile phase:, a mixture of 28 volumes, of acetonitrile,\n, 10 volumes, of methanof 62 volumes of 0.05 M\nphosphate buffer, prepared by dissolving, 6.8 g of \npotassium dihydrogen orthophosphate fa 1000 ml water,.\n0.1 scAnmepï ortho phosphoric acid and 0,1 volume of \ntriethylamine.\n- flow rate : 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrÒpÉótómetéf set at 224 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 p.1.\nInject the référence solution. The tési is not valid unless the \ncolumn .efficiency is not less than 4000 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0, and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 5.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and .test solution.: In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \nsecondary peak is not greater than 0.6 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in thé chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (0.3 per cent) and the sum of areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (0.5 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Creams.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the cream containing about \n25 mg of Terbinafine Hydrochloride with 70 ml of the \nmethçinol, warm on a water-bath al. 55° for 10 minutes with \nintermittent swirling. Sonicate.for 15 minutes with intermittent \nswirling to obtain a uniform dispersion. Cool, to room \ntemperature and dilute to 1Q0.Ô ml with methanol and filter.\nFurther dilute 5.0 ml to 25.0 ml with mobile phase. \n> ■\nReference solution. Dissolve 25 mg of terbinafine \nhydrochlorideIPRSin 30 ml ofwerAawoZand dilute to 50.0 ml \nwith thé methanol. Dilute 5.0 ml of thé solution to 50.0 ml with \nthe mobile phase. ;\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\ny mobile phase: : a! mixture of 500 volumes ’ of '■ water,\n400 volumes of acetonitrile, 100 volumes of methanol;\n3758.\nIP2°2^\n1 volume of orthophosphoric acid and; 1 volume \ntriethylamine, \n■ \n■ ffA\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, ■■ \n- spectrophotometer set at 282 nm, ■ \n- injection volume: 20 pl. \n®\nInject the reference solution, the test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 3000 theoretical plates, the'W;- \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C2iH25N,HCl in the cream.\nStorage. Store at à température not exceeding 30°. The cream ; ■ \nshould not be allowéd to freeze.\nTerbinafine Tablets\nTerbinafine Hydrochloride Tablets \n; ; i\nTerbinafine Hydrochloride Tablets contain not less than 90,0 \nper cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount \nof terbinafine, C2!H2SN. \n7\nUsual strength. 250 mg. ’\nj\nA. In the Assay, the principal, peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution;?; y d"},{"name":"Tests","text":". . ..... \n?:■.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 500 ml of a citrate buffer pH 3.0, prepared by \ndissolving 7.9 g of citric acid anhydrous and 2.3 ¿.sodium \ncitrate in water. \n' \n:\nSpeed arid time. 50 rpm and 30 minutes. \ni :\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate, suitably diluted with citrate \nbuffer if necessary, at the maximum at about 283 rim(2;.4.-7).\nCalculate the content of terbinafine, C2]H2;N, in thé medium \nfrom the, absorbance .obtained by repeating the determination \nusing a solution of known concentration oitorbinaf}n& \nhydrochloride IPRS in dissolution,medium.. . ;\nQ. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of ChHisN.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatq^^\n(2.4.14).\nTest.solution: Shake a quantity of the;powdered-tablets \ncontaining 20 mgof terbinafine with 50 ml of the mobiléphase\nwww.webofpharma.com\nwith the aid of ultrasound for 20 minutes, dilute to \nl^pnilwith mobile phase filter and use the filtrate.\nA solution of terbinafine hydrochloride\nli the mobile phase containing 0.00007 per cent w/v of\n¿ggâtographic system\nyTa'stairicssstecl column 15 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\n. octaylsilane chemically bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n¿^¿mo|)ile phase: 60 volumes of a buffer solution prepared\n• by dissolving 0.85 g oï monobasicpotassium phosphate \nand 1.0 g of sodium 1-decanesulfonate in 1000 ml of \nwater and adjusted to pH 3.0 with orthophosphoric\nacid and 40 volumes of acetonitrile, \nWjbsflbwrate: 1.8 ml per minute,\n( per cent v/v)\n— spectrophotometer set at 220 nm,\nJA\n¿fl\n- injection volume: 50 pl.\nu\nOu\n2\n4Ti\n70\nName \nRelative Correction\no\nJv\n/u\n■ \nretention time factor\n18\n30\n70\nA'-Methy1-1 -(naphthalene-1 -yl)\n24\n16\n84 '\nmethanamine \n0.14 \n0.67\n26\n5\n95\nTerbinafine \n1.0 \n—\n30\n0\n100\nUnknown \n— \n1.0\n37\n40\n■ 60\nTotal impurity \n— \n—\n45\n40\n60\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 10.0 per \n¿enE'\"\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram with the test solution, the area of impurity due \nto A-Methyl-1-( naphthalene- 1-yl) is not more than 0.57 times \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwithrefercnce solution (0.2 per cent), the area of any secondary \npeak is not more than 0.57 times the area of the principal peak \nih the:chromatogram obtained with reference solution (0.2 per \ncent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary peaks is \nnot more than 2 times the area of the peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (0.7 per cent).\nLimit of Terbinafine Dimer. Determine by liquid \nchromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 40 volumes of acetonitrile and TO volumes \nof water.\nfest solution. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 250 mg of terbinafine with 50 ml of, the solvent \nfixture, and mix with the aid of ultrasound for 20. minutes, \ndilute to 166.0 ml with solvent mixture and filter.\nReference solution. A solution of terbinafine hydrochloride \nfPRS in the solvent mixture containing 0.00014 per cent w/v of\nterbinafine.\nTERBINAFINE TABLETS\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous:silica,(5 pm),\n- column temperature: 52°, \n• \n.■\n- mobile phase: A. 1.0 ml of triethylgmine in 1000.0 ml of\nwater, ■ \n■ .<\nB. 1.0 ml of triethylamine in a 1000.0 ml \nof a mixture of 19 volumes of acetonitrile and 1 volume \nof water,\n- - a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n■ spectrophotometer set at 280 nm,\n- injection volume: 100 pl,\n. Time\nMobile phase B\nMobile phase A\n( per cent v/v)\n(in min.)\nCorrection\nName\nRelative \nretention time\nfactor\nTerbinafine\n1.0\nTerbinafine dimer1\n2.0\n0.48\n'(2E ,4E )-4-(4,4-Dirnethylpent-2-ynylidene)-jV7, W-dimethyl-W N5- \nbis(naphthalen-l-ylmethyl)pent-2-ene-l, 5-diamine.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 10.0 per \ncent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the\nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of \nimpurity due to terbinafine diamer is not more than 0.9 times \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (0.05 percent). ■\nOther tests. Comply with 'the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14);\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Shake a .quantity \nof the powder containing 20 mg of Terbinafine with 50 ml of \nthe mobile phase and mix with, the aid of ultrasound for \n20 minutes, dilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase and filter.\nReference solution; A 0^022J per cent w/v solution of \nterbinafine hydrochloride IPRS in the mobile phase. >\n3759\nwww.webofpharma.com\n■unKHUTAIilNE SULPHATE\nChromatographic system \n\"\n'': - a stainless steel column 15 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\noctaylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 urn),\n- mobile phase: 60 volumes of a buffer solution prepared\n■' > by dissolving 0.85 g of monobasic potassium phosphate\nand 1,0 g of sodium 1-decanesulfonate in 1000 ml of \nwater and adjusted to pH 3.0 with orthophosphoric\n;; : acidand 40 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.8 ml per minute,\n7 - < spectrophotometer set at 220 nm, ■ -; - r\n- injection volume: 5 pl. ■ \n■\nInject the reference solution! The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is hot more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C^JTsNin the tablets.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nLabelling. The label states the. strength in terms of the \nequivalent amount of terbinafine."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terbutaline-inhalation","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terbutaline Inhalation"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Terbutaline Inhalation\nTerbutaline Sulphate Inhalation; Terbutaline Inhalation \nAerosol (Terbutaline Sulphate Inhalation Aerosol\nTerbutaline Inhalation is a suspension of Terbutaline Sulphate,\näs^a' superfine powder, in a suitable liquid in a suitable \npressurised container. It may contain suitable pharmaceutical \naids such as-surfactants, stabilising agents, etc. '\nTerbutaline Inhalation delivers riot less than 75.0 per cent and\nnot more than 125.0 per cent of the stated amount of terbutaline \n»>>Ptate.(C12H19NO3)2,H2SO4, per inhalation, by actuation of \nthe valve.\nMsual strength. 250 pg in each metered dose."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Terbutaline Inhalation\nTerbutaline Sulphate Inhalation; Terbutaline Inhalation \nAerosol (Terbutaline Sulphate Inhalation Aerosol\nTerbutaline Inhalation is a suspension of Terbutaline Sulphate,\näs^a' superfine powder, in a suitable liquid in a suitable \npressurised container. It may contain suitable pharmaceutical \naids such as-surfactants, stabilising agents, etc. '\nTerbutaline Inhalation delivers riot less than 75.0 per cent and\nnot more than 125.0 per cent of the stated amount of terbutaline \n»>>Ptate.(C12H19NO3)2,H2SO4, per inhalation, by actuation of \nthe valve.\nMsual strength. 250 pg in each metered dose."},{"name":"identification","text":"determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating the \nPlate with silica gel GF254. , \n. . : \n. ..\n, TERBUTALINE: INHALATION\nMobile phase. A mixture of 65 volumes :pts2hpropanol, \n25 volumes of cyclohexane and 5 volumes of formic acid.\nTest solution. Remove the actuator from the pressurised \ncontainer, shake the container for about 30 seconds and place \nit in an inverted position in a small beaker containing 5 ml of \nwater. Discharge a sufficient number of deliveries containing \n5 mg of Terbutaline Sulphate, under the surface of the solvent.\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of terbutaline \nsulphate IPRSic water.\nApply to the plate 2 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air, spray with a 2 per cent w/v solution of \n4-aminoantipyrine ip methanol. Examine in daylight and under \nultraviolet light at 365 nm. The principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Inhalation \nPreparations (Pressurised metered-dose Preparations);\nFollow the procedure described under Assay wherever the \namount of active substance is to be determined in any test.\nAssay. Carry out the test for Content of active ingredient \ndelivered per actuation stated under Inhalation Preparations \n(Pressurised metered-dose Preparations). ,\nUse 35 ml of water and finally dilute to 50.0 ml with water. \nDilute.a.suitable volume of the solution with water to produce \na solution containing 50 pg of Terbutaline Sulphate per ml. To\n10.0 ml of the solution in a 50-ml volumetric flask add 35 ml of \nbuffer solution pH9.5. and 1.0 ml of 4-aminoantipyrine \nsolution. Mix, add 1.0 ml oipotassium ferricyanide,solution \nwith, vigorous swirling of the flask, dilute to volume with water \nand.mix.Exactly 75 seconds after the additionpf thepotoi^ifrm \nferricyanide.so lution measure the absorbance of the resulting\nsolution at the maximum at about 550 nm (2.4.7), using as the \nblank a solution prepared in the same manner using 10.0 ml o f \nwater in place of the solution of the substance under \nexamination. \n•• • \n■\nCalculate the Content of (e!2Hj9NO3)2,H2SO4 in the solution \nfrom the absorbance obtained by repeating the operation using \na solution containing 100 pg of lerbutciline sulphate IP RS in \nplace of the solution of the substance under examination.\nCalculate the amount of (C^HigNC^H^SO^ delivered per \nactuation of the valve. ..... ... \n, \n.. i(\nDetermine the content of active ingredient a second and third \ntime by repeating theprocedure on the middle ten and on the \nlast ten successive combined actuations of the valve. For \neach of the three determinations the average content of \n(CnH i9NO3)2,fI2SO4 delivered per actuation of the valve meets \nthe requirements. \n-v.\n3761\nwww.webofpharma.com\nStorage. Store protected from moisture at a temperature not \ncxcecding30°.\nLabelling. The* label states the amount of active ingredient \ndelivered per inhalation. \n-\nTerbutaline Injection\nTerbutaline Sulphate Injection\nTerbutaline Injection, is; a> sterile solution of Terbutaline \nSulphate in Water for Injections.\nTerbutaline Injection contains riot less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of terbutaline \nsulphate (Ci2Hi9NÔ3)2, H2SO:.\nUsual strength. 500 pg per ml.\nDetermine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating the \nplate with silica gel G .\nMobile phase.. Amixture of 65 volumes of 2-propanol, \n25 volumes of cyclohexane and 5 volumes of formic acid.\nTest solution. Use the injection.\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of terbutaline \nsulphate JPRS in saline, solution.\nApply to the plate 2 pl of each solution. After development,\ndry the plate in air, spray with a 2 per cent w/v solution of \n4-dminoàntipyriné in methanol. Dry the plate in air and spray \nwith a freshly prepared 8.0 per cent w/v solution ofpotassium \nferricyanide in a1 mixture of 4 volumes of strong ammonia\nsolution and 1 volume of water. The principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with thé test solution corresponds to \nspot in thé chromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nTests “ ’ <\npH (2.4.24). 3.0 to 5.0. \n.... .\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2:3). Not more than 1250.0,Endotoxin\nUnits per mg of terbutaline.sulphate. \n:■-■■■\nOther tests. Comply with thé tests stated under Parenteral \npreparations (Injections):\nAssay. Measure a volume containing about 5 mg of Terbutaline\nSulphate and add sufficient water to produce 50.0 ml. To \n5.0ml add 35 ml of a buffer solution prepared by dissolving \n36.3 g of tris (hydroxymethyl), aminomethane in 900 ml of \nwater., adjusted to pH.bétween 9.4 and 9.6 and adding sufficient \nwaterto produce 1000 rril. Add 1.0 ml of a freshly prepared 2.0 \nper.cent w/v.sblution of 4-aminoaniipyrine, mix.'and add 1.0 \nml of a freshly prepared 8.0 per cent w/v solution ofpotassium\n3762\nIP2Ô22\nTERBUTALINE INJECTION\nferricyanide with vigorous swirling and sufficient ofthe buff •\nsolution to produce 50.0 ml. Exactly 75; seconds;after the \naddition of the potassium ferricyanide solution measure the \nabsorbance of the resulting solution at the maximum at about \n550 nm (2.4.7), using water as the blank. \nJ /!\nCalculate the content of (Cl2Hi9NO3)2, H2SO4 from the \nabsorbance obtained by repeating the operation using a 0 01 \nper cent w/v solution of terbutaline sulphate IPRS and \nbeginning at the words “To 5.0ml add35 ml......”. \n;.,\nStorage. Store protected from light, in single dose containers\nLabelling...The label states that the injection should not be \nused if the solution is discoloured. ; ; 1 ,; \n...: /\nTerbutaline Tablets\nTerbutaline Sulphate Tablets \n. \n,.,_K\nTerbritalirie Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of terbutaline \nsulphate (C^H^NOi^, H2SO4.\nUsual strengths. 2.5 mg; 5 mg.\nA. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 20 mg \nof Terbutaline Sulphate wi"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Dissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml of wafer, \nSpeedand time. 100 rpmand 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Carry \nout the method as described under Assay beginning at the \n^drds'To 5.0 ml, add 35 ml of a buffer solution..,.”. Calculate \n^¿bntdnt of (Ci2HigNO3)2,H2SO4 in the medium from the \nabsorbance obtained from a solution of known concentration\nprepared in the same manner.\nQi Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of \n(CjjHbNOjb H2SO4. ■\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\nI . . .' \n. ;\nDisperse a quantity of powdered tablets \ncontaining: 75 mg of Terbutaline Sulphate with 35 ml of the \nmobile phase for 15 minutes and dilute to 50.0 ml with the \nrnobilephaseandfiltcm . \n<\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to \nSOiOjinl with the mobile phase. Further dilute 1.0 ml of the \nsolution to 10.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.00045 percent \nw/vof terbutaline sulphate IPRS and 0.00015 per cent w/v of \nterbutaline impurity C IPRS (l-(3,5-dihydroxyphenyl)-2- \nl(ld-dimethylethyl)amino)ethanone. IPRS) in the mobile \nphase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cnix 4.6 mm, packed with \n\\ base-deactivated octadecylsilane bonded to porous\nsilica (5 pm) (Such as Hypersil BDS C18),\n- mobile phase: dissolve 4.23 g of sodium hexane\nsulphonate in 770 ml of the 0.05 M ammonium formate\nsolution prepared by dissolving 3.15 g of ammonium \nformate in 980 ml of water, adjusted to pH 3.0 by the\n. adding about 8 ml of anhydrous formic acid and dilute\nto 1000 ml with water and 230jxA-\n), \nr flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\noimetha.no\n1. 7C spectrophotometer set at 276 nm, \n~ injection volume: 20 pl.\nThe retention time of terbutaline impurity C is about 9 minutes \n3nd;of terbutaline sulphate is about 11 minutes.\nhject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to terbutaline sulphate and \nterbutaline impurity C is not less than 2.0. \n: ?\nInject reference solution (a),(b)andthe testsolution. Rim the \nchromatogram 6 times the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area \nof any peak corresponding to terbutaline impurity C is not \nmore than twice the area of the peak due to terbutaline impurity \nC in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) \n(0.2 per cent), the area of any other secondary peak is not \nmore than the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent) and the sum \nof the areas of all the secondary peaks is not more than twice \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (0.4 per cent). Ignore any peak with \nan area less than 0.1 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.02 per \ncent) and any peaks with a retention time of less than 2.5 \nminutes.\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \nTablets.\nPowder one tablet, transfer to a 25-ml volumetric flask, add\n15 ml of 0.01 Mhydrochloric acid and shake for 10 minutes. \nDilute to volume with 0.01 M hydrochloric acid and filter, \nrejecting the first 5 ml of the filtrate. Dilute, if necessary, a \nsuitable volume of the filtrate with 0.01 M hydrochloric acid \nto produce a solution containing 0.01 per cent w/v solution of \nTerbutaline Sulphate. Carry out the method as described under \nAssay beginning at the words “To 5.0 ml add 35 ml of a buffer\nsolution....”. Calculate the content of (C12Hi9NO3)2, H2SO4 in \nthe tablet from the absorbance obtained by carrying out the \nAssay simultaneously using terbutaline sulphate IPRS.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh a quantity of the \npowder containingabout 5 mg of Terbutaline Sulphate, transfer \nto a 50-ml volumetric flask, add 30 ml of 0.01 Mhydrochloric \nacid and shake for 10 minutes. Dilute to volume with 0.01 M \nhydrochloric acid and filter, rejecting, the first 5 ml of the \nfiltrate. To 5.0 ml add 35 ini of a buffer solution prepared by \ndissolving 36.3 g of tris (hydroxymethyl) aminomethane in \n900 ml of water, adjusted to pH between 9.4 and 9.6 and adding \nsufficient water to produce 1000 ml. Add 1.0 ml of a fleshly \nprepared 2.0 per cent w/v solution of 4-aminoantipyrine, mix \nand add 1.0 ml of a freshly prepared 8.0 per cent w/v solution \nofpotassium ferricyanide with vigorous swirling and sufficient \nof the buffer solution to produce 50.0 ml. Exactly 75 seconds \nafter the addition of the potassium ferricyanide solution \nmeasure the absorbance of thé resulting* solution-at the \nmaximum at about 550 nm (2.4.7), using water as the blank.\nCalculate the content of (C^H.igNO^h, H2SO4 from the \nabsorbance obtained by carrying out the Assay simultaneously \nusing terbutaline sulphate, IPRS.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisUire.\n3763\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTESTOSTERONE PROPIONATE\nQJbA n \n; \nMol. Wt. 344.5\nTestosterone: Propionate is 3-oxoandrost-4-cn-17|3-yl \npropionate. \n:\nTestosterone Propionate contains not less than 97.0 per cent \nand not more than 103.0 per cent of CTTEA, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Androgen; anabolic steroid. ■; \n;\nDescription. A white or almost white powder or colourless \ncrystals.',,,-..; ... \n\\ ■. /...\n'G'\"'\"' ;\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). + 83.0° to+90.0\", determined \nin a 1.0 per cent w/v solution iii e/AtnW. . = , \n■„; \n;,\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(^14)^7^ \n■ ;\nTest solution... Dissolve 50 mg of the substance; under \nexamination^.methanol and. dilute to50 ml with the methanol.\nReference solution\" fa). Dissolve'2' mg of testosterone \npropionate IP RS and.2 mg-of'testosterone acetate -IPRS< in\nmethanoland dilute to 50 ml with the methanol. \n.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml \\\\G\\\\ methanol. . < ; "}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terbutaline-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terbutaline Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Terbutaline Injection\nTerbutaline Sulphate Injection\nTerbutaline Injection is a sterile solution of Terbutaline\nSulphate in Water for Injections.\nTerbutaline Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount ofterbutaline\nsulphate (CI2HI9N03)2, H2S04•\nUsual strength. 500 Ilg per m!."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Terbutaline Injection\nTerbutaline Sulphate Injection\nTerbutaline Injection is a sterile solution of Terbutaline\nSulphate in Water for Injections.\nTerbutaline Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount ofterbutaline\nsulphate (CI2HI9N03)2, H2S04•\nUsual strength. 500 Ilg per m!."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4. I7), coating the\nplate with silica gel G\nMobile phase. A mixture of 65 volumes of 2-propanol,\n25 volumes of cyclohexane and 5 volumes ofjormic acid.\nTest solution. Use the injection.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTERBUTALINE INJECTION\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution ofterbutaline\nsulphate RS in saline solution.\nApply to the plate 2 III of each solution. After development,\ndry the plate in air, spray with a 2 per cent w/v solution of\n4-aminoantipyrine in methanol. Dry the plate in air and spray\nwith a freshly prepared 8.0 per cent w/v solution ofpotassium\nferricyanide in a mixture of 4 volumes of strong ammonia\nsolution and 1 volume of water. The principal spot in the\nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to\nthat in the chromatogram obtainedwith the reference solution.\nA. Shake a quantity ofthe powdered tablets containing 20 mg\nofTerbutaline Sulphate with 50 ml of0.1 M sodium hydroxide\nIP 2010\nfor 10 minutes, dilute to 100 ml with 0.1 M sodium hydroxide\nand filter. Dilute 20 ml ofthe filtrate to 50 ml with 0.1 M sodium\nhydroxide.\nWhen examined in the range 230 urn to 360 nm (2.4.7), the\nresulting solution shows an absorption maximum at about\n296 urn.\nB. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\nthe plate with silica gel G\nMobile phase. A mixture of 65 volumes of 2-propanol,\n25 volumes of cyclohexane and 5 volumes offormic acid.\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets\ncontaining 10 mg ofTerbutaline Sulphatewith 4 ml ofa mixture\nof equal volumes of ethanol (95 per cent) and water for\n10 minutes, centrifuge and use the clear supernatant liquid.\nReference solution (a). A 0.25 per cent w/v solution of\nterbutaline sulphate RS in water.\nReference solution (b). A mixture ofequal volumes ofthe test\nsolution and reference solution (a).\nApply to the plate 2 III of each solution. After development,\ndry the plate in air, allow to stand for a few minutes in an\natmosphere saturated with diethylamine and spray with\ndiazotised nitroaniline solution. The principal spot in the\nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to\nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a)\nand the principal spot in the chromatogram obtained with\nreference solution (b) appears as a single compact spot."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH(2.4.24). 3.0to 5.0.\nOther tests. Complies with the tests stated under Parenteral\npreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Measure accurately a volume containing about 5 mg\nof Terbutaline Sulphate and add sufficient water to produce\n50.0 m!. To 5.0 ml add 35 ml ofa buffer solution prepared by\ndissolving 36.3 g of tris (hydroxymethyl) aminomethane in\n900 ml ofwater, adjusting the pH to between 9.4 and 9.6 and\nadding sufficient water to produce 1000 m!. Add 1.0 ml of a\nfreshly\nprepared\n2.0\nper\ncent\nw/v\nsolution\nof\n4-aminoantipyrine, mix and add 1.0 ml of a freshly prepared\n8.0 per cent w/v solution of potassium ferricyanide with\nvigorous swirling and sufficient of the buffer solution to\nproduce 50.0 m!. Exactly 75 seconds after the addition ofthe\npotassium ferricyanide solution measure the absorbance of\nthe resulting solution at the maximum at about 550 nm (2.4.7),\nusing water as the blank.\nCalculate the content of (CI2HI9N03)2, H2S04 from the\nabsorbance obtained by repeating the operation using a 0.01\nper cent w/v solution of terbutaline sulphate RS and\nbeginning at the words \"To 5.0 ml add 35 ml......\".\nStorage. Store protected from light, in single dose containers.\nLabelling. The label states that the injection should not be\nused\" if the solution is discoloured.\nTerbutaline Tablets\nTerbutaline Sulphate Tablets\nTerbutaline Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount ofterbutaline\nsulphate (CI2HI9N03)z, H2S04.\nUsual strengths. 2.5 mg; 5 mg.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No.2,\nMedium. 900 ml ofwater,\nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume ofthe medium and filter. Measure\nthe absorbance of the filtered solution, suitably diluted with\nthe medium if necessary, at the maximum at about 550 urn\n(2.4.7). Calculate the content of (ClzHI9N03)z.HzS04 in the\nmedium from the absorbance obtained from a solution of\nknown concentration ofterbutaline RS in the same medium.\nD. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of\n(CI2HI9N03)2.HZS04.\nUniformity of content. Comply with the test stated under\nTablets.\nPowder one tablet, transfer to a 25-ml volumetric flask, add\n1~.lTIl of 0, OJ Ml1ydrQcl}lorJc gciciand ~Qak:e f()r lQ l11illlJtes,\nDilute to volume with 0.01 M hydrochloric acid and filter,\nrejecting the first 5 ml of the filtrate. Dilute, if necessary, a\nsuitable volume ofthe filtrate with 0.01 M hydrochloric acid\nto produce a solution containing 0.01 per cent w/v solution of\n2198\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP20l0\nTerbutaline Sulphate. Carry out the method as described under\nAssay beginning at the words \"To 5.0 ml add 35 ml ofa buffer\nsolution....\". Calculate the content of(CI2HI9N03h H2S04 in\nthe tablet from the absorbance obtained by carrying out the\nAssay simultaneously using terbutaline sulphate RS.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh accurately a\nquantity ofthe powder containing about 5 mg ofTerbutaline\nSulphate, transfer to a 50-ml volumetric flask, add 30 ml of\n0.01 M hydrochloric acid and shake for 10 minutes. Dilute to\nvolume with 0.01 M hydrochloric acidand filter, rejecting the\nfirst 5 ml ofthe filtrate. To 5.0 mladd35 ml ofa buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 36.3 g of tris (hydroxymethyl)\naminomethane in 900 ml ofwater, adjusting the pH to between\n9.4 and 9.6 and adding sufficient water to produce 1000 ml.\nAdd 1.0 ml of a freshly prepared 2.0 per cent w/v solution of\n4-aminoantipyrine, mix and add 1.0 ml of a freshly prepared\n8.0 per cent w/v solution of potassium ferricyanide with\nvigorous swirling and sufficient of the buffer solution to\nproduce 50.0 ml. Exactly 75 seconds after the addition ofthe\npotassium ferricyanide solution measure the absorbance of\nthe resulting solution at the maximum at about 550 urn (2.4.7),\nusing water as the blank.\nCalculate the content of (C 12H I9N03h, H2S04 from the\nabsorbance obtained by carrying out theAssay simultaneously\nusing terbutaline sulphate RS.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terbutaline-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terbutaline Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Terbutaline Sulphate\n(Cï2H!9NO3)2,H2SO4 ' \n■ Mol. Wt. 548.7\nTerbutaline Sulphate is {^S)-2-(iert--bûtÿlamiiio)“l\"(3,5-\ndihydroxyphenyl)ethanol sulphate.\nTerbutaline Sulphate contains hot less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of'(C^Hj^Ô^, H2SO4, calculated \non the dried basis! \n/\nCategory. Beta-adrenoceptor agonist. \n>\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.: :"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Terbutaline Sulphate\n(Cï2H!9NO3)2,H2SO4 ' \n■ Mol. Wt. 548.7\nTerbutaline Sulphate is {^S)-2-(iert--bûtÿlamiiio)“l\"(3,5-\ndihydroxyphenyl)ethanol sulphate.\nTerbutaline Sulphate contains hot less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of'(C^Hj^Ô^, H2SO4, calculated \non the dried basis! \n/\nCategory. Beta-adrenoceptor agonist. \n>\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.: :"},{"name":"Identification","text":"T ; m •\nTest Amay be omitted if tests BIC and T) are carried out. Tests \nB and C may be omitted if tests A and T)are carried-out i\nA' Détermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare me specifurn with that ’ôbtàihèd'witb' ïèrirutàlinè \nsulphàteABRS or with the reference spectrum of terbutaline \nsulphate. . . ! \n>2'm T.d\\cdicriwd-c\n3760\nww.webofpharma.com\n.............................;............... IPW\nB. When examined in the-range 230 nmto 360 nm (2.4.?^ \n0.007 per cent W/v solution in 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid^df^ \nabsorption maxima at about 276 nm and 280 nm, which mayk* \nfused; absorbance at both 276 nm and. 280 nm, 0.46 to 0.49-;\nC. In the test for Related substances, the principal peak infti^ \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds \nthe peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a). ... . ; \n; \nefrf\nD. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution in carbon dioxide-free wateR \ngives reaction (A) of sulphates (2.3.1).\nTests-\nAppearance of solution. A2.0 per cent w/v solution in carôoh \ndioxide-free water is clear (2-.4.1);.absorbance;pfa 2-cm layer, \nat 400 nm, not more than 0.11 (2.4.7).\nAcidity. Dissolve 0.2 g in 10 ml of carbon dioxide-free wafer \nand titrate with. 0.01 Msodium hydroxide, using methyl red \nsolution us indicator. Not more than 1.2 ml of 0.01 M sodium \nhydroxide is required to change the colour of the solution to \nyellow. \n'\ntcrt-Buty Iamino-3,5-dihydroxyacetophenone. Absorbance of \na 2 per cent w/v solution in 0.01 M hydrochloric acid fl \nabout 330 nm, not more than 0.50 (2.4.7). \n: \n; \n.\nRelated substances: Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).- \n- \n•• •-\nTest solution. Dissolve 75 mg of the substance under \nexamination 50.0 ml ofthe mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 7.5 mg of 1 -(3,5-dlhydroxy' \nphenyl)-2-[(1, h-dimethylethyl)aminolethanpnefff.S\n(terbutaline impurity. CIRRS) and. 22.5 mg. of, terbytaline \nsulphate 1PRS in 50.0 ml ofthe mobile phase. Dilute l.Omlof\nthe solution.to. 100.0 ml with the mobile phase. ....!.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to \n50:0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 2.0 ml of the solution to \n20.0 mlwith the mobile phase. 7\nChromatographic system \nT:\n-- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packedwift\nendcapped octadecylsilane bonded to ■ poroussilica \n(5pm), . \n.\n- mobile phase: dissolve 4.23 g of sodium\nsulphonate in 770 ml of 0.05 M ammonium forfr^ \nsolution prepared by dissolving 3 ; 15 g of dmm^pd^\n; ' formate in about 980 ml of water,: adjusted topif^B:\n■ ': anhydrous formic acid and dilute to 1000 ml witfr\nand add 230 ml of methanol, \n- flow rate: 1ml per minute, .\n> - spectrophotometer set;at 276 nm,r\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nreference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \n^flon between the peaks due to terbutaline impurity C\nbutaline is not less than 2.0. \n... ..\ntreference solution (a), (b) and the test solution. Run the\ncWOihatogram6 times the retention time of the principal peak, \nj^^&hromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area\npeak due, to terbutaline impurity C is not more than\n< the. area of the peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nTerence solution (a) (0.2 per cent), the area of any other \n^ijndary peaks is not more than area of the principal peak in \nt^tfiromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (6.2\n;:^^h6^hd the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks\nQ^etthan terbutaline impurity C is not more than twice the \n^abf the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nTerence solution (b) (0.4 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area \n[ess-thani 0.1 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (0.02 per cent)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"^yy,ihetals(2.3.13). Mix 1.6'gwith0.6 got'anhydrous sodium \n^ft^Ie andignite without melting the sodium sulphate. Cool, \na4d3;jnl of 2.M, hydrochloric acid,.boil and dilute to 50 ml \nwith water. Cool and filter, 25 ml of the filtrate complies with \nthe liiriit fest for heavy metals, Method A (2 5 ppm).\nLosson drying (2.4.19). Not more than0.5 percent, determined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 3 hours. .. ;\nAssay., Dissolve 0.5 g in 60 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \ndciii widi, me aid of heat. Titrate with 0.1 Mperchloric acid, \ndetermining the end-pqint.potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry \nqufa blank titration. \n. \n’ ,\nLibi of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.05487 g of\n(C12H,9NO3)2,H2SO4.\nStorage, Stpre.protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/teriparatide-concentrateci-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Teriparatide Concentrateci Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Allergen products are. pharmaceutical preparations derived \nfrom extracts of naturally occurring source materials containing \nallergens, which are substances that lead to and/ or provoke \nallergic reactions. The allergenic components are most often \nof a proteinaceous nature. Allergen products are intended for \nin vivo diagnosis or treatment of allergic diseases attributed \nto these allergens.\nAllergen products are available as finished products and as \nfinished products used as customized therapeutic \npreparations. Allergen products are generally presented as \nparenteral preparations, eye preparations, preparations for \ninhalation, preparations for oral use, sublingual preparations \nor preparations for skin tests.\nFor diagnostic use, allergen products are usually prepared as \nunmodified extracts in a 50 per cent V/V solution of glycerol \nfor skin-prick testing. For intradermal diagnosis,or for \nprovocation tests by nasal, ocular or bronchial administration, \nsuitable dilutions of allergen products may be prepared by \ndilution of aqueous or glycerinated extracts, or by \nreconstitution of unmodified freeze-dried extracts.\nFor specific immunotherapy, allergen products are usually \nunmodified extracts. ."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Teriparatide Concentrateci Solution\nRecombinant Human Parathyroid Hormone ..\n(rhPTH!‘34)\nSVSEIQLMHN LGKHLNSMER VEWLRKKLQD VHNF\nC181H291N55O51S2 \nMol. Wt. approx. 4117.8 Da\nTeriparatide is a biologically active N-terminal fragment of \nendogenous (native) human parathyroid hormone produced \nby recombinant DNA technology in bacteria as host cells. It is \nproduced under conditions designated to minimize microbial \ncontamination of the product.\nCategory. Bone forming agent. \n: ;\nDescription. A clear and colourless solution\nHost cell-derived proteins. Not more than 100 ppm.\nHost cell-derived DNA. Not more than 10 ng per dose.\nEach batch of Teriparatide complies with the following\nA; The biological activity of teriparatide is estimated to \nbeequipotent to that teriparatide IPRS based on its \ncomparativeability to stimulate the accumulation ofCAMPih \nUMR-106 cells with respect to the appropriate reference\nstandard of teriparatide: \n;:;a\nDetermination of the biological activity of teriparatide is based \non the stimulation of adenylatecycl asev activity in-the fdt \nosteosarcoma, cell line UM R 106. Activation of the PTH \nreceptor initiates a cascade event which culminates in an \nintracellular rise in cAMP concentration.\nThe method uses cAMP end-point measurement by ELISA. \nIncubate UMR-106 cells in tissue culture plate until an even \nmonolayer is observed. Màiritain the cells in serum free media \nfor 18 to 26 hours in order to decrease the. levels of endogenous \ncAMP. Suitably wash thé cells and incubate with varying \ndilutions of test and reference preparations for up to8 dilutions \nfor the suitable Time. . Collect the cell lysate containing thé \ncAMP and determine the cAMP accumulation by using-a\n; suitable ELISA or suitable detection assay. , \n-rn.\nAnalyze the data by fitting^ a sigmoidal dose-response curVè \nto the data obtained and by using à suitable statistical method, \nfor example the 4-parameter or parallel line model. \n:\nThe relative potency is not less than 60 per cent and hot morè \nthan 120 per cent of the stated potency. ' \n■ b\nB. Determine by Peptide mapping (2.3.47). .\nTest solution. Dilute the preparation tinder examination s \n0.05. M tris-hydrochloridè buffer ^solution pH 7.0 to obtain ca \nconcentration of 1 mg per ml and transfer 500, pliof it toi a clean\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nTERIPARATIDE CONCENTRATED SOLUTION\nsample container. Add 10 pl of 1 mg per. ml trypsin solution (in \n.water) and incubate for 24 hours at .37C:. Stop the reaction by\nfreezing at or below-20°. \n■■■\nReference solution. Dissolve thé content of teriparatideIFRS\nin 0.05 M tris-hydrochloride buffer solution pH 7.0 tb obtain \na .concentration of 1 ; mg. per mb and transfer 500 pl of it to a \nclean sample, container. Add, 10 pl of 1 mg per ml trypsin \nsolution (in water) and incubate for 24 hours at 37°. Stop the\nreaction by freezing at or below-20°.\nChromatographic system\n■ - a stainless steel colùmn 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilyl silica gel (3 pm) with a pore size of 20 ran, \n- mobile phase: A.. 0.05 per cent v/v solution of\ntrifluorpacetic acid,\nB. to 800 ml of acetonitrile, add 0.5 ml of \ntrifluoroacetic acid and-200 nil of acetonitrile,\n- a linear gradient programme using the conditions given\nbelow, . \n...\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 214 nm, ... \n.....\n- injection volume: 50 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(ih'min.)\n(per cent v/v) :\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n95\n' VïïA\n30\n45\n55\n. 30 ■ ;\n. 0 \n' ’\n100\n\" . 35 ’\n0\n- 100 ' '\n35\n95\n5\n45 .\n. 95.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution shows \neight prominent peaks.as given in Fig.-l■. .. .. ■\nInject the test solution. The' profile of the chromatogram \nobtained with test solution1 corresponds' to that of the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solutiori.\nC. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as describedin \ntest for assay. In the test for assay, the principle peak in \nthechromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorrespondstothe peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe referencesolution. \nr? \n,C ?"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related Impurities. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). ’ \n...; \n;\nsolution.. Dilute the preparation under examination \nwithmobilc phase.A.to.obtaima protein concentration of 0.25 \nmgperml. \n, ,\nReference solution. To a volume of the test solution, add\nasuitable volume of. 0.06 per cent hydrogen peroxide and \n3 mM acetic acid to. obtain a final, protein concentration of \n0.88 mgper ml. Allow to. stand at room.temperature for.l hour. \nAdd about 30, mg ofL-methipnine per ml .of solution and mix.\nChromatographic system \n;\n— ■ a stainless steel column-15 cmx'4.6 mm, packed\n■ withoctadecylsilyl silica gel (3 pm) with a pore size of\n20nm,\n- mobile phase: A. 0.1 per. cent v/v. solution of\ntrifluoroacetic acid, ; \n. - ■ . \n.\n4697\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTERIPARATIDE CONCENTRATED : SOLUTION \nIP 2022\n; \nB. to 800 ml of acetonitrile and 200 ml of\nwater for chromatography, - ■ ■\n- a gradient programme using ihe conditions given below,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 214 nm,\n- injection volume: 40 pl.\nTime ; \n' (in min )\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n0.01\n. : ' 76 :\n. 24\n(per cent v/v)\n! (per ceiit v/v)\n26\n. ■ ■ ; 66.5 ■.■■■\n33.5\n26.01\n60\n40 \n'\n30\n60\n40\n30.01\n76\n24\n40\n76\n' ' L / 24 ? /\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the reference solution relative \nretention time of three peaks corresponding to oxidized \nTeriparatide with reference to principal peak arc 0.79,0.66 arid \n0.46.\nInject the test solution. The chromatogram obtained with the \ntest solution corresponds to that obtained with the reference \nsolution. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, \nthe sum of the area of any peaks other than the principal peak \nis not greater than 3.0 per cent of the total area of the peaks. \nThe sum of the area of peaks corresponding to the. oxidized \nform is not greater than 2.0 per cent of thetotal .area of the \npeaks.\nAcetate; Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) using \nthe normalisation procedure.\nStore the solutions at 2° to 8° and use them within 72 hours.\nTest solution. Dissolve the preparation under examination in \nmobile phase to obtain a concentration of 5 mg per ml.\nReference solution. Dissolve separately 100 mg, 200 mg and \n300 mg of anhydrous sodium acetate in mobile phase and \ndilute to 100 ml with mobile phase. Further dilute 1 ml of each \nsolution to 10 ml with the mobile phase to prepare a standard \ncurve with acetate concentrations in the range of0.072-0.216 \nmg per ml.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 9.0 mm, packed with ion\nexclusion resin (7.5 pm), \nj\n- mobile phase: 0.5 per cent v/v solution of dilute\nsulphuric acid,\n- flow rate: 1 nil per minute, \ni\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm.\n- injection volume: 100 pl,\n- runtime: 1.5 times the retention time of acetate.\nInject each concentration of reference solutions in triplicate.\nThe test is not valid unless (i) the area of the principal peak\nobtained with the repeatability of triplicate injections of 200 \nmg concentration reference solution has the relative standard \ndeviation of not more than 1.25 per cent; (ii) the correlation \ncoefficient (r) calculated for the standard curve is not less \nthan 0.999. .. .\nInject the test solution. The retention time of acetate is about\n10 minutes. Plot peak areas versus injected acetate content \narid; perform linear regression to create a standard curve. \nCalculate the acetate content using the standard curve arid \nthe area of the peak due to acetate in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution. The acetate content is not \nmore than 5.0 per cent..\nChibride. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) using \nthe normalisation procedure.\nTest solution. Dissolve the preparation under examination in \nwater to obtain a concentration of 1 mg per ml.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 165.9 mg of sodium chloride, \npreviously dried at 105° for 30 minutes, in water and dilute to\n100 ml with the same solvent.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 150 mg of sodium nitrite in \nwater and dilute,to 100 ml with the same solvent. Mix 1.0 ml of \nthe solution and 2.5 ml of reference solution (a) and dilute to\n100 ml with water.\nReference solutions. Dilute reference solution (a) with water \nto prepare a standard curve with at least 4 concentrations in \nthe range of 10 to 40 pg per ml.\nChromatographic system \n’\n- a stainless steel pre-column 5 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with\nstrongly basic anion-exchange resin (15 pm),\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with\nstrongly basic anion-exchange resin (15 pm),\n- mobile phase: dissolve 285.7 mg of sodium hydrogen\ncarbonate and 381.6 mg of anhydrous sodiuni \ncarbonate in water and dilute to 2 liters with the same \nsolvent, \n:.V;\n- flow rate: 2 ml per minute,\nconductivity detector: use a self regenerating anion \nsuppressor at 100 mA, \n. a\n- injection volume: 50 pl,\n- run time: 6 times the retention time of chloride. A\nInject reference ¡solution (b). The test is not valid unless (i) the \nresolution between the peaks due to chloride and nitrite is not \nless than 1.5; (ii) the symmetry factor for the peaks due to \nchloride and nitrite is not more than 2.0 (iii) the area of the \nprincipal peak due to chloride and nitrite obtained with the \nrepeatability of five injections has the relative standard \ndeviation of not more than 2.0 per cent; (iv) the correlation; \ncoefficient (r) calculated for the standard curve is not less;\nthan.0-999. ,\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nTERIPARATIDE INJECTION\nInject the test solution. The retention time of chloride is about\n1.6 minutes and of nitrite is about 1,8 minutes. Plot peak areas \nversus injected chloride content and perform linear regression \nto create a standard curve. Calculate the chloride content using \nthe standard curve and the area of the peak due to chloride in \nthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution. The chloride \ncontent is not more than 4.0 per cent.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test C may be omitted if tests A and B are carried out. Tests B \nmay be omitted if tests A and C are carried out;\nA. The biological activity of teriparatide is estimated to be \nequipotent to that teriparatide IPRS based on its comparative \nability to stimulate, the accumulation of cAMP.in UMR-106 \ncells with respect to the teriparatide IPRS.\nDetermination of the biological activity of teriparatide is based \non the stimulation of adenylate cyclase activity in the rat \nosteosarcoma cell line UMR-106. Activation of the PTH \nreceptor initiates a cascade event which culminates in an \nintracellular rise in cAMP concentration.\n4699\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTERIPARATIDE injection\nIP- 2022\nThe ¿method ¡uses cAMIb end-point measurement by ELISA. \nIncubate UMR-106 cells in tissue culture plate until an even \nmon<)layer is .obserycd. Maintain the cells inserum free media \nfor 18 to 26.hours in order to decrcase.the levels of endogenous \ncAMP. Suitably wash the cells and;.incubate with varying \ndilutions of test and reference preparations for up to ^dilutions \nfor the suitable time. Collect the cell lysate containing the \ncAMP and determine the c AMP accumulation by using ELISA \nor suitable detection assay. \n' \n1 :\nAnalyze the data by’fitting a-sigmoidal dbs'efrespbrise curve \nto the data obtained and by-using a suitablestatisticai method,\nfor example the 4-parameter or parallel line model, .\nThe relative potency is not less than 75 percent and not more \nthan 125 percent of the stated potency. ;- .\nB. Determine by Liquid chromatography (2.4J4) as‘described \nin assay. In assay, the retention time of the Teriparatide peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorresponds to that of the peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution.\nC. Determine by sodium dodecyl sulphate polyacrylamide gel \nelectrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) (2.4.12) under non-reducing \nconditions.\nGel dimensions. 1.5 mm thick, ,10 cnrx .10.5 cm. . -,\nResolving gel. 20 per cent acrylamide. Mix the components\nin the order shown: '\nSolution \n' \ncomponents '\nComponent volume (ml)\nL; per gel volume of 7.5 ml\nWater,-\n1.20\n. ■ 3.00 \n........ . '\n50 per cent acrylamide solution*\n■■ 2-49 .7- ; . .\nGlycerol\n?• - 079 \n-\nTris-SDS solution (pH 8.45)\n10 per cent APS**\n0.025 \n;\nTEMED***\n0.007 \n■- s. •\n*50 per cent acrylamide solution: 50 per cent acrylamide/bisacrylamidé \n(50:1) solution. \n; \n...\n**10 per cent APS: a 100 g per litre solution of ammonium persulphate.\nPrepare freshly.\n***TEMED: tetramethylethylenediamine.\nStacking gel. , 4 per cent acrylamide: Mix the components , in \nthe order shown. \n. \n. .\nSolution \nComponent volume (ml)\ncomponents :I per gel volume of 5.0 ml\nWater\nn* ? 0.65\nTris SDS solution (pH 8.45).,\n3.05\n30 per cent'acrylamide soliifio\nMethodA\n10 per cent APS**\n■ \n,0.05,.; . \n.\nTEMED*** -\n- ,-■■■= 0.01 ■ :\n4700\n*30 per cent acrylamide solution: 30 per cent acryiainide/bisacrylamide \n(29,2:0.8). solution. . .. .. \n; . . . \n.. .. \n. .. \n; \n... ;\n** 10 per.cent APS: a 100 g per liter solution, of ammonium persulphate. \nPrepare freshly....................... , \n< . .\n* TEM El) :. tetra m eih y 1 ethy len ed jam j ne.., \n;\nTri's-SDS solution: A mixture óf 0-3 per dent sodium dodecyl \nsulphate in 3.0 M tris-hydrochlórìde buffér soluti on, pH 8745 .\nSample buffer,: A mixture of 0,2 M iris+hydrOehloride buffer\nsolution pH 6.8 containing 48 per cent' glycerol, 2 per -cent \nsodium dodecyl sulphate, .0,4 mg per. ml.coomassie G230.\nTest solution. Dilute the preparation; under .examination Tn \nsample buffer to obtain a concentration of 8 .pg.per well...\nReference solution (a). Asolutipn of protein molecular weight \nmarkers suitable for calibrating SDS-polyacrylamide gels.\nReference solution (b). Dilute teriparatide IP RS in water,to \nobtain a concentration of 0.25 mg per ml. To 38.4 pl of this \nsolution add 15 pl of sample buffer. Dilute to 60 pl with water.\nReference solution (c). Dilute the test solution in sample buffet \nto obtain a concentration of 8 pg per well.1 \n;\nSample treatment. Boil for 2 minutes.\n‘Application: \n................ . \" '\nWell Solution(s) \nr Volume (pl)\n1 \nReference solution (a) \n> ■ ■■ -■ \n50\n2 \n' Reference solution (b) \n.........50\n3 \nTest solution \n' 50\n4 \n. Reference solution (c) ; \n50\nCathode buffer. 0,1 M tris and 0.1 M tricine and 0; 1 per cent \nsodium dodecyl sulphate. Do not adjust pH. ' : '\nAnode buffer. 0.2 M tris-hydrochloridefufSey, solution pH\n8.9. \n' \n. \n- \n/\nDetection. By silver staining. \nJ .\nThe test is not valid unless; a band is seen in the ( \nelectropherogram obtained with.reference solution (c)\nThe electropherogram obtained with the test solution shows \na single broad band corresponding in position and intensity / \nto the single broad band obtained with the reference f \nsolution (b).\nThe tests for identification are performed as late as possible in \nthe manufacturing process. In the case of products used on a \nnamed patient basis, the control is performed on the active\nsubstance and/ or at the intermediate stage between the active \nsubstance and the finished product.\nIdentity is confirmed by comparison with the IHRP using' \nprotein profiling by appropriate methods (for example, sodium \ndodecyl sulphate-polyaciylamide gel electrophoresis (2.4.12).\nIn exceptional cases if no IHRP is available, a representative \nbatch may be used to confirm identity.\nIdentity may also be confirmed by comparison with individual \nallergen specific reference standards, when available.\nThe tests are performed as late as possible in the \nmanufacturing process. In the case of products used on à \nnamed patient basis, the control is performed on the active \nsubstance and/ or at the intermediate stage between the active \nsubstance and the finished product. \n'\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 5 per cent for freeze- \ndriedproducts.In the case of oral lyophilizates the water; \ncontent may be higher than 5 per cent where, justified and. \nauthorised. \n'\nSterility (2.2.11). Allergen products presented as parente"},{"name":"Definition","text":"Allergen products are. pharmaceutical preparations derived \nfrom extracts of naturally occurring source materials containing \nallergens, which are substances that lead to and/ or provoke \nallergic reactions. The allergenic components are most often \nof a proteinaceous nature. Allergen products are intended for \nin vivo diagnosis or treatment of allergic diseases attributed \nto these allergens.\nAllergen products are available as finished products and as \nfinished products used as customized therapeutic \npreparations. Allergen products are generally presented as \nparenteral preparations, eye preparations, preparations for \ninhalation, preparations for oral use, sublingual preparations \nor preparations for skin tests.\nFor diagnostic use, allergen products are usually prepared as \nunmodified extracts in a 50 per cent V/V solution of glycerol \nfor skin-prick testing. For intradermal diagnosis,or for \nprovocation tests by nasal, ocular or bronchial administration, \nsuitable dilutions of allergen products may be prepared by \ndilution of aqueous or glycerinated extracts, or by \nreconstitution of unmodified freeze-dried extracts.\nFor specific immunotherapy, allergen products are usually \nunmodified extracts. ."},{"name":"Production","text":"Source Materials\nSource materials for the preparation1 of allergen products are \nproducts of animal or vegetable oriigin, mostly pollens, moulds, \nmites, animal epithelia and outgrowths (such as hair and \nfeathers) and/or dander, hymenoptera venoms, insects.\nThe source materials are defined by their origin, nature, method \nof collection or production and pretreatment. Control methods \nand acceptance.criteria relating to identity and purity are \nestablished: The acceptance criteria must ensure the \nconsistency of the allergenic source material from qualitative \nand quantitative point of view. The source materials are stored \nunder defined conditions justified by stability data.\nThe collection or production, as .well as the handling of the \nsource materials are such that uniform composition is ensured \nas far as possible from .batch to batch.\nPollens. Potential chemical contaminants, such as pesticides, \nheavy metals, must be minimised. The content of foreign pollen\nmust be limited to 1 per cent of total mixed pollens and 0.5 per \ncent of any individual pollen as determined by a microscopic \nparticle count. Detectable mould.spores must not exceed 1 per \ncent. The contamination with particles of plant origin other \nthan pollen must be kept to a minimum- The maximum allowed \ncontamination must be justified.\nMoulds. Biologically active contaminants such as mycotoxins \nin moulds must be minimised and any presence justified. \nAppropriate measures, have to be implemented to avoid \ncontamination by foreign mould strains. Care must be taken \nto minimize any allergenic constituents of the media used for \nthe cultivation of moulds as source materials. Culture media \nthat contain substances of human or animal origin must be \njustified and, when required, must be suitably treated to ensure\nthe inactivation or elimination of possible transmissible agents \nof disease,\nThe production method is validated to demonstrate that \nallergen products obtained from moulds and intended for \nparenteral administration, if tested, would comply with the \ntest for abnormal toxicity for immunosera and vaccines for \nhumanuse (2.2.1).\nMites. Appropriate measures have to be implemented to avoid \ncontamination by foreign mite strains. Care must be taken to \nminimise any allergenic constituents of the media used for the \ncultivation of mites as source materials. Culture media that \ncontain substances of human or animal origin mustbejustified \nand, when required, must be suitably treated to ensure the \ninactivation or elimination of possible transmissible agents of \ndisease.\nAnimal epithelia and outgrowths and/or dander. They are \nobtained from healthy animals selected to avoid, possible \ntransmissible agents of disease.\nHymenoptera venoms. The species of hymenoptera from which \nthe venom is extracted is identified and specified. The methods \nof insect collection and venom extraction are described and \nmust ensure that the source material is of proper quality.\nAllergen products are generally obtained by extraction, and \nmay be purified, from the source materials using appropriate \nmethods shown to preserve the allergenic properties of the \ncomponents. Allergens for which there are not enough patients \nto determine the total allergenic activity in vivo or in vitro, the \nextraction ratio indicating the relative proportions (m/V) of \nallergenic source materials and solvents is a minimum \nrequirement. Allergen products presented as parenteral \npreparations, eye preparations, preparations for inhalation and \npreparations for skin testing are manufactured under aseptic \nconditions.\nIn the manufacture, packaging, storage and distribution of \nallergen products intended for administration by other routes, \nsuitable measures are taken to ensure their microbial quality;\n4705\nwww.webofpharma.com\nALLERGEN PRODUCTS \nIP 2022\nrecommendations on this aspect are provided, in chapter 2.2,9. \nMicrobial contamination in non-sterile product. ■\nAll allergen preparations áre manufactured under conditions \ndesigned to minimise exogenous and endogenous enzymatic \ndegradation.\nAny purification procedure is designed to minimise the \ncontent of any potential irritant low molecular mass \ncomponents and hbn-allergenic components.\nAllergen products may contain suitable antimicrobial \npreservative, the nature and concentration of which have to \nbe justified.\nThe manufacturing process comprises various stages:\n— source material;\n—- active substance: it is generally a unmodified allergen\nextract; where applicable it is stored under conditions \nensuring its stability, for example freeze-dried;\n— finished product;\n— In-House Reference Preparation. An appropriate\nrepresentative preparation is selected as the In-House \nReference Preparation (IHRP), characterised and used to \nverify batch-to-batch consistency. The IHRP is stored in \nsuitably sized aliquots under conditions ensuring its \nstability, for example freeze-dried.\n— Characterisation of the In-House Reference\nPreparation. The extent of characterisation of the IHRP \ndepends on the nature of the allergenic source material, \nknowledge of the allergenic components and availability \nof suitable reagents, as well as the intended use. The \ncharacterised IHRP is used'as reference in the batch \ncontrol of active allergen substances or intermediate and, \nif possible, in the batch control of finished products."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tesfs","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tesfs"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tesfs \n. ■> --wm v- ■\nCategory. Antimigraine.\nWater (2.3.43). 5.0 to 8.0 per cent, determined on l.O gina I \nmixture of equal volumes of acetic acid and formamide^ I\npH (2.4.24), 4.5 to 7.0, determined in a solution constituted as\nDescription. A white to cream, crystalline powder.\ndirected on the label,, in Water for Inj ections.. : : \n<"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tesfs \n. ■> --wm v- ■\nCategory. Antimigraine.\nWater (2.3.43). 5.0 to 8.0 per cent, determined on l.O gina I \nmixture of equal volumes of acetic acid and formamide^ I\npH (2.4.24), 4.5 to 7.0, determined in a solution constituted as\nDescription. A white to cream, crystalline powder.\ndirected on the label,, in Water for Inj ections.. : : \n<"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Assay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2:4.14). \n|\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under' Parental\nTest sdlution. Dissolve 50 mg bf the 'substance under | \nexamination in 50.0 ml of the mobile phase. Dilute 2Q.0 mlof | \nthe solution to 100:0 ml with the mobile phase. \nI\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nPreparations (Inj ections). \nr\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with zolmitriptan \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of zolmitriptan.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2:3). Not more than 17.0 Endotoxin\nUnits per mg of Zoledronic acid.\nReference solution. A 0.02 per cent w/v. solution of zoledronic I \nacidIPRS in the mobile phase.. \n. I\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to. the peak in the\nSterility (2.2.11 ). Complies with the test for sterility?;\nchromatogram obtained with; the reference solution. <\nUse the chromatographic system as described under Related I \nsubstances. L \n................. \n1\nAssay.: Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve, the contents of;sealed container in \nsufficient mobile phase to give a concentration 0.16 mg per ml.\n|n the Assay?the principal peak m the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to that of the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\ninjeet the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and \nIhe tailing factor is not more than 2.0. \n. \n.\nZOLMITRIPTAN TABLETS\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than 5 times the àrea of thè principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained .with reference solution \n(0.5 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 15 times the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution (1.5 \nper cent). ' \n' '\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Nasal \nPreparations. \n, . <\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). ■:\nTest solution. Dilute a quantity of nasal spray containing \n50 mg of Zolmitriptan to 50.0 ml with thé mobile phase. Dilute \n5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of \nzolmitriptan IPRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile .phase:, a mixture of 80. volumes of 0.05 M\nammonium acetate in 0,1 per cent, v/v of ammonia \nsolution, adjusted to pH 4.0 with trifluoroacetic acid,\n10 volumes of methanol, and 10 volumes of acetonitrile, \n- flow rate : 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 225 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. Thé test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2,0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 percent.\nInject the reference solution and the test, solution.,\nCalculate the content ofCI6H2iN3O2 in themasal spray\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nLabelling. The label states that the amount of active ingredient \ndelivered by each actuation of the valve and the number of \ndeliveries available from the container.\nZolmitriptan Tablets\nZolmitriptan Tablets contain not less than 95.Ó per cent and \nnot more than 105,0 per cent of The Jstàtéd: amount of \nzolmitriptan,Ci6H2]N3O2. \n: ' :i,: '; -\nUsual strengths. 1 mg; 2.5 mg; 5 mg. f\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in thé chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to thé peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b). \n;\n4001\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZOLMITRIPTAN TABLETS"},{"name":"Tests","text":"r- <\nInject the reference solution; .The test as .not valid unless the | \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is notmore I \nthan 2.0 per cent, :: 'j-! . v-.; \n.-c;:..--1 I\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\nRefers rices o Iutieri. A 0.016 per cent w/v solution of zoledronic \nacidIPRSin the mobile phase. .\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. ? I\nTest solution: Dissolve 50 mg of the substance: under \nexamination in 50.0 ml of mobile phase A. ; : ;\nChromatographic system\nCalculate the content of C5H10N2O7P2. \n: r I\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nReference solution (a). A 0.001 per cent w/v solution of \nzolmitriptan IPRS in mobile phase A.\n' oct^ \nporous silica (5 pm) (Such\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture. ■ \n: I\nas Hypersil BDS),\n- mobile phase: mix 1 ml of triethylamine with 500 ml of\nLabelling. The label states whether or not the material is | \nintended for use in the manufacture of parenteral preparations. |\nReference solution' (bf' A 0.0001 per cent w/v solution of \nzolmitriptan'impurity C IPRS in reference sdlution (a).\nwater, adjusted to pH'3.2 with orthophosphoric acid,\n- flów rate: T ml per minute,\nChromatographic system : \n! -j!\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nZoledronic Acid Injection J !\n; - spectrophotometer set at 215 nm,\n~ injectionvolume: 20 pl.\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous, silica. (5, jmi) (Such\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nasX-TerraC18), \nu.\n- column temperature. 40°,\nZoledronic Acid Ihjcction is prepared by:dissolving thé | \ncontents bf a sealed container containing Zôlédrtiriic Acid I \nwith or without auxiliary substances inthe requisite amount I \nof wafer for ihj ection as directed on the label, A ; ' ■•; \n|\n-■ mobile phase. A. a mixture of 70volumes of buffer\nsolution prepared by dissolving 0.3>gVof potassium \ndihydrogen phosphate iwidif .5 gofsodium dihydrogen \nphosphate in 1000 mt of mrfer, adjusted to pH 9.8 with\nInject the reference solution and’the test solution?\nCalculatethc content of C5Hi0NT2O7P2 in the Injection.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements I \nClarity of solution and Particulate, matter stated under \nParenteralPrepardtiöns (Injections). , ’ \"/'I\" \nJ\nsodium hydroxide solution, 20 volumes of methanol \nand 10 volumes of acetonitrile,\nStorage. Store the sealed container at a temperature not \ndeeding 30°. Use the prepared solution within the period \n^commended by the manufacturer and stored strictly in \nacc°rdanc& with the instructions of the manufacturer.\nB. a mixture of 30 volumes of buffer\n; solutionand70 volumes.ofacetonitrile,\nUsual strength; 4 mg per vial. \n-m ig-r' ■ \n|\n399R\n3999\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZOLMITRIPTAN\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n~ a gradient programme using the conditions given below,\n- spectrophotometer set at 225 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nTime Mobile phase A ; Mobile phase B\n(in min) (per cent v/v) . (per cent v/v)\n0 \n. 100 \n0\n50 \n0 \n100\n55 \n100 \n0\nName \n,\nRelative \nretention time\nZolmiltriptan impurity B1\n0.26\nZolmiltriptan impurity A2\n0.34 \n•\nZolmiltriptan impurity D3\n0.54\nZolmiltriptan impurity C4\n0.80\nZolmiltriptan ......\n1.0\nI(s)-2-amino-3-(4-aminophenyl)propanol, .; \n..\n2(s)-4-(4-aminobenzyl)-1.3-oxozoIidin-2-one,\n3N-methyI-2-['5-(2-òxó'-l’3oxózóìidin’4-yl-niethyl)-lH-itìdol-3- \nyljethyl amine, \nc1. \nA;\n4 (s)-4-(-4-mtrobehzyI)-l .3-oxozólidin-2-one.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to zolmitriptan impurity C\nand zolmitriptan is not less than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than 0.15 times the area of the \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0.15 per cent) and the sum. of areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 0.5 times > the area of the: peak in the \nchropiatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0.5 per cent).\n(R)-4-[(3[2-(diniethylamino)ethyl]-lH-indol-5-yllmethyl)J- \n2-oxa^olidinpne (R-isomer). Not more than 0.15 per cent.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography.(2,4.14). \n. f\nTési solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in mòbile phase and dilute tb 20.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference solution (a), \n0,05 per pent w/v solution of\nzolmitriptan 1PRS in the mobile phase. : :\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 7.5 mg of R-isomer in\n100.0 ml of themobilephase. Dilute 0.5 ml of the solution to \n50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (c). Dilute 1.0 ini, each of, reference solution \n(a) and reference solution (b) to 10,0 ml withthemobilephase.\n4ooo\nChromatographic system\n- \na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.0 mm; packed^ t\nimmobilised a-1 acid glycoprotein on spherical s}|- |\nparticles, (Such as Chiral-AGP) (5 pm), \n. ■'\nmobile phase, dissolve 6.8'> g of potass ium dikydrOp-W \nphosphate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted to pH 6.9yath \nI\ntriethylam ine, \n' ‘3: (. ; Aa '■■■■ / I\n- flowrate: 0.8 ml per minute, •\n■ \nspectrophotometer set at 222 nm, ■ \nI\n— injectionvolume:20pl. \n■ -■■ ■'■'-a\nInject reference solution (c). The test is not valid urilesst^ I \nresolution between the peaks due to //-isomer and zolroitri^ I \nis not less than 1.5. \n- \n'a ' ■ ’ '-A \n' I\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solutibri. \nI\nCalculate the content of R-isomer. \n■ : ■ I\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 2.0 g complies with the limit test er I\nheavy metals, Method B (10 ppm). \n’ p I\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent. \nI\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 percent, determined I\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°. A . I\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). ~ \nI\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 nig of the substance under I\nexamination in 50.0 ml of mobile phase A. Dilute 5.0 mlof the \nsolution to 50.0 ml w"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/testosterone-propionate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Testosterone Propionate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Testosterone Propionate Injection\nTestosterone Propionate Injection is a sterile solution of \nTestosterone Propionate in Ethyl Oleate or any ¿other spitable \nester, in a suitable fixed oil or in any mixture of these. .. ;;\nTestosterone Propionate Injection; contains not less than \n92.5 per cent and not more than 107.5 per cent of the stated \namount of testosterone propionate,\n■Nff f; ■ , ■' {■ ('f: ’ : : ‘'.p' i ’•?’ T . .-'a ’;. : \n' • • • •': . 7 '«•.• ' • - Hi'.' \n'\nUsual strength. 25 mg per • ml; 50 mg per ml. \n,; n ji a;inl\nwww.webofpharma.com\nnlÉìvea volunic containing 50 mg of Testosterone Propionate\n(40?t0 60?) and extract with three \n-;|g|ssl||g^ieach of 8 ml, of a mixture of 7 volumes of glacial\n$^A^v# and 3 volumes of water. Wash the combined \n■ with 10 mJof light petroleum (40° to 6(f), dilute with\nthe; solution becomes turbid,, allow to stand for \nm ice and filter. The precipitate, after washing with\nat 105°, complies with the following tests.\nT^^rrnlne by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nrapare the ¡spectrum with that obtained with testosterone \n^^PnatedPRS or with the reference spectrum of\nl^tetone propionate. \nc \n;\ng-ffétéfinine by thin-layer chromatography (2:4.17), coating \n^IJt^ithsihhtìfgefG. \n;\nSotyent mixture. A mixture of .90 volumes of light petroleum\nan<^ 1 $ v°himes of liquid paraffin...\nMobile phase. A mixture of 30 volumes of water and \n¿Oyp.lumes of glacial acetic acid..\nfesfs&l&ioh; Dissolve 25 mg of the residue in 10 ml of the \nsolvent mixture. ;\nReference solution (a). A 0.25' per cent w/v solution ,of \ntestosterone propionate IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Mix equal volumes of the test solution \nand reference solution (a). \n- -\nPlace the dry plate in a tank containing a shallow layer of the \nsolvent mixture, allow the solvent mixture to ascend to the \ntop, remove the plate from. the. tank and. allow the solvent to \nevaporate. Use within 2 hours, with the flow of the mobile \nphase in the direction in which the aforementioned treatment \nwas done.\nApply tfr the plate 2 p.1 of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to fise 12 cm. Dry the plate in a current of warm air, allow \nthe solvent to evaporate, heat at 120° for d 5 minutes and spray \nthe hot plate with ethanolic sulphuric acid (20 per cent v/v). \nHeat at 120° for a further 10 minutes, allow to cool and examine \nin daylight and under ultraviolet light at 365 nm. The principal \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncpnespqnds ,to that in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a), The principal spot in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) appears as a single,\ncompact spot A.\nTests . \n............\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral\npreparations (Injections).\nAssay. To an measured volume containing about Ò.T g of \nTestosterone Propionate add sufficient chloroform to produce"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Testosterone Propionate Injection\nTestosterone Propionate Injection is a sterile solution of \nTestosterone Propionate in Ethyl Oleate or any ¿other spitable \nester, in a suitable fixed oil or in any mixture of these. .. ;;\nTestosterone Propionate Injection; contains not less than \n92.5 per cent and not more than 107.5 per cent of the stated \namount of testosterone propionate,\n■Nff f; ■ , ■' {■ ('f: ’ : : ‘'.p' i ’•?’ T . .-'a ’;. : \n' • • • •': . 7 '«•.• ' • - Hi'.' \n'\nUsual strength. 25 mg per • ml; 50 mg per ml. \n,; n ji a;inl\nwww.webofpharma.com\nnlÉìvea volunic containing 50 mg of Testosterone Propionate\n(40?t0 60?) and extract with three \n-;|g|ssl||g^ieach of 8 ml, of a mixture of 7 volumes of glacial\n$^A^v# and 3 volumes of water. Wash the combined \n■ with 10 mJof light petroleum (40° to 6(f), dilute with\nthe; solution becomes turbid,, allow to stand for \nm ice and filter. The precipitate, after washing with\nat 105°, complies with the following tests.\nT^^rrnlne by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nrapare the ¡spectrum with that obtained with testosterone \n^^PnatedPRS or with the reference spectrum of\nl^tetone propionate. \nc \n;\ng-ffétéfinine by thin-layer chromatography (2:4.17), coating \n^IJt^ithsihhtìfgefG. \n;\nSotyent mixture. A mixture of .90 volumes of light petroleum\nan<^ 1 $ v°himes of liquid paraffin...\nMobile phase. A mixture of 30 volumes of water and \n¿Oyp.lumes of glacial acetic acid..\nfesfs&l&ioh; Dissolve 25 mg of the residue in 10 ml of the \nsolvent mixture. ;\nReference solution (a). A 0.25' per cent w/v solution ,of \ntestosterone propionate IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Mix equal volumes of the test solution \nand reference solution (a). \n- -\nPlace the dry plate in a tank containing a shallow layer of the \nsolvent mixture, allow the solvent mixture to ascend to the \ntop, remove the plate from. the. tank and. allow the solvent to \nevaporate. Use within 2 hours, with the flow of the mobile \nphase in the direction in which the aforementioned treatment \nwas done.\nApply tfr the plate 2 p.1 of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to fise 12 cm. Dry the plate in a current of warm air, allow \nthe solvent to evaporate, heat at 120° for d 5 minutes and spray \nthe hot plate with ethanolic sulphuric acid (20 per cent v/v). \nHeat at 120° for a further 10 minutes, allow to cool and examine \nin daylight and under ultraviolet light at 365 nm. The principal \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncpnespqnds ,to that in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a), The principal spot in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) appears as a single,\ncompact spot A.\nTests . \n............\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral\npreparations (Injections).\nAssay. To an measured volume containing about Ò.T g of \nTestosterone Propionate add sufficient chloroform to produce"},{"name":"TETRACYCLINE","text":"100.0 ml and mix. Dilute 3.0 ml to 50.0 ml with chloroform and \nto 5.0 ml of the solution add 10 ml of isoniazid solution and \nsufficient methanol to produce 20.0 mi. Allow to stand for 45 \nminutes and measure the absorbance of the resulting solution \nat the maximum at about 380 nm (2.4.7), using- as the blank a \nsolution prepared by treating 5 ml of chloroform in the same \nmanner.\nCalculate the content of C^HnCf from the absorbance \nobtained by repeating the operation using a 0.006 per Cent \nw/v solution of testosterone propionate IP RS inchloroform \nand beginning at the words “to 5.0 ml of the solution,.,,,”.\nStorage. Store protected from light."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/testosterone-propionate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Testosterone Propionate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Testosterone Propionate\no\n~2H3203\nMol. Wt. 344.5\nTestosterone Propionate is 3-oxoandrost-4-en-17~-yl\npropionate.\nTestosterone Propionate contains not less than 97.0 per cent\nand not more than 103.0. per cent ofC22H320 3, calculated on\nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Androgen; anabolic steroid.\nDose. By intramuscular injection, 5 to 25 mg, once or twice\nweeldy.\n2199\nTESTOSTERONE PROPIONATE\nDescription. A white or almost white powder or colourless\ncrystals; odourless."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Testosterone Propionate\no\n~2H3203\nMol. Wt. 344.5\nTestosterone Propionate is 3-oxoandrost-4-en-17~-yl\npropionate.\nTestosterone Propionate contains not less than 97.0 per cent\nand not more than 103.0. per cent ofC22H320 3, calculated on\nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Androgen; anabolic steroid.\nDose. By intramuscular injection, 5 to 25 mg, once or twice\nweeldy.\n2199\nTESTOSTERONE PROPIONATE\nDescription. A white or almost white powder or colourless\ncrystals; odourless."},{"name":"Identification","text":"TestA may be omittediftests Band Care carriedout. Tests B\nand C may be omitted iftest A is carried out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with testosterone\npropionate RS or with the reference spectrum oftestosterone\npropionate.\nB. In the test for Related substances the principal peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to\nthe peak due to testosterone propionate in the chromatogram\nobtained with reference solution (a).\nC. Melting range. 119°to 123° (2.4.21)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Specific optical rotation (2.4.22). +83.0° to +90.0°, determined\nin a 1.0 per cent w/v solution in ethanol.\nRelated substances. Detelmine by. liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under\nexamination in methanol and dilute to 50 ml with the same\nsolvent.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 2 mg ofthe substance under\nexamination and 2 mg oftestosterone acetate RS in methanol\nand dilute to 50 ml with the same solvent.\nReference solution (b).\nDilute 1 ml of the test solution to\n100 m1 with methanol.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 !lm),\n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 20 volumes of water and\n80 volumes ofmethanol,\nflow rate. 1.5 m1 per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\ninjection volume. 20 !ll.\nInject the test solution and the reference solutions. Continue\nthe chromatography for twice the retention time for testo-\nsterone propionate.\nThe relative retention time of about four impurities with\nreference to testosterone propionate (retention time, about\n9 minutes) range from 0.5 to about 1.4.\nThe test is not valid unless in the chromatogram obtained\nwith reference solution (a) the resolution between the peaks\ndue to testosterone and testosterone acetate is not less than\n4.0.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTESTOSTERONE PROPIONATE INJECTION.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area\nof any peak other than the principal peak is not more than\n0.5 times the area ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.5 per cent); the sum of\nthe areas of any secondary peaks is not greater than the area\nof the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with\nreference solution (b) (1.0 per cent). Ignore any peak with an\narea less than 0.05 times the area ofthe principal peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (0.05 per\ncent).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 0.5 percent, determined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 2 hours.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 25 mg, dissolve in sufficient\nethanol to produce 250.0 ml, mix, dilute 5.0 ml to 50.0 ml with\nthe same solvent and measure the absorbance ofthe resulting\nsolution at themaximum at about 241 urn (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content of C22H320 3 taking 490 as the specific\nabsorbance at 241 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tests","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tests"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Category. Blood product.\npH (2.4.24). 6.5 to 7.6.\n)bU!ï^\nor freeze-dried, sterile, nón-pyrogenic preparation ol \nStable marker to a sample of the plasma pool are included\n4556\n4557\nwww.webofpharma.com\nOsmolality (2.4.23). Mini mum 240 mosmol per kg.\nTotal proteinMinimum 4.5 per cent w/v.\nDilute with a 0.9 per cent w/v solution of sodium chloride to \nobtain a solution containing about 15 mg of protein in 2 ml. \nPlace 2.0 ml of this solution in a round-bottomed centrifuge \ntube and add 2 ml of a 7.5 per cent w/v solution of.sodium \nmolybdate and 2 ml of a mixture of 1 volume of nitrogen-free \nsulphuric acid and 30 volumes of water. Shake, centrifuge for\n5 minutes, decant the supernatant and allow the inverted tube \nto drain on filter paper. Determine the nitrogen in the residue \nby the method of sulphuric acid digestion (2.3.30) and calculate \nthe quantity of protein by multiplying the result by 6.25. :\nActivated coagulation factors (2.8.3). Complies with the test \nfor activated coagulation factors. Carry out the test with \n0.1 ml of the preparation under examination instead of 1 O-fold \nand 100-fold dilutions. The coagulation time for the preparation \nunder examination is not less than 150 seconds.\nAriti-A and anti-B haemagglutinins (2.8.13). The presence of \nhaemagglutinins '(anti-A or anti-B) corresponds to the blood \ngroup stated on the label.\nHepatitis A virus antibodies\nMinimum 1 IU per ml, determined by a suitable immuno­\nchemical method (2.2.14),\nHuman hepatitis A immunoglobulin reference preparation \nis suitable for use as a reference preparation.\nIrregular erythrocyte antibodies\nThe preparation under examination does not show the \npresence of irregular erythrocyte antibodies when examined \nwithout dilution by an indirect antiglobulin test.\nCitrate. Maximum 25 mmol per litre'\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute the preparation under examination with \nan equal volume of a 0.9 per cent w/v solution of sodium \nchloride. Filter the solution using a filter with.0.45 pm pores.\nReference solution. Dissolve 0.3 g of sodium citrate in water\nand dilute to 100.0 ml with the same solvent.\nChromatographic system\n■ a stainless steel column 30 cm x 7.8 mm, packed with\ncation exchange resin (9 pm),\n- mobile phase: 0.051 per cent w/v solution of sulphuric\n’ acid, \n'\n- flow fate: 0.5 ml per niinute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 215 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nEquilibrate the column for about 15 minutes.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. The \nretention time of citrate is about 10 minutes.\n4558\nPLASMA (POOLED AND TREATED FOR VIRUS INACTIVATION)\nIPW ip 2022\ng^fcHPEATELETCONCEN'rRAl'E\nProduction . ■ \n¿4\nCalcium. Maximum 5.0 mmol per litre, determined by Me® In the same manner, determine the coagulation time of 4 two-, \nA for atomic absorption spectrometry (2.4.2), measuri^B f0Iddilutions (1 in 10 to 1 in 80) of human normal plasma in \n622 nm and using calcium hollow-cathode lamp ai|J ¡^{dazole buffer solution pH 7.3.\ntransmission band preferably of 0.5 nm and the atomisé the validity of the assay arid calculate the potency of\nPlatelet Concentrate is \nblood\nthat have not been allowed \n2(T' Platelet rich\nplasma (PRP) is separatedg^gbpletion \nof the phlebotomy. The\nresuspended platelets in an amount of pjaitha adequate to \nmaintain an acceptable pH generally 40 used.\ndevice as air-acetylene or acetylene-propane flame. O preparation by the usual statistical methods for a \nPotassium. Maximum 5.0 mmol per litre, determined by M^g paralle 1-1 i ne assay.\nA for atomic emission spectrometry (2.4.3), measuriijl ^imated potency is not less than 0.5 IU per ml. The\n766.5nm. \n.^¿^gdancelimits(P = 0.95)arenotlessthan80.0percentand\nSodium. Maximum 2.00 x 102 mm