n inhalation Solution is a sterile, non pyrogenic) \npreservative free solution of Tobramycin or Tobramycin \nSulphate in Watef for Injection containing sodium \nchloride, f f'\nTobramycin inhalation Solution contains hot less than 90.0\nper cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount \noftobramycin Cl8H37N5O9.\n^suMsfrength.3OO mg per 5 ml. ■ ■■ .\nMeijfificatioji ;\nAssay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \n(lie test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nC^mit°gram obtained with the reference solution(b).\nsubstances. Determine by liquid Chromatography\nTest solution A 0.0192 per cent of the substance under \nexaminationinwater.\nReference solution(a).A 0.11 per cent w/v solution of \ntobramycin IP RS in water, and adjusted to pH 6.0 with \n0.5 M sulphuric acidpnà. dilute with water.\nReference solutionfb). A 0.022 per cent of tobramycin from \nthe reference solution (a) in water.\nReference solution (c). Heat a portion of reference solution \n(a) in a suitable sealed glass container at 100° for 8-9 hours.Cool \nto room temperature, and dilute with water to obtain a 0.022 \nper cent w/v solution of tobramycin.\nReference solution(d). A 0.11 per cent w/v solution of \ntobramycin IPRS prepared by adding 0.5M sulphuric acid, \nusing 2.0 per cent of the final volume, and enough water to \ndissolve the tobramycin, and dilute with water to volume.\nReference solution(e). 0.00011 per cent of tobramycin from \nthe reference solution (d) in water.\nBlank.Water. . \n.......\nDeriv'dtization procedure. Heat all solutions at the sarhe \ntemperature and for the samé duration as indicated. Move all \nflasks to 60° constant water-bath at the samé time. To separate \n50 ml flasks, transfer 15.0 ml of reference solution(b), 15.0 rnl \nof reference solution (c), 15.0 ml of reference solution (e), 15,0 \nml of test solution, and 15.0 ml of blank. To.each flaskadd 10 \nml of 2,4-dinitxofluoro.benzene reagent, and 10 ml of \ntris (hydroxymethyl) aminomethane reagent, shake, and insert \nthe stopper. Place the flasks in a constant temperature bath at \n60°, and heat for 50 minutes. Remove the flasks from the bath, \nand allow to stand fór 10 minutes, Add 2.0 ml of acetonitrile \nallow to cool to room temperature, and dilute with acetonitrile', \nto 50.0 ml. Allow the solutions to stand for 16 hours. The \nsolutions thusobtained are derivatized reference solution (b), \nderivatized reference solution (c), reference solution (e), test \nsolution, and blank solution. ' \n' ...........' \" J\nChromatographic system \n.'■\"■■.-w :....\n- \n. a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \nphenyl group chemically bonded to porous silica (5 um),\n- mobile phase: ,A.a mixture of ^volumes, of acetonitrile,\n95 volumes of water and 0.08, volumes of. ortho-\n... \n■ phosphoric acid, .. \n.-.eg r .. \n■■ \n.\nB. a mixture of 75 .volumes of acetonitrile, \n25 volumes of water, and 0.08 .volumes pf ortho-\n.. phosphoric acid, , \nmot; ■ \nrm\nT- a gradient programme using the, conditions given\nbelow,-., j / .• \n..y, .-\n, - nowraie:1.2mlperminute, . \n. •\n■ spectrophotometer set at 365 nm,\n- injection volume: 45.pl.\n3815\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOBRAMYCIN INHALATION SOLUTION\nTime m\nMobile phase A\n1; Mobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n... ,... 0\n..............21\n... .14 ;;;\n... .66\n25 ..\n30\n>(i 35 .\n. 70 ........... ..\n40\n'• ' '20^';\n50\n5\n95\n2 Relative j \nretention time .\n; ; ■ f ; \\ \n\\ - i ■ ■ : '\nName\nSpecified unidentified impurity \n, : : ;\n; \n0.36\nDeoxystreptaniine kanosaminide\n0.66\n004 \n'\nKanàmycin B:\n0.96\nv \n10\nTobramycin\nInject derivatised reference solution (c).The test is not valid \nunless the capacity factor is not less than 15.5 for tobramycin, \nand .the; resolution is not less than 1.0 between the nebramine \nand kanamycin peaks. \n, .• ;r r... .,.\nCompare the ; derivatised reference solution (b) and- (c) \nchromatograms to • identify degradation -peaks. \nDeoxystrcptamine kanosaminide and nebramine will increase \nin response in derivatized reference solution (c) \n!\nInject the derivatized reference solution (b), (e) and blank. \nThe relative standard deviation for replicate injections is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent with reference .solution (e). Disregard \nany peak corresponding to those from the derivatised blank \nsolution and substract the quantities of any such peaks f"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tobramycin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tobramycin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tobramycin\nC18H37N5O9 \nMol. Wt. 4673\nTobramycin is 6-0-(3-amino-3-dcoxy-a-D-glucopyranosyl)-2- \ndeoxy-4-(7-(2,6-diamino-2,3,6-trideoxy-fr-D,i./^(i_\nhexopyranosyl)-D-streptamine, an antimicrobial substance \nproduced by Streptomyces tenebrarius or by any other means.\nTobramycin has potency not less than 900 mcg of CTTNA \nper mg, calculated on anhydrous basis. \n, ~\nCategory.Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.; P"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tobramycin\nC18H37N5O9 \nMol. Wt. 4673\nTobramycin is 6-0-(3-amino-3-dcoxy-a-D-glucopyranosyl)-2- \ndeoxy-4-(7-(2,6-diamino-2,3,6-trideoxy-fr-D,i./^(i_\nhexopyranosyl)-D-streptamine, an antimicrobial substance \nproduced by Streptomyces tenebrarius or by any other means.\nTobramycin has potency not less than 900 mcg of CTTNA \nper mg, calculated on anhydrous basis. \n, ~\nCategory.Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.; P"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel H. \n: '\nMobile phase. A mixture of 60 volumes,of methanol, \n40 volumes of strong ammonia solution and 20 volumes of \nchloroform. .\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.4 g of the substance under \nexamination in 100 ml of water.\nReference solution fa). A 0.4 per cent w/v solution of\ntobramycin IPRS in water.\nReference solution (b). A' solution containing 0.4 per cent \nw/v each of kanamycin sulphate IPRS, neomycin sulphate \nIPRS and tobramycin IPRS in water. \n} k :\nApply to the plate 5 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in warm air, spray with a mixture of equal volumes \nof a 46 per cent w/v solution of sulphuric acid and a 0.2 per \ncent w/v solution of 1,3-naphthalenediol in ethanol (95 PP \ncent) and heat at 105° for 10 minutes. The principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution CQ? \nThe test is not valid unless the chromatogram obtained wi \nreference solution (b) shows three clearly separated principa1\nspots. ■■ \n' -P¡pP ■- '\n3812\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOBRAMYCIN\næ2022\npjssolve about 5 mg in 5 ml ,qf wafer, acid 5 ml of a 0.1 per\n■ \n« water-bath for 3 minutes; a violét-blue 'colour\nheat in« .\ndevelop?:.;///:.• 7 7/• \n■' '\nU solution of ninhydrin in ethanol (95 per cent) and\nvu:.///■■■ \n.. \n2\nH(2 4 24). 9-0 t0 H O, determined in a 10.0 per cent w/v\nsolution. ; Q.Çfr ..■■■ : 1 r; •• .\nc^ificoDtical rotation (2.4.22). +138° to + 148°, determined\nin a 4.0 per cent w/v solution.\n2.Methyl-l-ProPai*°l- Not more than 1.0 per centw/w, \ndetermined by gas chromatography (2.4.13). \n; \n.\nTest solution (a).A10 per cent w/v solution of the substance\nunder examination in water..\nTeslsoliltiilnf^fl A solution containing 10 per cent w/v of the\nsubstance under examination and 0.2 per cent v/v of \n2-propanol (internal standard). . : \n. \nfr\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.1 per cent w/v of \n2-methyl-l’prppQnol and 0.2 per cent v/v of thé infernal\nstandard. / / ^hfr. 77 \n.•\nChromatographie system : \n, : \n,\n- aglasscolumn 1.5mx4mm,packedwithporouspdlymer\nbeads (80 to 100 mesh) (Such as Porapak Q),\n- temperature, column 165°, \n. , ; / \n,\nCalculate the percentage w/w of 2-methy l-l-propanol.\nRelated substances. Dete