and mobile \nphase B. ..\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of powdered tablets \nwritairiingdO mg ofTolterodine Tartrate in 1 ml of methanol,\n5 ml of the solvent mixture with the aid of ultrasound and\ndilute to 10.0 ml with the solvent mixture, filter.;\\.\nReference solution ’! (a)i A 0.001 per:cent w/v solution of \nio/reTO^/ne7dr/raieZP7?iS'prepared by dissolving in methanol\nand diluted with the solvent mixture. \nI;c .\nReferenc'solution (b). A 0.03 percent w/v solution of tartaric\ncrirf in the solvent mixture. \n■'■■■■■■-:-u\n^uohiatographic system\n~ a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \nb • hctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica.(5pm),\n~ Triobile phase: A. dissolve 6.8 g .of, potassium\n■ :dihydrogen orthophosphate in 1000 ml water,.adjusted\n- \n3.5 with.or^hpphpsphoric, acid. , \n.\nB. acetonitrile,\na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \nflowrate: 1 ml per,minute,.\nspectrophotometer set at 215 nm,\ninjection volume: 20;pl.■ \n■< ■ • \n-on . m\nTOLTERODINE TARTRATE TABLETS\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)1\n0\n¿5 \n-\n: \n\"• 35 •• /\n' ' 65 •'\n35\n20\n50\n• - 50 !:‘\n40\n30\n70\n50\n65 \n......\n35 . s .\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and \nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test solution, in the\nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (1.0 \nper cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary peaks\nis not more than twice the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (2.0 per \ncent). Ignore the peak due to tartaric acid corresponding to \nthe peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b).\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \nTablets.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as described \nunder Assay using following modification. \n■ :\nTest solution. Disperse 1 intact tablet in the mobile phase with \nthe aid of ultrasound for 20 minutes and dilute to 10.0 ml with \nthe mobile phase, filter. Dilute further with the mbbile phase if \nnecessary. \n■■\nCalculate the content of C^H37NQ7-in the tablet. \n• ?: w -\nOther tests. Comply with the; tests stated under Tablets. . ■\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4,14).\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof powder containing 10 mgofTolterodiric Tartrate in 70 ml of \nthe mobile phase with1 the aid of ultrasound for 20 minutes and \ndilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase, filter.\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of lolterodine \ntartrate IPRS in thé mobile phase. 1 \n; ■ \n।\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n•T- mobile phase: ainixtiire of 65 volumes of buffer solution \nprepared by mixing 2.2 ml of orthophosphoric acid to\n1000 ml with water, adjusted to pH 3.0 with triethylamine \nand 35 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 280 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 p. I. s \n. \n1\n3831\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOLVAPTAN • ■; , /..■\nInj ect the reference solution. The test is. not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 ^theoreticalplates, the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation fop replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test "},{"name":"Tests","text":"i \n; to ; 0,,to ÿ jj (R; f f ;\nAssay. Détermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nInternal standard solution.. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of\nprogesteronef\\x\\ methanol. \n, . , ; \nA\nTest solution. Transfer a weighed quantity ’ofthetopical\npowder containing about 5.0 mg of tolnaftate to a scretf* \ncapped, 50 ml centrifuge tube. Add 25 ml of we^u/JoApi2® \nthe cap on the tube arid rotate on a rotating device for \nminutes, and centrifuge at about 2000 rpm for 5 minutes, fife*\nthe supernatant and transfer 20 ml ofthc filtrate to \nvolumetric flask, retaining the remaining portion of the \nfor identification test. Add 5.0 ml of internal standard solution\nto the flask and dilute to volume with methanol and mix-\nReference solution. A 0.02 per cent w/v solution\nIPRS in methanol. To 20 ml of the solution add 5.0 ml of internal \nstandard solution and dilute to 50.0 ml with methanol\n3828\nwww.webofpharma.com\nff.W-i-.................................................................................................\nrhromatograPhic sysKra\n, a stainless; steeb cQlunm 30 em x4 mm, packed with\n‘T: octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (10 pm), .\nmobile phase: a mixture, of 2 volumes of acetonitrile \nand 1 volume of water,\n- flowratefl ml per minute, ;\n' J.' spectrophotometer set at 254 nm, ..\n_ injection volume: 10 pl. \n;\nfhe relative retention time of progesterone and tolnaftate are \nabout0.7 minute and 1.0 minute respectively.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \n(¿solution between the tolnaftate and internal standard peak\nis not less than 3 0 and the relative standard deviation for\nreplicate injections is; not more than 3.0 per cent/\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C j 9H, -NOS in the powder. \n,\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTolnaftate Topical Solution\nTolnaftate lopical Solution contains not less than 90.0 per\nrent and not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntolnaftate, _C!9H f?NOS.\nUsual strength, 1 percent w/v.\nIdentification ;\nIk tenninebythin-layerchromatography (2.4.17), coating the \nplatesilica gel.Gl?254. \n.......... r •\nTake an measured volume containing about 1.0 mg of\nahd SO inltof chloroform and extract with5 50 ml of\nFilter the chloroform layer througha \n1^, ^im-washed cotton pledget into a 250- ml volumetric\nan4dxtract the aqueous layer with two 45 ml portions of\nTOLTERODINE TARTRATE\nchloroform, filtering eachiinto the flask. Add chloroform to \nvolume and mix (Solution A)wDilute 25 ml of the solution with \nchloroform to 100 mland mix. Measure the absorbance.of the \nresulting solution at the maximum at about 258 nm (2.4.7), \nusing 0.001 per cent w/v solution of tolnaftate IPRS in \nchloroform.\nCalculate the content of C19H)7NOS in topical solution.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture..; v - .: v :\nTolterodine Tartrate\nC22H3 ¡NO, CiHA \nMol Wt. 475.6\nTolterodineTartrate is 2-[(17?)-3-[bis(l-methylethyl)amino]-\n1 -phenylpropyl]-4-methylphenol tartrate.\nTolterodine Tartrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of \n¡NO,C4H6O6 ^calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticholinergic.\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline powder t\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained;with tqlterpfine \ntartrate IPRS or with the reference; spectrum.of tolterodine \ntartrate.. \n.. \nfu\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution. ■ ; '\nJ;y: mdr/'W -nav-i/C-i\nSpecific optical rotation;(2.4,22). + 27° to.+.34°,determined in\n1.0 per cent w/v solution in methanol. b Ti-T' .Arnm id\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). \n\" \" \"J ' \n.....\nSolvent mixture. Equal volumes of mofiilephase A and mobile \nphase B.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexaminationin 10 ml of wafer and dilute to50.0 ml withthe \nsolvent mixture. \nw\n3829\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOLTERODINE ■ TARTRATE\nReference solution (a)i Dissolve about 10 mg of tolterodine \ntartratelPRSin 20 ml of wafer and dilute to 100.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture; Dilute 5.0 ml of thdsolútión to 100.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture? -e \nîh mm.-. •;-<* J\nReference solution (b). A ()'03 per cent w/vsolution of tartaric\nacid in the solvent mixture.\nChromatographie system '............... ' ' \n........... .......... .\n- a stainless steel-column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noçtadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm) (Such \nas Inerts il),\n- mobile phase: A. a 0.05 Mpotassium dihydrogen ortho­\nphosphate, adjusted to pH 3 .5 with orthophosphoric\nacid,\nB. acetonitrile, \nm -\n; - ^gradient programme using the conditions given below,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n-1 spectrophotometer set at 215 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n65\n1 ' 35\n< \n35 .\n20\n■\" \n50 \n'\n, 40 T -\n• 30 \n—-\n■■ \n70 •\n' 41\n' '65\"\n¿\"35\n50\n65\n35' \"\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is hot more \nthan 5.0 per cent. \n.\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area'of any \nsecondary peak is not more than the area of the principal peak \nin the Chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0.5 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than twice the area of thc principai peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(1.0 per centj. Ignore the peak due to tartaric acid corresponding \nto the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b). \n3’\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). 1 ! 1 ' J 7 ? ; i : -\nSulpliated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0. Tper cent. ”\"r’\nLoss on drymg (2,4.1.9). Not more than 0.5 pe"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolnaftate-topical-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolnaftate Topical Solution"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolnaftate Topical Solution\nTolnaftate Topical Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntolnaftate, C I9H ',NOS.\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/v."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tolnaftate Topical Solution\nTolnaftate Topical Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntolnaftate, C I9H ',NOS.\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/v."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), wati4013c__ \nplate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. Toluene.\nTOLNAFTATE TOPICAL POWDER \nfp 2618 \n1P 2018 \nTOLTERODINE TARTRATE\nTest solution. Evaporate 25 ml of solution A on steam bath to \ndryness and dissolve the residue in 1.0 ml of the ethanol\nInject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(0.5 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than twice the area of the principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) \n(1.0 per cent). Ignore the peak due to tartaric acid corresponding \nto the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b).\nA.Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with  tolterodine \ntartrate RS or with the reference spectrum of tolterodine\n(95 per cent).\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of tolnaftate \nRS in ethanol (95 per cent).\ntartrate.\nApply to the plate 10 gl of each solution. Allow the m obile\nB.In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\n phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air and examine under \nultraviolet light at 254 nm. The principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nCalculate the content of C 26H 371\\107 in the tablet. \n101*1 \n. Other tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of tolvaptan \nRS in the solvent mixture.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotothetry (24.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tolvaptan RS \nor with the reference spectrum  of tolvaptan.,\nUse thechromatographic system as described under Related \nsubstances.\nAsstty. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4 14).\n2 \n3403\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 3000 theoretical plates , the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C 26H25C1N203.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTopiramate\no,9\n\\/S NH2\nO\nH 3C\nO \nH3C\n0\" 1 \nCH3\nCl2H2INO8S \t\nMol. Wt. 339.4\nTopiramate is 2,3:4,5-di-0-1-isopropylidene-13-D-fructo-\npyranose sulphamate.\nTopiramate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C l2H2INORS, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Anti-convul sant.\nDose. 25 mg daily.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with topiramate RS \nor with the reference spectrum of topiramate.\nB. Melting range (2.4.21). 120° to 130°.\nA.weigh a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 1 g of \nTopiramate, disperse in 30 ml ofdichloromethane, centrifuge \nat 2000 rpm for 10 minutes and filter. Evaporate the filtrate to \ndryness. The residue complies with the following test.\n11, \nThe retention times of topiramate impurity A and impurity B \nare about 2.9 and 4.5 minutes and the relative retention times \nwith respect to topiramate are 0.54 and 0.84 respectively.\nThe retention time of topiramate is about 5 minutes.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for the replicate injections is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent, the column efficiency is not less than \n3000 theoretical plates and the tailing factor is not more than \n2.0\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peak due to topiramate impurity A and \ntopiramate impurity B is not less than 3.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with topiramate RS \nor with the reference spectrum of topiramate.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram for 40 minutes. For the calculation of content, \nmultiply the peak areas of the following impurities by the \ncorresponding correction factor: topiramate impurity A= 1.09; \ntopiramate impurity B = 0.94. The area of any secondary peak \ndue to topiramate impurity A and B is not more than 0.15 and \nthe area of any other secondary peak is not more than 0.1 per \ncent. Ignore any peak due to lactose.  1ST \nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nInject the test solution. The content of any individual impurity \nis not more than 1.0 per cent and the sum of areas of all impurities \nis not more than 2.0 per cent. Calculated by area normalisation. \nIgnore any peak due to identified excipients in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nA.Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with topotecan \nhydrochloride RS or with the reference spectrum of topotecan \nhydrochloride.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Heavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). +27° to +34°, determined in\n1.0 per cent w/v solution in methanol.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nAssay. Take an measured volume containing about 10 mg of \nTolnaftate, add 50 ml of  chloroform and extract with 50 ml of\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\n0.1 M sodium hydroxide. Filter the chloroform layer through a \nchloroform-washed cotton pledget into a 250- ml volumetric\nSolvent mixture.  10 volumes of mobile phase A and 10 volumes \nof mobi le phase B.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nflask and extract the aqueous layer with two 45 ml portions of \nchloroform, filtering each into the flask. Add chloroform to \nvolume and mix (Solution A). Dilute 25 ml of this solution with \nchloroform to 100 ml and mix. Measure the absorbance of the \nresulting solution at the maximum at about 258 nm (2.4.7), \nusing 0.001 per cent w/v solution of tolnaftate RS in \nchloroform.\nSolvent mixture. 10 volumes of mobile phase A and 10 volumes \nof mob i le phase B.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10 ml of water and dilute to 50.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 20 ml of water, sonicate and dilute to 100.0 ml \nwith the solvent mixture. Dilute 5.0 ml of this solution to \n50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve about 10 mg of tolterodine \ntartrate RS in 20 ml of water and dilute to 100.0 ml with the \nsolvent mixture. Dilute 5.0 ml of this solution to 100.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture.\nCalculate the content of C I9H ',NOS in topical solution.„01\nReference solution. Dissolve about 50 mg of tolterodine \ntartrate RS in 20.0 ml of water, sonicate and dilute to 100.0 ml \nwith the solvent mixture. Dilute 5.0 ml of this solution to \n50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution  (b).  A 0.03 per cent w/v solution of tartaric \nacid in the solvent mixture.\n Storage. Store protected from moisture.\nChromatographic system\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm) (Such \nas Inertsil), \n-\nmobile phase: A. a  0.05 M potassium dihydrogen \northophosphate, adjusted to pH 3.5 with  ortho-\nphosphoric acid,\nTolterodine Tartrate\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm) (Such \nq as Inertsil), \n- mobile phase: A. a 0.05 M potassium dihydrogen ortho-\nphosphate, adjusted to pH 3.5 with orthophosphoric \nn Iacid,\nH 0 COOH\nI) \t\nB. acetonitrile, \na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 215 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 201A1.\nH3C\nB. acetonitrile, \n-\na gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n-\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 215 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 IA\nHO OC OH\n4111\nTime \n(in min)\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\nTime \n(in min)\nC22113 1NO, C4H606 \t\nMol Wt. 475.6\nMobile Phase A\nMobile Phase B\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v) \n0 \n65 \n35 \n5 \n65 \n35 \n20 \n50 \n50 \n40 \n30 \n70 \n41 \n65 \n35 \n50 \n65 \n35 \nInject reference solution (a). The test is not-valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injeciiorf$ \nore \nthan 5.0 per cent.\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v) \n0 \n65 \n35 \n5 \n65 \n35 \n20 \n50 \n50 \n21 \n65 \n35 \n25 \n65 \n35 \nInject reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ntheor6iical ?Wes of the principal peak is not less than 2000 \nand the tailingfgctor is not more than 2.0. The relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nTolterodine Tartrate is 2-[(1R)-3-[bis(1-methylethypamino] -\n 1-phenylpropy1-4-methylphenol tartrate.\nTolterodine Tartrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C22H31NO,C41-1606, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticholinergic.\ndaily.\nbite to off-white crystalline powder.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C22H31NO.C41 -1606\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTolterodine Tartrate Tablets\nTolterodine Tablets contain Tolterodine Tartrate.\nTolterodine Tartrate Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amounts of \ntolterodine tartrate, C26H37N07.\nUsual strengths. 1 mg; 2 mg.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No.1, \nMedium. 900 ml of gastric juice, artificial (without enzyme) \nprepared by dissolving 2 g of sodium chloride in 80 ml of 1 M \nhydrochloric acid and dilute to 1000 ml with water,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 10 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Use the filtrate.\nReference solution. A solution of tolterodine tartrate RS \nprepared by dissolving in methanol and diluted with the \ndissolution medium to get the similar concentration as that of \nthe test solution.\nUse chromatographic system as described in the Assay using \ninjection volume: 100 pl.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C 26H37NO, in the medium.\nD. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of C 26H37N07\n Related substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. Equal volumes of mobile phase A and mobile \nphase B.\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of powdered tablets \ncontaining 10 mg of Tolterodine Tartrate in 1114-6f methanol, \n5 ml of the solvent mixture with the aid of ulOasound and \n-- -- \ndilute to 10.0 ml with the solvent mixture, filter:\n17111111  \nIP 2018 \t ip 2018 \nTOLTERODINE TARTRATE TABLETS \nTOLVAPTAN\nReference solution (a). A 0.001 per cent w/v solution of \ntolterodine tartrate RS prepared by dissolving in methanol \nand dil"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolu-balsam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolu Balsam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolu Balsam\nBalsam of Tolu\nTolu Balsam is a solid or semi-solid, balsam obtained by incision \nfrom the trunk of My r oxy Ion balsamum (Linn.) Harms \n(Fam. Leguminosae).\nTolu Balsam contains not less than 35.0 per cent and not more \nthan 50.0 per cent of total balsamic acids, calculated as \ncinnamic acid,’ C9HSO3, on the dry, ethanol-soluble matter.\nCategory. Expectorant, antiseptic.\nDescription. A soft, tenacious, brownish yellow or brown \nmass, when first, collected; subsequently, becoming harder \nand finally brittle. Transparent in thin films; odour, aromatic \nand vànillà-Iikè. Warmed and pressed between pieces of glass \nand examined with a lens, it exhibits crystals of cinnamic acid."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tolu Balsam\nBalsam of Tolu\nTolu Balsam is a solid or semi-solid, balsam obtained by incision \nfrom the trunk of My r oxy Ion balsamum (Linn.) Harms \n(Fam. Leguminosae).\nTolu Balsam contains not less than 35.0 per cent and not more \nthan 50.0 per cent of total balsamic acids, calculated as \ncinnamic acid,’ C9HSO3, on the dry, ethanol-soluble matter.\nCategory. Expectorant, antiseptic.\nDescription. A soft, tenacious, brownish yellow or brown \nmass, when first, collected; subsequently, becoming harder \nand finally brittle. Transparent in thin films; odour, aromatic \nand vànillà-Iikè. Warmed and pressed between pieces of glass \nand examined with a lens, it exhibits crystals of cinnamic acid."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. To a solution in ethanol (90 per cent) add ferric chloride \ntest solution-, a green colour is produced.\nB. To 1 g add to 5 ml of water, heat to boiling, filter, add 30, mg \nof potassium permanganate and continue heating; the odour \nof benzaldehyde-is produced.\nTests.......................\nAcidity. A solution in ethanol (90per cent) is acidic to litmus \nsolution.\n4308\n1P2022\nAcid value (2.3.23). 97 to 160, calculated on the dry, ethanol \nsoluble matter, determined by the following method. Dissoly' \n5.0 g in 50 ml of boiling ethanol (90 percent;, add 3 ml Of \nphenolphthalein solution and titrate the hot solution\n1 M ethanolic potassium hydroxide. When the colour \nbecomes dark brown, attach to a reflux condenser, boil for.a \nfew minutes to break up the precipitate and complete the* \ntitration. , . ..\nEthanol-insoluble matter. Not more than 5 per cent, determined \nby the following method. Digest 2.5 g with 50 ml of ethanol\n(90per cent), filter through a sintered glass crucible, transfer \nthe residue to the filter crucible with the aid of more ethanol \n(90 per cent), wash with hot ethanol (90 pericent). until all \nsoluble matter is removed and dry to constant weight at 100°\nColophony. Add 5 g to 25 ml of carbon disulphide, warm gently \non a water-bath under a reflux condenser, filter, evaporate the \nsolution to dryness, dissolve the residue in 6 ml of light \npetroleum (40° to 60°) and shake with 10 ml of a 0.5 per cent\nw/v solution of cupric acetate-, the light petroleum layer is \nnot coloured green.\nEster value (2.3.26). 47 to 95, calculated on the dry, ethanol- \nsoluble basis.\nSaponification value (2.3.37). 170 to 230, calculated on the \ndry, ethanol-soluble basis. \n......\nLoss on drying (2,4.19). Not more than 4.0 per cent, determined \non 2.0 g by drying in a thin layer in an oven at 60“ over \nphosphorus pentoxide at a pressure not exceeding 2.7 kPa..\nAssay. Weigh accurately 2.0 g and boil with 25 ml of dilute \nethanolic potassium hydroxide solution under a‘'reflux \ncondenser for 1 hour. Remove the ethanol and. digest the \nresidue with 50 ml of hot water until diffused. Cool the liquid, \nadd 150 ml of water and 1.5 g of magnesium sulphate \ndissolved in 50 ml of water. Mix thoroughly and set’aside for\n10 minutes. Filter, wash the residue on the, filter with 20 ml of \nwater,, acidify the combined filtrate and washings with \nhydrochloric acid and extract with successive quantities of \n50,40,30,30 and 30 ml of ether. Combine the ether extractsand \ndiscard-the aqueous portion. Extract with successive \nquantities of 20,20,10,10 arid 10 ml of sodium bicarbonate \nsolution, washing each aqueous extract with the same 20 ml \nof ether. Discard the ether layers, acidify the combined \naqueous extracts with• hydrochloric acid extract with \nsuccessive quantities of 30,20, 20 and 10 ml of chloroform, \nfiltering each chloroform extract through a plug of cotton wool \non which a layer of anhydrous sodium sulphate is placed. \nEvaporate the chloroform on a water-bath untilabout 10 ml\nremains and remove the remainder in a current of air stoppmg \nimmediately when the last trace of solvent is removed. Dissolve \nthe residue by: warming with 10 ml of ethanol (95 percent), \npreviously neutralised to phenol red solutions cool and titrate\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRAGACANTH\nfp2022\n().] , M sodium hydroxide using phenol red solution as\ny]dicat°r-\nj mi of Oil M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.01482 g of \n^[balsamic acids, calculated as cinnamic acid, C5H8O2.\nStorage Store protected from light and moisture. Avoid \nclosure & excessive heat.\nfragacanth\n^ragacanth is the air-hardened gummy exudate, flowing \n¿^ly or,obtained by incision, from the trunk and branches\nofds/raga/teS’ gummifer Labill. and certain other species of \nAstragalus (Fam. Fabaceae).\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Pale yellow, thin, flattened ribbons or brittle \npieces; odourless and almost tasteless. On the addition of \nabout 10 .times its weight of water, it forms a mucilaginous gel.\nIt has the macroscopic and microscopic characteristics \ndescribed under Identification tests A and B.\n^.Macroscopic — Occurs as thin, flattened pieces, 30 mm \nlong, 10 mm wide and up to 1mm in thickness, more or less \ncurved, marked on the surface by fine longitudinal striae-and \nconcentric transverse ridges; white, translucent, horny; \nfracture, short. May also be in the form of thicker, less brittle. \npieces; white to pale yellow and more opaque.\nB. Microscopic — Reduce to powder. Examine under a \nmicroscope; the powder shows in the gummy mass numerous \nstratified cellular membranes which turn violét ón the addition \nof-iodinated zinc chloride solution. The mass includes starch \ngranules, isolated or in small clusters, rounded or occasionally \ndeformed, diameter 4 to 10 pm, and up to 20 pm, with a central \nhilum, visible in polarised light.\nC-Moisten 0.5 g ofthe powdered material withl ml of ethanol \nfitycr cent) and add gradually, while shaking, 5 Ó ml of water \nuntilahomogeneousmucilage is obtained. To 5 ml of the \nmucilage add 5 ml of water and 2 ml of barium hydroxide\nA slightly flocculent precipitate is formed which, \nWlfiin heated for 10 minutes on a water-bath, gives an intense \nyellow colour.\n1.0g..' -\nPiAddA.ml of a 0.5 per cent w/v dispersion in water to 0.5 ml \nhydrochloric acid and heat on a water-bath for 30 minutes. \nTo one half of the resulting liquid add 1.5 ml of sodium \nhydroxide solution and 3 ml of alkaline cupric-tartrate \nsolution and heat on a water-bath; a reddish brown precipitate\nis formed. To the other half of the liquid, add a few drops of \nbarium chloride solution^ no precipitate is formed (freedom"},{"name":"Tests","text":"...... ....................\nfromagar). \n; \n'\nAcacia and other soluble gums. To 20 ml of a 2.5 per cent w/v \nsuspension of the powdered material prepared with freshly \nboiled water add 10 nil of lead acetate solutionna flocculent \nprecipitate isformed.Filter and add to the filtrate 10 mi of tead \nsubacetate solutionna slight cloudiness may appear, but there\nis no precipitate. .'■'.ív \n; \n\"\nKaraya gum. Boil 1 g with20 nil of water untila mucilage is \nformed, add 5 ml of hydrochloric acid and again boil for \n5 minutes; no pink or red colour develops.\nSterculiá. A. Shake 012 g ofthe powdered materialwith 10 nd \nof ethanol (60 per cent) in a 10-ml stoppered cylinder; any \ngel formed occupies not more than 1.5 ml.\nB. Shake 1 g of the powdered material with 100 ml of water and \ntitrate with 0.01 M sodium hydroxide,t using methyl red \nsolution as indicator. Not more than 5.0 ml is required to change \nthe colour of the solution. \n\" ; ;\nForeign matter. Not more than 1.0 per cent, determinedby the \nfollowing method. To 2.0 g of the powdered material in a \n250.0 ml round-bottomed flask add 95 nd of methanol, swirl to \nmoisten the powder and add 60 ml of 7 M hydrochloric acid. \nAdd a few glass beads and heat under a reflux, condenser in a \nwater-bath for 3 hours, shaking occasionally. Remove the glass \nbeads and filter the hot suspension under reduced pressure \nthrough a sintered-glass crucible (porosity No, 1). Rinse the \nflask with a small quantity of water, passing the rinsings \nthrough the filter.,Wash the residue on the filter with about \n40 ml of methanol and dry to constant weight at 110°. ,\nArsenic (2.3.10). Mix 3.3 g with 3 g of anhydrous sodium \ncarbonate, add 10 ml of bromine solution and mix thoroughly.; \nEvaporate to dryness on a water-bath, gently, ignite,; and \ndissolve the cooled residue in 16 ml- of brominated \nhydrochloric acid AsTand 45 ml of water. Remove the excess \nof bromine with 2 ml of stannous chloride AsT.Ahc resulting \nsolution complies with the limit test for,arsenic (3 ppm).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 0.5 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals. Method B (20 ppm). \\\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 4.0 per cent, determined on\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). 1. g is Êçee frera Escherichia \ncoli and 10 g is free from Salmonella.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\n4309\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTULASI ;\nÏP2022?"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolvaptan","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolvaptan"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolvaptan\nC26H25C1N2O3 \nMol Wt .448.9\nTolvaptan is TV-{4-{[(5Æ)-7-chloro-5-hydroxy-2,3,4,5- \ntetrahydro-1 H-1 -benzazepin-1 -yl]carbonyl} -3 -methylphenyl} - \n2-mcthylbcnzamidc.\nTolvaptan contains not less than 98.0 per cent and, not more \nthan 102.0 per cent, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Vasopressin Receptor Antagonist. v yf-v..\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline powder. ; ; < ? ;"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tolvaptan\nC26H25C1N2O3 \nMol Wt .448.9\nTolvaptan is TV-{4-{[(5Æ)-7-chloro-5-hydroxy-2,3,4,5- \ntetrahydro-1 H-1 -benzazepin-1 -yl]carbonyl} -3 -methylphenyl} - \n2-mcthylbcnzamidc.\nTolvaptan contains not less than 98.0 per cent and, not more \nthan 102.0 per cent, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Vasopressin Receptor Antagonist. v yf-v..\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline powder. ; ; < ? ;"},{"name":"identification","text":"f\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spcctrophoiometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum .with that obtained with tolvaptan IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of tolvaptan. ; < 2 \n: ,; ; ;\nB. In the assay,;.the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with test solution corresponds to the the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid, chromatography\n(2.4.14). \n. A: \nTA ymm A\nSolvent mixture. 50 voltimes of solution A and 50 volumes of \nacetonitrile. \" ’..... J \" \n'\nSolution A. A mixture of 95 volumes of buffer prepared by \ndissolving 1.36 g of potassium dihydrogen orthophosphate\n3832\n........................................................   j\ninto 1000 ml of water, adj usted to pH 3.0 with/Vzosp/,^ \nA|\nand 5 volume of acetonitrile. \nCa^ f I\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance u :: s \nexamination in the solvent mixture and'dilute to 25 0 ml • l I \nthe solvent mixture. \n. \na > \n10 . |\nReference solution. A 0.1 percent w/vsolution of totvant \"■ \nIPRS in the solvent mixture. \n.. \n'\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 12.5 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded.to porous silica (5 pm),\n- \ncolumn temperature: 30°,\nA-; mobile phase: a mixture of 57 volumes of soIutionAand\n43 volumes of a mixture of 95 volumes of acetonitrile \nand 5 volumes of water,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute, ’ '' \n'g'1'\n- \nspectrophotometer set at 260 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\ns\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumri efficiency is not less than 3000 theoretical plates and \nthe tailing factor is not more be than 2.0. \n;; '■■■'\nInject the test solution. The area of any secondary peak is not \nmore than 0.3 per cent and the sum ofareas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than 1.0 per cent, calculated by area \nnormalization: \nr< ■■: \n' *4 r ■ \n:-o.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1:0g complies with limittestforheaiy \nmetals, Method D (20 ppm). \n.; <\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent:'\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined on \n0 3 g. \ny-yy\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).. y ■\nTest solution.: Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the.solvent mixture and dilutc to ^O.Qmlwilh \nthe solvent ■. mixture,. Dilute.5.0 mh of the sqlutipn.to^Q.^ml \nwith the solvent mixture. ................. -A \nA\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution oftolyaplan \nIPRS in the solvent mixture. \n;.... ;,. ...,, 3 <. \na\nUse the chromatographic system as described jundp^; Related\nsubstances. . ....... \n~\nInject the reference solution. The test is npr^ali^upje^^: \ncolumn efficiency is riot lessthan 3000 iheorcititaPpMes» dte \ntailihg factor is hot more than 2.0 and the relativhstandard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0percenL\nInj ect the reference solution, and the test splutiony y -\n....... y i\nCalculate the content of CAl-UClNhOs. ;A: ■\n; \nw y Au A ■■■..\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.y ag \n'\nwww.webofpharma.com\n' \nMol.Wt.339.4\n^^®e;;U;2?3:4,5-di-p-l -isopropylidene-p-D-fructo- \npyranosesulphamate. . ;\nj0piraii>ate;contams tl°t less than 98.0 per cent and not more\n102,0 percent ofCi2H2iNOsS. calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis1. ' :\nCategory. Anti-convulsant. \n'\nDescription. A white to off-white powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with topiramate \nIPRSwvntit the reference spectrum of topiramate.\nB.Meltingrange (2.4.21). I20c to 130°.\nim miE-.1' jí)í; ¿i í.-íjí íT j .:?(7kTr: ■Wl’:.>';í4l?'í w:: i7?,rd \nAu Weigh »quantity of the powdered tablets containing! g of\nTopiramate, disperse in 30rnl.df dichloro met hone, ccvWxdwgQ \nat2000rpm.for lOmmiitesandfiltcr. Evaporatc thefíltratelo \ndryness. The residue,complies with the following test.\nDetermine byinfrared absorptionspectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with topiramate \n/PTbS’ or with the reference spectrum of topiramate.; at i... ■ í q >:\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak iri the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained wim the reference Solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Specific optical rotation (2.4.22). - 36.0° to-. 28.0°; determined\nin a 0,4 per cent w/v solution in methanol at 20°.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14). , .....\nTest solution. Weigh 0.4 g of the substance under examination\nand dissolve in 10 ml of amixtureof50 volumes of water- and \n50 volumes of acetonitrile...........\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution each of \n(2,3:4,5-Bis-O-(l -methylethylidene)-beta-D-fructopyranose \nIPRS (topiramate impurity A IFRS) and 2,3-O-(l-methyl- \nethylidene)-beta-D-fructopyranqse sulphamate IPRS\n{topiramate impurity B IPRS) in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\na stainless,steel column 25 cm x 4,6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n“ column temperature: 40°,\nmobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of water and \n,i 50 volumes of acetonitrile,\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n~ refractive index detector, \n:,r> . 1 \n: 1j ..\ndetector cell temperature 50c,: \nI:..-\n’nJectionvolume:50pl.\nTOPIRAMATE TABLETS\nThe retention times, of topiramate' impurity B and impurity A \nare about 2.9 and 4,5 minutes and the relative retention times \nwith respect to topiramate are 0.54 and 0.84 respectively.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peak due to topiramate impurity A and \ntopiramate impurity B is not less than 3. \ni /\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram for 40 minutes. For the òàièuiatiori of content,\nmultiply the peak areas of the following impurities by the \ncorresponding correction factor: topiramate impurity A=1.09; \ntopiramate impurity B - 0.94. The area of any secondary peak \ndue to topiramate impurity A and B is not more than 0.15 and \nthe area of any other secondary peak is not more than 0.1 per \ncent. Ignore any peak due to lactose. \n.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). \n;i ?0 -\nSulphated ash (2,3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.;\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined on 1.0 g.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2:4.14). : : --\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under \nexamination in mobile phase and dilute to 4 0.0 ml with the \nmobilephase.:\nReference solution. A1.0'per cent w/v solution of topiramate \nIPRS in the mobile phase. \nj a. : f: n s ; z\nChromatographic system ■\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), . ; )\n. - column temperature: 50° \n: \n;\n- \nmobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of water and 50 \nvolumes of acetonitrile, \n; \n..........\n- \nflow rate: 0.6 ml per minute,\n- refractive index detector (Detector cell temperature: 50°) \n- injection volume: 50 jri. \n'■ m \n/,//■':. ■\nInject the reference solution. The test is nOtAzalid unless thè \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injectionsis hòt more \nthan 2.0 per cent. \n;,,y\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. ; ;. ..\nCalculate the content of C12H21NO§S . ; \n4 \n; \n>\nStorage. Store protected from light, at a temperature not \nexceeding 30°. \n' 'r \n............\nTopiramate Tablets\nTopiramate Tabléts contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the'stated amount of \ntopiramate, Ci2H2iNO8S. . ; \n:\n3833\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOPIRAMATE TAB LETS\nUsual strengths. 25 mg,50 mg.100 mg, 200 mg,300 mg arid \n400mg. ; ;; \n37.iidm yrifhnñ aMbcrnc; AT mw 0.2 mods w>\n,;T:.qq = E. . f ,; Op ■. .7/ . , 6 J ;\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle), \n■ \n<o.o-\nMedium, 500 ml of water,, •;■<. ,r¡- \\ ;:: \n>\nSpeed and time,. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.; :\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), using the \nchromatographic' conditions described under test for Related \nsubstances. \n.-..w.-m ';iAw:i .•>;.= --n <'?•'?>\nTest solution. Use the filtrate, dilute, if necessary,-.with The \ndissolution medium. / n J <5. > ; ,>s : \n7\nReference- sbl'tiiioni Dissolve an weighed quantity of\ntopiramate IPRS in the dissolution, médium arid dilute with \nthe dissolution medium tó obtain a solution having a known \nconcentration similar to the expected coricentratidh of the \ntest solution. \n\"f:..........  '■\nCalculate the content of C^f biNOgS in lhemedium. ■ ■ ■.\nQ; ,N o t l c s s than 7D p er re ent of the statedamo unt; o f\n■C12H21NO8S.. ' : .: ii.a.agrj; vteogó WwPo \n.'i\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). .mrnCoa tete wf.bog í.mimm;-. 'wmbir wh tewte\nTest solution. To a quantity of thc powdered tablets containing \n,100 mg offopi ramate, add sufficient mobile phase to,produce\n100 ml, mix and centrifuge. Use the supernatant liquid?\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of topiramate \nIPRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system \n;r7... ,.p\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 rimi packed wiih \nmm ■: ■ octadecylsilane bonded; to>porous silica. (5 pm) (Such \nT; as Inertsil ODS 3), m-.....- -w. \n: •; 'ti’r.dr urn'm\n- column temperature: 50°, \n¿.D/.. p/;• \niqos\n3834\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of aeeton^j\nand 50 volumes of water,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- refractive index detector (Detector cell temperature 5q^ \n- injection volume: 100 pl.\nThe retention time of topiramate is about 5 minutes.\nInject the reference solution- The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for the replicate injections is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent, the column efficiency is not less than \n3000 theoretical plates and the tailing factor is not more than \n..\ninject the test solution. The content of any individúa!‘impurity \nis not more than 1.0 per cent and the sum of areas of dll impurities\nis not moré:than 2.0 per cent. Calculated by area normalisattoii \nIgnore: any peak;due ;to :identified excipientslsin the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets. <\nAssay. Determine?by .liquid chromatography .(.2.4,14) as \ndescribed"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/topiramate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Topiramate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Topiramate\nis\n2,3 :4,5-di-O-l-isopropylidene-~-D­\nfructopyranose sulphamate.\nTopiramate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0per cent ofClzHzlNOsS,calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis.\nCategory. Anti-convulsant.\nDose. 25 mg daily.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Topiramate\nis\n2,3 :4,5-di-O-l-isopropylidene-~-D­\nfructopyranose sulphamate.\nTopiramate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0per cent ofClzHzlNOsS,calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis.\nCategory. Anti-convulsant.\nDose. 25 mg daily.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with topiramate RS\nor with the reference spectrum oftopiramate.\nB. Melting range (2.4.21). 120° to 130°.\nA. Weigh a quantity ofthe powdered tablets containing 1 g of\nTopiramate, disperse in 30 ml ofdichloromethane, centrifuge\nat 2000 rpm for 10 minutes and filter. Evaporate the filtrate to\ndryness. The residue complies with the following test.\nDetennine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with topiramate RS\nor with the reference spectrum oftopiramate.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Specific optical rotation (2.4.22). -28.0° to -36.0°, detennined\nin a 0.4 per cent w/v solution in methanol at 20°.\nRelated substances. Detennine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\n.\nTest solution. Weigh accurately about 0.4 g of the substance\nunder examination and dissolve in 10 ml of a mixture of 50\nvolumes of water and 50 volumes of acetonitrile.\nReference solution. Weigh accurately about 10 mg each of\n(2;3;'4;5-Bis-O-(l-methylethylidene)-beta-D-fructopyranose\nRS\n(topiramate\nimpurity A . RS)\nand2,3-0-(l-\nmethylethylidene)-beta-D-fructopyranose sulphamate RS\n(topiramate impurity B RS) and dissolve in sufficient mobile\nphase to produce 10.0 ml.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\ncolumn temperature. 40°,\nmobile phase: a mixture of50 volumes ofwater and 50\nvolumes of acetonitrile,\nflow rate. 1ml per minute,\nrefractive index detector,\ndetector cell temperature 50°,\ninjection volume. 50 Ill.\nInject the reference solution. Run the chromatogram for 40\nminutes. The retention times of topiramate impurity A and\nimpurity B are about 2.9 and 4.5 minutes and the relative\nretention times with respect to topiramate are 0.54 and 0.84\nrespectively. The test is not valid unless the resolution between\ntopiramate impurity A and topiramate impurity B is not less\nthan 3.\nInject the test solution and the reference solution. For the\ncalculation ofcontent, multiply the peak areas ofthe following\nimpurities by the corresponding correction factor: topiramate\nimpurity A = 1.09; topiramate impurity B = 0.94\n.\nNot more than 0.15 percent each ofimpurity Aand impurity\nB, and not more than 0.1 per cent of any other impurity is\nfound.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for\nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nWater (2.3.43). Notmore than LOpercent, deterrninedon 1.0 g.\nAssay. Detennine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh accurately about 0.1 g of the substance\nunder examination and dissolve in 10 ml ofthe mobile phase.\nReference solution. Weigh accurately ;1bout 0.1 g oftopiramate\nRS and dissolve in 10.0 ml ofthe mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 Ilm),\ncolumn temperature. 50°\nmobile phase: a mixture of50 volumes ofwater and 50\nvolumes of acetonitrile,\nflow rate. 0.6 ml perminute,\nrefractive index detector (Detector cell temperature: 50°)\ninjection volume. 50 Ill.\nInject the reference solution. The relative standard deviation\nfor replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the test solution and the reference solution.\nCa.lculate the coIiteIit ofClzHzlNOsS\nStorage. Store protected from light, at a temperature not\nexceeding 30°.\n2242\nIP 2010\nTOPOTECAN HYDROCHLORIDE\nTopiramate Tablets\nTopiramate Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\ntopiramate, C12H21NOsS.\nUsual strengths. 25 mg; 50 mg.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\n.\n.\nTest solution. To a quantity ofthe powdered tablets containing\n100 mg ofTopiramate, add sufficient mobile phase to produce\n100 ml, mix and centrifuge. Use the supernatant liquid.\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution oftopiramine\nRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 Ilm) (Such\nas Inertsil ODS 3),\n-\ncolumn temperature. 50°,\n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 50 volumes ofwater,\n.\n-\nflow rate. 1ml perminute,\n-\nrefractive index detector (Detector cell temperature 50°),\n-\ninjection volume. 20 Ill.\nInject the reference solution. The relative standard deviation\nfor the replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent. The\nretention time oftopiramate is about 5 minutes. The test is not\nvalid unless the column efficiency\nis not less than 3000\ntheoretical plates and the tailing factor is notmore than 2.0\nInject the test solution. Determine the amount of related\nsubstances by the area normalization method. The content of\nany individual impurity is not more than 1.0 per cent and the\nsum ofall impurities is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nC23H23N30s.HCI\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 1,\n2243\nwww.webofpharma.com\nMedium. 500 ml ofwater,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume ofthe medium and filter.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), using the\nchromatographic conditions described in the test for Related\nsubstances.\nTest solution. Dilute the filtrate, if necessary, with the\ndissolution medium.\nReference solution. Dissolve an accurately weighed quantity\nof topiramate RS in the dissolution medium and dilute with\nthe dissolution medium to obtain a solution having a lchown\nconcentration similar to the expected concentration of the\ntest solution.\nD. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amount of\nC12H21NOsS.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) as\ndescribed in the test for Related substances.\nTestsolution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh accurately\na quantity of the powder containing. about! OOmg of\nTopiramate, disperse in 100.0 ml with the mobile phase and\ncentrifuge. Use the supernatant liquid.\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution oftopiramate\nRS in the mobile phase.\nInject the test solution and the reference solution.\nCalculate the content OfC12H21NOsS in the tablets.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not\nexceeding 30°."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/topotecan-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Topotecan Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Topotecan Hydrochloride\nTopotecan Hydrochloride is (45)-1 ()-| (dimethylamino) methyl} \n4-ethyl-4,9-dihydroxy-l/f-pyrano[3,,4/:6i,7j]ind<iHzino[l!-\nb\\quinoline-3,14(477,1277)-dione hydrochloride. íPa;\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOPOTECAN HYDROCHLORIDE\nlp2022\ntecan Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \n°dnot more than 102.0 per cent.ofCsaH^NjC^HCl, calculated\non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory.Ant>eancer.\nDescription A light yellow to greenish yellow powder.\nQ^j^jQj^^ top°tecan Hydrochloride is cytotoxic; extra\ncare is required to prevent inhaling particles and exposing\nthe skin tq fl \n'"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Topotecan Hydrochloride\nTopotecan Hydrochloride is (45)-1 ()-| (dimethylamino) methyl} \n4-ethyl-4,9-dihydroxy-l/f-pyrano[3,,4/:6i,7j]ind<iHzino[l!-\nb\\quinoline-3,14(477,1277)-dione hydrochloride. íPa;\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOPOTECAN HYDROCHLORIDE\nlp2022\ntecan Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \n°dnot more than 102.0 per cent.ofCsaH^NjC^HCl, calculated\non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory.Ant>eancer.\nDescription A light yellow to greenish yellow powder.\nQ^j^jQj^^ top°tecan Hydrochloride is cytotoxic; extra\ncare is required to prevent inhaling particles and exposing\nthe skin tq fl \n'"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A Determine; by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with topotecan \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of \ntopotecan hydrochloride.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH(2.4.24).3.5 to 4.5, determined in a 1.0 per cent w/v solution.\nSpecific optical rotation (2:422). + 30.0° to + 38.0°, determined\nin a 1.0 per cent w/v solution in methanol.\nTotal chloride. 7.6 per cent to 8.1 per cent.\nWeigh 0.5 g, dissolve in 10 ml of methanol, add 20 ml of water \nand 2^fl\\otglacial acetic acid: Titrate with 0:1 M silver \nnitrate, solution using eosin yellow solution as indicator. \nColour changes from orange to dark pink....\n1 ml of 0.7 M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.003545 g of Cl.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(Ul$<hei\nSolvent mixture. 30 volumes of acetonitrile and 70 volumes \nof a buffer solution prepared by diluting 1 ml of trifluoroacetic \nacid in i000 ml of water.\nTest solution.Dissolve 40 nig of the substance under \n«àmihâtîônin100 ml of the solvent mixture. ■\n(a). A 0.04 per cent w/v solution of\nIPRS in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (jb). Dilute 1 ml of reference solution (a) to\nÈ $$ ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (c). A solution containing 0.006 per cent\neachof 10-hydroxy comptothecinJPRS and camptothecin\nN,N’-dimethylformamide. Add 1.0 ml of the solution\ncept w/y solution of topotecan hydrochloride \nv^^JAlhe solventmixture.\nthe chromatographic system described under Assay.\nInject reference solution (c). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative retention:time, for the 10-hydroxy camptothecin and \ncamptothecin is about 1.87 and 2.62 with respect to topotecan \nhydrochloride and the relative response factors for 10-hydroxy \ncamptothecin and camptothecin are about 0.79 and 0.85 with \nrespect to topotecan hydrochloride.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than 0.15 times the area of the \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b) (0.15 per cent) and the sum of the areasof all. the secondary \npeaks is not more than the area of the peakin the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (1.0 per cent):\nHeavy metals. (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). . vT / - =\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.;\nWater (2.3.43); Not more than 13.0 per cent, determined oh 0.1g.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 16 Endotoxin Units \nper mg of topotecan hydrochloride. ■ \no. :•<- ;\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Total aerobic viable count is \nnot more than 103 CFU per g. 1 g is free from Escherichia co fl \nStaphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas aeruginosa. g \nis free from Salmonella..and Shigella. \n.. ._• .\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2:4: 14)/ - >; J\nSolvent mixture, a mixture of 30 volumes of acetonitrile and\n70 volumes of a buffer solution, prepared by diluting 4i nil of \ntrifluoroacetic acid to 1000 ml with water. \n,.\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 25.0 ml of the solvent mixture.\nReference solution. A 0.04 per cent w/v solution'Of topotecan\nhydrochloride IPRS in the solvent mixture. !' D\nChromatographic system \n-■ ■ '';:i '\n■.: a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), ; .\n- mobile phase: A. a solution of 1 ml of trifluoroacetic\nacid in 1000 ml of water,\nB. acetonitrile^ ■ '■\n• --flowrate: 1.2 ml per minute,\n■ a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- spectrophotometer set at 260 nm, .\n: - ; injection volume: 10 pl. : i; = ?•\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n( in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n85\n< •: 15\n28\n70\n. ' 30\n38\n77 \n:\n30 '\n. 43 \n' .\n■ 85 \\ .\n■ 15\n48\n85\n15\n3835\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOPOTECAN INJECTION\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unlessthe \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan2.0 per cent. nn,-- E bnn v ; n:,\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. j \"\nCalculate the content of C23H23N3O5HCI. ...\nStorage. Store protected from light, at a temperature between\nTopotecan Injectioh' ■;\n■ ■'*! ’ ‘ z\nTopotecan Hydrochloride Inj ection - . n. v :