/monographs/topotecan-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Topotecan Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Topotecan Injection\nTopotecan Hydrochloride Injection\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nTorsemide\nTopotecan Injection is a sterile, stabilised solution of\nTopotecan Hydrochloride in Water for Injection.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent. \t\n1,\n0 CH 3\nTopotecan Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of the \ntopotecan, C23}123N305 .\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 13.0 per cent, determined on 0.1g.\nCH 3 \nH H \nNH\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 16 Endotoxin Units \nper mg of topotecan hydrochloride.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Total aerobic viable count is \nnot more than 102 CFU per g. 1 g is free from Escherichia coli, \nStaphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas aeruginosa. 10 g\nDescription. A clear, light yellow solution."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Topotecan Injection\nTopotecan Hydrochloride Injection\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nTorsemide\nTopotecan Injection is a sterile, stabilised solution of\nTopotecan Hydrochloride in Water for Injection.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent. \t\n1,\n0 CH 3\nTopotecan Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of the \ntopotecan, C23}123N305 .\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 13.0 per cent, determined on 0.1g.\nCH 3 \nH H \nNH\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 16 Endotoxin Units \nper mg of topotecan hydrochloride.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Total aerobic viable count is \nnot more than 102 CFU per g. 1 g is free from Escherichia coli, \nStaphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas aeruginosa. 10 g\nDescription. A clear, light yellow solution."},{"name":"Identification","text":"CH 3\nis free from Salmonella and Shigella.\nA. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nC161420N403S \nMol. Wt. 348.4\nSolvent mixture. a mixture of 30 volumes of acetonitrile and \n70 volumes of a buffer solution, prepared by diluting I ml of\namino]carbony1]-4-[(3-methylphenypamino]-1-Isopropyl-3 [(4- \nTorsemide is 3-pyridinesulfonamide, N-[[(1-methylethyl)\nB. It gives the reaction of chlorides (2.3.1).\ntrifluoroacetic acid to 1000 ml with water.\nin-toludino-3-pyridyl) sulfonyl] urea."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/torsemide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Torsemide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Torsemide \nv'\nCi6H20N4O3S \n■: , , \n' Mol..Wt.348.4\nTorsemide is 3-pyridinesulfonamide, A(-[[(lrmpthylethyi) \namino Jcarbony l]-4-[(3-methylphenyl)amino] -1-Isopropyl-3 [(4- \nm-toludino-3-pyridyl) sulfonyl] urea. . \n,,V1..\nTorsemide contains hot less than 9 8.0' per cent and not more \nthan. 102.0 per cent of Ci6II?.oN403S, calculated qn;the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Diuretic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder, , (;"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Torsemide \nv'\nCi6H20N4O3S \n■: , , \n' Mol..Wt.348.4\nTorsemide is 3-pyridinesulfonamide, A(-[[(lrmpthylethyi) \namino Jcarbony l]-4-[(3-methylphenyl)amino] -1-Isopropyl-3 [(4- \nm-toludino-3-pyridyl) sulfonyl] urea. . \n,,V1..\nTorsemide contains hot less than 9 8.0' per cent and not more \nthan. 102.0 per cent of Ci6II?.oN403S, calculated qn;the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Diuretic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder, , (;"},{"name":"Identification","text":";/\\7.\nA.; Determine by infrared, absorption spectrophotometer}' \n(2.4.6). Compare the,, spectrum with that;obtained w.ith \ntorsemide IPRS or with the reference spectrum of torsemide-\nB. In the Assay, the principal . peak in the cnrqmatograip \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution. ; v\nTests , \n, \n. t .\nRelated’'substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4;14)< v- I-.. W; :■ ■\nTest solution. Dissolve 38 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 30 ml of methanol, witii tlie aid of ultrasoun \nfor 10 minutes; Add 45 ml of 0.02 Mpotassiumphospha^\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022 \n..........................................................................................\n& fferpH 3'5 ¡and dilute to 100.0 ml with the mobile phase,\nfilter. --'••• \n\", t . \\ \n■\nt> ference solution (a). Dissolve 9.5 mg, each of, torsemide\n^effcompound \ntorsemiderelated compound,B\n\\p^ s^dpr^emide related compound CIPRS in 30 ml of \n^^¿>4 with the aid of ultrasound about 10 minutes and\ndd45 ml df 0- @2 Mpotassium phosphate buffer pH 3.5and \n¿iute tóTOO.O ml with the mobile phase: Dilute 1.0 ml of the \nelution to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 3 mg, each of torsemide \nIPRS and torsemide related compound A IPRS in 30 ml of\npjg/Jjanp/, y/ith the aid of ultraspund for IQ minutes and add \n45 ml of P- 02 Mpotassium phosphate buffer pH 3.5 and dilute \nto100 0 mlAVith thc mobile phase. .\nUse the chromatographic system as described under Assay.\nInject réference Solution (b).: The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to torsemide and torsemide \nrelated compound A is not less, thanl .0, the tailing factor is \nnot more than 2.0 and the relative standard deviation for \nreplicate injections is hot more than 10.0 per cent for the\ntorsemide peak. -■■■.} r.;. .\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution; Run the \nchromatogram 3 times the retention time of the principal peak \nfortés