l degradation products.\n'[i J2',4iTriazoioi4,3-a]pyfidin-3(2iO-one,\n21 -(3 -Chlorophenyl )piperazine,\n33-[4’(3-chiorophenyl)piperaziii-l-yl]propan-l-ol,\n4l-{3-[4-(3-chloropheriyi)piperazin-l-yl]propyI}-[l,2,4]triazoldi4,3- \najpyridin-r-ium-3-dlateJ \n■'>\n51,1-Bis {2-chlor6-[4-(3-{T,2,4,-triazolo[453-a jpyf idin-3-(2ZT)-on-2- \nyl}propyl)piperazine-1 -yljphenyl}ethane trihydrochloride,\n6l-(3-Qhlorophenyl)-4-(3-chlQropropyl)piperazine, . ... , \n'l,31-B.is(4-(3-chlorpphenyL)piperazin-l-yl)propane,.,\n8l,4-bis(3-chlorophenyl)piperazine. \n■\n3854\n.................................................. :...................... .......................\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid \nthe resolution between the peaks due to trazodone \ncompound C and trazodone is not less than 2 5 ¡R ■ \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b), the tail \nfactor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard devi \nfor replicate injections is not more than 5.0 per cent in u \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a). \n-\nInject reference solution (a) 'and the test-solution; In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of^ \nsecondary peak is not more than 10 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the Chromatogram obtained with reference­\nsolution (a) (1.0 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all \nsecondary peaks is not more than 20 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (2.0 per cent). ' r .,\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as \ndescribed under Dissolution with the following modifications\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof the powder containing 50 mg of Trazodbne Hydrochloride \nin 0.0 IM hydrochloric acid, with the aid ofultrasoundfor3Q \nminutes and dilute to 50.0. ml with 0.01 M..hydrochloric acid, \nDilute 5.0 ml of the solution to 50.0 ml with 0.01 M hydrochloric \nacid. \n.\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of trazodone \nhydrochloride IPRS in 0.01 Mhydrochloric acid.\nOil\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\n0.6\nCalculate the content of Ci9H22CIN5O, HCl in the tablets. i\nStorage, Store protected from light and moisture^ at a \ntemperature not exceeding 30°. : .. ■■■ ■■■\n0.97\n1.0\n7 1.5 ■ ■"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamcinolone-acetonide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamcinolone Acetonide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triamcinolone Acetonide Injection\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection is a sterile suspension of \nTriamcinolone Acetonide in very fine particles in Water for\nInjections containing suitable dispersing agents.\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of triamcinolone acetonide, C24H31 F06.\nUsual strength. 40 mg per ml."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triamcinolone Acetonide Injection\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection is a sterile suspension of \nTriamcinolone Acetonide in very fine particles in Water for\nInjections containing suitable dispersing agents.\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of triamcinolone acetonide, C24H31 F06.\nUsual strength. 40 mg per ml."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Extract a volume containing about 50 mg of Triamcinolone \nAcetonide with two quantities, each of 10 ml, ofperoxide-free \nether and discard the ether extracts. Filter the aqueous layer \nthrough a sintered-glass filter, wash the residue wittyfOur \nquantities, each of 5 ml of water and dry at 105° for -1 hout The \nresidue complies with the following tests.\nTRIAMCINOLONE ACETONIDE \nTRIAMTERENE\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \npreparations (Injections).\nTest A may be omitted if tests B and C are carried out. Test c \nmay be omitted if tests A and B are carried out.\nA. When examined in the range 250 nm to 380 nm (2.4.6), a \n0.001 per cent w/v solution in a mixture of 9 volumes  of ethanol \n(95 per cent)  and 1 volume of I M hydrochloric acid shows \nabsorption maxima at about 262 nm and 360 nrn, and a shoulder \nat about 285 nm.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (14.6) \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with triamcinolone. \nacetonide RS  or with the reference spectrum of triamcinolone \nacetonide.\nTest solution. Shake and mix contents of 10 containers.\nvolume of the freshly mixed injection with 0.01 per \nDissolve\n \ncent w/v a \nv solution of prednisolone in methanol (internal\n)to obtain a final concentration of 0.02 per cent w/v \nstandard)\nB. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with  silica gel G.\nB.A 0.1 per cent w/v solution in anhydrous formic acid, when \nexamined under ultraviolet light at 365 nm shows an intense \nblue fluorescence. Solutions in other acids also exhibit a blue \nfluorescence.\noftriamcinolone acetonide, shake and filter.\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.02 per cent \nw/v of triamcinolone acetonide RS in internal standard.\nSolvent mixture. 90 volumes of acetone and 10 volumes of\n.formamide. \t\n, i•\nReference solution (b). Prepare in the same manner as test \nsolution but omitting the internal standard.\nThe final solution obtained in the Assay has a bluish \nfluorescence and when examined in the range 250 nisi to t Cthi-1)2C)7 - \n380 nm (2.4.7), shows an absorption maximum at about_ Tribityl. Citrate contains not less than 99.0 per cent of \n360 nm. \t\nc4,8143207, Calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nTRICHLOROMONOFLUOROMETHANE \nTRIAMTERENE\n(2.4.17), coating the plate with \nsilica gel G.\nCI\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tributyl citrate\nMobile phase. A mixture of 90 volumes of ethyl acetate,\nCI - C - F\n10 volumes of 18 M ammonia and 10 volumes of methanol.\nCl\nRS or with the reference spectrum of tributyl citrate.\nTest solution. Dissolve a quantity of the contents of the \ncapsules containing 0.1 g of Triamterene in sufficient dimethyl \nsulphoxide to produce 20 ml and dilute 2 ml to 50 ml with \nmethanol.\n13. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nCC13F \t\nMol. Wt. 137.4\nTrichloromonofluoromethane contains not less than 99.6 per \ncent and not more than 100.0 per cent of CC1 3F, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nReference solution. Dilute 1 volume of the test solution to \n200 volumes with methanol."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Mobile phase. A mixture of 115 volumes of  cyclohexane, \n56 volumes of chloroform and 29 volumes of toluene.\nAcidity. Boil 1.0 g with 20 ml of  water  for 5 minutes, cool, filter \nand wash the filter with three quantities, each of 10 ml, of \nwater. Combine the filtrate and washings and add 0.3 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution. Not more than 1.5 ml of 0.01 M \nsodium hydroxide  is required to change the colour of the \nsolution.\ny s t be\na a st ot a g rnal pe sh si\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10 ml of the solvent mixture.\nm \ns\nsteel\nonded to porous silica (10 gm), \ncolumn 25 cm x 4 mm, packed with\nC h r_o moctadecylsilane\nReference solution (a).  Dissolve 25 mg of Mari, \nacetonide RS in 10 ml of the solvent mixture.\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 56 volumes  of methanol and\n44 volumes of water, \n- flow rate: 1.4 ml per minute, \n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nReference solution (h).  Mix equal volumes of the test solution \nand reference solution (a). \t\nit\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nPlace the dry plate in a tank containing a shallow layer of the \nsolvent mixture, allow the solvent mixture to ascend to the \ntop, remove the plate from the tank and allow the solvent to \nevaporate. Use within 2 hours, with the flow of the mobile\nTest solution. Dissolve 10 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 10.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nInject reference solution (a). Adjust the flow rate of the mobile \nphase such that the separation of the triamcinolone acetonide \nand internal standard is optimised with a retention time of \nabout 15 minutes for triamcinolone acetonide.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\nphase in the direction in which the aforementioned treatment \nwas done.\n100.0 ml with the mobile phase. Further dilute 1.0 ml of this \nsolution to 10.0 ml with the mobile phase. \nReference solution (b).  A 0.0005 per cent w/v solution of \nnitrosotriaminopyrimidine RS (triamterene impurity A RS) \nin the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml of this solution to 100.0 ml \nwith the mobile phase.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of  C24 H31F06in the injection.\nApply to the plate 5 gl of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 12 cm. Dry the plate in a current of warm air, allow \nthe solvent to evaporate, heat at 120° for 15 minutes and spray \nthe hot plate with  ethanolic sulphuric acid (20 per cent vly).\nStorage. Store protected from light.\n40111fr\nHeat at 120° for a further 10 minutes, allow to cool and examine \nin daylight and under ultraviolet light at 365 nm. The principal \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorresponds to that in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a). The principal spot in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) appears as a single,\nReference solution (c). Dissolve a suitable quantity of 2,7- \ndiamino-6-phenylpteridin-4-ol RS (triamterene impurity B \nRS) in 0.5 ml of the mobile phase with the aid of ultrasound. To \nthis solution, add 0.5 ml of the test solution. \nChromatographic system\nTriamterene\netim \tH2N \nilt\nN\nNH2\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4 mm, packed  with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), \n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 0.2 volume ofbutylamine, 20 \nvolumes of acetonitrile,  20 volumes of methanol and \na6cOvolumes of water, adjusted to pH 5.3 with acetic \nacid,\ncompact spot.\nC. Heat 0.5 ml of chromic-sulphuric acid in a test-tube (5 cm x\nN\nN\nabout 6 mm) in a naked flame until white fumes are evolved:\n NH2\nthe solution wets the sides of the tube readily and there is no\ngreasiness. Add about 2 mg of the substance under \nexamination and again heat in a naked flame until white fumes \nappear; the solution does not wet the sides of the tube and \ndoes not pour easily from the tube.\nC,2H„N7\nMol. Wt. 253.3 \nTriamterene is 247-triamino-6-phenylpteridine.\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 320 nm and 355 nm, \ninjection volume: 501.d.\nTriamterene contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C l2H I , N7, calculated on the dried basis. \nCategory. Potassium sparing diuretic.\nThe relative retention time with reference to triamterene for \ntriamterene impurity A is about 0.6, for triamterene impurity B \n. is aboit-0,8 and for triamterene impurity C is about 1.7.\npH (24,24). 5.0 to 7.5.\nBactirial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 4.4 Endotain\nDose. 150 to 250 mg daily, in divided doses.\nInject reference solutions (b) and (c). The test is not valid \nunless the resolution between the peaks due to triamterene\nDescription A yellow, crystalline powder: odourless.\n Units per rtg of triamcinolone acetonide.\n304- \n3425\nimpurity B and triamterene at 355 nm is not less than 1.5 in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) if \nnecessary, increase the quantity of water in the mobile phase \nand signal-to- noise ratio for the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) at 320 nm \nis not less than 10. Calculate the contents of the impurities in \nthe test solution using the correction factors, 1.8 for triamterene \nimpurity B and 1.5 for triamterene impurity C.\nInject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to triamterene impurity A at 320 nm is not \nmore than the area of the corresponding peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (50 ppm). \nThe area of any peak corresponding to triamterene impurities \nB and C at 355 nm is not more than the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0.1 per cent). The area of any other secondary peak at \n355 nm is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per \ncent). The sum of areas of a"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamcinolone-acetonide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamcinolone Acetonide"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triamcinolone Acetonide\no\nCZJf31F06\nMol. Wt. 434.5\nTriamcinoloneAcetonide is 9a-fluoro-I1 ~,21-dihydroxy­\n16a,17a-isopropylidenedioxy-l ,4-pregnadiene-3,20-dione.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIAMCINOLONE ACETONIDE\nTriamcinolone Acetonide contains not less than 96.0 per cent\nand not more than 104.0 per cent ofCz4H3IF06, calculated on\nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Corticosteroid.\nDose. By deep intramuscular injection, 40 mg to 100 mg.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nodourless or almost odourless."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triamcinolone Acetonide\no\nCZJf31F06\nMol. Wt. 434.5\nTriamcinoloneAcetonide is 9a-fluoro-I1 ~,21-dihydroxy­\n16a,17a-isopropylidenedioxy-l ,4-pregnadiene-3,20-dione.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIAMCINOLONE ACETONIDE\nTriamcinolone Acetonide contains not less than 96.0 per cent\nand not more than 104.0 per cent ofCz4H3IF06, calculated on\nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Corticosteroid.\nDose. By deep intramuscular injection, 40 mg to 100 mg.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nodourless or almost odourless."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test A may be omitted iftests Band C are carried out. Test C\nmay be omitted iftests A and B are carried out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with triamcinolone\nacetonide RS.\nB. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\nthe plate with silica gel G.\nSolvent mixture. A mixture of 90 volumes of acetone and\n10 volumes offormamide.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 115 volumes of cyclohexane,\n56 volumes ofchlor(}form and 29 volumes oftoluene.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under\nexamination in 10 ml ofthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 25 mg of triamcinolone\nacetonide RS in 10 ml ofthe solvent mixture.\nReferencesolution (b). Mix equal volumes ofthe test solution\nand reference solution (a).\nPlace the dry plate in a tan1e containing a shallow layer ofthe\nsolvent mixture, allow the solvent mixture to ascend to the\ntop, remove the plate from the tan1e and allow the solvent to\nevaporate. Use within 2 hours, with the flow of the mobile\nphase in the direction in which the aforementioned treatment\nwas done.\nApply to the plate 5 /-ll of each solution. Allow the mobile\nphase to rise 12 cm. Dry the plate in a current ofwarm air, allow\nthe solvent to evaporate, heat at 120° for 15 minutes and spray\nthe hot plate with ethanolic sulphuric acid (20 per cent vlv).\nHeat at 120° for a further 10 minutes, allow to cool and examine\nin daylight and in ultraviolet light at 365 mn. The principal\nspot in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution\ncorresponds to that in the chromatogram obtained with\nreference solution (a). The principal spot in the chromatogram\nobtained with reference solution (b) appears as a single,\ncompact spot.\ne. Heat 0.5 ml ofchromic-sulphuric acidin a test-tube (5 cm x\nabout 6 mm) in a naked flame until white fumes are evolved;\nthe solution wets the sides ofthe tube readily and there is no\ngreasiness. Add about 2 mg of the substance under\nIF 2010\nexamination and again heat in a naked flame until white fumes\nappear; the solution does not wet the sides ofthe tube and\ndoes not pour easily from the tube."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Light absorption (2.4.7). When examined in the range 230 run\nto 360 mn, a 0.001 per cent w/v solution in ethanol (95 per\ncent) shows an absorption maximum at about 239 nm;\nabsorbance at about 239 nm, 0.34 to 0.37.\nSpecific optical rotation (2,4,22). +100° to +107°, determined\nin a 1.0 per cent w/v solution in dioxan.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nNOTE - CarlY out the test protectedii\"om light.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under\nexamination in 7 ml ofmethanol and dilute to 10 ml with water.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 2 mg of triamcinolone\nacetonide RS and 2 mg oftriamcinolone in the mobile phase\nand dilute to 100 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1 ml of the test solution to\n100ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 /-lm),\nmobile phase: a mixture prepared by mixing 525 ml of\nmethanol with 400 ml ofwater, allowingto equilibrate,\nadjusting the volume to 1000.0ml with water and mixing\nagain,\nflow rate. 1.5 ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\ninjection volume. 20 /-l1.\nEquilibrate the column with the mobile phase for about\n10 minutes.\nInject reference solution (b). Adjust the sensitivity of the\nsystem so that the height of the principal peak is at least\n50 per cent ofthe full scale ofthe recorder.\nInject reference solution (a). The retention times are;\ntriamcinolone, about 5 minutes and triamcinolone acetonide\nabout 17 minutes. The test is not valid unless the resolution\nbetween the peaks corresponding to triamcinolone and\ntriamcinolone acetonide is at least 15; ifnecessary, adjust the\nconcentration of methanol in the mobile phase.\nInject the test solution and reference solution (b). Continue\ntbe~bromJ:ltogmpbyfor 3.5 timesJhe retentiontimeofthe\nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the test\nsolution. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution\nthe area ofany peak other than the principal peak is not more\nthan 0.25 times the area of the principal peak in the\n2254\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 20ID\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (b)\n(0.25 per cent); the sum ofthe areas ofall the peaks other than\nthe principal peak is not greater than half the area of the\nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (b) (0.5 per cent). Ignore any peak due to the solvent\nand any peak with an area less than 0.05 times the area ofthe\nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (b).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.2 percent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 2.0 per cent, detennined on 0.2 g.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 25 mg and dissolve in sufficient\nethanol to produce 100.0 ml and mix. Dilute 5.0 ml to 100.0 ml\nwith ethanol (95 per cent) and measure the absorbance of\nthe resulting solution at the maximum at about 239 nm (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content ofC24H31F06 taking 354 as the specific\nabsorbance at 239 urn.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamcinolone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamcinolone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triamcinolone\nTriamcinolone is 9a~ fluoro-11 ß, 16a, 17a,21 -tetrahydroxy- \npregna-1,4-diene-3,20-dione. \n\\;;\nTriamcinolone contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103.0 per cent of C2 JI27FO6. calculatedon thedri\nbasis.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIAMCINOLONE TABLETS\n.............................................................................................\n^^Corticosteroid.\n' •Xtinn A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nne5cnpllun- .\nSl^^r0SC0P’C*\npetennine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\n* -»»¿fhe spectrum with that obtained with triamcino lone \njpjjy^xvlththe reference spectrum of triamcinolone.\ng^heii examined in the range 220 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \nO^ feH'W^v.solution in methanol shows an absorption\ninaXiinumatabout238 nm; absorbance at about 238 nm, about\n■ ■ '■ \n■ i\nC O *n 0 bf ethanol (95 per cent), add .5 ml of \na 1 per cent w/v solution of butylated hydroxytoluene in \nethanol (95 cwt) an^ 5 ml of 1. M sodium hydroxide and \nheat ¿n a Watei-bath under a reflux condenser for 20 minutes; \napihkish lavender colour is produced."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triamcinolone\nTriamcinolone is 9a~ fluoro-11 ß, 16a, 17a,21 -tetrahydroxy- \npregna-1,4-diene-3,20-dione. \n\\;;\nTriamcinolone contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103.0 per cent of C2 JI27FO6. calculatedon thedri\nbasis.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIAMCINOLONE TABLETS\n.............................................................................................\n^^Corticosteroid.\n' •Xtinn A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nne5cnpllun- .\nSl^^r0SC0P’C*\npetennine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\n* -»»¿fhe spectrum with that obtained with triamcino lone \njpjjy^xvlththe reference spectrum of triamcinolone.\ng^heii examined in the range 220 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \nO^ feH'W^v.solution in methanol shows an absorption\ninaXiinumatabout238 nm; absorbance at about 238 nm, about\n■ ■ '■ \n■ i\nC O *n 0 bf ethanol (95 per cent), add .5 ml of \na 1 per cent w/v solution of butylated hydroxytoluene in \nethanol (95 cwt) an^ 5 ml of 1. M sodium hydroxide and \nheat ¿n a Watei-bath under a reflux condenser for 20 minutes; \napihkish lavender colour is produced."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Specific optical rotation (2.4.22).+65.0° to + 72.0°, determined\nin a h0 pet cent w/v solution'in dimethylformainide.\n. ■ -\nRelatedsubstances.Determinc by liquid chromatography\nNOTE—Prepare the following solutions immediately before \nuse and protect from light-.'\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in a mixture of equal volumes of methanol and \nwater and dilute to 10 ml with the same solvent mixture.\nReference solution. Dilute 1 mlof the test solution to 100 ml \nwith a mixture of equal volumes of methanol and water.I\nIdentification ;;\nChromatographic system\n- a.stainless;steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n: ba^e-deactiVated end-capped octadecyl silane bonded\ni to porous silica (5 pm), '\n~ nibbiléphàse: a mixture prepared by mixing 525 ml of\nmethanol with 400 ml of water, allowing to equilibrate, \nadjusting the volume to 1000.0 ml with water and mixing \nagain, \"\n~ flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n“ spectrophotometer set at 238 nm,\ninjection volume: 20 pl.\ninjectthe reference solution and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 4.5 times the retention time of triamcinolone \n(about 11 minutes).' In the chromatogram obtained with the\nDissolution (2.5.2).\n!esi solution the area of any peak other than the principal\nis not more than the area of the principal peak in the\nc Oinatôgram obtained with thé reference solution (1 per \n^nt), nbt more than two such peaks have an area more than\n• tunes' the; area of the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the reference solution (0.5 per cent); the sum of \nthe areas of all the peaks other than the principal peak is not \nmore than twice, the area of the; principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (2 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.05 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference solution (0.05 per cent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 0.8 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy‘metals, Method B (25 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.2 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19): Not more than 2:0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 60° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7 kPa for 3 hours.\nAssay. Dissolve 25 mg in sufficient ethanol (95 per cent) to \nproduce 100.0 ml and mix. Dilute 2.0 ml to 50.0 ml with ethanol \n(95 per cent) and measure the absorbance of the resulting \nsolution at themaximum at about 238 nm (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content bfC21H27FO6/taking 389 as the specific \nabsorbance at 238 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nTriamcinolone Tablets\nTriamcinolone Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntriamcinolone, C21H27FO6.\nUsual strengths. 2 mg; 4 mg; 8 mg.\nA. Dissolve 1 mg of the residue obtained in the test.for Related \nsubstances in 6 ml of ethanol (95 percent), add 5 mlof a 1 per \ncent w/v solution'of butylated hydroxy toluene in ethanol \n(95 per centf ww5.5 ml of / :M sodium hydroxide and heat on \na water-bath under a reflux condenser for 20 minutes; a pinkish \nlavender colour is produced.\nB. In the Assay, the chromatogram obtained with the principal \npeak obtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak \ndue to triamcinolone in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a). . \n. .\nApparatus No. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.01Mhydrochloric acid,\nSpeed and time. 100 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable, volume of the medium and filter.Measure \nthe absorbance Of the filtrate, dilute suitably if necessary with\n3855\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIAMCINOLONE TABLETS\nthe medium, at the maximum at about 238 nm (2.4.7). Calculate \nthe content of C?|H27FO6 in the medium from the absorbance \nobtained from a solution of known concentration of \ntriamcinolone IP RS the dissolution medium. '\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of thè stated amount of C211127FO6.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Shake a quantity of the, powdered tablets \ncontaining 15 mg of Triamcinolone with 15 ml of ethanol for\n15 minutes, filter under reduced pressure through a fine filter \npaper (such as Whatman No : 42) and evaporate the filtratelo \ndryness using a rotary evaporator. Reserve 1 mg of the residue \nfor Identification test A. Dissolve the remainder of the residue \nin 15 ml of methanol. . .\nReference solution (a).. Dilute 4 ml.of the. test solution to\n100 ml with methanol.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 5 ml of thè test solution to 50 ml \nwith methanol. Dilute 5 ml of the resulting solution to 50 ml \nwith the same solvent. \n.\nChromatographic system \n. ,\n- a stainless steel column 20 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of methanol and water, adjusted\nso that the retention time' of triamcinolone is about \n5 minutes (approximately equal volumes of methanol\n. and water), ....... \n, .\n- flow rate: 2 ml per minute, \nj\n- \n- spectrophotometer set at 238 nm, \n- injectionvolume:20pl.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency determined from the principal peak is not \nless than 2000 theoretical plates.\nInject.reference solution (b). Run the chromatogram 4 times \nthe retention time of the triamcinolone peak. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than twice the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(b) (2.Ó per cent) and not more than one such peak has an area \nmore than that of the principal' peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) (1.0 per cent) and the sum \nof the areas of any such peaks is not more than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (4.0 per cent).\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \nTablets.\nCrush one tablet to a fine powder, add 100 ml of ethanol \n(95per cent) and shake for 10 minutes. Filter, d"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test A may \nomitted if tests B and C are carried out. Test C\nmay beomitted if tests A and B are carried out.\nA Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompareihespcctrum with that obtained with triamcinolone\nacetonide IPBS: • ■ \n•\nB. Determine bythin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\nthe plate with silica gel G.\nSolvent mixture .90 volumes of acetone and 10 volumes of\nformamide. vA\nMobile phase. A mixture of 115 volumes of cyclohexane,\n56 volumes of chloroform and 29 volumes of toluene.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10 ml of the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 25 mg of triamcinolone\nsolvent mixture.\nReference-solution (b). Mix equal volumes of the test solution\nand reference solution (a).\nPlace the dry plate in a tank containing a shallow layer of the \nsolvent mixture, allow the solvent mixture to ascend to the\ntop, remove, the plate from the tank and allow the solvent to \nevaporate. Use within 2 hours, with the flow of the mobile \nphase in the direction in which the aforementioned treatment\nlOminutes.\nwas done.\nApply to thp, plate 5 pl of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 12 cm. Dry the plate in a current of warm air, allow \nthe solvent to evaporate, heat at 120° for 15 minutes and spray \nthe hotplate with ethanolic sulphuric acid (20per cent v/v). \nHeat at 120° for a further 10 minutes, allow to cool and examine\ndaylight and under ultraviolet light at 365 nm. The principal\nsP°t.ihthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution\nCOrresponds to that in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a). The principal spot in the chromatogram \nobtained witli reference solution (b) appears as a single,\ncompact spot.\n: \n9*5 ml of chromic-sulphuric acid in a test-tube (5 cm x\n* \n$ mrn) in a naked flame until white fumes are evolved;\nsolution; wets the sides of the tube readily and there is no \nEasiness. Add about 2 mg of the substance under\nTRIAMCINOLONE ACETONIDE\nexamination and again heat in a naked flame until white fumes \nappear; the solution does not wet the sides of the tube and \ndoes not pour easily from the tube.\nLight absorption (2.4.7). When examined in the range 230 nm \nto 360 nm, a 0.001 per cent w/v solution in ethanol (95 per \ncent) shows an absorption maximum at about 239 nm; \nabsorbance at about 239 nm, 0.34 to 0.37.\nSpecific optical rotation (2,4,22). + 100° to +107°, determined \nin a 1.0 per cent w/v solution in dioxan.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nNOTE - Carry out the test protectedfrom light.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 7 ml of methanol and dilute to 10 ml withwater.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 2 mg of triamcinolone \nacetonide IPRS and 2 mg of triamcinolone in the mobile phase \nand dilute to 100 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1 ml of the test solution to\n100 ml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture prepared by mixing 525 ml of\nmethanol with 400 ml of water, allowing to equilibrate, \nadjusting the volume to 1000.0 ml with water and mixing \nagain, \n- \nr ‘\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 ul.\nEquilibrate the column with the mobile phase for about\nInject reference solution (b). Adjust the sensitivity, of the \nsystem so that the height of the principal peak is at least \n50 per cent of the full scale of the recorder.\nInject reference solution (a). The retention times are; \ntriamcinolone, about 5 minutes and triamcinolone acetonide \nabout 17 minutes. The test is not valid unless the resolution \nbetween the peaks corresponding to triamcinolone and \ntriamcinolone acetonide is at least 15; if necessary, adjust the \nconcentration of methanol in the mobile phase.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. Continue \nthe chromatography for 3.5 times the retention time of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \nthe area of any peak other than the principal peak is not more \nthan 0.25 times the area of the principal peak in the\n3857\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIAMCINOLONE ACETONIDE INJECTION\nchromatogram1 obtained with reference solution (b) \n(0.25 per cent); the sum of the areas ofall the peaks other than \nthe principal peak is not greater than half the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b) (0.5 per cent). Ignore any peak due to the solvent \nand any peak with an area,less than 0.05 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (b). ; ,,\nSuIphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.2 per cent. \n- -■\nWater (2.3.43); Notnidre than 2.0 per cent, determined oh 0.2 g.\nAssay. Dissolve 25 mg in sufficient ethanol to produce\n100.0 ml and mix. Dilute 5.0 ml to 100;0 ml with ethanol \n(95 per cent) and measure the absorbance of the resulting \nsolution at the maximum at about 239 nm (2.4.7)..\nCalculate the content of C24H3iFO6 taking 354 as the specific \nabsorbance at 239 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture. ,\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection is a sterile suspension of \nTriamcinolone Acetonide in very fíne particles in Water for \nInjections containing suitable dispersing agents.\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount bftriamcinolone acetonide, C24H3iFO6.\nUsual strength. 40 mg per ml.\nExtract a volume containing about 50 mg of Triamcinolone \nAcetonide with two quantities, each of 10 ml, of peroxide-free \nether and discard the ether extracts. Filter the aqueous layer \nthrough a sintered-glass filter, wash the residue with four \nquantities, each of 5 nil of waterand dry at 105° for 1 hour. The"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamterene","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamterene"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triamterene\nMol. Wt. 253.3\nTriamterene is 2,4,7-triamino-6-phenylpteridine.\nTriamterene contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent ofC1ZHlIN7, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Potassium sparing diuretic.\nDose. 150 to 250 mg daily, in divided doses.\nDescription. A yellow, crystalline powder; odourless."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triamterene\nMol. Wt. 253.3\nTriamterene is 2,4,7-triamino-6-phenylpteridine.\nTriamterene contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent ofC1ZHlIN7, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Potassium sparing diuretic.\nDose. 150 to 250 mg daily, in divided doses.\nDescription. A yellow, crystalline powder; odourless."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. When examined in the range 250 mn to 380 nm (2.4.6), a\n0.001 per cent w/v solution in a mixture of9 volumes ofethanol\n(95 per cent) and 1 volume of 1 M hydrochloric acid shows\nabsorption maxima at about 262 urn and 360 urn, and a shoulder\nat about 285 nm.\nB. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution in anhydrousformic acid, when\nexamined in ultraviolet light at 365 nm shows an intense blue\nfluorescence. Solutions in other acids also exhibit a blue\nfluorescence.\nThe final solution obtained in the Assay has a bluish\nfluorescence and when examined in the range 250 nm to\n380 nm (2.4.7), shows an absorption maximum at about\n360nm."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Acidity. Boil 1.0 g with 20 ml ofwaterfor 5minutes, cool, filter\nand wash the filter with three quantities, each of 10 ml, of\nwater. Combine the filtrate and washings and add 0.3 ml of\nphenolphthalein solution. Not more than 1.5 ml of 0.01 M\nIP 2010\nsodium hydroxide is required to change the colour of the\nsolution.\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography\n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel G\nMobile phase. A mixture of 90 volumes of ethyl acetate,\n10 volumes of 18 M ammonia and 10 volumes ofmethanol.\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under exami-\nnation in 20 ml ofdimethyl sulphoxide and dilute 2 ml ofthe\nresulting solution to 50 ml with methanol.\nReference solution. Dilute 1 volume of the test solution to\n200 volumes with methanol.\nApply to the plate 5 III of each solution. After development,\ndry the plate in air until the odour of solvent is no longer\ndetectable and examine in ultraviolet light at 365 urn. Any\nsecondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with the test\nsolution is not more intense than the spot in the chromatogram\nobtained with the reference solution.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.0percent, determined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.15 g, dissolve in 5 ml of\nanhydrous formic acid, add 100 ml of anhydrous glacial\nacetic acid. Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric acid, determining\nthe end-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank\ntitration.\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02533 g of\nClzHIIN7.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nTriamterene Capsules\nTriamterene Capsules contain not less than 95.0 per cent and\nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of\ntriamterene, ClzHIIN7'\nUsual strength. 50 mg.\nRelated substances. Deteiil'iineby thin~layeichromatograpliy\n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel G\nMobile phase. A mixture of 90 volumes of ethyl acetate,\n10 volumes of 18 M ammonia and 10 volumes ofmethanol.\n2256\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nTest solution. Dissolve a quantity of the contents of the\ncapsules containing 0.1 g ofTriamterene in sufficientdimethyl\nsulphoxide to produce 20 ml and dilute 2 ml to 50 ml with\nmethanol.\nReference solution. Dilute 1 volume of the test solution to\n200 volumes with methanol.\nApply to the plate 5 III of each solution. After development,\ndry the plate in air until the odour of solvent is no longer\ndetectable and examine in ultraviolet light at 365 nm. Any\nsecondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with the test\nsolution is not more intense than the spot in the chromatogram\nobtained with the reference solution.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay. Weigh accurately a quantity of the contents of\n20 capsules containing about 0.1 g ofTriamterene, dissolve in\n50 ml ofa mixture ofequal volumes ofglacial acetic acidand\nwater with the aid of gentle heat, cool and add sufficient\nwater to produce 500.0 ml. Dilute 5.0 ml of this solution to\n100.0 ml with 1 M acetic acid and measure the absorbance of\nthe resulting solution at the maximum at about 360 urn (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content of C l2H IlN 7 from the absorbance\nobtained by repeating the operation using triamterene RS in\nplace of the contents of the capsules.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tributyl-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tributyl Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tributyl Citrate\nMol. Wt. 360.5\nTributyl Citrate contains not less than 99.0 per cent of\nC1sH320 7, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Excipient."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Tributyl Citrate\nMol. Wt. 360.5\nTributyl Citrate contains not less than 99.0 per cent of\nC1sH320 7, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Excipient."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry(2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tributyl citrate\nRS or with the reference spectrum oftributyl citrate.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\n2257\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRICHLOROMONOFLUOROMETHANE\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6),\ncompare\nthe\nspectrum\nwith\nthe\nobtained\nwith\ntrichloromonofluromethane RS."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Weight per ml (2.4.29). 1.037 to 1.045.\nRefractive index (2.4.27). 1.443 to 1.445.\nAcidity. Dissolve 32.0 g in 30 ml of ethanol (95 per cent),\npreviously neutralised to bromothymol blue, add bromothymol\nblue solution and titrate with 0.1 M sodium hydroxide to a\nfaint blue endpoint; not more than 1.0 ml is required.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.2 percent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 2.0 g complies with the limit test for\nheavy metals, Method B (10 ppm).\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. Dissolve about 300 mg of substance under\nexamination in 10 ml oftoluene.\nReference solution. A solution containing 3.0 per cent w/v of\ntributyl citrate RS and acetyltributyl citrate RS in toluene.\nChromatographic system\n-\na glass or stainless steel column 30 m x 0.32 mm, packed\nwith bonded with a 0.5-llm layer ofphase 042,\n-\ntemperature:\ncolumn. 80° to 220° from 0.5 to 10 minutes,\ninlet port. 85° to 250° from 0.5 to 10 minutes (increased\n@ 20° per minute) and detector 275°,\n-\na flame ionisation detector,\n-\nflow rate. 2.3 ml perminute ofthe nitrogen as carrier gas.\nInject 1III ofthe reference solution. The test is not valid unless\nthe relative retention times with reference to acetyltributyl\ncitrate for tributyl citrate is about 0.9, the resolution between\nthe peaks corresponding to tributyl citrate and acetyltributyl\ncitrate is not less than 1.5 and the relative standard deviation\nfor replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject 1 III ofthe test solution and the reference solution.\nCalculate the content C 1sH320 7•\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTrichloromonofluoromethane\nCI\nI\nCI-C-F\nI\nCI\nMol. Wt. 137.4\nCC~F\nTrichloromonofluoromethane contains not less than 99.6 per\ncent and not more than 100.0 per cent ofCChF, calculated on\nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (Excipient).\nTRICHLOROMONOFLUOROMETHANE\nBoiling temperature. Transfer a 100ml sample at about 24° to\na tared, pear-shaped, 100-ml centrifuge tube containing a few\nboiling stones, and weigh. Suspenda thermometer in the liquid,\nand place the tube in a medium maintained at a temperature of\n32° above the expected boiling temperature. When the\nthennometer reading becomes constant, record as the boiling\ntemperature the thelmometer reading afterat least 5per cent of\nthe substance under ex,amination has distilled. Retain the\nremainder of the substance for the determination of High-\nBoiling Residues.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.001 per cent (Method 3) with\nthe following modifications (a) provide the closed-system\ntitrating vessel with an opening through which passes a\ncoarse-porosity gas dispersion tube connected to a sample\ncylinder; (b) dilute the reagent with anhydrous methanol to\ngive a water equivalence factor ofbetween 0.2 and 1.0 mg per\nml, age this diluted solution for not less than 16 hours before\nsanitation; (c) obtain a 100 g sample as directedunder inhalation\npreparation, and introduce the same into the titration vessel\nthrough the gas dispersion tube at a rate of about 100 ml of\ngas per minute.\nHigh-boiling residues. Not more than 0.01 per cent, Allow 85\nml ofthe sample to distill as directed in the test forApproximate\nBoiling Temperature, and transfer the centrifuge tube\ncontaining the remaining 15 ml of substance to a medium\nmaintained at a temperature 10° above the boiling temperature.\nAfter 30 minutes, remove the tube from the water-bath, blot\ndry, and weigh. Calculate the weight ofthe residue.\nInorganic chlorides. Place 5 ml ofanhydrous methanol in a\ntest tube, add 3 drops of a saturated solution of silvernitrate\nin\nanhydrous\nmethanol,\nshake,\nand\nadd\n7\ng of\nTrichloromonofluoromethane; no opalescence or turbidity is\nproduced.\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. Introduce the liquid phase of Trichloromono-\nfluoromethane into an evacuated headspace vial\nReference solution.\nIntroduce a liquid-phase mixture of\ndichlorodifluoromethane, dichlorotetrafluoroethane, and\ntrichloromonofluoromethane into an evacuated headspace yjal.\nChromatographic system\n-\na steel column 1.8 m x 2 mm, packed with support S12,\ncontaining 1 per cent phase G25,\nIP 2010\ntemperature:\ncolumn 70° to 170° @100 per minute and 170° for 5\nminutes,\ninlet port at 110° and detector at 200°,\na flame ionisation detector,\nflow rate. 20 ml per minute ofthe nitrogen as carrier gas\nHead-space conditions which may be used:\nbath temperature 100°,\nvalve/loop temperature 105°,\nsampling time is 3 seconds.\nThe\nrelative\nretention\ntime\nwith\nreference\nto\ntrichloromonofluoromethane for dichlorodifluoromethaneis\nabout 0.5 and for dichlorotetrafluoroethane is about 0.8. The\nsum ofthese two peak areas is not more than 0.2 per cent of\nthe total ofall peak areas and the sum ofthe areas ofall peaks\nother than that for trichloromonofluoromethane is not more\nthan 0.4 per cent ofthe total ofall peak areas.\nInject the test solution and reference solution. The test is not\nvalid unless the resolution between the peaks due\ndichlorotetrafluoroethane and trichloromonofluoromethane is\nnot less than 2.0.\nCalculate the content ofCCI3F.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture and prevent exposure\nto excessive heat."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triclofos-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triclofos Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triclofos Oral Solution\nDetermine the .weight per ml of the oral solution (2.4.29) and \ncalculate the content of C2H3Cl3NaO4P.\nTest solution. Disperse 1.0 ml of the substance under \nexamination in 50.0 ml of dichloromethane.\nTriclofos Oral Solution contains not less than.; 95.0 per cent | \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of C2H3Cl3NaO4P. \n|\nReference solution. Disperse 1.0 ml of the substance under \nexamination and 0.5 ml of methyl tridecanoate in 50.0 ml of \ndichloromethane.\nTriethyl Citrate\n.0 \nr^O”CH2CH3"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triclofos Oral Solution\nDetermine the .weight per ml of the oral solution (2.4.29) and \ncalculate the content of C2H3Cl3NaO4P.\nTest solution. Disperse 1.0 ml of the substance under \nexamination in 50.0 ml of dichloromethane.\nTriclofos Oral Solution contains not less than.; 95.0 per cent | \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of C2H3Cl3NaO4P. \n|\nReference solution. Disperse 1.0 ml of the substance under \nexamination and 0.5 ml of methyl tridecanoate in 50.0 ml of \ndichloromethane.\nTriethyl Citrate\n.0 \nr^O”CH2CH3"},{"name":"Identification","text":"|\nChromatographic system\nDisperse a quantity of the oral solution containing 0.25 g of I \nTriclofos Sodium with 40 ml'of water, add 5 ml of 1 Kisulphuric \n|\nacid and extract with 25 ml of isoamyl alcohol. The extract 1\n- a fused silica column 30 m x 0.32 mm, packed with poly\n(dimethyl) siloxane (5 pm),\n- temperature:\nHO”G^O-CH2CH3\n...I\\^O~CH2CH3\ncolumn.200°,\ninjection port and detector at 220°,\n- a flame ionization detector, \n'\ncomplies with the following tests. \nI\nA. \nEvaporate 5 ml to dryness, heat the residue with 1 ml of a |\n50 per cent v/v solution of sulphuric acid and 1 ml of a 5.0 per \n|\nas carrier gas. . \n,\n- linear velocity about 26 cm per second using nitrogen\ncent w/v solution of potassium permanganate in a water­\nbath for 5 minutes, add 7 ml of water and decolourise the I\nCpHjoOv \nMol. Wt.276.3\nInject 1 pl of the reference solution. The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to triethyl citrate and \nmethyl tridecanoate is not less than 1.5.\nsolution with 1 ml of a 5.0 per cent w/v solution of oxalic acid \n|\nTo 1 ml, add 1 ml of pyridine and 1 ml of 2 Pl sodium hydroxide, \n|\nheat in a water-bath for 1 minute, stirring continuously; a pink |\nTriethyl Citrate is triethyl 2-hydroxypropane-l, 2, 3- \ntricarboxylate.\nTriethyl Citrate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not\ncolour is produced in the pyridine layer. ■ \nI\nB. \nEvaporate 10 ml to dryness, heat the residue with 1 g of J \nanhydrous sodium carbonate at a dull red heat for 10 minutes, \nI\nInject 1 pl each of the test solution and the reference solution. \nRun the chromatogram twice the retention time of the principal \npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, \nthe area of any secondary peak is not more than 0.2 per cent \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution. The sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 0.5 per cent the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution. Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.04 per cent \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution.\nmore than 101.0 per cent of Ci2H2qO7, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\ncool, extract the residue with water arid filter The residue । \ngives the reactions of chlorides (2.3.1) arid of phosphates |\nCategory. Excipient. ‘\n(2-3.1)., \n... 'I\nOther tests, Comply with the tests stated under. Oral Liquid |\n^Amaybe omitted if tests B and C are carried out. Tests B\nandCtnay be omitted if test A is carried out.\nAssay. To a quantity containing 16 mg of Triclofos Sodium, \n|\nadd 15 ml of water, 1 ml of 7 M sodium hydroxide and 15 ml \n|\nether, shake for 1 minute, allow to separate, wash the eih# I \nlayer with 1 ml of water and discard the .ether solution- A |\n■^•Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \n^W^-the spectrum with that obtained with triethyl citrate \n^8or with the reference spectrum of triethyl citrate.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 4.0 g in 8 ini of ethanol (95 per \ncent) and dilute to 20 ml with water. 12 ml of the resulting \nsolution complies with the limit test for the heavy metals,\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIFLUOPERAZINE HYDROCHLORIDE\nmethod D (5 ppm). Prepare the ^standard solution using lead \nstandard solution (T ppm Pb) obtained by diluting, lead \nstandard solution (100-pprmPb); with a mixture of equal \nvolumes of ethanol (95 per cent) and water.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.25 per cent, determined on\n1.0 g.\nAssay . To 1.5 g, add 25 ml of 2-propanol, 50 ml of water, \n25:0 ml of 1 Idsodium hydroxide and a few glass beads into a \n250-ml borosilicate-glass flask fitted with a reflux condenser. \nHeat under ri reflux: condenser for 1 hour and allow to:cool. \nTitrate with 1 M hydrochloric racid, using 1 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 7 M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.0921 g of\nC12H20O7.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture. ; -\nTrifluoperazine Hydrochloride\nC2IH24F3N3S,2HC1 \nMol. Wt. 480.4\nTrifluoperazine hydrochloride is 10-[3-(4-met]iylpiperazin-l- \nyl)propyl] -2-trifluoromethylphenothiazine dihydrochloride.\nTri fluoperazine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C21H24F3N3S, 2HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic; antiemetic.\nDescription . A white to pale yellow, crystallinepowder; slightly \nhygroscopic. . .\nNOTE— Iri the following procedures, the test and standard \nspécimens and thé solutions containing them should be \nprotected by carrying out the tests -without delay and in\nsubdued light. ■' • •\nA. Dissolve 20 mg in 10 ml of water, make the solution alkaline \nto litmus paper with 5 M sodium hydroxide and extract with\ntwo quantities, each of 20 ml, of light petroleum (60° to 80°). \nCombine the extracts, wash with lOmlof water, shake with 5 g\nof anhydrous sodium sw/pAate, filter and evaporate thefilt« : \ncarefully to dryness. -■ \n••••-•■. :y\nOn the residue, déterminé by infrared absornp \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with th \nobtained with trifluoperazine hydrochloride IPRSixe^,^' \nthe same manner or with the reference spectrum \ntrifluoperazine. \"■ \n■ \"\nB. Complies with the test for identification of phenothiazines \n(2.3.3), using as reference solution a 0.2,per cent w/v solution \nof trifluoperazine hydrochloride IPRSw\\chloroform.\nC. When examined iri the rangé'280 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7) a \n0.01 per cent w/v solution in 0.7; ^ hydrochloric acid \nmeasured immediately after preparation shows an absorption \nmaximum at about 305 nm. Dilute 10 :ml of the solution to\n100 ml with 0.1 MAyt/rocZtZorte aczi/. When=exainined in the \nrange 230 nm to 280 nm, the resulting solution shows an \nabsorption maximum at about 256 nm; absorbance at about \n256 nm, about 0.65.\nD. Dissolve 5 mg in 2 ml of sulphuric acid and allow to stand \nfor 5 minutes; an orange colour "},{"name":"Tests","text":"|\nDescription.A clear, viscous, colourless or almost colourless, \nhygroscopic liquid.\npH (2.4.24). 1.7 to 2.6, determined in a 10.0 per cent w/v solution.\nRelated substances. Complies with the test for related \nsubstances in phenothiazines (2.3.5), using mobile phase A.\nSulphated ash (2.3:18). Not more than 0.-1 percent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° at a pressure not exceeding \n0.7 kPa.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.4 g in 50 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \nacid add 10 ml of mercuric acetate solution. Titrate with \n0.1 M perchloric acid, using crystal violet ¿solution as \nindicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02402 gof \nc21H24F3N3s,2Ha.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triclofos-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triclofos Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triclofos Sodium\nCI3C O\" xONa\nC2H3CI3NaO4P \nMol.Wt.251.4\nTriclofos Sodium is sodium 2,2,2-trichloroethyl hydrogen \northophosphate.\nTriclofos Sodium contains not less than 41:3 per cent and not \nmore than 43.2 per cent of Cl and not less than 97.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C2H3C13NaO4P, both \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; hygroscopic.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the; spectrum with that obtained with triclofos sodium \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of triclofos sodium.\nB. It gives the reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\n3863\nwww.webofpharma.com\nC, Heat 0.1g with 1 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate to a \ndull red heat and maintain for 10 minutes, cool, extract the \nresidue with water and filter. The filtrate gives the reactions\nof chlorides (2.3 .Hand of phosphates Í2.3.T). ;\nTests •. \n„..i.\npH (2.4.24).3.0 to 4.5, determined ona2.0 per centw/v solution.\nAppearance of solution. A 2.0,per cent w/y solution is clear \n(2.4.1). \n. ;? ,7;\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 0.5, g in 50 ml of water. 15 ml of \nthe solution complies with the limit test for chlorides (0.17 per \ncent). \n,\nPhosphates. Not more than 1.0 per cent, calculated as PO4, \nwhen determined by dissolving 25 mg in 10. ml of water, add \n4 ml of 1 M sulphuric acid, 1 ml of ammonium molybdate \nsolution and 2 ml of methylaminophenol-sulphite reagent\nand allow to stand for 15 minutes and dilute to 25 ml with \nwater, allow to stand for a further 15 minutes and measure the \nabsorbance of the solution at 730 nm (2.4.7): Calculate the \ncontent of phosphate from a calibration curve prepared by \ntreating suitable volumes of a 0.00143 per cent w/v solution of \npotassium dihydrogen orthophosphate in the same manner.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 5.0 percent, determined \non 1 g by drying at 100°, at a pressure not exceeding 0.7 kPa \nfor 3 hours.\nAssay. For Cl — Mix 0.25 g with 1 g bi anhydrous sodium \ncarbonate in a nickel crucible about 3 cm in diameter, fill the \ncrucible completely with anhydrous sodium carbonate and \ninvert into a nickel crucible about 4 cm in diameter; cover the \nsmaller crucible with anhydrous sodium carbonate, well \npressed down, using 25 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate in\nall. Heat for 30 minutes at a dull red heat, cool, transfer to a \n400 ml beaker, add 150 ml of water and boil gently for \n10 minutes. Filter through absorbent cotton into a 600 ml beaker, \nwashing the residue with hot water, until 400 ml of filtrate has \nbeen collected. Cool, cautiously add nitric acid until the \nsolution is neutral to litmus paper and add 3 ml of nitric acid \nin excess. Add 50 ml of 0.1 M silver nitrate, allow to stand \nuntil precipitation is complete, filter, wash the precipitate with \nwater and titrate the combined filtrate and washings with \n0.1 M ammonium thiocyanate using ammonium iron(III) \nsulphate solution as indicator.\n1 ml of 0.1M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.003545 g of Cl.\nFor CdCCliNaO^P—Heat 0.2 g in a Kjeldahl flask with 2 ml \nof sulphuric dcid; and 2.5 ml of nitric acid until brown fumes \ncease to be evolved, cool, add 1 ml of nitric acid and heat \nagain. Continue adding nitric acid and heating until brown\n3864 \nMmBmIWI!\nTRICLOFOS SODIUM\n^2022\nTRIETHYL CITRATE\nfumes are no longer evolved and the solution is colour} \nwhen cold. Heat until dense, white fumes are evolved e \ntransfer the solution to a flask with the aid of 150 ml of\n,Q^iFf sulphuric acid to the combined aqueous layer \nthings, extract with four 10-ml quantities of isoamyl \nihol$0^ cac^ extract t0 stand until good separation \nf y ^^ed,combine the isoamyl alcohol extracts, remove\nB. To 0.5 ml add 5 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and 4 ml of \ndilute sodium hydroxide solution. Boil under reflux for about\n■■■■ Í\nadd 50 ml of citric-molybdic acid solution and heat slowlv ’’ I \nboiling. Swirli"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triclofos Sodium\nCI3C O\" xONa\nC2H3CI3NaO4P \nMol.Wt.251.4\nTriclofos Sodium is sodium 2,2,2-trichloroethyl hydrogen \northophosphate.\nTriclofos Sodium contains not less than 41:3 per cent and not \nmore than 43.2 per cent of Cl and not less than 97.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C2H3C13NaO4P, both \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; hygroscopic.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the; spectrum with that obtained with triclofos sodium \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of triclofos sodium.\nB. It gives the reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\n3863\nwww.webofpharma.com\nC, Heat 0.1g with 1 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate to a \ndull red heat and maintain for 10 minutes, cool, extract the \nresidue with water and filter. The filtrate gives the reactions\nof chlorides (2.3 .Hand of phosphates Í2.3.T). ;\nTests •. \n„..i.\npH (2.4.24).3.0 to 4.5, determined ona2.0 per centw/v solution.\nAppearance of solution. A 2.0,per cent w/y solution is clear \n(2.4.1). \n. ;? ,7;\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 0.5, g in 50 ml of water. 15 ml of \nthe solution complies with the limit test for chlorides (0.17 per \ncent). \n,\nPhosphates. Not more than 1.0 per cent, calculated as PO4, \nwhen determined by dissolving 25 mg in 10. ml of water, add \n4 ml of 1 M sulphuric acid, 1 ml of ammonium molybdate \nsolution and 2 ml of methylaminophenol-sulphite reagent\nand allow to stand for 15 minutes and dilute to 25 ml with \nwater, allow to stand for a further 15 minutes and measure the \nabsorbance of the solution at 730 nm (2.4.7): Calculate the \ncontent of phosphate from a calibration curve prepared by \ntreating suitable volumes of a 0.00143 per cent w/v solution of \npotassium dihydrogen orthophosphate in the same manner.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 5.0 percent, determined \non 1 g by drying at 100°, at a pressure not exceeding 0.7 kPa \nfor 3 hours.\nAssay. For Cl — Mix 0.25 g with 1 g bi anhydrous sodium \ncarbonate in a nickel crucible about 3 cm in diameter, fill the \ncrucible completely with anhydrous sodium carbonate and \ninvert into a nickel crucible about 4 cm in diameter; cover the \nsmaller crucible with anhydrous sodium carbonate, well \npressed down, using 25 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate in\nall. Heat for 30 minutes at a dull red heat, cool, transfer to a \n400 ml beaker, add 150 ml of water and boil gently for \n10 minutes. Filter through absorbent cotton into a 600 ml beaker, \nwashing the residue with hot water, until 400 ml of filtrate has \nbeen collected. Cool, cautiously add nitric acid until the \nsolution is neutral to litmus paper and add 3 ml of nitric acid \nin excess. Add 50 ml of 0.1 M silver nitrate, allow to stand \nuntil precipitation is complete, filter, wash the precipitate with \nwater and titrate the combined filtrate and washings with \n0.1 M ammonium thiocyanate using ammonium iron(III) \nsulphate solution as indicator.\n1 ml of 0.1M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.003545 g of Cl.\nFor CdCCliNaO^P—Heat 0.2 g in a Kjeldahl flask with 2 ml \nof sulphuric dcid; and 2.5 ml of nitric acid until brown fumes \ncease to be evolved, cool, add 1 ml of nitric acid and heat \nagain. Continue adding nitric acid and heating until brown\n3864 \nMmBmIWI!\nTRICLOFOS SODIUM\n^2022\nTRIETHYL CITRATE\nfumes are no longer evolved and the solution is colour} \nwhen cold. Heat until dense, white fumes are evolved e \ntransfer the solution to a flask with the aid of 150 ml of\n,Q^iFf sulphuric acid to the combined aqueous layer \nthings, extract with four 10-ml quantities of isoamyl \nihol$0^ cac^ extract t0 stand until good separation \nf y ^^ed,combine the isoamyl alcohol extracts, remove\nB. To 0.5 ml add 5 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and 4 ml of \ndilute sodium hydroxide solution. Boil under reflux for about\n■■■■ Í\nadd 50 ml of citric-molybdic acid solution and heat slowlv ’’ I \nboiling. Swirling the flask continuously, add 25 ml of \nI\nsolution at first drop wise and then in a steady stream, heat *\n10 minutes, 2 ml of the solution gives the reactions of citrates \n(2.3.1).\njj^er water that separates and dilute to 50 ml with\nC. It gives the reactions of esters (2.3.1).\na water-bath for 5 minutes and cool. Filter, wash the Pfecipit^ |\nTransfer 10 ml of the solution to a 20 ml \nampoule, add 10 ml of alcoholic potassium hydroxide \nJo/u/ion» seal the ampoule, mix well and heat in an autoclave\nwith water until free from acid, transfer the precipitate to I \nflask with the aid of 100 ml of water, add 50 ml of 0.5 Msodium |"},{"name":"Tests","text":"2‘hours. Allow the ampoule to cool, transfer the \ncon^iits (o a A^k with ^e \n20 ml of 2 Mnitric acid and\nadd 25^0f $2 M silver nitrate. Titrate with 0.02 M \n^mbniumthiocyanate using ammonium iron(III) sulphate \n^/¿fojixd^indicator. Repeat the titration using 10 ml of\nhydroxide and shake until dissolved. Titrate the excess of $\nAppearance of solution. The substance under examination is \nclear (2.4.1) and not more intensely coloured than reference \nsolution BYS6 (2.4.1). \n■ \\\nalkali with 0.5 Mhydrochloric acid using phenolphthalein. I\nthymol blue solution as indicator. \"■’K\"--|\nAcidity. Dilute 10g with 10 ml ofpreviously neutralized et/zow/\n1 ml of 0.5 Mspdium hydroxide is equivalent to 4.835 mg of \nC2H3Cl;NaO4P. Correct the result for the content of phosphate \nas determined by the test described above;.! mg of PQ ¡$ \nequivalent to 2.65 mg of C2H3Cl3NaO4P..\n(95 per cent). Add 0.5 ml of bromothymol blue solution. Not \nmore than 0.3 ml of 0.7 M sodium hydroxide is required to \nchange the color of the indicator to blue.\nalcoholic potassium hydroxide solution in place of the\ncontentsof the ampoule. The difference between the titrations \nrepresents the amount of silver nitrate required.\nRefractive index (2.4.27). 1.440 to 1.446.\n.. §\nTriclofos Oral Solution is a solution of Triclofos Sodium in a I \nsuitable flavoured vehicle. , \n|\n1 ml of 0 02 AT silver nitrate is equivalent to 1.676 mg of \nCAOjNad^.\nRelated substances. Determine by gas chromatography \n(2.4.13)."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triethyl-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triethyl Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triethyl Citrate\nMol. Wt.276.3\nTriethyl Citrate is triethyl 2-hydroxypropane-l, 2, 3-\ntricarboxylate.\nTriethyl Citrate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 per cent of ClzHzo07, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Excipient.\n.Description. A clear, viscous, colourless or almost colourless,\nhygroscopic liquid.\n2258\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triethyl Citrate\nMol. Wt.276.3\nTriethyl Citrate is triethyl 2-hydroxypropane-l, 2, 3-\ntricarboxylate.\nTriethyl Citrate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 per cent of ClzHzo07, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Excipient.\n.Description. A clear, viscous, colourless or almost colourless,\nhygroscopic liquid.\n2258\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010"},{"name":"Identification","text":"Test A may be omittediftests Band C are carriedout. Tests B\nand C may be omitted iftest A is carried out.\nA. Detennine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with triethyl citrate\nRS or with the reference spectrum oftriethyl citrate.\nB. To 0.5 ml add 5 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and 4 ml of\ndilute sodium hydroxide solution. Boil under reflux for about\n10 minutes, 2 ml ofthe solution gives the reactions ofcitrates\n(2.3.1).\nC. It gives the reactions ofesters (2.3.1).\nA. Dissolve 20 mg in 10 ml ofwater, make the solution alkaline\nto litmus paper with 5 M sodium hydroxide and extract with\ntwo quantities, each of20 ml, oflightpetroleum (60° to 80°).\nCombine the extracts, wash with 10 ml ofwater, shake with 5 g\nofanhydrous sodium sulphate, filter and evaporate the filtrate\ncarefully to dryness.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption\nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that\nobtained with trifluoperazine hydrochloride RS or with the\nreference spectrum oftrifluoperazine hydrochloride.\nB. Complies with the test for identification ofphenothiazines\n(2.3.3), using as reference solution a 0.2 per cent w/v solution\nof trifluoperazine hydrochloride RS in chloroform.\nC. When examined in the range 280 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a\n0.01 per cent w/v solution in 0.1 M hydrochloric acid\nmeasured immediately after preparation shows an absorption\nmaximum at about 305 nm. Dilute 10 ml of the solution to\n100 ml with 0.1 M hydrochloric acid. When examined in the\nrange 230 nm to 280 nm, the resulting solution shows an\nabsorption maximum at about 256 nm; absorbance at about\n256 nm, about 0.65.\nD. Dissolve 5 mg in 2 ml ofsulphuric acid and allow to stand\nfor 5 minutes; an orange colour is produced.\nE. A 10 per cent w/v solution gives the reactions ofchlorides\n(2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance ofsolution. The substance under examination is\nclear (2.4.1) and not more intensely coloured than reference\nsolution BYS6 (2.4.1).\nAcidity. Dilute 109with 10rnl ofpreviously neutralized ethanol\n(95 per cent). Add 0.5 ml of bromothymol blue solution. Not\nmore than 0.3 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide is required to\nchange the color of the indicator to blue.\nRefractive index (2.4.27). 1.440to 1.446.\nRelated substances. Determine by gas chromatography\n(2.4.13).\nTest solution. Disperse 1.0 ml of the substance under\nexamination in 50.0 ml ofdichloromethane.\nReference solution. Disperse 1.0 ml of the substance under\nexamination and 0.5 ml of methyl tridecanoate in 50.0 ml of\ndichloromethane.\nChromatographic system\n-\na fused silica column 30 m x 0.32 mrn, packed with poly\n(dimethyl) siloxane (5 /lm),\ntemperature:\ncolumn.200°,\ninjection port and detector at 220°,\na flame ionization detector,\nlinear velocity about 26 cm per second using nitrogen\nas carrier gas\nInject 1 III ofthe reference solution. The test is notvalid unless\nthe resolution between the peaks due to triethyl citrate and\nmethyl tridecanoate is not less than 1.5.\nInject 1 /ll each ofthe test solution and the reference solution.\nRun the chromatogram twice the retention time ofthe prinCipal\npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution,\nthe area of\"any secondary peak is not more than 0.2 per cent\nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the reference solution. The sum of the areas of all the\nsecondary peaks is not more than 0.5 per centthe area ofthe\n2259\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIFLUOPERAZINE HYDROCHLORIDE\nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference\nsolution. Ignore anypeak with an area less than 0.04 per cent\nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the reference solution.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 4.0 g in 8ml ofethanol (95per\ncent) and dilute to 20 ml with water. 12 ml of the resulting\nsolution complies with the limit test for the heavy metals,\nmethod D (5 ppm). Prepare the standard solution using lead\nstandard solution (l ppm Pb) obtained by diluting lead\nstandard solution (l00 ppm Pb) with a mixture of equal\nvolumes of ethanol (95 per cent) and wate/:\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nWater (2.4.19). Not more than 0.25 per cent, detennined on\nLOg.\nAssay. To 1.5 g, add 25 ml of2-propanol, 50 rnl ofwater, 25.0\nml of1M sodium hydroxide and a few glass beads into a 250-\nml borosilicate-glass flask fitted with a reflux condenser. Heat\nunder a reflux condenser for 1 hour and allow to cool. Titrate\nwith 1 M hydrochloric acid, using 1 ml ofphenolphthalein\nsolution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 1 M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.0921 g of\nClzHzo07.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nTrifluoperazine Hydrochloride\n~\nY\"N~Nl\nsn\n~N'CH\nI\n\"\n3\n~\nCF3\n,2HCI\nCzIHz~3N3S,2HCl\nMol. Wt. 480.4\nTrifluoperazine hydrochloride is 10-[3-(4-methylpiperazin-1-\nyl)propyl]-2-trifluoromethylphenothiazine dihydrochloride.\nTrifluoperazine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per\ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent ofCzIHz4F3N3S, 2HCl,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic; antiemetic.\nDose. As antipsychotic, orally, the equivalent of2 to 30 mg of\ntrifluoperazine daily, in divided doses; by intramuscular\ninjection, the equivalent ofl to 3 mg oftrifluoperazine daily, in\ndivided doses. As antiemetic, orally, the equivalent of 2 to 4\nmg of trifluoperazine daily; by intramuscular injection, the\nequivalent of 1 to 3 mg of trifluoperazine daily, in divided\ndoses.\nTRIFLUOPERAZINE HYDROCHLORIDE\nDescription. A white to pale yellow, crystalline powder;\nodourless or almost odourless; slightly hygroscopic.\nNOTE -\nIn thefollowing procedures, the test andstandard\nspecimens and the solutions containing them should be\nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in\nsubdued light.\npH (2.4.24). 1.7 to 2.6, determined in a 10.0percentw/v solution.\nRelated substances. Complies with the test for related\nsubstances in phenothiazines (2.3.5), using mobile phase A.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 1.0percent, determined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°at apressure not exceeding\n0.7kPa.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.4 g, dissolve in 50 ml of\nanhydrous glacial acetic acid add 10 ml ofmercuric acetate\nsolution. Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric acid, using crystal\nvialelsalm/on as~indic~ator.~Cany~ouCablaiilnitfation.~~-~-~~\n1 .tril of 0.1 Mperchl()ric~ acid is equivalent to 0.02402 gof\nC2IH2<tF3N3S,2HCl.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trifluoperazine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trifluoperazine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trifluoperazine Injection\nTrifluoperazine Hydrochloride Injection\nTrifluoperazine Injection is a sterile solution of \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections. :\nTrifluoperazine Injection contains riot less than 90:0 pet ce$ \narid not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount\ntrifluoperazine,C2!H24F3N3S; \n;\n3866\nwww.webofpharma.com\nstrength The equivalent of 1 mg of trifluoperazine per ml."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Trifluoperazine Injection\nTrifluoperazine Hydrochloride Injection\nTrifluoperazine Injection is a sterile solution of \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections. :\nTrifluoperazine Injection contains riot less than 90:0 pet ce$ \narid not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount\ntrifluoperazine,C2!H24F3N3S; \n;\n3866\nwww.webofpharma.com\nstrength The equivalent of 1 mg of trifluoperazine per ml."},{"name":"Identification","text":".pgÿripribn.Âclear, colourless solution.. '\n' ’ fflp— thyfollowing procedures, the test and standard\n\\ àhd i^e solutions containing them should be \n^fSrledby carrying out the tests-without delay and in\n^hen examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), the final \nsolution obtained in Assay shows an absorption maximum at\nabout 256 nm\nTestssk Aw\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 tb 5.0.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 172.0 Endotoxin\nUnits per mg of trifluoperazine.\nOther tests. .Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \npreparations (Injections)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Assay To an measured volume of the injection containing \nabout 50 mg of trifluoperazine add 10 ml of 2 Msulphuric acid\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nand extract.with three quantities, each of 25 ml, of carbon \ntetrachloride: Discard the carbon tetrachloride extract after\neach extraction. Add 10 ml of strong ammonia solution and \nextract with five quantities, each of 50 ml, of cyclohexane.\nExtractthecombined cyclohexane extracts with five quantities, \neach of 50 ml, of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid. Dilute the combined\nacid extracts to 500.0 ml and mix. Measure the absorbance of \nthé resulting solution at the maximum at about 256 nni (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content of C21H24F3N3S taking 743 as the specific \nabsorbance'at 256 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nLabelling, The label states the strength in terms of the \nequivalent amount of trifluoperazine per ml.\nTrifluoperazine Tablets\nTrifluoperazine;Hydrochloride Tablets\nWpqperazine Tablets contain not less than 92.5 per cent\nand not more than 107.5 per. cent of trifluoperazine,\nThe tabiets are coated.\nstrengths. The equivalent of 1 mg and 5 mg of\n: ^uoperazinei\n. 7^n ^!e following procedures, the test and standard \nsPe<dntens and the solutiohs containing them should be \nProtected bycarrying out the tests without delay and in \ns^duedlight.\nTRIFLUOPERAZINE TABLETS\nA. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 20 mg \nof trifluoperazine with 30 ml of 1 M hydrochloric acid for\n10 minutes, filter, make the filtrate alkaline to litmus paper with \n5 M sodium hydroxide and extract with two quantities, each \nof 20 ml, of lightpetroleum (6(P to 8(f). Combine the extracts, \nwash with 10 ml of water, shake with 5 g of anhydrous sodium \nsulphate, filter and evaporate the filtrate carefully tp dryness.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with trifluoperazine hydrochloride IPRS treated in \nthe same manner or with the reference spectrum of \ntrifluoperazine.\nB. Extract a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 5 mg \nof trifluoperazine with 5 ml of acetone, filter and evaporate the \nfiltrate to dryness. Add 2 ml oí sulphuric acid to the residue \nand allow to stand for 5 minutes; an orange colour is produced.\nApparatisNo. 1 (Basket),\nMedium. 900 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric acid, \nSpeed and time. 50 rpm, 30 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter. Measure \nthe absorbance of the filtrate, dilute suitably if necessary with \nthe medium, at the maximum at about 255 nm and at about 278 \nnm (2.4.7). Calculate the content of C2iH24F3N3S. in the.medium \nfrom the difference in absorbance at 255 nm and.at 278 nm, \nobtained from a solution of known concentration of \ntrifluoperazine hydrochloride IPRS in the dissolution \nmedium.\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of \nQJI^N.S. \n...\nUniformity of content. Complies with the test stated under \nTablets. \n■ /\nPlace one tablet in a 100-ml volumetric flask, add 50 ml of a \nmixture of 19 volumes of water and 1 volume ofhydrochloric \nacid, shake until the tablet has completely disintegrated, dilute \nto volume with the same mixture, mix and filter, rejecting the \nfirst few ml ofthe filtrate. Dilute suitably, if necessary with the \nsame solvent mixture and measure the absorbance of the \nresulting solution at the maximum at about 256 nm (2.4.7). \nCalculate the content of C2lH24F3N3S taking 743 as the specific \nabsorbance at 256 nm.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets: Weigha quantity of the \npowder containing1 about £ mg of trifluoperazine, shake for\n15 minutes with 400 ml of a mixture of 19 volumes of water and\n3867\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIFLUPROMAZINE ■ HYDROCHLORIDE\n1 volume of hydrochloric acid, dilute to 500.0 ml with the \nsame solvent mixture, mix and filter. Measure the absorbance \nof the filtrate at the maximum at about 256 nm (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content of C2J H24F3N3S taking 743 as the specific \nabsorbance at 256 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nLabelling. The label states the strength in terms of the \nequivalent amount of trifluoperazine."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triflupromazine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triflupromazine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triflupromazine Hydrochloride\nFlupromazine Hydrochloride\nI Hel\nMoL Wt. 388.9\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride is 10-[3-(dimethylamino)-\npropyl)]-2-trifluoromethylphenothiazine hydrochloride.\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride contains not less than\n97.0percent and not more than 103.0 per centofClsHl9F3N2S,\nHCI, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic; antiemetic.\nDose. As antipsychotic, orally, 30 to 150 mg oftriflupromazine\ndaily, in divided doses; by intramuscular injection, 10 mg\nrepeated 4-hourly, ifnecessary; by intravenous injection, 1to\n3 mg repeated 4-hourly, ifnecessary. As antiemetic, 20 to 30\nmg oftriflupromazine daily; by intramuscular injection 1to 3\nmg.\nDescription. A white to pale yellowish brown, crystalline\npowder; odour slight and characteristic.\nNOTE -\nIn the following procedures, the test and standard\nspecimens and the solutions containing them should be\nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in\nsubdued light."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triflupromazine Hydrochloride\nFlupromazine Hydrochloride\nI Hel\nMoL Wt. 388.9\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride is 10-[3-(dimethylamino)-\npropyl)]-2-trifluoromethylphenothiazine hydrochloride.\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride contains not less than\n97.0percent and not more than 103.0 per centofClsHl9F3N2S,\nHCI, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic; antiemetic.\nDose. As antipsychotic, orally, 30 to 150 mg oftriflupromazine\ndaily, in divided doses; by intramuscular injection, 10 mg\nrepeated 4-hourly, ifnecessary; by intravenous injection, 1to\n3 mg repeated 4-hourly, ifnecessary. As antiemetic, 20 to 30\nmg oftriflupromazine daily; by intramuscular injection 1to 3\nmg.\nDescription. A white to pale yellowish brown, crystalline\npowder; odour slight and characteristic.\nNOTE -\nIn the following procedures, the test and standard\nspecimens and the solutions containing them should be\nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in\nsubdued light."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Dissolve 20 mg in 10 ml ofwater, make the solutionalkaline\nto litmus paper with 5 M sodium hydroxide and extract with\ntwo quantities, each of20 ml, oflightpetroleum (60° to 80°).\nCombine the extracts, wash with 10 ml ofwater, shake with 5g\nofanhydrous sodium sulphate, filter and evaporate the filtrate\ncarefully to dryness.\nThe residue complies with the following test. Determine by\ninfrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the\nspectrum with that obtained with trijlupromazine hydro-\nchloride RSor with the reference spectrum oftriflupromazine\nhydrochloride.\nB. Complies with the test for identification ofphenothiazines\n(2.3.3), using as reference solution a 0.2 per cent w/v solution\nof triflupromazine hydrochloride RS in chloroform.\nC. When examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a\n0.00I per cent w/v solution in 0.1 M hydrochloric acidshows\nTRIFLUPROMAZINE INJECTION\nan absorption maximum atabout256 nm anda less well-defined\nmaximum at about 305 nm.\nD. A 10 per cent wlv solution gives the reactions ofchlorides\n(2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Related substances. Complies with the test for Related\nsubstances in phenothiazines (2.3.5), using mobile phase A.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 0.5 per cent, determined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105\n0 for 2 hours.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.8 g, dissolve in 50 ml of\nanhydrous glacial acetic acid add 10 ml ofmercuric acetate\nsolution. Titrate with 0.1 M perchloric acid, using clystal\nviolet solution as indicator. Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 M perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.03889 g of\nClsHI9F3N2S, HCI.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triflupromazine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triflupromazine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triflupromazine Injection\nTriflu p rom azine Tablets\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride Injection; Flupromazine \nHYdrochloride Injection; Flupromazine Injection\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride Tablets; Flupromazine \nHydrochloride Tablets; Flupromazine Tablets\nTriflupromazine Injection is a sterile solution ofTriflupromazine \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nTriflupromazine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent \nand than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntrithiPrcelaiine hydrochloride, C 181-1 19F3N2S, HCI.\nTriflupromazine Injection contains not less than-90.0 perCent , \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated -:,amount-of \ntriflupromazine hydrochloride, C I8H 19F3N2S. Hel.\nkfsitiil strengths. 10 mg; 25 mg.\nNOTE - In the following procedures, the test and standard \nspecimens and the solutions containing them should be \nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in\nsubdued light.\nI dentification\nA. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 20 mg \nof Triflupromazine Hydrochloride with 30 ml of 1 M \nhydrochloric acid for 10 minutes, filter, make the filtrate alkaline \nto litmus paper with 5 M sodium hydroxide and extract with \ntwo quantities, each of 20 ml, of light petroleum (boiling \nrange 60° to 80°). Combine the extracts, wash with 10 ml of \nwater, shake with 5 g of anhydrous sodium sulphate, filter and \nevaporate the filtrate carefully to dryness.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with triflupromazine hydrochloride RS, treated in \nthe same manner or with the reference spectrum of \ntriflupromazine hydrochloride.\nB. Extract a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 5 mg \nof Triflupromazine Hydrochloride with 5 ml of  acetone, filter \nand evaporate the filtrate to dryness.\nWhen examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.001 per cent w/v solution in 0.1 M hydrochloric acid shows \nan absorption maximum at about 256 nm."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Triflupromazine Injection\nTriflu p rom azine Tablets\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride Injection; Flupromazine \nHYdrochloride Injection; Flupromazine Injection\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride Tablets; Flupromazine \nHydrochloride Tablets; Flupromazine Tablets\nTriflupromazine Injection is a sterile solution ofTriflupromazine \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nTriflupromazine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent \nand than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntrithiPrcelaiine hydrochloride, C 181-1 19F3N2S, HCI.\nTriflupromazine Injection contains not less than-90.0 perCent , \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated -:,amount-of \ntriflupromazine hydrochloride, C I8H 19F3N2S. Hel.\nkfsitiil strengths. 10 mg; 25 mg.\nNOTE - In the following procedures, the test and standard \nspecimens and the solutions containing them should be \nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in\nsubdued light.\nI dentification\nA. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 20 mg \nof Triflupromazine Hydrochloride with 30 ml of 1 M \nhydrochloric acid for 10 minutes, filter, make the filtrate alkaline \nto litmus paper with 5 M sodium hydroxide and extract with \ntwo quantities, each of 20 ml, of light petroleum (boiling \nrange 60° to 80°). Combine the extracts, wash with 10 ml of \nwater, shake with 5 g of anhydrous sodium sulphate, filter and \nevaporate the filtrate carefully to dryness.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with triflupromazine hydrochloride RS, treated in \nthe same manner or with the reference spectrum of \ntriflupromazine hydrochloride.\nB. Extract a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 5 mg \nof Triflupromazine Hydrochloride with 5 ml of  acetone, filter \nand evaporate the filtrate to dryness.\nWhen examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.001 per cent w/v solution in 0.1 M hydrochloric acid shows \nan absorption maximum at about 256 nm."},{"name":"Tests","text":"1; niformity of content. (For tablets containing 10 mg or less.\nComplies with the test stated under Tablets.\nCrush one tablet and transfer to a 100-m1 volumetric flask with \nthe aid of a mixture of 19 volumes of water and 1 volume of \nhydrochloric acid. Shake well, dilute to volume with the same \nmixture and complete the procedure described under the Assay \nbeginning at the words \"mix and filter,...\".\nCalculate the content of C 1 ,1-1 19F3N2S, HC1 in the tablet.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh a quantity of the \npowder containing about 50 mg  of Triflupromazine \nHydrochloride and shake for 15 minutes with 200 ml ofa mixture \nof 19 volumes of water and 1 volume of hydrochloric acid. \nDilute to 500.0 ml with the same solvent mixture, mix and filter, \nrejecting the first few ml of the filtrate. Dilute 10.0 ml to\n....\n100.0 ml with the same solvent mixture. Measure the absorbance \nof the filtrate at the maximum at about 256 nm (2.4.7).\nCalculate the content of C I8H 19F3N 2S, IICI takiliw \t\n4 \n.700 .the\nspecific absorbance at 256 nm.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moistUre.\nTRIFLUPROMAZINE TABLETS \nTRIFLURIDINE EYE DROPS \n1P 20\nmobile phase: A. 0.05M sodium dihydrogen ortho-\nphosphate, adjusted to pH 6.0 with ammonia,\nUsual strength. 1.0 per cent w/v.\nTritluridine\nTrifluridine is 5-Trifluoromethyl-2'-deoxyuridine.\n25 \t\n95 \t\n5 \nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plate and\npH (2.4.24). 4.0 to 6.5.\nTrifluridine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and no \nthan 102.0 per cent of C 10H 1i F3N205, calculated on \nbasis.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\n'1\nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nSolvent mixture. 5 volumes of acetonitrile and 95 volumes of \na 0.05M sodium dihvdrogen orthophosphate, adjusted to pH \n6.0 with ammonia.\nCategory. Pyrimidine nucleoside analogue; antiviral \nviruses).\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than 0.5 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \n• solution (a) (0.5 per cent). The sum of areas of all the secondary \npeaks is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (1.0 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 times the area \nof the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b) (0.05 per cent).\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder.\nTest solution. Dilute the eye drops if necessary  to obtain a \nsolution containing 0.02 per cent w/v of Trifluridine in the \nsolvent mixture.\nLoss on 