; \n\"\nggtyloxy)-!7p-hydroxy-2p-(piperidin-l-yl)-5a-androstan-\nSmethylpip'eridinium, \n''\n^(piperidin-l-yl)-5d -androstane-3a, 170 -diyl diacetate.\nTerence solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nefficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and\n^¿¿t’refercnce solution (b), (c) and the test solution. Run \n^chromatogram 2.5 times the retention time of the principal \ni/'f^^l^hzthe chromatogram obtained with the test solution, \n£ ^Jafeuofanypeak corresponding to vecuronium bromide\nSajphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\n^atery(2,3.43). Not more than 4.0 per,cent, determined on\n: - -.I- \n■■-■■■/■ g;\nAssay. Dissolve 0.45 g in 50 ml of glacial acetic 'acid. Titrate\nper chloric acid, determining the end-point \npotentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration,\n4 ml of 0.7-M-perchloric acid is equivalent to .0,0638 g of \nCjJfoBrNiC.r- ■ \n;\nVecuronium Bromide intended for use in the manufacture of \nppreyiteral preparations, without a further appropriate \nprocedure for the removal of bacterial endotoxins complies\nwith the following additional requirement.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 10.0 Endotoxin \nUnks per mg of vecuronium bromide.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/venlafaxine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Venlafaxine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Venlafaxine Hydrochloride\nCI7H28C1NO2 \n< Mol. Wt. 313.9\nVECURONIUM BROMIDE INJECTION\nIp202i\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride is l-[(17?iST)-2-(dimethylamino)-uir \nmethoxyphenyl)elhyl|cyclohexanol hydrochloride.\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 percent\nand, not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci7ll2SClNO2, calculate \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant. \nU’/y.'\nDescription. A white or almost white powder. It shows \npolymorphism (2.5.11)."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Venlafaxine Hydrochloride\nCI7H28C1NO2 \n< Mol. Wt. 313.9\nVECURONIUM BROMIDE INJECTION\nIp202i\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride is l-[(17?iST)-2-(dimethylamino)-uir \nmethoxyphenyl)elhyl|cyclohexanol hydrochloride.\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 percent\nand, not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci7ll2SClNO2, calculate \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant. \nU’/y.'\nDescription. A white or almost white powder. It shows \npolymorphism (2.5.11)."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6)\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with.venlqfaxine \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of \nvenlafaxine hydrochloride. \n: - : < ;\nNOTE —If the spectra obtained in the solid state show \ndifferences, dissolve the substance under examination and \nreference substance in 2-propanol, evaporate to dryness and \nrecord new spectra using the residues.\nB. It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1). \n, r ,\nTests ' \nr '\nAcidity or alkalinity. Dissolve in 0.2 g in IQ, nfr of curhqw \ndioxide free water. Add 0.05 ml of methyl red solution and 0.1 \nml of 0.01M hydrochloric acid. The solution is pink. Not \nmore than 0.2 ml of 0.01Msodium hydroxide is required to \nchange the colour of the indicator to yellow.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 25:0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\nReference soliition(a): Dilute l;0 ml of the test’solution to\n10.0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 1.0 ml ofthe solutionto \n100.0 ml with the mobile phase. \n- . 2 \n? -\nReference' so lutiori(b). Dissolve the contents of a vial of\nvenlafaxine for system suitability IPRS (containing impurities \nD and F) in 1.0 ml of the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm * 4.6 mm, packed with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n■ \n■ mobile phase: a mixture of 149 volumes: of a buffer \nsolution prepared by dissolving 1.7 g of ammonium \ndihydrogen orthophosphate in 149 ml of water, adjusted \nto pH 4.4 with brthophosphoric acid and 51 volumes of \nacetonitrile, \n' \n- .\n- flow rate: 1.2 ml per minute, \n■ \n-\n• - spectrophotometer set at 225 nm, : v ; / - ■ \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\n3930\nwww.webofpharma.com\n~ : Relarive\nVENLAFAXINE PROLONGED-RELEASE CAPSULES\n________________________________________\ns : \n■ >. retentiontime\nWn|afnxinc impurity D \n0.9\nVenlafaxine (Retention time is about 9 minutes) \n1.0\nVenlafaxine impuntyf2_____________________ ' 3-4\nt!^f^j^$J-l-(4-riiethoxypheny[)-2-(rnethyIatnino)ethyl] cyclohexanol,\n^j^-2!-(cycl6hex-l-enyl)-2-(4-niethoxyphenyl)-M^dimethyl- \nethanamine.\nRun the .chromatogram 10 times the retention time of \nvenlafaxine.\nInJeW^crknce solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolutionubetween the peaks due to venlafaxine and \nvenlafaxine impurity D is not less than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of the \npeak due to venlafaxine impurity F is not more than the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0.1 per cent), the area of any other secondary \npeak is not more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per \ncent), the sum of areas of all the secondary peaks is not more \nthan twice the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent). Ignore any \npeak with an area less than 0.5 times of the area of the principal \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a) (0105 per cent).\nSolvent mixture. 30 volumes of cyclohexane and 70 volumes \nof dichloromethane.\nDissolve a quantity of the powdered tablets containing about \n350 mg of venlafaxine in a 100 ml of the solvent mixture for 30 \nminutes, filter the extract through anhydrous sodium sulphate \nand evaporate the filtrate to dryness., Wash the residue with \nthe solvent mixture, filter and dry the residue. On the residue, \ndetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with venlafaxine \nhydrochloride IPRS treated in the same manner or with the \nreference spectrum of venlafaxine hydrochloride.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of the powdered capsules \ncontaining 200 mg of venlafaxine in 80 ml of a 2.4 per cent v/v \nsolution of orthophosphoric acid with: the aid of ultrasound \nfor 30 minutes, cool and dilute to 100.0 ml with water and \ncentrifuge.\nReference solution (a). Dilute L0 ml of test solution to\n500.0 ml with mobile phase A.\nReference solution (b). A 0.2 per cent w/v solution of \nvenlafaxine impurity IPRS (containing impurity D and F) in \nmobile phase A.\nReference solution (c). Dilute 25.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with mobile phase A.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), (Such \nas Partisil CDS 3),\n- \nmobile phase: A. a mixture of 1 volume of triethylamine, \n20 volumes of acetonitrile and 80 volumes of water, \nadjusted to pH 3.5 with drthophosphoric acid,\nB: a mixture of 1 volume of triethylamine, \n50 volumes of acetonitrile and 50 volumes of water, \nadjusted to pH 3.5 with orthophosphoric acid,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given\nbelow,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 226 nm,\n- \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\n3931\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTime\nMobile; phase Ay î ? -Mobile phase B ; '\nnmin.) : (per cent v/v)\n(percentv/v)\n0\n10Ó\n20\n? : 100; r •=\n30\n0\n100\n45\n0\n. 1(j¿)\n. 48\n; 100 ;\n???>??? ,???0?? ,V.\n60\n100\nInject reference -solution (b), Thetest ismorivalid unless; the \nresolutionbetweenthé; peaks due to .venlafaxine and \nvenlafaxine impurity Dis not lessthan. 1.5.\nInject reference solution (a), (c) and the test solution: In the \nchrdmatogfam obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak due to venlafaxine impurity D (1 -[(l/tS)-1 -(4- \nmethoxyphenyl)“2-(methylamino) ethyl] cyclohcxanol) or \nvenlafaxine impurity F ((27?S)-2-(cyclohex-1 -enyl)-2 -(4- \nmethoxyphenyl)-.V,Mdimethylethanaminc) is not more than \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent), the area of any b'iher \nsecondary peak is not more than the.arça of the principalpeak \nin the chrom"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Sulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1,0 g by drying in a vacuum at 80° for 3 hours.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.25g dissolve in a mixture of\n5 mlof 0.0 J M hydrochloric acid and 50 ml of ethanol (95per \ncent). Titrate with 0.1 M sodium hydroxide. Determining the \nend-point potentiometrically (2.4.25). Read the volume added \nbetween the 2 points of inflexion.\n1 ml of 0.1 Msodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.03139 g of \nC17H28C1NO2.\nVenlafaxine Prolonged-release \nCapsules\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride Prolonged-release Capsules\nVenlafaxine Prolonged-release Capsules manufactured by \ndifferent manufacturers, whilst complying with the \nrequirements of the monograph, are not interchangeable, as \nIhe dissolution profile of the products of different \nmanufacturers may not be same.\nVenlafaxine Prolonged-release Capsules contain not.less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of venlafaxine, C17H27NO2.\nUsual strengths. 37.5 mg; 75 mg; 150 mg.\nDissolution (2.5.2). Complies with the test stated under Tablets.\nRelated substances; Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). ■ \n■' 'v,‘ ’ \n' ” ' : ' ' ' ’ \"\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of powdered tablets \ncontaining 200 dig of venlafaxine in 80 ml of a 2.4 per cent v/v \nsolution of orthophosphoric acid, with the aid of ultrasound \nfor 30 minutes, cool and dilute to 100.0 ml with water and filter.\nReference solution fa). Dilute 1.0 ml. of the test solution to\n100.0 mlwith mobile phase A and further Dilute, 1.0 ml of the \nsolution to 5.0 ml with mobile phase A.\n3932\nwww.webofpharma.com\nReference solution (b). A 0.2 per cent w/v. solution of \n^lafaxine) impurity IPRS (containing impurity D and F) in \njnobilephaseA;\n^^¿^'sdiutioh (c). Dilute 25.'0 ml of reference solution (a)\nt0100.0 ml with mobile phase A.\nchromatographic system; \n’\n; - a stainless steel column 25 cm * 4.6 mm. packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica, (5 gm), (Such \nas Partisil ODS 3), \n, s\n-v mobile phase: A. amixtureof 1 volume of triethylamine,\n^0 volumes of acetonitrile and. 80 volumes of water \nAdjusted to pH 3.5 with orthophosphoric acid.\n{■- 'Vi :Ai \n' -' B: a mixture of 1 volume of triethylamine,\n50 volumes of acetonitrile and 50 volumes of water \nadjusted to pH 3.5 with orthophosphoric acid.\n~ a gradient programme using the conditions given below,\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute,....\n- spectrophotometer set at 226 nm,\n~ injection volume: 20 pl.\nTime. \nMobile phase A Mobile phase B\n(in min.)\n.. (per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n100\n0\n\"720 :\n' J 100 ; \n.\n\" 0''\n. .. . 30\n; 0\n. 100..\n■0 ■ ;'\n100\n' 48'\n100\n0\n' 60\n100 ■ \n.\n, 0\nName\" ' \n'' ' Relative\n-■ \nretention time\nVenlafaxine impurity D1 \n0.8\nVenlafaxine (Retention time is about 13 minutes) 1.0\nVenlafaxine impurity F2 \n: 1.7\nt(4-methoxyphenyl)-2-(methylamino) .ethyl] cyclo- \nhcxanol,\n:((2&S)-2-(cycIohex-l-enyl)-2-(4-niethoxyphenyl)-N,Ar-dimethyl-\nethananiine. ;'''\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to venlafaxine and \nvenlafaxine impurity D is not less than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (a), (c) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak due to venlafaxine impurity D or venlafaxine impurity F is \nnot more than the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.2 per \ncent), the area of any other secondary peak is not more than \nthe area .pf the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained\nWith reference solution (a) (0.2 per cent), .the sum of areas of\nVENLAFAXINE TABLETS\nall the secondary peaks is not more than 2.5 times the area of \nthe principal peak in the chromatogram,obtained with reference \nsolution (a) (0’5 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less \nthan the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with reference solution (c) (0.05 per cent).\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Disperse a quantity \nof powder containing 85 mg of Venlafaxine in 50.0 ml of \nmethanol with the aid of ultrasound for 30 minutes,, mix and\nfiiteii dilute 1. 0 ml of the solution to 20.0 ml with the mobile \nphase.'\nReference solution (a). A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of\nvenlafaxine IPRS in the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of\nvenlafaxine impurity IPRS (containing impurity D) in the \nmobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm * 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), (Such \nasZorbaxRxC18), ....\n- mobile phase; a mixlure of 20 volumes of acetonitrile\n. and 80 volumes, of a 1 per cent v/v solution of\nacid, , \n..\ni triethylamine adjusted to pH 3.0 with orthophosphoric\n■ \nflow rate: 1 mJ per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 226 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to impurity D and venlafaxine \nis riot less than 1.0.' \n- \n’ ■\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C17H27NO2 in the tablets.\n1' mg of C17H27NO2, HCI is equivalent to 0.884 mg ofCi7H27NOi.\nLabelling. The label states the strength in terms, of the \nequivalent amount of venlafaxine.\nVenlafaxine Tablets\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride Tablets\nVenlafaxine Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nvenlafaxine, C./H^/NOi-\nUsual strengths. 25 mg; 37.5 mg; 75 mg. . ■\n3933\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVENLAFAXINE TABLETS\nDissolution (2.5.2).\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),.\nMedium. 900 ml of water,\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 45 minutes.\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the mediüm and filter. Reject \nfirst few ml of the filtrate and dilute a suitable volume of the \nfiltrate with dissolution medium to obtain a 0.0025 per cent \nw"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/verapamil-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Verapamil Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Verapamil Hydrochloride\n■^apanril Chloride; Iproveratril Hydrochloride\n^H38N2O4, HC1, \nMol. Wt. 491.1\nye^apamil Hydrochloride is (R5)-2-(3,4-dimethpxyphenyl)-\n5-[[2-(3,4-dimethoxyphenyl)ethyl](methyl)amino] -\n, 2-(l -mcihyiethyljpentanenitrile hydrochloride.\nVerapamil Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \naid not more than 101.0 per cent of C27H3SN2O4, HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Calcium channel blocker.\n^scription. A white, crystalline powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Verapamil Hydrochloride\n■^apanril Chloride; Iproveratril Hydrochloride\n^H38N2O4, HC1, \nMol. Wt. 491.1\nye^apamil Hydrochloride is (R5)-2-(3,4-dimethpxyphenyl)-\n5-[[2-(3,4-dimethoxyphenyl)ethyl](methyl)amino] -\n, 2-(l -mcihyiethyljpentanenitrile hydrochloride.\nVerapamil Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \naid not more than 101.0 per cent of C27H3SN2O4, HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Calcium channel blocker.\n^scription. A white, crystalline powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"t^t.Atnay be omitted if tests B, C andD are carried out Tests\nfandC may be omitted if tests A and D are carried out\n■^Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\n;; Compare the spectrum with that obtained with verapamil\nVERAPAMIL HYDROCHLORIDE\nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of \nverapamil hydrochloride.\nB. When examined in the range 220 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.002 per cent w/v solution in 0.01 Mhydrochloric acid shows \nabsorption maxima at about 229 rim and 278 nm and there may \nbe a shoulder at about 282 nm. The ratio of the absorbance at \nthe maximum at about 278 nm to that at the maximum at about \n229 nm is 0.35 to 0.39.\nC. In the test for Related substances, the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthe peak due to verapamil in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a).\nD. It gives reaction (B) of chlorides (2.3.1). \n...\nAppearance of solution. A 5.0 per cent w/v solution in carbon \ndioxide-free water prepared with the aid of gentle heat is clear \n(2.4.1), and colourless (2.4.1).\npH (2.4.24). 4.5 to 6.0, determined in a 5.0 per cent w/v solution \nprepared with the aid of gentle heat.\nRelated substances^ Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n.\nSolvent mixture. 63 volumes of mobile phase A and 37 volumes\nof mobile phase B.\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 5 mg, each of, of verapamil \nhydrochloride IPRS and (2RS)-2-(3,4-dimethoxyphenyl)-2- \n[2-[[2-(3,4-dimethoxy-phenyl)ethyl] (methyl)amino] ethyl] - \n3-methylbutanenitrile IPRS (verapamil impurity A IPRS), in \n20.0 ml of the solvent mixture. Dilute 1 ml of the solution to \n10 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with the solvent mixture. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution \nto 10.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mrh, packed with\npahnitamidopropylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. a 0.7 per cent w/v solution of\ndipotassium hydrogen phosphate adjusted to pH 7.2 \nwith orthophosphoric acid,\nB. acetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given\nbelow,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 278 nm,\n. - injection volume: 10 pl.\n3935\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVERAPAMIL HYDROCHLORIDE\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v) r; ;z (per cent v/v)\ny 0\n. o: toto.^toto'toto\ntotornJÏVto'to\n22.-..\nto,.. . \n37 \n>\n27toto A ■? to; 35tototo',..:J.\n' to- i 65 Tto.T\n; ■ 35 -\n' A;-'35; TV-';;\n-obtoto^^oxtoto ■\nto.to ^ ■.^to-'\n50\n63\n’ ' ' ' \n37'\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not validUnless the \nresolution between the peaks due to verapamil and verapamil\nimpurity A is not less than 5.0. \n' / \n■ ■■ .'. ■■\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \nsecondary peak is not more than the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) \n(0.1 per cent) and the sum of areas of.all the secondary peaks \nis not. more than 3 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference.solutiop.(b) (0.3 per \ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.1 times the area \nof the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b)(0.01 percent). . : \n:\nSulphated ash (2.3:18). Not more than 0.1 percent. ; r\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay. Weigh 0.4 g, dissolve in 40 ml of anhydrous glacial \nacetic acid, add 6 ml qf mercuric acetate solution. Titrate \nwith 0.1 M perchloric acid, determining the end-point \npotentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 M:perchloric acid is equivalent to 0.04911 g of\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/verapamil-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Verapamil Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Verapamil Injection\nVerapamil Hydrochloride Injection; Verapamil Chloride \nInj ection; Iproveratril Hydrochloride Inj ection\nVerapamil Injection is a sterile: solution of. Verapamil \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections. ■\nVeräpamirInjection contains not less than 90.0 per Cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of verapamil \nhydrochloride, CVILsVO.-,, HCl. \n\"\nUsual strength. 2.5 mg per ml. , ."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Verapamil Injection\nVerapamil Hydrochloride Injection; Verapamil Chloride \nInj ection; Iproveratril Hydrochloride Inj ection\nVerapamil Injection is a sterile: solution of. Verapamil \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections. ■\nVeräpamirInjection contains not less than 90.0 per Cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of verapamil \nhydrochloride, CVILsVO.-,, HCl. \n\"\nUsual strength. 2.5 mg per ml. , ."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Dilute a volume containing. 10 mg of Verapamil \nHydrochloride to 5 ml with 0.1 M hydrochloric ac/ri, extract\nwith 5 ml of ether, discard the ether extract and make thé \naqueous layer just alkaline with? 2 M potassium carbonate \nExtract with 5 ml of ether, filter.' the ether layer through \nanhydrous sodium sulphate and evaporate to dryness. The \nresidue complies with the following-test. -to\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6) \nCompàre the specif urn with that obtained with verapamil \nhydrochloride IPRS treated in the Same mariner or with the \nreference spectrum of verapamil. ; \n'\nB. To a volume containing 5 mg.of Verapamil Hydrochloride \nadd 0.2 ml of a.5 per cent w/v solution of mercuric chloride- a \nwhite precipitate is produced. \n. y\nC. To a volume containing 5 mg of Verapamil Hydrochloride \nadd 0.5 ml of 3 M sulphuric acid and 0.2 ml of dilutepotassium \npermanganate solution', a violet precipitate is produced which\nquickly dissolves to produce a very pale yellow solution, ;\nA. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 0.1g \nofVerapamil Hydrochloride with 25 ml of 0.1 Mhydrochloric \nàdidfdfi 0'minutes, filter, extract the filtrate with 25 ml of ether, \ndiscard the ether extract and make thé aqueous \nlayer just alkaline with 2 M potassium carbonate \nsesquihydrate. Extract with 25 nil Of ether, filter thé ether layer \nthrough anhydrous sodium sulphate' and evaporate to \ndryness /i’he residue complies with the following tests.\nDetermine by-infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with verapamil \nhydrochloridetreated in the;same manner or with the \nTÇfergûceispeçtrum of verapamil.\nB. Chlorides (2.3.12).' Dissolve a quantity of powdered tablets \ncontaining 0.24 g of verapamil hydrochloride with 6 ml of \nmethanol for a few minutes^ allow to stand for 5 minutes and \ndiluting 0.7 ml ofthe supernatant liquid to 2 ml with wafer. The \nresulting solution yields complies with the limit test for\nchlorides. \n;\nA. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 0.1 g \nofVerapamil Hydrochloride with 25 ml cfO.IM hydrochloric \nacid, filter, extract the filtrate with 25 ml of ether, discard the \nether extract and make the aqueous.layer just alkaline with \n2 Mpotassium carbonate. Extract, with 25 ml of.etfer,.filter the \nether layer .through anhydrous sodium sulphate and \nevaporate to dryness. The residue complies with the, following \ntests. . ,l -... \n. .\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare;the spectrum with that; obtained with verapamil \nhydrochloride IPRStreated’in-the same manner or with the \nreference spectrum of verapamil. ;;\nB. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 0.1 g \nof Verapamil Hydrochloride with TO ml of.dichloromethane- \nfilter, evaporate the filtrate to diyness and dissolve the residue \nin 10 ml of water(solution A). To 2 ml of the resulting solution \nadd 0.2 ml of a 5 per cent w/v solution of mercuric chloride', a \nwhite precipitate is produced. \n.\n...............................          IP 2022\nC; To 2 ml of solution A obtained in test B add0.5 ml of 3 \nsulphuric acid and 0.2 ml of dilute potassium permanganate \nsolution; a violet precipitate is produced which\nQuickly\ndissolves to produce a very pale yellow solution. ;"},{"name":"Tests","text":"■ ■ \n;-er.; ;\npH (2.4.24). 4.5 to 6.0.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute the injection with the mobile phase to \nobtain a solution containing.0.125 per cent w/v of Verapamil \nHydrochloride.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with the mobile phase. Further dilute 1.0 ml the solution \nto 10.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.005 per cent \nw/v, each of verapamil hydrochloride IPRS and verapamil \nimpurity I ((2RS)~2-(3',4'diméthàxyphenyl)-2~[2-[]2-(3,4 \ndimethoxyphenyl) ethyl] (methyl) amino] ethyl]-3-methyl- \nbutanenitrile) IPRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system \n■ j\n- a stainless steel column 12.5 cm x 4 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (3 pm) (Such \nas Hypersil ODS), \n\\ ' ' , ■ '\n- mobile phase: a mixture of I volume of n-heptylamihe,\n. \n4; 7 volumes of glacial acetic acid,. =58 volumes of\n- flow rate: 0.85 ml per minute, - . ..<• : Çt-r-h- sat tto\n- spectrophotometer set at 278 nm, . . , f y \n- injection volume: 10 jil.\n: acetonitrile arid 137 volumes of 0.01Msodium acetate,\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to verapamil and verapanul\nimpurity I is not less than 2.0.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. Run the \nchroriiatbgrairi 4 times the retention tinde of the principal \nIn thé chromatogram obtained with the test solution; the area \nof any secondary peak is not more thanthe area of the principal\n3936\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVERAPAMIL HYDROCHLORIDE PROLONGED-RELEASE TABLETS\nw02£\n^^ in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n^ (0,1 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary \n^ksts not more than three times the area of the principal\npeakin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution\n£3)(0.3 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.5 \n¿nes that of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a) (0.05 per cent).\njgicterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 16.7 Endotoxin\nlimits per mg of verapamil hydrochloride. , . .., ; ;\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \npreparations (Injections). \n. \n?...\nÄSSäy Dilute a measured volume containing 5 mg of Verapamil \nHydrochloride to 100.0 ml with 0.01Mhydrochloric acid 'àué. \nmeasure the absorbance of the resulting solution at1 the \n^WitnünTat about 278 nm (2.4.7). Calculate thé content of \n■GnHisNlOl’HCl taking 118 as the specific^ absorbance at\n278nm. \n'■ \n■\" ■ \n’ \"■ ' \n■\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nVerapamil Hydrochloride Prolonged- \nrelease Tablets\nVerapamil Hydrochloride Sustained-release Tablets; \nVerapamil Hydrochloride Extended-release Tablets\nVerapamil Hydrochloride. Prolonged-release Tablets \nmanufactured by different manufacturers, whilst complying \nmttthe requirements of thé monograph, are not \ninterchangeable, as thedissolution profile of the products of \ndifferent manufacturers may hot be the same.\nVerapamil Hydrochloride Prolonged-release Tablets contain \nnot liess than 95.0 per cent and not more than 105,0 per cent of \nthe stated amount of verapamil hydrochloride C^HagN^Ö^Cl.\nUsual strengths. 120 mg; 240 mg.\n;\nDissolution (2.5.2). Complies with the test stated under Tablets.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid; chromatography \n(2.4.14). \nV-.-. . .\nSolvent mixture. N equal volumes of 0.0 IM hydrochloric acid \nand methanol. \n’ \n■■■ ■■• ■•\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of the powdered tablets \ncontaining 0.24 g of verapamil hydrochloride, with 95,0 ml of \nthe solvent mixture with the aid of ultrasound for 5 minutes \nshaking occasionally,dilute to 100.0 ml with solvent mixture \nand mix. Cenfrifiige 50 ml of the resulting solution, dilute'the \nsupernatant; liquid with the solvent mixture to obtain 0.072 per \ncent w/v solution of verapamil, hydrochloride and filter through \n0.45 pm filter.\nReference solution(a). Dilute 1 volume of testsolution to 200 \nvolumes with solvent mixture. \n; \n”\nReference solution ;(bf- Jdiïùte T volume of reference\nsolution(a) to 5 volumes with solvent mixture;\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 12.5 cni * 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bondedto porous silica (3 pm),\n- \ncolumn temperature 40°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of700 volumes of 0:082 per cent\nw/v solution of anhydrous sodium acetate and 3.3 per \ncent w/v solution of anhydrous acetic acid, 300 volume \nof acetonitrile and 3 volume of 2-heptylamine, '\nflowrate: 0.8 ml perminute, \n•••••••.\n- spectrophotometer set at 278 rim,\n- injectionvolume: 20 ul.\nInject test solution (a). The area of any secondary peak is hot \nmore than 0.4 times the area of the principal peak' in \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (a) (0.2 \nper cent) and the sum of the areas of all the secondary is hot \nmore than the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.5 per cent). Disregard \nany peak with an area less than the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained ¡with réference solution (b) \n(O.lper cent)., \n; \n•\nOther tests. Comply with' the test's^ stated under Tablets:\nAssay'.Determine by liquid chromatography (2,4.14).\nD:. V •: : l\"1 > g : J . • V 1 \n• : ' ' • •h-' • h '■ t H \n■ ■ : ■ \n• • • • •\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 labiets.Dispersc a quantity \nofthe powder containing 0.12 g of verapamil hydrochloride in\n3.937\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVERAPAMIL TABLETS\nmethanol, mix with the aid of ultrasound for 30 minutes and \noccasionally shaking for10 minutes . Add sufficient methanol \nto obtain 0.048 per centw/v solution of verapamil hydrochloride \nrnixandfilter. \n^ \n■ - \n■/ \n■ • r-\nReference solution. A 0.048 per dent w/v solution of verapamil \nhydrochloride IP RS in the methanol.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 30 cm x 3.9 mm, packed with\nbctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica or ceramic\n; microparticles ( 10 pm), \n<•-\n- mobile phase"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/veterinary-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Veterinary Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Veterinary Oral Suspension\nSulphadiazine and Trirnethoprim Mixture; Trimethoprim\nand Sulphadiazine Veterinary Oral Suspension;\nCo-trimazine Oral Suspension; Co-trimazine Mixture\nSulphadiazine and Trimethoprin'-t Veterinary Oral Suspension\nis a suspension of Sulphadiazine and Trimethoprim in the\nproportion of five parts to one part respectively, containing\nsuitable suspending and dispersing agents. It may contain\nsuitable antimicrobial preservatives.\nSulphadiazine and Trimethoprim Veterinary Oral Suspension\ncontains not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than\n110.0 per cent of the stated amounts of sulphadiazine,\nCIOHION40 2S, and oftrimethoprim, C14HISN403'\nUsual strengths. 40 per cent w/v of Sulphadiazine and 8 per\ncent w/v ofTrimethoprim; 4.55 per cent w/v ofSulphadiazine\nand 0.91 per cent w/v ofTrimethoprim."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Veterinary Oral Suspension\nSulphadiazine and Trirnethoprim Mixture; Trimethoprim\nand Sulphadiazine Veterinary Oral Suspension;\nCo-trimazine Oral Suspension; Co-trimazine Mixture\nSulphadiazine and Trimethoprin'-t Veterinary Oral Suspension\nis a suspension of Sulphadiazine and Trimethoprim in the\nproportion of five parts to one part respectively, containing\nsuitable suspending and dispersing agents. It may contain\nsuitable antimicrobial preservatives.\nSulphadiazine and Trimethoprim Veterinary Oral Suspension\ncontains not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than\n110.0 per cent of the stated amounts of sulphadiazine,\nCIOHION40 2S, and oftrimethoprim, C14HISN403'\nUsual strengths. 40 per cent w/v of Sulphadiazine and 8 per\ncent w/v ofTrimethoprim; 4.55 per cent w/v ofSulphadiazine\nand 0.91 per cent w/v ofTrimethoprim."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating the\nplate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 75 volumes of ethyl acetate,\n15 volumes ofdimethylformamide and 5 volumes ofwater.\nTest solution (a).\nA dilution of the oral suspension in\n1.4 M methanolic ammonia containing the equivalent of\n0.2 per cent w/v ofSulphadiazine.\nTest solution (b). A dilution of the oral suspension in\n1.4 M methanolic ammonia containing the equivalent of\n0.2 per cent w/v ofTrimethoprim.\nReference solution (a). A 0.2 per cent w/v solution of\nsulphadiazine RS in 1.4 M methanolic ammonia.\nR-ele,:e-l1ce- -soTiltio-iz-(bTA-o~2- pe-r-cenf w7vsoliltion-6T\niiimidhopriiii RS iii 1.4MiiiethiJnolicainiJ1oniiJ.\nApply to the plate 5 Jll of each solution. After development,\nilly the plate in air and spray with a 0.1 per cent w/v solution\n2692\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP20l0\nof 4-dimethylaminobenzaldehyde in a mixture of I ml of\nhydrochloric acid and 100 ml ofethanol (95per cent), allow\nto dry and spray with dilute potassium iodobismuthate\nsolution. The spot in the chromatogram obtained with test\nsolution (a) having Rrvalue of about 0.7 corresponds to the\nprincipal spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (a). The spot in the chromatogram obtained with test\nsolution (b) having Rrvalue of about 0.3 corresponds to the\nprincipal spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (b)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Other tests. Complies with the tests stated under Veterinary\nOral Liquids.\nAssay. For sulphadiazine -\nTransfer an accurately weighed\nquantity of the oral suspension containing about 0.125 g of\nSulphadiazine, into a separator containing 20 ml of 0.1 M\nsodium hydroxide and extract with four quantities, each of\n50 ml, ofdichloromethane. Wash each dichloromethane extract\nwith the same two quantities, each of 10 ml, of0.1 M sodium\nhydroxide. Combine the aqueous washings and the aqueous\nlayer from the separator and reserve the combined\ndichloromethane extracts for the Assayfor trimethoprim.\nDilute the combined aqueous solutions to 250.0 ml with water,\nfilter and dilute 10.0 ml ofthe filtrate to 200.0 rnl with water. To\n2.0 ml ofthe resulting solution add 0.5 ml of4 Mhydrochloric\nacid and I ml ofa 0.1 per cent w/v solution ofsodium nitrite\nand allow to stand for 2 minutes. Add I ml of a 0.5 per cent\nw/v solution ofammonium sulphamate and allow to stand for\n3 minutes. Add I ml of a 0.1 per cent w/v solution of\nN-(l-naphthyl)ethylenediamine dihydrochloride, allow to\nstand for 10minutes. Dilute the solution to 25.0 ml with water\nand measure the absorbance of the resulting solution at the\nmaximum at about 538 urn (2.4.7), using as the blank a solution\nprepared in the same manner using 2 ml ofwater and beginning\nat the words \"add 0.5 ml of4M hydrochloric acid........\".\nCalculate the content of C lOH lON40 ZS from the absorbance\nobtained by carrying out the procedure simultaneously, with\n2.0 ml ofa 0.0025 per cent wIv solution ofsulphadiazineRSin\n0.0005 M sodium hydroxide and beginning at the words \"add\n0.5 ml of4 Mhydrochloric acid.......\".\nFor trimethoprim -\nExtract the combined dichloromethane\nextracts from the Assayfor sulphadiazine with four quantities,\neach of50 ml, ofa 5 per cent v/v solution of 6 M acetic acid;\nwash the combined aqueous extracts with 5 ml of\ndichloromethane, discard the dichloromethane layer and\ndilute to 250.0 ml with a 5 per cent v/v solution of6 M acetic\nacid. Dilute 20.0 ml to 100.0 ml with water and detennine the\nabsorbance ofthe resulting solution at the maximum at about\n271 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content OfCl4HlSN403 taking 204\nas the specific absorbance at 271 urn.\n2693\nSULPHAQUINOXALINE\nDetennine the weight per ml ofthe suspension (2.4.29), and\ncalculate the contents of sulphadiazine and trimethoprim,\nweight in volume.\nLabelling. The label states the strength in tenns ofthe amounts\nofSulphadiazine and Trimethoprim."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vidanga","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vidanga"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vidanga\n3.2.2. THIN LAYER CHROMATOGRAMS OF HERBS AND HERBAL PRODUCTS\nIP 2022"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Vidanga\n3.2.2. THIN LAYER CHROMATOGRAMS OF HERBS AND HERBAL PRODUCTS\nIP 2022"},{"name":"IPRS TEST","text":"(Under day light after spraying\n(Under UV light at 366 nm)\nwith Dragendorff’s reagent)\nTLC Chromatogram of Vasaka Extract"},{"name":"IPBRS TEST","text":"(Under UV light at 366 nm) \nTLC Chromatogram of Vidanga\n926\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\n3.2.2. THIN LAYER. CHROMATOGRAMS OF HERBS AND HERBAL PRODUCTS"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vildagliptin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vildagliptin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vildagliptin\nVildagliptin is (S)-1 -[2-(3-Hydroxyadamant-1 -ylamino)acetyl] \npyrrôlidine-2-carbonitrile.\nVildagliptin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 1Q2.0 per cent of C17H25N3O2, calculated on the dried \nbasis..-./.\nCategory. Antidiabetic.\nDescription. A white to off white powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Vildagliptin\nVildagliptin is (S)-1 -[2-(3-Hydroxyadamant-1 -ylamino)acetyl] \npyrrôlidine-2-carbonitrile.\nVildagliptin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 1Q2.0 per cent of C17H25N3O2, calculated on the dried \nbasis..-./.\nCategory. Antidiabetic.\nDescription. A white to off white powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A- Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry \n(2.4;6)-J/Compare the spectrum with that obtained with\nvildagliptin IPRS or with the reference spectrum of \nVldagliptin.\nb. ïn thé Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to thé peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nVILDAGLIPTIN\nAppearance of solution: A 5.0 per cent w/v solution is clear \n(2.4.1 ) and not more intensely colored than reference solution \nBYS5 and GYS5 (2.4.1).\nEnantiomeric purity. Not more than 0.5 per cent.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. Equal volumes of n-hexane and ethanol.\nTest solution. Dissolve 100 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 5 ml of ethanol, with the aid of ultrasound and \ndilute to 10.0 ml.with n-hexane....\nReference solution (a). A' 0.01 per cent w/v solution of\nvildagliptin IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of reference solution (a)\nto 20.0 mJ with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve 100 mg of vildagliptin IPRS\nand 1 mg of R-enantiomer of vildagliptin (l-[(3- \nHydr oxy adamant-1 -ylamino)-acetyl] -pyrrolidine-(2R)- \ncarbonitrile) in 5 ml of ethanol, with the aid of ultrasound and \ndilute to 10.0 ml with «-hexmîë.■. .\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\namylase tris-3,5-dimethyl phenylcarbamate (10 pm) \n(Such as chiralpak AD),\n- column temperature: 40°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 90 volumes of n-hexane, 5\nvolumes of ethanol, 5 volumes of methanol and 0.1 \nvolume of trifluoroacetic acid,\n- flow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 2 Î5 nm,\n- injection volume : 10 pl.\nThe relative retention time with reference to vildagliptin for R- \nenantiomer of vildagliptin is about 0.8.\nInject reference solution (a) and (c). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to vildagliptin and R- \nenantiomer of vildagliptin is not less than 1.5 in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c) and the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 1.0 per cent in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution(a).\nInject reference solution (a), (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to R-enantiomer of vildagliptin is not more \nthan 0.5 times the area of principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (0.5 percent). Ignore any \npeak with an area less than the area of principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (0.05 per \ncent).\n3939\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDiazabicyclo undecene Impurity. Not more than 0.1 per cent\nDetermine by. 1 ¡quid chromatography (2.4,14). \n;.\nSolvent mixture.1 Ovolumes ofacetonitrile'dv\\d90 volumes \nofwater \n.< \n•'£.\nTest solutions ^Dissolve 150 mg;of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 10.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution.' A 0.0015 per cent w/v solution of \ndiazab icy clou ndecene IBRSvifh& solvent ¡mixture. :\nChromatographic system \n':'5 i!'\"'' ‘;;\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm 8'3 mm; packed with \n.. :. octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (3.5 pm), (Such\nas XTerra MS), . . \n•> \"k m -w..-. V ■?.?., ■\n- \ncolumn temperature: 60°,\n- \nmobile phase: A. a buffer solution prepared by \ndissolving 2.7