chromatogram obtained wjft \nreference solution (b) (2.2 per cent). Ignore any peak with an \narea less than the ar ea of the principal peak in the chromatogra^ \nobtained with reference so lution (c) (1.0 per cent), . . \nJ J yf\nOther tests.; Comply with the tests? stated under Tablets;\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as \ndescribed under Related substances With’ the following\n0\nmodifications. \n< a\no, <■\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless thé \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more\n■■ 10. ■■\n8\n30\n70\nthan 2.0 per cent. \n■ ’ .\n. , \n8.1 '\n. . - 100'.’\n0\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution. ? .. ;\n■ ' -'A V 11 \n■ .. ■ri ri-.p. 100 ‘C ••.’••••..\n0 ri’-\n.Calculate, the content of C^HisN.ip^ in .the tablets. ; \n. r/. f\n- Storage. Store protected at a temperature not exceeding 30°.\nCorrect"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vinblastine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vinblastine Injection","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vinblastine Injection\nVinblastine Sulphate Injection\nVinblastine Injection is a sterile material consisting of\nVinblastine Sulphate with or without auxiliary substances. It\nis filled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents ofthe\nsealed container in the requisite amount ofsterile 0.9 per cent\nw/v solution ofSodium Chloride, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for\nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under\nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately\nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nVinblastine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 11 0.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nvinblastine sulphate, C46H5sN409,H2S04.\nUsual strength. 10 mg.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powdersfor Injection) and with thefollowing requirements.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVINBLASTINE INJECTION"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Vinblastine Injection\nVinblastine Sulphate Injection\nVinblastine Injection is a sterile material consisting of\nVinblastine Sulphate with or without auxiliary substances. It\nis filled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents ofthe\nsealed container in the requisite amount ofsterile 0.9 per cent\nw/v solution ofSodium Chloride, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for\nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under\nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately\nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nVinblastine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 11 0.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nvinblastine sulphate, C46H5sN409,H2S04.\nUsual strength. 10 mg.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powdersfor Injection) and with thefollowing requirements.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVINBLASTINE INJECTION"},{"name":"Identification","text":"CAUTION -\nHandle Vinblastine Injection with great care\nsince it is a potent cytotoxic agent. Avoid contact with eyes;\nirritant to tissues.\nA. When examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), the\nsolution obtained in the Assay shows an absorption maximum\nat about 267 nm.\nB. In the test for Related substances, the principal spot in the\nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to\nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nC. To 1 ml add 0.2 ml of a freshly prepared 1 per cent w/v\nsolution of vanillin in hydrochloric acid; a pink colour is\nproduced in about 1 minute (distinction from Vincristine\nSulphate)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24).3.5 to 5.0, deterrnined in a 0.15 percentw/v solution\nof the dried contents.\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography\n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobilephase. A mixture of80 volumes oftoluene, 40 volumes\nof chloroform and 6 volumes of diethylamine.\nTest solution. Dissolve a quantity ofthe contents ofa container\nin sufficient methanol to produce a solution containing the\nequivalent of 1 per cent w/v of dried vinblastine sulphate.\nReference solution (a). A 1per cent w/v solution ofvinblastine\nsulphate RS in methanol.\nReference solution (b). A 0.02 per cent w/v solution of\nvincristine sulphate RS in methanol.\nApply to the plate 5 III of each solution. After development,\ndry the plate in air and examine in ultraviolet light at 254 nm.\nAny secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained with the\ntest solution is not more intense than the spot in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 10.0 Endotoxin\nUnits per mg of the dried contents.\nAssay. Weigh the contents of 20 sealed containers. Weigh\naccurately a quantity ofthe mixed contents containing about\n20 mg ofdried vinblastine sulphate and dissolve it in 100.0 ml\nof methanol. Dilute 10.0 ml to 100.0 ml with methanol and\nmeasure the absorbance of the resulting solution at the\nmaximum at about 267 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content of\nC<ji;HssN<j09,HzSO<j taking 185·as· thespecificaosoroance-at\n267nm.\nStorage. Store in sealed containers in a deep freezer (Below\n-18°).\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nLabelling. The label states (1) the strength in terms of the\nweight of dried vinblastine sulphate contained in it; (2) the\nnames ofauxiliary substances, ifany; (3) that the contents are\nto be used by intravenous injection only; (4) the storage\nconditions."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vinblastine-sulphate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vinblastine Sulphate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vinblastine Sulphate Injection\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDose. By intravenous injection, according to the n \npatient.\nVinblastinc Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nVinblastine Sulphate with or without auxiliary substances. It \nis filled in a sealed container.\nReference solution (c). A 0.002 per cent w/v solution of \nvinblastine sulphate RS in water.\nDescription. A white or yellowish, amorphous or crystalline \npowder; very hygroscopic.\nReference solution (d).  A 0.0001 per cent w/v solution of \nvinblaitine sulphate RS in water.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile 0.9 per cent \nw/v solution of Sodium Chloride, immediately before use.\nCAUTION- Handle Vinhlastine Sulphate with \nsince it is a potent cytotoxic agent. Avoid contact wi\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 rim), (b) a guard \ncolumn packed with a suitable silica gel placed between \nthe pump and the injection device, \nmobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of methanol, \n38 volumes of a 1.5 per cent v/v solution  of diethylamine \nadjusted to pH 7.5 with phosphoric acid and 12 volumes \nof acetonitrile, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 262 nm, \ninjection volume: 10 p3.\nirritant to tissues.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections)."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Vinblastine Sulphate Injection\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDose. By intravenous injection, according to the n \npatient.\nVinblastinc Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nVinblastine Sulphate with or without auxiliary substances. It \nis filled in a sealed container.\nReference solution (c). A 0.002 per cent w/v solution of \nvinblastine sulphate RS in water.\nDescription. A white or yellowish, amorphous or crystalline \npowder; very hygroscopic.\nReference solution (d).  A 0.0001 per cent w/v solution of \nvinblaitine sulphate RS in water.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile 0.9 per cent \nw/v solution of Sodium Chloride, immediately before use.\nCAUTION- Handle Vinhlastine Sulphate with \nsince it is a potent cytotoxic agent. Avoid contact wi\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 rim), (b) a guard \ncolumn packed with a suitable silica gel placed between \nthe pump and the injection device, \nmobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of methanol, \n38 volumes of a 1.5 per cent v/v solution  of diethylamine \nadjusted to pH 7.5 with phosphoric acid and 12 volumes \nof acetonitrile, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 262 nm, \ninjection volume: 10 p3.\nirritant to tissues.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections)."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with vinblastine \nsulphate RS.\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nVinblastine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nvinblastine sulphate, C46H58N409,H2SO4.\nC.A 10 per cent w/v solution gives the reaction of sulphates \n(2.3.1).\nUsual strength. 10 mg.\nNOTE - Store all solutions in ice before use.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powdersfbr Injection) and with the following requirements.\nInject each solution and record the chromatograms for 3 times \nthe retention time of the peak due to vinblastine.\nCAUTION -- Handle Vinblastine Injection with great care \nsince it is a potent cytotoxic agent. Avoid contact with eyes; \nirritant to tissues.\npH (2.4.24). 3.5 to 5.0, determined in a 0.15 per cent w/v solution.\nRelated substances. In the Assay, the area of any secondary \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution is \nnot greater than that of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) and the sum of the areas\nCalculate the percentage content of C46H58N409, H 2SO4.\nA. When examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), the \nsolution obtained in the Assay shows an absorption maximum \nat about 267 nm.\nVinblastine Sulphate intendedibr use in the manufacture of \nparenteral preparations without a further appropriate \nprocedure for the removal of bacterial endotoxins complies\nof any such peaks is not greater than 2.5 times the area ofthC \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (c). Ignore any peak with an area less than th4 \nof the peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (d). Aff\nB. In the test for Related substances, the principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a). \nC. To 1 ml add 0.2 ml of a freshly prepared 1 per cent w/v \nsolution of vanillin in hydrochloric acid; a pink colour is \nproduced in about 1 minute (distinction from Vincristine \nSulphate).\nwith the following additional requirement.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 10.0 Endotoxin \nUnits per mg of vinblastine sulphate.\nVinblastine Sulphate intended for use in the manufacture of \nparenteral preparations without a further appropriate \nsterilisation procedure complies with the following \nadditional requirement.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 15.0 per cent, \ndetermined by Method B, on an appropriately calibrated \ninstrument using about 3.0 mg, weighed. Heat the substance \nunder examination at the rate of 5\" per minute between ambient \ntem-Orature\" and 200° in a current of nitrogen for \nchmniatograity with a flow rate of 40 ml per minute. From the \ntherniogram, determine the accumulated loss in weight betwee n\nTes ts..\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility._\n.\nStorage. Storeprotected from light and moishire in a-\n(2424). 3.5to 5.0, determined in a 0.15 per cent w/v solution \nof the dried contents.\nfreezer (Below -18\"). If the material is intended for use in the\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with  silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 80 volumes of toluene, 40 volumes\nof chloroform and 6 volumes of diethylamine.\nTest solution. Dissolve a quantity of the contents of a container \nin sufficient methanol to produce a solution containing the \nequivalent of 1 per cent w/v of dried vinblastine sulphate.\nReference solution (a). A 1 per cent w/v solution of vinblastine\nsulphate RS in methanol.\nReference solution (b). A 0.02 per cent w/v solution of\nvincristine sulphate RS in methanol.\nApply to the plate 5 p.1 of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm. Any secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test  solution is not more intense than the spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 10.0 Endotoxin \nUnits per mg of the dried contents. \nAssay. Weigh the contents of 20 sealed containers. Weigh a \nquantity of the mixed contents containing about 20 mg of \ndried vinblastine sulphate and dissolve it in 100.0 ml of \nmethanol. Dilute 10.0 ml to 100.0 ml with  methanol and measure \nthe absorbance of the resulting solution at the maximum at \nabout 267 nm (2.4.7). Calculate the content of\nC46H58N409,H2SO4 taking 185 as the specific absorbance at\n267 nm. \nStorage. Store in sealed containers at a temperature of 2° to 8°.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the strength in terms of the \nweight of dried vinblastine sulphate contained in it; (2) the\nnames of auxiliary substances, if any; (3) that the contents are \nto be used by intravenous injection only; (4) the storage \nconditions.\nVincristine Sulphate\nOH C H\nH 2SO4\nN \nH \nH COOC\nH3 CO\nC46H56N4010,H2SO4\nVincristine Sulphate is methyl (3aR,4R,5S,5aR,1 013R,13)\nh I- 9 5 5 7 R  9S)-5-ethy1-5-hydroxy-\nacetoxy-3a-et y-[(\nVINBLASTINE INJECTION \nip 2018 \n11)20 18 \nVINCRISTINE INJECTION\nChromatographic system\n9- methoxycarbonyl- 1 ,4,5,6,7,8,9, 1 0-octahydro-2H-3, 7. \nmethanoazacycloundecino[(5,4-b)indo 1-9-y 1]-6-formyi..5. \nhydroxy- 8-methoxy-3a,4,5,5a,6, 1 1 , 1 2, 1 3a-octahydro- /11.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm  packed with \noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 .tm), \nmobile phase: A. a 1.5 per cent v/v solution of \ndiethylamine, adjusted to pH 7.5 with orthophosphoric \nacid,\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctyl"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. A 0.5 percent w/v solution in carbon \ndioxide-free water is clear (2.4.1), and not more intensely \ncoloured than reference solution YS5 (2.4.1).\nThe assay is not valid unless the resolution between the peaks \ndue to vincristine and vinblastine in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) is at least 4 and the signal-\nto-noise ratio of the peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (d) is at least 5.\nreference solution (d).\nAppearance of solution. A 0.5 per cent w/v in carbon  dioxide-\nSterility (2.2. 1 1 ). Complies with the test for sterility.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 12.0 per cent, \ndetermined by Method B, on an appropriately calibrated \ninstrument using about 3.0 mg, weighed. Heat the substance \nunder examination at the rate of5° per minute between ambient \ntemperature and 200° in current of nitrogen for chromato-\ngraphy with a flow rate of 40 ml per minute.\nfree water is clear (2.4.1), and not more intensely  coloured\nthan reference solution YS7 (2.4.1).\nStorage. Store protected from light in a deep freezer (Below \n-18'). If the material is intended for use in the manufacture of \nparenteral preparations, it should be stored in sterile, tamper-\nevident glass containers and sealed so as to exclude micro-\norganisms.\npH (2.4.24). 3.5 to 4.5. Determined in a 0.1 per cent w/v solution.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nNOTE- Keep the solutions in iced water before  use.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). \nTest solution.  Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under exam i-\nnation in 100 ml of water.\nLabelling. The label states whether or not the contents are \nsuitable for use in the manufacture of parenteral preparations.\nexamination in 10.0 ml of carbon dioxide-free  water. Dilute \nTest solution. Dissolve about 50.0 mg of the substance uncles,\n1.0 ml of this solution to 5.0 ml with  water."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vinblastine-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vinblastine Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vinblastine Sulphate\nC4óHS8N4O9.H2SO4 \n- - Mol. Wt. 909.1\nVINBLASTINE SULPHATE\n.........................................................................................\nVinblastine Sulphate is methyl (3a/?,4R,5S,5aK, 10bR, 13 a/?)- 4- \nacetoxy-3a-ethyl-9-^[(5tS,l77?^9iS')-5-ethyl-5^hydroxy- \n9-methoxy carbonyl-1,4,5,6,7,8,9,10-octahydro-2/Z-3,7- \nmethanoazacycloundecino[(5,4-h)indoI-9-yl]-5-hydroxy-8- \nmcthoxy-6-methyl-3 a, 4,5,5 a,6,11,1'2; 13a-octahydro-1/7- \nindolizino[8,l-ci/]carbazole-5~carboxylate sulphate.\nVinblastine Sulphate contains hot less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 104.0 per cent of C46i LSN4O9, H2SQ4, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A, white or yellowish/ amorphous or crystalline \npowder; very hygroscopic.\nCAUTION—Handle Vinblastine Sulphate with great care \nsince it is a potent cytotoxic agent. Avoid contact with eyes; \nirritant to tissues.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with vinblastine \nsulphate IPRS or with the reference spectrum of; vinblastine \nsulphate: \", < \n■ •?-.. • c\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nC. A 10 per cent w/v solution gives the reaction of sulphates \n(2.3.1). \n( \nJ\nTests ■ ■ ■ \n■ ■\nAppearance of solution. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution in carbon \ndioxide-free water is clear (2.4.1), and hot more intensely \ncoloured than reference solution YS5 (2.4.1).\npH (2,4.24)- 3.5 to 5.0,dctemiined in a 0.15 per cent w/v solution.\nRelated substances. In the Assay, the area of any secondary \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the test , solution is \nnot greater than that of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) and the sum of the areas \nof any such peaks is not greater than 2.5, times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (c). Ignore any peak with an area less, than that \nof the peak in the chromatogram Obtained with reference \nsolution (d).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 15.0 per cent,\ndetermined by Method B, on an appropriately calibrated \ninstrument using about 3.