 Sulphate is methyl (3a/?,4R,5S,5aK, 10bR, 13 a/?)- 4- \nacetoxy-3a-ethyl-9-^[(5tS,l77?^9iS')-5-ethyl-5^hydroxy- \n9-methoxy carbonyl-1,4,5,6,7,8,9,10-octahydro-2/Z-3,7- \nmethanoazacycloundecino[(5,4-h)indoI-9-yl]-5-hydroxy-8- \nmcthoxy-6-methyl-3 a, 4,5,5 a,6,11,1'2; 13a-octahydro-1/7- \nindolizino[8,l-ci/]carbazole-5~carboxylate sulphate.\nVinblastine Sulphate contains hot less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 104.0 per cent of C46i LSN4O9, H2SQ4, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A, white or yellowish/ amorphous or crystalline \npowder; very hygroscopic.\nCAUTION—Handle Vinblastine Sulphate with great care \nsince it is a potent cytotoxic agent. Avoid contact with eyes; \nirritant to tissues.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with vinblastine \nsulphate IPRS or with the reference spectrum of; vinblastine \nsulphate: \", < \n■ •?-.. • c\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nC. A 10 per cent w/v solution gives the reaction of sulphates \n(2.3.1). \n( \nJ\nTests ■ ■ ■ \n■ ■\nAppearance of solution. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution in carbon \ndioxide-free water is clear (2.4.1), and hot more intensely \ncoloured than reference solution YS5 (2.4.1).\npH (2,4.24)- 3.5 to 5.0,dctemiined in a 0.15 per cent w/v solution.\nRelated substances. In the Assay, the area of any secondary \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the test , solution is \nnot greater than that of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) and the sum of the areas \nof any such peaks is not greater than 2.5, times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (c). Ignore any peak with an area less, than that \nof the peak in the chromatogram Obtained with reference \nsolution (d).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 15.0 per cent,\ndetermined by Method B, on an appropriately calibrated \ninstrument using about 3.0'ing, weighed! Heat the substance \nunder examination at the rate of 5° per minute between ambient \ntemperature and 200° in a current of nitrogen for \nchromatography with a flow rate of 40 ml per minute. From the \nthermogram, determine the accumulated loss in weight between\n3945\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVINBLASTINE SULPHATE\nambient temperature and a point; on the plateau before \ndecomposition is indicated (àt’about 160°). A-j ; / r.-mm;\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).! : J f ' \n■■/A-/,' a-: ri <. \n''AU..? û.;U \n;îî wb'.-m; xa' ; ; rflw ■\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 ;g .qf the substance under \nexamination in.100 ml of water. . ; . \nA . ; .\nReference solution (a). A solution containing. 0.1 per cent\nw/v.each of vinblastine sulphate IPRS and vincristine \nsulphate IPR.S in water .......  ’ \" 1 \" rî , \n\"\nReference solution (b). A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of\nvinblastine sulphate IP RS in water, ! j 1\nReference solution (c). A 01002 per cent w/v solution of \nvinblastine sulphate IPRS in water. \" r\nRéference solution (d): N(.}S}()b\\ pox cent w/v solution of\nvinblastine sulphate ÎPRS xd wdiér:\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed .with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), (b) a guard\n< • ” • 1 column-packed with a suitable silica gel placed between\n/v : ' the pump and the injection device. ; \n•. w.\nmobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of methanol, \n38 volumes of a 1.5 per cent v/v solution ofdiethyldmine\nof acetonitrile,... .^,. j.\n- flowrate:! ml per, minute, - ,, f. f \n- spectrophotometer set at 262 nm,\n... ,. \nadjusted to pH 7.5. withphospjoriç.qcid and 12 volumes\ninjection volume: l Oui. •i 1\nNOTE — Store all solutions in ice before use.\nInject each solution and record the chromatograms for 3 times \nthe retention time of the pea