rmine by thin-layer\nALxC_.xi00 \n<. .j|\nAj x C + A^\nInject réference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks due to talo-zanamivir and \nzanamivir.is not less than 1.5 in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (b) and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 1.5 in the chromatogram \nobtained;with reference solution (a).\nchromatography (2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel \nGF254. \n'..........\nwhere, Aj =' peak response of imidazole;\nMobile phase. A mixture of 90 volumes of dichloromethane \nand 10 volumes of methanol.\nAz = ' peak response of zanamivir; \nC - correction factor.\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.2 g of the substance under \nexamination in 10.0 ml of methanol.\nInject, the test;solution at 234 nm.. The .area. ■ of any peak I \ncorresponding to imidazole carboximidamide is not more than 1 \n0.01 per cent and the area of any peak corresponding to 4- 1 \nbiguanide zanamivir is not more than 0.2 per cent, calculated | \nby using the expression. \n' i\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.01 per cent w/v,\nCalculate the content of C12H20N4O7.\neach of, zidovudine IPRS and triphenylmethanol in methanol.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not \nexceeding30°.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise to about three-fourths of the height of the plate. \nDry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at 254 \nnm. Any secondary spot observed in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponding to \ntriphenylmethanol is not .more intense than the corresponding \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \n(0.5 per cent). No other secondary spot in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution is more intense than the \nprincipal spot in the chromatogram obtained with the reference \nsolution (0.5 per cent).\n■' A(- X C ... \n■••■■■■■; <■:■■■.■■. J\n------------------xlOO \n| \nAC X C + A 7 .... -• \ni.(7; II\nWhere, Ac =' peak response of imidazole carboximidamide g\nZidovudine\nfactor\n'■ ;J - or4-biguanidezanamivir, ' ■' \n|\n0.4\n' Az-' sum of the responses of all the peaks including |\no\n0.3\n• \n■ \nthezanamivir, \n\"■ \n'' \nI\nC - correction factor for imidazole carboximidamide \n|\nor 4-biguanide zanamivir. \n< ■<' ' \n|\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution at 234 g \nnm, the area of any peak corresponding to O-triazinyl zanamivir |\nis not more than 0.3 per cent, the area of any peak \ncorresoondinij tn \n-------\nSpray the plate with a mixture of 0.5 g of carbazole in 95 ml of \nethanol (95 per cent) and 5 ml of sulphuric, acid, heat for\n0.63\nlO.minutes at 120° and compare the intensities of any secondary \nspots, observed in the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution with those of the principal spots in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution. No spot corresponding \nto triphenylmethanol (Rf value about 2.3 relative to the Rf of\nx  — ....nun» vu uica analog and4-amino zahanuvif | \neach of, i"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zidovudine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zidovudine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zidovudine Injection\nZidovudine Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nzidovudine, C10II13N5O4. \n;\nCalculate the content of CwHb W-T in the medium.\nZidovudine Injection is a sterile solution of Zidovudine in\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of\nWater for Injections. - ~ \n—\nZidovudine Injection contains not less than.90.0 per cent and\nCioHijNjO.-\nElated; substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\nUsual strength. 100 mg. \n:\nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zidovudine Injection\nZidovudine Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nzidovudine, C10II13N5O4. \n;\nCalculate the content of CwHb W-T in the medium.\nZidovudine Injection is a sterile solution of Zidovudine in\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of\nWater for Injections. - ~ \n—\nZidovudine Injection contains not less than.90.0 per cent and\nCioHijNjO.-\nElated; substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\nUsual strength. 100 mg. \n:\nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of"},{"name":"Identification","text":"SK\n(2.4.14).\nC10H13N5O4. \n...\nTest solution. Weigh a quantity of the mixed contents of 20\nA.Transfer a quantity of the mixed contents of thehapsules \ncontaining about 15 mg of Zidovudine to a 100-ml vbWetric\nUsual strengths. 10 mg per ml.\nCapsulescontaining about 100 mg of Zidovudine and transfer\n3987\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZIDOVUDINE INJECTION\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\nIdentification .;• ixt ■\nA. When examined in the range 220 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.0015 per cent w/v solution in a mixture of 75 volumes of \nmethanol and 25 volumes of water shows absorption maxima \nsimilar to those obtained with a solution of zidovudine IPRS \nof the same concentration.\nB. In the Assay, the, principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (a). ,\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14) \n.\nin the test for Related substances. \nfe\nand mix. Dilute 5.0 ml of the solution to\nA. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to thé peak due \nto Zidovudine in the chromatogram obtained with, reference\nIniect reference solution (a) and the test solution.\n(a). Weigh 100 mg of zidovudine IPRS \n^dOO-ml volumetric flask, add about 50 ml of \n^^¡¿biiephaséfmix with the aid of ultrasound to dissolve, \n^^Bgluihewith the mobile phase and mix.\nsolution (a). \n' y'n >\nCalculate the content of C10H13N3O4 in the injection^\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture. :,\nB. Remove the coating from a few tablets and crush. thepiina \nmortar so that no large pieces remain. \nç,\nWeigh 20 mg of thymine IPRS and \na ¿bo ml volumetric flask, add about 150 ml of the \ng^l^^imi^with the aid of ultrasound to dissolve, dilute \n^^j^with the mobile phase and mix. . ? ■ : w ■ ■ -\nShake a quantity of powdered tablets containing.0.1 g of \nZidovudine with 40 ml of methanol, filter and evaporate the \nfiltrate to dryness. The residue complies with;the following"},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 3.5 to .7.0, in a mixture containing a volume of \ninjection containing 150 mg of zidovudine and 5 ml of 0.12 M \npotassium chloride.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dilute a measured volume of .the injection \ncontaining 25 mg of Zidovudine, to 25 ml with methanol.\nReference solution (a).. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \nzidovudine IPRS in methanol.\nReference solution. (bf.A solution containing 0.1:per cent \nw/v ofzidovudine IPRS and 0.001 per cent w/v of zidovudine \nimpurity C (thymine):*;\nChromatographic system\n. — a stainless steebcolumn 25 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 80 volumes of water and 20\nvolumes of methanol,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 265 nm,\n- injection volume: 10 pl. ;\nInject reference solution (b) The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to zidovudine and thymine \nis not less than 4.0, the tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and \nthe relative standard deviation for replicate injections is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution the area of any \npeak corresponding to thymine is not greater than twice the\narea of the corresponding peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (b) (2.0 per cent). ■\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections). . :\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 1.0 Endotoxin \nUnit per mg of zidovudine. \n;\n3988\nZIDOVUDINE TABLETS\n...................................................... .......................\n.... Volumetric flask, dissolve and dilute to volume with"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zidovudine-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zidovudine Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zidovudine Oral Solution \nJ W\ntest.\nZidovudine Oral Solution is a solution of Zidovudi^^; \nsuitable flavoured vehicle.\nTrans^r W-P ml of the reference \nritipff^rence splutioa(b) to a,IQO-ml \n^^^^^ask>and make up. lo the volume with the: mobile\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6)'. \nCompare.the spectrum with that obtained with zidovudine \nIP RS or with the reference spectrum of zidovudine. ,\nZidovudine Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0^^\nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated àhioig^ \nzidovudine, CioH^NsO^ \n'"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zidovudine Oral Solution \nJ W\ntest.\nZidovudine Oral Solution is a solution of Zidovudi^^; \nsuitable flavoured vehicle.\nTrans^r W-P ml of the reference \nritipff^rence splutioa(b) to a,IQO-ml \n^^^^^ask>and make up. lo the volume with the: mobile\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6)'. \nCompare.the spectrum with that obtained with zidovudine \nIP RS or with the reference spectrum of zidovudine. ,\nZidovudine Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0^^\nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated àhioig^ \nzidovudine, CioH^NsO^ \n'"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Cartographic system\niUl^taibless steel column 12.5 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with\nUsual strength. 50mgper5ml.\nDissolution (2.5.2).\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n— mobile phase: a mixture of 90 volumes of 0.04 Msodium\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle),\nMedium. 900 ml of water, \n.) \n.\nSpeed and time. 50 rpm and 30 minutes.\n'. 5.3 vnfa glacial acetic acid, \n¡w\n- \nflow rate: 1.3 ml per minute, 5 ■::7f fe \n: ■ fe7■ -: ;;.\n- spectrophotometer set at 265 nm, ; : \n; '\npH (2.4.24). 3(0 to 4.0, determined in a mixture containing \nvolume of the preparation under examination ¿contaming\nZidovudine Tablets\n150 mg of zidovudine and 5 ml of 0.12 Mpotassium chloride\n■ -injectionvolume:' 10 pl: ’ \n‘CW'\nZidovudine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than, 110.0 per ;cent of the stated amount of \nzidovudine, C10H13N5O4.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid;chromatography\nInject the reference solution. The tailing factor is not more \nthan 2.0 for zidovudine peak and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not mote than 2.0 per cent. -\n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Transfer a measured volume of the preparation \nunder examination containing about 100 mg of zidovudine to\nf'sual strengths. 100 mg; 300 mg.\n3989\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZIDOVUDINE TABLETS\nInject the reference solution and the test solution and measure \nthe peak responses of the major peak.\nCalculate the content of C mH) 3N5O4 in the medium. ....\nQ. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of \nCjoHuNsO^.\nRelated substances Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nTes [solution: Disperse the powdered tablets containing about \n1:5 g of Zidovudine in 50 nil of water, shake for 30 minutes. \nAdd about 150 ml of niethahol( sonicate for 10 minutes and \ndilute to 500.0 ml with water. Dilute 4.0 ml of the solution to\n100;0 ml with water, filter \nu ;.\nReference solution (a): A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of 3'~\nchloro-3 ’-deoxythymidine (zidovudine impurity B IPRS) in \nmethanol.\nReference solution (b). A 0.02 per cent w/v solution of thymine. \nIPRS (zidovudine impurity CIPRS) in methanol.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve about 30 mg óf zidovudine \nIPRS in 3.0 ml of methanol, add 2.5 ml bf reference solution \n(a), 5.0 ml of reference solution (b) and dilute to 250.0 ml with\nwa/er. f?i Ei, \n'ZU\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsi lane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: dissolve 3.0 g of sodium acetate and\n1.3 g of sodium I-octanesulpho nate in 900 ml of water. \nAdd 90 ml of methanol and 40 ml of acetonitrile, \nadjusted to pH 513 with glacial acetic acid, filter,\n- flow rate: 1.3 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometeriset at 265 nm,\n- , injection volume: 20 pl. -, ■ < . n .\nThe relative retention time with: reference to zidovudine for \nzidovudine impurity C is about 0.17 and for zidovudine impurity \nBis about 1.2. \n■ ver:,.:*..-\nInject réference solution (c). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between thè peaks due to zidovudine and \nzidovudine impurity.B is not less than 2.5,the tailing factor of \nthè principal peak is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is nÒt more than 2 0 per cent.\nInject reference solution (c) and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained the.test solution, the area offhe peak \ndue to zidovudine impurity Ç is. not more than 1.5. per cent, \nmultiplying with response factor of 1.7 and the area of any \nother secondary peaks is not more than 0.2 per cent. The sum \nof areas of all the secondary peaks is. not more than 2.0 per \ncent. : \n: \n-y -- ■-■■■ ■■■■'\nOther tests:. Comply with the tests stated-under Tablets.: ;\n3990\nAssay. Determine by;liquid chromatography (2.4.14)||u \ndescribed under Related substances. ..... . \nfÿf\nInject reference solution (c) and the test solution. ‘\nCalculate the content of C10H :3N5O/, in the tablets.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture. , ,\nZidovudine, Lamivudine and J3\nNevirapine Tablets\n. ...... \n-, \\ \n... . ........ \n. . yf\nZidovudine, Lamivudine and Nevirapine Tablets contain not \nless than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of Ùïe \nstated amounts of zidovudine, Ci0H¡3N5O4, lamivudine, \nCJ I, 1N3O3S and nevirapine, Ci5H|4N4O.\nUsual strength. Zidovudine, 300 mg, Lamivudine, 150 mg and \nNevirapine, 200 mg. :"},{"name":"Identification","text":"f ¿ce/ute, 9 volumes of methanol, 1 volume of acetonitrile\nA. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17),coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\n.-.iigììtóibiZVblùme of glacial acetic acid,\n-Mlótvrate:T ml per minute,\n-f (spectrophotometer set at 240 nm,\n-injectionvolume: lOpl. ■\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter promptly \nthrough a membrane filter disc having an average pore diameter \nnot greater than 1,0 pm, rejecting the first few ml of the filtrate \nand dilute a suitable volume of, the filtrate,, if necessary with \nwater. '. \n' ■ '. \n‘ \n..\nMobile phase.. A mixture, of 40 volumes ofèl^bututiQ^ \n30 volumes.pf heptane, 30 volumes of acetone and 10 volumes ' | \nof strong ammonia solution.\nInject reference solution (c). The test is not valid unless the \nrelative retention times are about 0.12 for thymine and 1.0 for \nzidovudine, the resolution between zidovudine and thymine\nTest- solution. Dilute the preparation under examination with | \nmethanol to obtain a solution containing 5 mg of zidovudine \nper ml. . \n.. ?\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). ! J\nis not less than 4.0, the tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and\nTest solution. Use the filtrate, dilute if necessary, with the\nthe relative standard deviation for replicate injections is not\nReference solution. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution of zidovudine \nIPRS in a mixture of 75 volumes of methanol and 25volumes|\nmore than 2.0 per cent.\ndissolution medium. \n.\nReference solution. A known quantity of zidovudine IPRS is \ndissolved in 1 ml of methanol and suitably dihitcdwith H’ufer \nto obtain a solution having a. similar concentration, as that of\nof water. ■ ■■■■■ ■ \n■ \n- \n■ I\nInjectxeference solution (c) and the test solution and measure \nthe responses for the thymine peak. The content of thymine\nApply to the plate 5 pl of each solution. After development, I \ndry the plate in air and examine under ultraviolet light at | \n254 nm. The principal spot in the chromatogram obtained with | \nthe test solution corresponds to that in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution. ' 7 \n\\\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram j\nin the oral solution should not be more than 3.0 per cent.\nthe.test solution. \n. \n......... ..\nOther tests. Comply with the tqsts stated under Oral Liquids.\nAssay, Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), as\nChromatographic system\n. - a stainless steel column .15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\ndescribed under Related substances.\noctadecylsilane bonded to. porous, silica r(5 pm) (Such\n. . asHypersilBDSCl8), \n..y..\n- .. mobile phase: a mixture of 90 ml of methanol and 40 ml\ninject ; reference solution (c) and the test solution.\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the ì \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (c).' \nI\nCalculate the content of Ci0H!3N5O4Ìn the Oral Solution.\n, . of acetonitrile.and. a buffer prepared by,dissolving\n3.0 g of sodium acetate and 3.0 g of sodium \n1-bctanesulphbnate in 900 ml of wafer, adjusted to pH\nStorage: Store protected front light and moisture.\nA. In the Assay, the three principal peaks in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution correspond to .the peaks due \nto zidovudine, lamivudine and nevirapine in.the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution. ; w mi\nTests ' \n; ;\nDissolution(2.5.2). \n•; ■\nApparatus No. 2 (Paddle), . :\nMedium. 900 ml of///M/w/rac/z/ivv’c aczU, ‘ \n' \"dffff\nSpeed and time. 7 5 rpm and 30 minutes. \n' J ' : ;\nWithdraw a suitable volume of the medium and filter.promptly \nthrough a membrane filter disc haying an average pore, diarnetet \nnot greater than 1.0 gm, rejecting the first few ml of the filtrate \nand dilute a suitable volume of the filtrate, if necessaj^yitlí \nwater. 3 \n>■.. \n\\\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). ■< -;j\nTest solution: Use the 'filtrate, dilute if necessary/ with the \ndissolution medium. . r< ... ..... • .. \n'\nReference solution. Weigh 0.3 g of zidovudine IPRS$ 0;|J\nlamivudine IPRS and 0.2 g of nevirapine IPRS and transfer to \na 100 ml volumetric flask. Add about 20 ml of methanol,tact, \nwith the aid of ultrasound to dissolve and dilute tovolume \nwith a mixture of equal volumes of methanol andiy^E^|^ \n5.0 ml ofthe solution to 50.0 ml with 0.1 M hydrochlori'cacid-\nChromatographic'system ' \n:\n- a stainless steelcolumn 15 cm x 4.6 mm,packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous Silica (5 \nasHypersilBDSCl8),\nwww.webofpharma.com\n---\nZIDOVUDINE, LAMIVUDINE AND NEVIRAPINE TABLETS\n(jr2022i\ngpfnibbilc phase: a mixture of 35 volumes of methanol and\n' .Bn;;65¡ volumes of abuffer solution prepared by dissolving \n; 00:68 g ofpòtàssiùm dihydrogen phosphate and 1.0 g of\noctanesulphonate m 1000.0 ml of water to which\n\"of triethylamine is added and adjusted to pH 2.5 \np/iospAorzc acid,\n^.jflow rate; ! ml per minute, :\n1 spectrophotometer set at 266 nm, \ninjection volume: 10 pl. ....\njnjeet the reference solution. The tailing factor for the individual \n¿¿ovudinè, lamivudîne and nevirapine peaks is not more than\n2;Q^d therelative standard deviation for replicateinjcctions \nof ad Ac analyte peaks is not more’ than 2.0 per cent. ; ■ - c\nfôjëcfffie reference solution and the test solution and measure \nthe peak responses of the major peaks due to zidovudine, \nlaœivùdinë arid nevirapine/i \n\"\nCalculate the contents of Ct0H 13N5Ô4, C8HnN3O3S àrid\nin the medium. \n- \n\\\nQ. Not less than 70 per cent of the stated amounts of\nC10H1ÎN5O4, C8H11N3O3S and C15H14N4O.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nSdlve'tiP mixture. 60 volumes of water and 40 volumes1 of \nmethanol.. . \n, ■ a ■:. .. \n; . \n,■ \n. \n, ,\nTest solution. Disperse a quantity of thè powdered tablets \ncontaining 50 mg of Lamivudine, in the solvent mixture and \ndilute to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture, filter. \ni:\nReference solution (a). A solution containing 0.0001 per cent \nfefepilamivudine diastereomer IPRS, 0.001 per cent w/v of \nzidovudine impurity BIPRS, 0.05 per cent w/y o^damiyudine. \nI"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zidovudine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zidovudine"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zidovudine\nMol. Wt. 267.2\nZidovudine is 1-(3-azido-2,3-dideoxy-{3-o-ribofuranosyl)-\n5-methylpyrimidine-2,4(lH,3H)-dione.\nZidovudine contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0 per cent ofC IOH13Ns0 4, calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral.\nDose. 300 mg twice daily.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zidovudine\nMol. Wt. 267.2\nZidovudine is 1-(3-azido-2,3-dideoxy-{3-o-ribofuranosyl)-\n5-methylpyrimidine-2,4(lH,3H)-dione.\nZidovudine contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0 per cent ofC IOH13Ns0 4, calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral.\nDose. 300 mg twice daily.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with zidovudine RS\nor with the reference spectrum ofzidovudine.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak due\nto zidovudine in the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (a).\nC. Melting range (2.4.21). 122°to 125°,\nA.Transfer a quantity of the mixed contents of the capsules\ncontaining about 15 mg ofZidovudine to a 1OO-ml volumetric\nflask. Add about 80 ml ofa mixture of75 volumes ofmethanol\nand 25 volumes of water, shake for 10 minutes and dilute to\nvolume with the same solvent mixture, mix and filter. Dilute\n10 ml ofthe filtrate to 100 ml with the same solvent mixture.\nWhen examined in the range 200 nm to 300 nm (2.4.7), the\nresulting solution shows an absorption maximum at about\n265nm.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak due\nto zidovudine in the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (c)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Specific optical rotation (2.4.22). +60.so to +63.0°, determined\nin a 1.0 per cent w/v solution in ethanol (95 per cent).\nRelated substances. A.\nDetermine by thin-layer\nchromatography (2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel\nGF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 90 volumes of dichloromethane\nand 10 volumes ofmethanol.\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.2 g of the substance under\nexamination in 10 ml ofmethanol.\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.01 per cent w/v\neach of zidovudine RS and triphenylmethanol in methanol.\nApply to the plate 10 /ll of each solution. Allow the mobile\nphase to rise to about three-fourths ofthe height ofthe plate.\n2329\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZIDOVUDINE\nDry the plate in air and examine in ultraviolet light at 254 om.\nAny secondary spots observed in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the test solution correspond to those of the principal\nspots in the chromatogram obtained with the reference\nsolution. No secondary spot in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the test solution is more intense than the principal spot\nin the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution\n(0.5 per cent).\nSpray the plate with a mixture of0.5 g ofcarbazole in 95 m10f\nethanol (95 per cent) and 5 ml of sulphuric acid, heat for\n10 minutes at 120° and compare the intensities ofany secondary\nspots observed in the chromatogram obtained with the test\nsolution with those ofthe principal spots in the chromatogram\nobtained with the reference solution. No spot corresponding\nto triphenylmethanol (Rrvalue about 2.3 relative to the Rrof\nzidovudine) is more intense than the corresponding spot in\nthe chromatogram obtained with the reference solution\n(0.5 per cent).\nNo secondary spot in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution is more intense than the\nprincipal spot in the chromatogram obtained with the reference\nsolution.\nB. Deterinine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 g of the substance under exami-\nnation in 100 ml ofmethanol.\nReference solution (a). A 0.001 per cent w/v solution of\nzidovudilJe RS in methanol.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.1 per cent\nw/v of zidovudine RS and 0.001 per cent w/v each of\nzidovudine-related compound B RS and zidovudine- related\ncompound C RS in methanol.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane' bonded to porous silica (5 /lm),\nmobile phase: A. water\nB. methanol,\nflow rate. 1ml per minute,\na linear gradient programme using the conditions given\nbelow,\nspectrophotometer set at 265 om,'\ninjection volume. 10 /ll.\nMobile phase B\nTime\nMobile phase A\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\no\n90\n(per cent v/v)\n10\n15\n35\n65\n30\n35\n65\n35\n5\n95\n40\n5\n95\n45\n90\n10\nZIDOVUDINE\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the\nresolution between the peaks due to zidovudine and\nzidovudine-related compound B is not less than 1.5 and the\ntailing factor for zidovudine is not more than 1.5.\nSeparately inject the test solution and reference solution (a).\nThe area of the peak corresponding to zidovudine- related\ncompound B is not greater than 1.0 per cent and of that to\nzidovudine-related compound C is not greater than 2.0 per\ncent.\nThe sum of the percentages of related substances by tests A\nand B is not greater than 3.0 per cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with limittest for heavy\nmetals, Method B (20 ppm).\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.25 percent.\nWater (2.3.43). Notmore than 1.0per cent, determined on 0.5 g.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh accurately about 100 mg ofthe substance\nunder examination, dissolve in a suitable quantity ofmethanoI\nin a 50-ml volumetric flask and make up to volume with the\nmobile phase. Dilute 5.0 ml ofthis solution to 50.0 ml with the\nmobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Weigh accurately about 100 mg of\nzidovudine RS, dissolve in a suitable quantity ofmethanol in\na 50-ml volumetric flask and make up to volume with the mobile\nphase (solution A). Dilute 5.0 ml ofsolutionAto 50.0 ml with\nthe mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Transfer 2.0 ml ofa 0.005 percentw/v\nsolution ofzidovudine-related compound B RS in the mobile\nphase to a 50-ml volumetric flask, add 5.0 ml ofsolutionA and\nmake up to volume with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 Ilm),\n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 70 volumes of water and\n30 volumes ofmethanol,\n-\nflow rate. 1ml perminute,\n-\nspectrophotometer set at 265 nm,\n-\ninjection volume. 20 Ill.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the\nresolution between the peaks due to zidovudine and\nzidovudine-related compound B is not less than 2.0.\nInject reference solution (a). The relative standard deviation\nfor replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\n..........Separately-inject·the test solution-andreferencesolution(a}\nand measure the responses for the principal peak.\nCalculate the content ofC IOHJ3Ns0 4•\nStorage. Store protected from light.\nIP 2010\nZidovudine Capsules\nZidovudine Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nzidovudine, CIOHJ3Ns0 4•\nUsual strength. 100 mg.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Weigh accurately a quantity of the mixed\ncontents of20 capsules containing about 100 mg ofZidovudine\nand transfer to a 100-ml volumetric flask. Add about 60 ml of\nthe mobile phase, mix with the aid ofultrasound for 10 minutes,\ndilute to volume with the mobile phase, mix and filter. Dilute\n5.0 ml ofthe filtrate to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Weigh accurately about 100 mg of\nzidovudine RS and transfer to a 100-ml volumetric flask.\nDissolve in about 60 ml of the mobile phase and dilute to\nvolume with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Weigh accurately about 20 mg of\nthymine and transfer to a 200-ml volumetric flask, dissolve\nand make up to volume w"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-chloride-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Chloride Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Chloride Injection\nZinc Chloride Injection is a sterile solution of Zinc Chloride in \nWater for Injection.\nZinc Chloride Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nzinc, Zn.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml.\nIn the Assay, the test solution exhibits an absorption maximum \nat about 213.8 nm.\npH (2.4.24). 1.5 to 2.5.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 25.0 Endotoxin \nUnits per mg of zinc.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Inj ections).\nAssay. Sodium chloride solution. Dissolve 450 mg of sodium \nchloride in water and dilute to 500 ml with water.\n4133\nwww.webofpharma.com"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zinc Chloride Injection\nZinc Chloride Injection is a sterile solution of Zinc Chloride in \nWater for Injection.\nZinc Chloride Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nzinc, Zn.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml.\nIn the Assay, the test solution exhibits an absorption maximum \nat about 213.8 nm.\npH (2.4.24). 1.5 to 2.5.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 25.0 Endotoxin \nUnits per mg of zinc.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Inj ections).\nAssay. Sodium chloride solution. Dissolve 450 mg of sodium \nchloride in water and dilute to 500 ml with water.\n4133\nwww.webofpharma.com"},{"name":"ZINC CHLORIDE INJECTION","text":"Reference solution. Dissolve 3.11g QÏzinc oxide, in 80 ml of\n0.5 M sulphuric acid, warm, cool and dilute to 250 ml with \nwater. This stock solution contains 10 mg of zinc per ml. Dilute \na volume of the solution with 'water to obtain a solution \ncontaining 125 pg of zinc per ml. Transfer 3.0, 4.0, and 5.0 ml, \nrespectively, of the solution to three separate 500-ml vohimetric \nflasks, each containing 5 ml of sodium chloride solution, dilute \nthe contents of each flask with water to volume, and mix. \nThese sohitions contain, respectively, 0.75,; 1.0, and 1.25 pg of \nzincperml. N:,v. .y/.-,y • ■ ¿..y. , ■ ■ • . <\nTest solution: Dilute a measured volume of injection, containing \nabout 5 mg of zinc,to 500 mi with w/c-r Transfer 10.0 ml ofthe \nsolution .tQ a 100-ml volumetricflask. From the labeled amount \nof sodium chloride, if any, in the injection, calculate the amount, \nin mg, of sodium chloride in the 10.0 ml portion and add \nsufficient sodium chloride solution to bring the total sodiüm \nchloride content Of thé 100-ml volumetric flask to \n0.9 ing and dilute to volume with water '.\nDetermine the absorbances of the solutions at the zinc \nemission line at 213.8 nm, with a suitable atomic absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.2) equipped with a zinc hollow-cathode \nlamp and an air-acetylene flame, using water as the blank. \nPlot thé absorbances of the reference solutions versus the \nconcentration, in pg per ml, of zinc, and draw the straight line \nbest fitting the three plotted points^ From’the graph so \nobtained, determine the concentration, in pg per ml, of zinc in \nthe test solution. Calculate the quantity, in mg, of zinc in each \nml of the injection taken by the formula:\n5C ........\n: \n- y \n■\nin which C is the concentration, in pg per ml, of zinc in the test \nsolution and V is the volume,' in ml, of injection taken.\nStorage. Store in single-dose or multiple-dose containers, \npreferably of Type I or Type II glass.\nLabelling. The label states the injection to be diluted with \nWater for Injection or other suitable fluid, to appropriate \nstrength prior to administration."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Chloride\nZnCI2\nMol. Wt. 136.3\nZinc Chloride contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent of ZnCl2.\nCategory . Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white or practically white, crystalline powder; \nvery deliquescent."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zinc Chloride\nZnCI2\nMol. Wt. 136.3\nZinc Chloride contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent of ZnCl2.\nCategory . Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white or practically white, crystalline powder; \nvery deliquescent."},{"name":"Identification","text":"À. T6 2 g add 38 ml of carbon dioxide-free water prepared \nhorn distilled water and add 2 M hydrochloric acid dropwise \nuntil sblution is complete and dilute to 40 ml with carbon \ndioxide-free water prepared from distilled water (solution \nA); Solution A gives the reaction of zinc salts (2.3.1 ).\nB- A5 per cent w/v solution in 2 M nitric acid gives the \nreactionsof chlorides (2.3.1).\nThsts"},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24). 4.6 to 6.0, determined in a solution prepared by \ndissolving 1.0 g in 9 ml of freshly boiled and cooled water, \nignoring any slight turbidity.\nAliiihniium, calcium, heavy metals, iron and magnesium. To \n8 ml bf solution A add 2 ml of strong ammonia solution and\nthe solution is clear (2.4.1), and colourless (2.4.1). Add \nNil of a 9 per cent w/v solution of disodium hydrogen \nphosphate', the resulting solution remains clear for at least\n$biihutes. Add 0.2 ml of sodium sulphide solution', a white\nprecipitate is produced and the'supernatant liquid remains \ncolourless. \n' \n—\nAmmonium salts. To 5 ml of a 10 percent w/v solution add\n1 M sodium hydroxide until the precipitate first formed is \nredissolved and then warm the solution; no odour of ammonia \nis perceptible.\nOxychlorides. Dissolve 1.5 g in 1.5 ml of carbon dioxide-free \nwater, the solution is not more opalescent than opalescence \nstandard OS2 (2.4.1). Add 7.5 ml of ethanol (95 per cent)', the \nsolution may become cloudy within 10 minutes but becomes \nclear on the addition of 0.2 ml of 2 Mhydrochloric acid.\nSulphates (2.3.17). 15 ml of solution A complies with the \nlimit test for sulphates (200 ppm).\nAssay. Dissolve 3.0 g in 125 ml of water, add 3 g of ammonium \nchloride and add sufficient water to produce 250.0 ml. To \n25.0 ml of the resulting solution add 100 ml of water and 10 ml \nof strong ammonia-ammonium chloride solution. Titrate with \n0.1 M disodium edetate, using eriochrome black T solution \nas indicator until a deep blue colour is obtained. Carry out a \nblank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 M disodium edetate is equivalent to 0.01363 g of \nZnCl2.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, in non-metallic \ncontainers."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Citrate\nCi2Hi0O14Zn3,2H2Q: \n, Mol. Wt. 610.4\n4134\n. -..................................:............ \nf\nZinc Citrate is 2-1 lydroxy-1,2,3-propanetricarboxylic acidzinc\nZinc Citrate contains not less than 31.3 per cent ofz^ \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n'\nCategory. Mineral supplement\nDescription. A white or almost white powder. ’ \ny"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zinc Citrate\nCi2Hi0O14Zn3,2H2Q: \n, Mol. Wt. 610.4\n4134\n. -..................................:............ \nf\nZinc Citrate is 2-1 lydroxy-1,2,3-propanetricarboxylic acidzinc\nZinc Citrate contains not less than 31.3 per cent ofz^ \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n'\nCategory. Mineral supplement\nDescription. A white or almost white powder. ’ \ny"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. A 10 per cent w/v solution, gives reaction of Zinc salt \n(2.3.1). \n____ _ \n' ^7\nB. \nA 10 per cent w/v solution, gives reaction (Bj of citrate \n(2.3.1). \n; \n;"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Chlorides (2.3.12). 0.5 gcomplies.with the limit test for chlorides \n(500ppm).\nSulphates (2.3.17). 0.3 g complies with the limit test for \nsulphates (500 ppm).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with limit test for heavy \nmetals, Method B (20 ppm). \n,;\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 2 hours.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.35 g in 60 ml of water, add 10 nil ammonia­\nammonium chloride buffer, and titrate with\nedetate using 0.1 ml of eriochrome black T solution & \nindicator, until a blue colour is obtained. Carry out a blank \ntitration. \n' \n'•....... ‘\n1 ml of 0.05 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.0654 gof\nZn. . \n\"\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-oxide-and-salicylic-acid-paste","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Oxide and Salicylic Acid Paste"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Oxide and Salicylic Acid Paste\nZinc Oxide and Salicylic Acid Paste contains not less than \n22.5 per cent and not more than 25.5 per cent w/w of zinc \noxide, ZnO and not. less than 1.9 per cent and not more than \n2 J-per cent w/w bf salicylic acid, C7H6O3.\nZinc oxide, finely sifted \n240 g\nSalicylic acid, finely sifted \n20 g\nStarch, finely sifted \n240 g\nWhite Soft Paraffin \n. 500 g\nExtemporaneous Preparation. Melt the soft paraffin, \nincorporate the Zinc Oxide, the Salicylic Acid and the starch \nand stir until cold.\nUsual strength, 24 per cent of zinc oxide and 2 per cent of \nsalicylic acid w/w. ' . \n, \n- ;\nA. Shake 1 g of substance under examination in lOmlofrra/er, \nand filter. To thé filtrate, add 1.0 ix& oí ferric chloride solution; \nan intense reddish violet colour is produced. To the solution, \nadd 1 ml of acetic acid; the colour is not dispersed. To the \nsolution, add 2.0 ml of 2 Mhydrochloric acid; the colour is \ndispersed and a white crystalline precipitate is formed? ’\nB. Heat 0.5 g gently in a porcelain dish over a very small flame \nuntil the base is completely volatile or charred. Increase the \nheal until all the carbon is removed. The residue is yellow; \nwhen hot and white; when cool, . _. . . . \n,.\nOther tests. Comply with thé tests stated under Topical Semi­\nSolid Preparations. ,\nAssay ; For''Salicylic Acid— : Shake-500 mg of the paste in\n10 ml of 1 M hydrochloric acid and add 10 ml of ether until \nfully dispersed. Decant and reserve the aqueous layer. Extract \nthe ether layer with two further 10 ml quantities of 1 M \nhydrochloric acid, combine the:, aqueous extracts with the \nreserve aqueous layer, wash with 10 ml of ether and reserve \nfor the Assay of Zinc Oxide. Combine the ether extract, add\n15 ml of petroleum spirit (boiling range, 40° to 60°)and \nextract with successive quantities of 20 mJ, 10 ml and 10 ml of \na mixture of equal volumes of ethanol (95 per cent) and 1 M \nsodium hydroxide. Dilute the combined extracts to 100 ml \nwith 2 Mhydrochloric «czX further dilute 15 ml bf the resulting \nsolution to 50 ml with the same solvent and measure the \nabsorbance of the final solution at the maximum at 302 nm \n(2.4.7.) Calculate the content of C-H6O3 using260 as a specific \nabsorbance at 302 nm.\nFor Zinc Oxide ~ To the combined aqueous extracte obtained\nin the Assay for Salicylic Acid,, add 20 ml of■ 1 M sodium \nhydroxide and 50 mg of xylenol .orange triturate.. To the\n3993\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZINC STEARATE \n.up/- •\nresulting solution, add sufficient hexamine to change the \ncolour of the solution to red and then a further 3 g of hexamine \nand titrate with 0.1M disodium edetate. Carry but a blank \ntitration. HUrKiTí/ VU/'P //■; or:/ /.Ui ■■■...■ ..\nÍ ml of 0.1 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.008139 g of \nzinc oxide, ZnO.\nStorage. Stoic protected frommoisture.\nZinc Stearate .. . ..^,.....¿.3 7....\n(CI7H35COO)2Zn r / A Mol. Wt.632.3\nZinc Stearate consists mainly of zinc stearate hut many \ncontain variable proportions of zinc palmitate (Cj 5H3! COO)2 \nZn, and zinc oleate (Ci7H33COO)2Zn.\nZinc Stearate contains not less than 10.0 per -cent and not \nmore than 12.0 per cent of zinc, Zn.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (dustingponder).\nDescription. Afine, white,¡bulky,amorphous powder, free from \ngrittiness. \n. •--v. •"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zinc Oxide and Salicylic Acid Paste\nZinc Oxide and Salicylic Acid Paste contains not less than \n22.5 per cent and not more than 25.5 per cent w/w of zinc \noxide, ZnO and not. less than 1.9 per cent and not more than \n2 J-per cent w/w bf salicylic acid, C7H6O3.\nZinc oxide, finely sifted \n240 g\nSalicylic acid, finely sifted \n20 g\nStarch, finely sifted \n240 g\nWhite Soft Paraffin \n. 500 g\nExtemporaneous Preparation. Melt the soft paraffin, \nincorporate the Zinc Oxide, the Salicylic Acid and the starch \nand stir until cold.\nUsual strength, 24 per cent of zinc oxide and 2 per cent of \nsalicylic acid w/w. ' . \n, \n- ;\nA. Shake 1 g of substance under examination in lOmlofrra/er, \nand filter. To thé filtrate, add 1.0 ix& oí ferric chloride solution; \nan intense reddish violet colour is produced. To the solution, \nadd 1 ml of acetic acid; the colour is not dispersed. To the \nsolution, add 2.0 ml of 2 Mhydrochloric acid; the colour is \ndispersed and a white crystalline precipitate is formed? ’\nB. Heat 0.5 g gently in a porcelain dish over a very small flame \nuntil the base is completely volatile or charred. Increase the \nheal until all the carbon is removed. The residue is yellow; \nwhen hot and white; when cool, . _. . . . \n,.\nOther tests. Comply with thé tests stated under Topical Semi­\nSolid Preparations. ,\nAssay ; For''Salicylic Acid— : Shake-500 mg of the paste in\n10 ml of 1 M hydrochloric acid and add 10 ml of ether until \nfully dispersed. Decant and reserve the aqueous layer. Extract \nthe ether layer with two further 10 ml quantities of 1 M \nhydrochloric acid, combine the:, aqueous extracts with the \nreserve aqueous layer, wash with 10 ml of ether and reserve \nfor the Assay of Zinc Oxide. Combine the ether extract, add\n15 ml of petroleum spirit (boiling range, 40° to 60°)and \nextract with successive quantities of 20 mJ, 10 ml and 10 ml of \na mixture of equal volumes of ethanol (95 per cent) and 1 M \nsodium hydroxide. Dilute the combined extracts to 100 ml \nwith 2 Mhydrochloric «czX further dilute 15 ml bf the resulting \nsolution to 50 ml with the same solvent and measure the \nabsorbance of the final solution at the maximum at 302 nm \n(2.4.7.) Calculate the content of C-H6O3 using260 as a specific \nabsorbance at 302 nm.\nFor Zinc Oxide ~ To the combined aqueous extracte obtained\nin the Assay for Salicylic Acid,, add 20 ml of■ 1 M sodium \nhydroxide and 50 mg of xylenol .orange triturate.. To the\n3993\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZINC STEARATE \n.up/- •\nresulting solution, add sufficient hexamine to change the \ncolour of the solution to red and then a further 3 g of hexamine \nand titrate with 0.1M disodium edetate. Carry but a blank \ntitration. HUrKiTí/ VU/'P //■; or:/ /.Ui ■■■...■ ..\nÍ ml of 0.1 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.008139 g of \nzinc oxide, ZnO.\nStorage. Stoic protected frommoisture.\nZinc Stearate .. . ..^,.....¿.3 7....\n(CI7H35COO)2Zn r / A Mol. Wt.632.3\nZinc Stearate consists mainly of zinc stearate hut many \ncontain variable proportions of zinc palmitate (Cj 5H3! COO)2 \nZn, and zinc oleate (Ci7H33COO)2Zn.\nZinc Stearate contains not less than 10.0 per -cent and not \nmore than 12.0 per cent of zinc, Zn.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (dustingponder).\nDescription. Afine, white,¡bulky,amorphous powder, free from \ngrittiness. \n. •--v. •"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. To 5.0 g add 50 ml of ether and 40 ml of a 7.5 per cent v/v \nsolution of nitric acid in distilled Water and heat under a \nreflux condenser until dissolution is complete. Allow to: cool,\nseparate the aqueous layer arid shake the ether layer with two \nquantities, each of 4 ml, of distilled water. Combine the \nwashings with the aqueous layer, wash with 15 ml of ether \nand heat on a water-bath until ether is completely eliminated. \nAllow to cool and dilute to 50.0 ml with distilled water (solution \nA). Evaporate the ether layer to. dryness and dry the residue \nat 105°. .The freezing point of the residue is not lower than 53° \n(2.4.11). \n’/ \\ \n. L / . \n__ \n’\nB. Neutralise 5 ini of solutiori A to red litmus paper with 10 M \nsodium hydroxide: The solution gives the reactions of zinc \nsalts(2.3.1).' \n' '■ \n•.-••• - '\nTests ■■■:■■ \n'\n.Appearance of solution. Solution A is not more intensely\ncoloured than reference solution YS6 (2.4.1).\nAcidity or alkalinity. Shake 1.0 g with 5 rxA oí ethanol (9 5per\ncent) and add 20 ml oiparbon dioxide-free water and 0.1ml of \nphenol red solution. Not more than 0.3 ml of.0.1 M\nhydrochloric acid ox 0.1 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide is \nrequired to change the colour of the solutiori.\nAlkalis and alkaline earths: Add 1.0 g to a mixture of 25 ml of \nwater arid 5 ml of hydrochloric acid, boil,\" filter immediately \narid wash with 25 ml of hot water: Add dilute ammonia\n3994\n.............................................................................   _ ® 2022\nsolution to make the filtrate just alkaline’and then add \nammonium sulphide solption in excess to precipitate the zinc \nas zinc sulphide completely. Filter, add 0.5 mlof \nacrifto the filtrate, evaporate to dryness and ignite to constant ' \nweight; the residue weighs not more than20.mg._-.\nArsenic (2.3.-10). Mixó.Olgwith \noí bromine solution^\nevaporate to. dryness on a Water-bath. Ignite, gently dissolve \nthe cooled residue, ignoring any carbon, in 50 ml of water and\n14 ml of brominated hydrochloric aeidAsT arid remove the \nexcess of bromine with 2 ml of stannous chloride solution \nAsT. The resulting solution complies with' the limit test for \narsenic (2 ppm). ' \n.. \n- . ,^ ..7 \" '\nHeavy metals (2.3,13). Heat 5.0 g with 40 ml of 2 M acetic acid \nand allow to cool. Filter, wash the residue with two successive \nquantities, each of 5 ml, of warm water and dilute theconibined \nfiltrate and Washings to 100.0 ml with water. 12 iril of ihesolution\ncomplies with thè limit test for heavy metals; Method D \n(20 pprn). Use 1.0 ml oilead standard solution (1 Opprn Pb) to \nprepare th esta nd ar d. ' \na ■\nChlorides (2.3.12). 10 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for chlorides (250 ppm). \n<\nSulphates (2.3.17). Dilute 1 ml of solution Ato 50 ml with \nwater. Dilute 12.5 ml of the’solution to 1'5 ml with water. It \ncomplies with the limit test for sulphates (0.6 per cent).\nAssay. Weigh 1.0 g and boil with 50 ml of 2 M acetic acid until \nthe fatty acid layer which separates is clear, adding more w/er \nif necessary to maintain the original volume. Cool, filter and \nwash the filter and the flask thoroughly with water until thè \nlast washing is not acidic,to blueririw.y paper: To the combined \nfiltrate and washings add about 50 mg of xylenol orange \ntriturate and sufficient hexamine to produce violet-pink \ncolour. Add a further 2 g of hexamine and titrate with 0.1 tá \ndisodium edetate until the colour changes to yellow.\n1 ml of 0.1 M disodium edetate is equivalent to 0.00654 g of\nZU. \n.-i:/-- \nU/f . \n. í. V V ' : T :\nZinc Sulphate Eye Drops\nZinc Sulphate Eye. Drops are a sterile solution containing \n0.25 per cent w/v ofZiric Sulphate in Purified Water.\nZinc Sulphate Eye Drops contain not less than 0.22 per cent \nand not more than 0.28 per cent w/v of zinc sulphate, ZnS(h,\n.'777'777'733; ;:77 3;373' ' 33;\nDescription. Aclear, colourless solution. - \nu ■\nIt gives the reactions of zinc salts and of sulphates (2.3.1);\nwww.webofpharma.com\n■:othertests.Comply wirhthetests slalec^ under Eye Drops.\n5.0 ml add 50 ml of water and 5 ml oUimmonia buffer \n■ 7 and titrate with. 0,01 M. disodium edetate; using\nmfrffant black 11 solution sls indicator.;\nI\nofO/ M disodium edetate is equivalent to 0.002875 g of\nX7H2O. .. \n_ ; \n.....\nlinage. Store in containers of glass or any other non-metallic\nMaterial and sealed so as to exclude micro-organisms.\n¡eUndecenoate\n2 \n0\n^GH2\nH2C\nO.\nO\" >0\n'^HjgO^Zii\nMol. Wt. 431.9\nZinc Undecenoaie is zinc di(undec-10-enoate).\nZine Undecenoate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nhot more than 102.0 per cent of C^HjgC^Zn, calculated on the\ndriedbasis.. \n■\nCategory. Antifungal (topical).\nDescription A white or almost white, fine powder.\nA. To 2'.5 g add 10 ml ôf water and 10 ml of f Msulphuric acid \nandextraciwith two quantities,each, of ,10 ml, pf ether. Reserve \ntte aqueous layer for test G. Wash the combined ether extracts, \nwith water and evaporate to dryness. To the residue add 2 ml \nof freshly. distilled anilineand boil under a reflux condenser \nfor 10 minutes, cool and add. 30 ml of ether.. Extract with three \nquantities, each of 20 ml, of 2 Mhydrochloric acid and then \nwith 20 ml of water. Evaporate the ether extract to dryness on\na water-bath. The residue, after recrystallising twice from \nethanol (70per cent) and drying at a pressure riot exceeding \n2 kPa for 3 hours, melts at about 67° (2.4.21). \n\" :\nDissolve 0.1 g in a mixture of 2 ml of 1Msulphuric acid and \n5 ml of glacial acetic, acid and add, dropwisc, 0.25 ml of\npotassium permanganate solution', the pink colour of\npermanganate is discharged.\nC- Amixture of 1 ml of the aqueous layer reserved in test A and \n4mlof wqter giyes thereactiqn of zinc salts (2.3.1).\nTests. ...\nD-Melting range (2.4.21). 115° to 121°. A\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6), \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with ziprasidone \nhydrochloride monohydrate IP RS or with the reference \nspectrum of ziprasidone hydrochloride monohydrate.\nB. Dissol"},{"name":"Tests","text":"Alkalinity. Mix 1.0 g with 5 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and \n0-5 ml of phenol red solution., add 50 ml of carbon dioxide-\nZÏNG UNDECENOATE OINTMENT\nproduced. . \n■■ m'.A \n.w/T, f\nfree water and examine-immediately; no reddish colour is\nAlkalis and alkaline earths. Add 1.0 g to a mixture of 25ml of \nwater and 5 ml of hydrochloric acid, boil, filter immediately \nand wash with 25 ml of hot water. Add dilute ammonia \nsolution to make the filtrate just alkaline and then add \nammonium sulphate solution in excess to precipitate the zinc \nas zinc sulphide completely. Filter, add 0.5 ml of sulphuric \nacid to the. filtrate, evaporate to dryness and ignite to constant \nweight; the residue weighs not more than 20 mg.\nSulphates (2.3.17). To 0;25 g add amixture of 10 ml of distilled \nwu/er and 5 ml of 2 M hydrochloric, acid. Cool, filter and \ndilute to 20 ml with distilled water. The resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for sulphates (600 ppm).. ..\nDegree of unsaturation. Dissolve 0.1 gin amixture of 5 ml of \n2 M hydrochloric acid and 30 ml of glacial àcètic acid and \ntitrate with 0.05 'Mbrohiine using 0.05 ml of ethoxychrysoidine \nhydrochloride solution, added towards the end of the titration, \nas indicator. Not less than 9.1 ml arid hot more than 9.4 ml of \n0,05 M bromine is required to discharge the fed colour.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 1.5 per cent, determined \non 0.5 g by drying in an oven at 105°. .\nAssay. Weigh 0.35 g, add 25 ml of 2 M acetic acid,h&at to \nboiling, cool arid dilute to 50 ml with water. Add about 50 mg \nof xylenol orange triturate and sufficient hexamine to produce \na violet-pink colour. Add a further 2 g of hexdmine arid titrate \nwith 0.1 Mdisodium erie/qte until the colour changes to. yellow.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.04319 g of\nStorage: Store protected from light and moisture. ;\nZinc Undecenoate Ointment\nZinc Undecylenate Ointment\nZinc Undecenoate Ointment contains 20 per cent w/v of Zinc \nUndecenoate in a suitable ointment basis. .......\nZinc Undecenoate Ointment contains not less than 18.0 per \ncent and not more than 22.0 per cent of zinc undecenoate, \nC22H3gO4Zn, and not less than 4.5 per cent and not more than \n5.5 per cent of free undecenoic acid, C 11 lUCb-.. . ;\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated wider Ointments.\nAssay. For Zinc Undecenoate — Weigh 2.0 g, add 20 ml of \ndilute hydrochloric acid and boil under a reflux condenser \nfor at least 20 minutes or until the fatty layer is clear. Filter \nwhile hot and wash the residue with hot water. Cool the\n3995\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZINC UNDECENOATE OINTMENT\ncombined filtrate and; washings, ¿neutralise to litmus paper \nwith dilute ammonia solution, add 3 ml of dilute hydrochloric \nacid and 5 g of hexamine and titrate with .0.05 M disodium\nusing xylenol orange solution as indicator, until the \ncolour changes to yellow. , \n. U.\n1 ml of 0.05 Mdisodium edetate is equivalentto 0.02160 g of \nCjifljsCXiZn. \n.. \n• ■\" '■<\nFor free uhdecenoic acid Weigh 5.0 g, add 100 ml of dilute \nhydrochloric acid and heat to 70° with constant stirring. Cool \nand transfer to a separator with the aid of fo ur q uantities, each \nof 25 ml of ether and add the rinsings to the mixture in the \nseparator. Dilute the aqueous phase to 300 ml, saturate it with \nsodium chloride and\" shake the mixture. Transfer the aqueous \nlayer to a second separator and extract with another 100 mhof \nether. Wash the combined ether, extracts with successive \nquantities, each of 10 ml, of water until the washings are free \nfrom chloride. Transfer the ether solution to a beaker and \nevaporate on a water-bath to about 5 ml. Add 20 ml oicarbon \ntetrachloride, mix, transfer the mixture to a small separator \nand drain the carbon, tetrachloride layer into a 100-ml \nvolumetric flask. Rinse the beaker with three quantities, each \nof 5 ml, of carbon tetrachloride and transfer the rinsings to \nthe volumetric flask, dilute to volume with carbon tetrachloride \nand mix. Evaporate 50.0 ml of the resulting solution to about \n5 ml, add 100 ml of ethanol (95 per cent),■ previously \nneutralised, and .0.15 ml oiphenolphthalein solutionand titrate \nthe total undecenoic acid with 0,1 M sodium hydroxide.\n1 ml of O.'l M sodium 'hydroxide is equivalent to 0.01843 g of \nC’lfhoO?. \n•• \n, ■ '\nCalculate the content of free undecenoic acid from the \ndifference between the total undecenoic acid and the \nundecenoic acid equivalent to the determined zinc \nundecenoate (the content of zinc undecenoate multiplied by \n0.8533 gives the equivalent of undecenoic acid).\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nZiprasidone Hydrochloride \nMonohydrate\n■ - ' ■'' ' ; ;\n- HCt’ H2O\n.. \n/x. J\\k J . \n........................\nC25H22CI2N4OSJ l?O :S\\>; \nMol. Wt. 467.4\n3996 \n■■■■lllla\nZiprasidone Hydrochloride Monohydrate is 5-[2-[4-(i2; \nBenzisothiazol-3-yl)piperazin-1 -yl]ethyl]-6-chloro-I k \ndihydroindoE2(7V)-one hydrochloride monohydrate. ■ ■■\nZiprasidone Hydrochloride Monohydratc contains hofrli^ \nthan 98.0 per cent and not more than 102.0 per cental \nC21H22CI2N4OS, calculated on the anhydrous basis: ■\nCategory.Neuroleptic.\nDescription. A white or slightly pink powder. It shows \npolymorphism(2.5.11). \n1r ' \n■\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.i4). \n' \nA / \ni ''Mi!' S\nNOTE — Carry out the test protectedfrom light, andprepare \nsolutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture A. 40 volumes of water and 60 volumes of \nmethanol.\nSolvent mixture B. 0.04 volume of hydrochloric acid, \nvolumes of water and 80 volumes of methanol. .;. . y\nTest solution fa). Dissolve 23 mg of the substance ¿under \nexamination in solvent mixture A and dilute to 100.0 ml with \nsolvent mixture A. ■ \n■ h..-^ ■ u-\nTest solution (b). Dissolve 23 mg of the substance under \nexamination in solvent mixture B and dilute to 50.0 ml with \nsolvent mixture B. \nL\" ~ f'.........\nReference,solut,iop.r,t)\\\\nlQ.. 1.0 ml of test solution (b) to. 100.Q.\nml with solvent mixture B. Dilute "}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-oxide-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Oxide Cream"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Oxide Cream\nZinc Cream\nZinc Oxide Cream contains 32 per cent w/v ofZinc Oxide in a\nsuitable water-in-oil emulsified base.\nZinc Oxide Cream contains not less than 30.0 per cent and not\nmore than 34.0 per cent w/w ofzinc oxide, ZnO."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zinc Oxide Cream\nZinc Cream\nZinc Oxide Cream contains 32 per cent w/v ofZinc Oxide in a\nsuitable water-in-oil emulsified base.\nZinc Oxide Cream contains not less than 30.0 per cent and not\nmore than 34.0 per cent w/w ofzinc oxide, ZnO."},{"name":"Identification","text":"The residue obtained in the Assay is yellow when hot and\nwhite when cool."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Other tests. Complies with the tests stated under Creams.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.5 g in a porcelain dish, heat\ngently overa smallflame until the base is completely volatilised\nor charred. Increase the heat until all the carbon is removed.\nDissolve the residue in 10 ml of 2 M acetic acid and add\nsufficient water to produce 50 ml. To the resulting solution\nadd about 50 mg of xylenol orange triturate and sufficient\nhexamine to produce violet-pink colour. Add a further 2 g of\nnexamineandtitrate with O:IM disodiilmedeti:tte~untilthe\nsolution becomes yellow.\n1ml of0.1 M disodiumedetate is equivalent to 0.008138 g of\nZnO.\n2336\nIP 2010\nZINC SULPHATE"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-oxide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Oxide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Oxide\nÀi-\nZnO\nMol. Wt 81.4\nZinc Oxide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not ■ ;'f' \nmore than 100.5 per cent of ZnO, calculated on the ignited a \nbasis. . \n; \n;\nCategory. Mild astringent; topical protectant? 1 \n; ‘; \n‘\nDescription. A soft, white or faintly yellowish white \namorphous powder, free from grittiness. It gradually absorbs \ncarbon dioxide from air. \n: A"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zinc Oxide\nÀi-\nZnO\nMol. Wt 81.4\nZinc Oxide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not ■ ;'f' \nmore than 100.5 per cent of ZnO, calculated on the ignited a \nbasis. . \n; \n;\nCategory. Mild astringent; topical protectant? 1 \n; ‘; \n‘\nDescription. A soft, white or faintly yellowish white \namorphous powder, free from grittiness. It gradually absorbs \ncarbon dioxide from air. \n: A"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A.' It becomes yellow when strongly heated; the yellow colour\ndisappears on cooling.\nB. Dissolve 0.1 g in 1,5 ml of 2 M hydrochloric acid and di lute\nto 5 ml with water. The solution gives the reaction of zinc salts\nTests au-Kx R.w: \n.A'. AÁA\"\n■ -A <- AAAAíÍÜ-A-\nAlkalinity. Shake 1.0 g with 10 ml of boiling water, addQ.1 fl“\nofphenolphthalein solution and filter. If the filtrate is red, not\nmore than 0.3 ml of 0.1 M hydrochloric acid: \nto\ndischarge the'colour.\" ' \n■\nJ i’/ i / -.A i ’. i'7 ¿ A ; í.í'a Í ; l r. :;■!■ ■'' i ; • Ai/O \n.A ;. .j \n^i-‘ _\nCarbonate and substances insoluble in acids. Dissolve Lüg\nin 15 ml of 2 M hydrochloric acid; no,,effervescence 1S\nproduced and the solution,is not more opalgsjcgnf than\nopalescence standard OS2 (2.4.1), and colourless £244)•\n3992\nwww.webofpharma.com\n^(2.3.10). Dissolve 2.0 g in 15 ml of brominated\n'and 45 ml of water and remove the\n■\n•^^^fWoinihe-5 with a few drops of stannous chloride \n^^^ffS/’-The resulting -solution complies with the limit \n^Ibr arsenic(5 ppm). ■ \n• ’\n4); Dissolve 0.1 g in a mixture of 5 ml of water and \nacidanA dilute to 40 ml with water. The \nresultingsolution complies with the limit test for iron \nMflOppm). Use 0.5 ml of thioglycoIlic acid in the test. ; :\nDissolve 2 g in a mixture of 20 ml water and 5 ml of\nAcetic acid and add 0.25 ml 6i potassium chromate\nsolution; the solution remains clear.\nl^Mignitibn (2.4.20). Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined \nM®^fey igniting at 500°.\n¿ssay^issolye 0.15 g in 10 ml of 2 Macetic acid and dilute to \n50i^jyith water. To the resulting solution add about, 50 mg of\nxylenol orange triturate and sufficient hexamine to produce \nviolet-pink colour. Add a further 2 g of hexamine and titrate \nwith Oil M disodium edetate uptil the solution becomes yellow.\nZnQ. j ,\nI ml of 0.7 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.008138 g of\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nZinc Oxide Cream\nZmq'Cream \n‘ ’\nThe residue obtained in the Assay is yellow when hot and \nwhite when cool."},{"name":"Tests","text":"ZiricQxide Cream contains 32 per cent w/w of Zinc Oxide in a \nsuitable water-in-oil emulsified base. \n-\nZinc Oxide Cream contains not less than 30.0 per cent and not \nmore than 34.0 per cent w/w ofzinc oxide, ZnO.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Creams.\nAssay. Weigh 0.5 g in a porcelain dish, heat gently over a \nsmall flame until the base is completely volatilised or charred, \nhcrease the heat until all the carbon is removed. Dissolve the \nresidue in 16 ml of 2 M acetic acid woAadd sufficient water to \nproduce 50 nil. To the resulting solution add about 50 mg of \nxylenol orange triturate and sufficient hexamine to produce \nviolet-pink colour. Add a further 2 g of hexamine and titrate \nwith 0.1 Mdisodium edetate until the solution becomes yellow.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.00813 8 g of\nZnQ. ■' ,. .. . \n■. \n■. ■ ■ ■\nZINC OXIDE AND SALICYLIC ACID PASTE"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-stearate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Stearate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Stearate\nZn \t\nMol. Wt. 632.3\nZinc Stearate consists mainly of zinc stearate but many \ncontain variable proportions of zinc palmitate (CI5H3IC00)2 \nZn, and zinc oleate (C I,H33C00)2Zn.\nZinc Stearate contains not less than 10.0 per cent and not \nmore than 12.0 per cent of zinc, Zn.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (dusting powder).\nDescription. A fine, white, bulky, amorphous powder, free from \ngrittiness; odour, faint and characteristic."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zinc Stearate\nZn \t\nMol. Wt. 632.3\nZinc Stearate consists mainly of zinc stearate but many \ncontain variable proportions of zinc palmitate (CI5H3IC00)2 \nZn, and zinc oleate (C I,H33C00)2Zn.\nZinc Stearate contains not less than 10.0 per cent and not \nmore than 12.0 per cent of zinc, Zn.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (dusting powder).\nDescription. A fine, white, bulky, amorphous powder, free from \ngrittiness; odour, faint and characteristic."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. To 5.0 g add 50 ml of ether and 40 ml of a 7.5 per cent v/v \nsolution of nitric acid in distilled water and heat under a \nreflux condenser until dissolution is complete. Allow to cool, \nseparate the aqueous layer and shake the ether layer with two \nquantities, each of 4 ml, of distilled water. Combine the \nwashings with the aqueous layer, wash with 15 ml of ether \nand heat on a water-bath until ether is completely eliminated. \nAllow to cool and dilute to 50.0 ml with distilled water (solution \nA). Evaporate the ether layer to dryness and dry the residue \nat 105°. The freezing point of the residue is not lower than 53° \n(2.4.11).\nB. Neutralise 5 ml of solution A to red litmus paper with 10 M \nsodium hydroxide.  The solution gives the reactions of zinc\nsalts (2.3.1).\narsenic (2 ppm).\nA.A 5 per cent w/v solution of the substance under examination \nin carbon dioxide-free water  (solution A) gives the reactions \nof sulphates (2.3.1).\nChlorides (2.3.12). 20 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for chlorides (250 ppm).\nHeavy metals  (2.3.13). Heat 5.0 g with 40 ml of 2 Macetic acid \nand allow to cool. Filter, wash the residue with two successive \nquantities, each of 5 ml, of warm water and dilute the combined\nAssay. Dissolve 0.5 g in 5 ml of 2 Macetic acid and dilute to \n50 ml with water. To the resulting solution add about 50 mg of \nxylenol orange triturate and sufficient hexamine to produce \nviolet-pink colour. Further, add 2 g of hexamine and titrate \nwith 0.1 Mdisodium edetate until the colour changes to yellow.\nB. Solution A gives the reaction of zinc (2.3.1).\nfiltrate and washings to 100.0 ml with  water. 12 ml of the solution \ncomplies with the limit test for heavy metals, Method D \n(20 ppm). Use 1.0 ml of  lead standard solution (10 ppm Pb) to \nprepare the standard."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. Solution A is not more intensely \ncoloured than reference solution YS6 (2.4.1).\nAcidity or alkalinity. Shake 1.0 g with 5 ml of ethanol (95 per \ncent) and add 20 ml of carbon dioxide-free water and 0.1 ml of \nphenol red solution. Not more than 0.3 ml of 0.1 M\nhydrochloric acid or 0.1 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide is \nrequired to change the colour of the solution.\nAlkalis and alkaline earths. Add 1.0 g to a mixtpleof 25 Hof \nwater and 5 ml of hydrochloric acid, boil, tilterlienmediately \nand wash with 25 ml of hot water. Add dilute ammonia\n35-42\nZINC STEARATE \nIP 2018 \nII' 2018 \nZINC SULPHATE ORAL SOLUTION\nsolution to make the filtrate just alkaline and then add \nammonium sulphide solution in excess to precipitate the zi nc\nZinc Sulphate Monohvdrate\nZnSO4,H-20\nAppearance of solution. Solution A is clear (2.4.1), and \ncolourless (2.4.1).\n as zinc sulphide completely. Filter, ad 0.5 ml of \nsulphuric \nacid to the filtrate, evaporate to dryness and ignite to consta nt \nweight; the residue weighs not more than 20 mg. \n,qiie\nMol. Wt. 179.5\nZinc Sulphate Monohydrate contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of ZnSO 4,H20.\npH (2.4.24). 4.4 to 5.6, determined in solution A.\nArsenic (2.3.10). Mix 5.0 g with 10 ml of bromine solution and \nevaporate to dryness on a water-bath. Ignite gently, dissol ve\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 1.0 g in 50 ml of water and add 10 ml \nof Atannated hydrochloric acid AsT. The resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for arsenic (10 ppm).\nCategory. Astringent.\n the coled residue, ignoring any carbon, in 50 ml of \n water and \n14 ml of brominated hydrochloric acid AsT and remove the\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or \ncolourless, transparent crystals.\n exces of bromine with 2 ml of \nstannous chloride solution \nAsT. The resulting solution complies with the limit test for\nIron (2.3.14). Dissolve 0.4 g in 20 ml of water. The resulting \nsolution complies with the limit test for iron (100 ppm).\nAppearance of solution.  Solution A is clear (2.4.1) and \ncolourless (2.4.1).\nChlorides (2.3.12). 10 ml of solution A complies with the limit \ntest for chlorides (250 ppm).\n1 ml of 0.1 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.02875 g of \nZnSO4, 7H20.\npH  (2.4.24). 4.4 to 5.6 for solution A.\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 0.83 g in 15.0 ml of water. The \nsolution complies with the limit test for chlorides (300 ppm).\nSulphates (2.3.17). Dilute 1 ml of solution A to 50 ml with \nwater. Dilute 12.5 ml of this solution to 15 ml with water. It \ncomplies with the limit test for sulphates (0.6 per cent).\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, in non-metallic \ncontainers.\nIron (  2.3.14). Dilute 8 ml of solution A to 10 ml with water \nusing 0.5 ml of thioglycollic acid. The solution complies with \nthe limit test for iron (100 ppm).\nAssay. Weigh 1.0 g and boil with 50 ml of 2 Macetic acid until \nthe fatty acid layer which separates is clear, adding more water \nif necessary to maintain the original volume. Cool, filter and \nwash the filter and the flask thoroughly with water until the\nAssay. Dissolve 0.3 g in 5 ml of dilute acetic acid into a 500-m1 \nconical flask and add 200 ml of water. Add about 500 mg of \nxylenol orange triturate and hexamine until the solution \nbecomes violet-pink; add 2 g of hexamine in excess. Titrate \nwith 0.1 M disodium edetate until the violet-pink colour \nchanges to yellow.\nZinc Sulphate Eye Drops\nlast washing is not acidic to blue litmus paper. To the combined \nfiltrate and washings add about 50 mg of xylenol orange \ntriturate and sufficient hexamine  to produce violet-pink \ncolour. Add a further 2 g of hexamine and titrate with Od M \ndisodium edetate until the colour changes to yellow.\nZinc Sulphate Eye Drops are a sterile solution containing \n0.25 per cent w/v of Zinc Sulphate in Purified Water.\nZinc Sulphate Eye Drops contain not less than 0.22 per cent \nand not more than 0.28 per cent w/v of zinc sulphate, ZnSO 4, \n7H20.\n1 ml of 0.1 Mdisodium  edetate is equivalent to 0.01795 g of \nZnSO4,H20.\n1 ml of 03 M disodium edetate  is equivalent to 0.00654 g of \nZn.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\nStorage.  Store in a non-metal I ic container."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-sulphate-monohydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate\npH (2.4.24). 2.5 to 4.5.\nZnSO4,H2O \nMol. Wt. 179.5\nZinc Sulphate Monohydrate contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of ZnSO4,H2O.\nCategory. Astringent.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or \ncolourless, transparent crystals."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate\npH (2.4.24). 2.5 to 4.5.\nZnSO4,H2O \nMol. Wt. 179.5\nZinc Sulphate Monohydrate contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of ZnSO4,H2O.\nCategory. Astringent.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or \ncolourless, transparent crystals."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A-A5 per cent w/v solution of the substance under examination\nm carbon dioxide-free water (solution A) gives the reactions \nofsulphates (2.3.1).\nD-Solution A gives the reaction of zinc (2.3.1)."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Appearance of solution. Solution A is clear (2.4.1) and\nc°lourless (2.4.1).\nPH (2.4.24). 4.4 to 5.6 for solution A.\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 0.83 g in 15.0 ml of water. The \nelution complies with the limit test for chlorides (300 ppm).\nIron (2.3.14). Dilute 8 ml of solution A to 10 ml with water \nusing 0.5 ml of thioglycollic acid. The solution complies with \nthe limit test for iron (100 ppm).\nAssay. Dissolve 0.3 g in 5 ml of dilute acetic acid into a 500-ml \nconical flask and add 200 ml of water. Add about 500 mg of \nxylenol orange triturate and hexamine until the solution\nbecomes violet-pink; add 2 g of hexamine in excess. Titrate \nwith 0.1 M disodium edetate until the violet-pink colour \nchanges to yellow.\n1 ml of 0.1 M disodium edetate is equivalent to 0.01795 g of \nZnSO4,H2O.\nStorage. Store in a non-metallic container."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-sulphate-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Sulphate Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Sulphate Oral Solution\nZinc Sulphate Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nzinc sulphate monohydrate, Zn SO4.H2O. It may contain one \nor more suitable flavors and sweeteners.\nA. Complies with the test for sulphates (2.3.1).\nB. Complies with the test for zinc (2.3.1).\nWeight per ml (2.4.29). 1.18 g to 1.24 g.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Oral Liquids.\nAssay. To a 250-ml flask, transfer a volume of oral solution \ncontaining about 99 mg of Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate, add \n50 ml of water and 10 ml of ammonia- ammonium chloride \nbuffer and 0.3 ml of eriochrome black Tsolution. Titrate with \n0.05 Mdisodium edetate to a green colour.\n1 ml of 0.05 Mdisodiulm edetate is equivalent to 0.008973 g \nofZnSO4.H2O.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nLabelling. Label the oral solution in terms of zinc sulphate \nmonohydrate and in terms of elemental zinc.\nZinc Sulphate Dispersible Tablets\nDispersible Zinc Sulphate Tablets\nZinc Sulphate Dispersible Tablets contain not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated\n4135\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZINC SULPHATE DISPERSIBLE TABLETS\namount of zinc sulphate monohydrate, ZnSO4)H2O. It may \ncontain one or more suitable flavours and sweeteners.\nUsual strengths. 10 mg; 20 mg of elemental zinc (as zinc \nsulphate monohydrate)."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zinc Sulphate Oral Solution\nZinc Sulphate Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nzinc sulphate monohydrate, Zn SO4.H2O. It may contain one \nor more suitable flavors and sweeteners.\nA. Complies with the test for sulphates (2.3.1).\nB. Complies with the test for zinc (2.3.1).\nWeight per ml (2.4.29). 1.18 g to 1.24 g.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Oral Liquids.\nAssay. To a 250-ml flask, transfer a volume of oral solution \ncontaining about 99 mg of Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate, add \n50 ml of water and 10 ml of ammonia- ammonium chloride \nbuffer and 0.3 ml of eriochrome black Tsolution. Titrate with \n0.05 Mdisodium edetate to a green colour.\n1 ml of 0.05 Mdisodiulm edetate is equivalent to 0.008973 g \nofZnSO4.H2O.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nLabelling. Label the oral solution in terms of zinc sulphate \nmonohydrate and in terms of elemental zinc.\nZinc Sulphate Dispersible Tablets\nDispersible Zinc Sulphate Tablets\nZinc Sulphate Dispersible Tablets contain not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated\n4135\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZINC SULPHATE DISPERSIBLE TABLETS\namount of zinc sulphate monohydrate, ZnSO4)H2O. It may \ncontain one or more suitable flavours and sweeteners.\nUsual strengths. 10 mg; 20 mg of elemental zinc (as zinc \nsulphate monohydrate)."},{"name":"Identification","text":"Solution A. Shake a quantity of powdered tablets containing \nabout 5 g of Zinc Sulphate with 100 ml of water and filter. The \nfiltrate complies with the following tests.\nA; To 5 ml of solution A, add 1 mleach of 20 per cent w/w \nsolution of hydrochloric acid and barium chloride solution. \nA white precipitate is formed.\nB. To 5 ml of solution A, add 0.1 ml of 10 Msodium hydroxide. \nA white precipitate is formed. Further add 2 ml of 10 Msodium \nhydroxide to dissolve the precipitate. Add 10 ml of ammonium \nchloride solution and the solution remains clear. Add 0.1 ml \nof sodium sulphide solution. Awhile precipitate is formed.\n4136 \ng—WllliMa\n........_.......... ................................ .....\nA. Macroscopic — It is a greyish-brown powder. —\nB; Microscopic — It reveals cells of the upper and lower \nepidermis of the leaves, some cells containing large cystoliths, \nup to 36 pm in diameter and 180 pm long, with a hilum-shaped \nscar in the large end; 1 -4 celled non glandular hair; disk-shaped \nglandular hairs, 8 celled head and very short stalk; diacytic \nstomata mostly on the lower epidermis; stem epidermal cells, \nsome cells containing cystoliths, stomata, non glandular hair \nand glandular hair similar to those of the leave; thin-walled\n4142\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCURCUMA LONGA PPI\n(pW2\nrenchyrna cells; collenchyma cells, acicular phloem fibres;\nIsseis; with spiral and scalariform thickening:\ng:\nJ \njayer chromatography (2.4.17), coating the plate with\nO^:gaGF^-\ntfdfalephase. A mixture of 6 volumes of chloroform, 2.5\nVoSSne® of toluene and 1.5 volumes of methanol.\n^¡solution. Sonicate about 0.5 g of powdered substance \nHBd^exarhihation with 10 ml of methanol for 30 minutes and \nfilter iSohicate the residue further 2 x 10 ml of methanol and \niiiiin Combine all the filtrates and concentrate under vacuum \ntoSW\nReference solution. Sonicate about 0.5 g of Andrographis \npaniculata IPBRS with 10 ml of methanol for 30 minutes and\nfilter.Sonicate the residue further 2 x 10 ml of methanol and \nfilter.Combine all the filtrates and concentrate under vacuum\nAbf-'L... ' \nor\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of \nandrographolide IPRS, neoandrographolide IPRS, 14 \ndeoxy-H, 12-didehydroandrographolide IPRS and \nandrograpanin IPRS in methanol.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution as bands of 8 mm by \n2 min. Allow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air \nand examine under ultraviolet light at 254 nm and 366 nm, \nspray with vanillin sulphuric acid reagent. Heat the plate at\n105°: for 5 minutes and examine in day light. The \nchromatographic profile of the test solution is similar to that \nof the reference solution.\nTests .\nForeign organic matter (2.6.1), Not more than 2.0 per cent.\nEthanol-soluble extractive (2.6.2). Not less than 3.0 per cent.\nWater-soluble extractive (2.6.3). Not less than 12.0 per cent \nby Method I.\nTotal ash (2.3 . 19). Not more than 15.0 per cent.\nAcid-insoluble ash (2.3.19). Not more than 3.0 per cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nToss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 12.0 per cent, \ndetenninedon 5.0 g by drying in an oven at 1,05°.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Complies with the microbial\ncontamination tests.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\n^st solution. Sonicate about 0.5 g of powdered substance\nunder examination with 10 ml of methanol for 30 minutes and \nfilter Sonicate the residue further 2 x 10 ml of methanol and\nfilter. Combine all the filtrates and concentrate under vacuum \nto 5.0 ml.\nReference solution: A solution containing 0:1 per cent w/v, \neach of' andrographolide IPRS, neoandrographolide IPRS, \n14-deoxy-l 1,12-didehydroandrographolide IPRS and \nandrograpanin IPRS in methanol..\nChromatographic system \\ \n.\n- a stainless steel column 25cm-^4.6 mm packed with\noctadecylsilarie bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n: \nmobile phasei A. a buffer solution dissolving 0.136 g of\n’ ' potassium ^fhyd’roge/? orthophosphate in 500 ml of\n__ water and-0^ \nacid, dilute to\n• 1000 ml withVWer,\nB. acetôhtâiïëfêC.C\n•••-, flow rate: 1 ml per minute, :\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- spectrophotometer set at 223 nm,\n'••• injection volume: 10 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v) ■\n0\n95\n5 ' ' 7 '' ' \" '\n18\n55\n45\n25\n20\n80\n28\n20 ;\n80 \n..\n40\n95\n5'' Olli\n35\n55\n' 45' '\n45\n95\n•5.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of diterpene lactones, calculated on the \ndried basis as the sum of the andrographolide, \nneoandrographolide, 14-deoxy-l 1,12-didehydroandro- \ngrapholide and andrograpanin. \n;\nStorage. Store protected froth heat and moisture.\nCurcuma longa PPI\nCurcuma longa Linn.\nMarker Compounds. Curcumin, Desmethoxycurcumin, bis- \nDesmcthoxycurcumin and ar-turmcrone.\nSource. Curcuma Longa PPI is derived from dried root parts \nof the Curcuma longa Linn. (Fam. Zingeberaceae). It contains \nnot less than 1.0 per cent w/w of curcùminoids calculated on\n4143\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCURCUMA LONGA; PPI\nthe dried basis as the sum of curcumin, desmethoxycurcumin, \nbis-desmethoxycurcumin and ar-turmerone.\nCategory. Antioxidant,- antibacterial, antifungal,\nantiinflammatory, platelet aggregation inhibitor, cytotoxic, \nantiallergic, hypotriglyceridemic, enterokinase inhibitor, CNS \ndepressant, analgesic.\nSource description. Curcuma Longa Linn, is a stout \nunderground rhizomatous aromatic herb. Leaves die down in \nlate autumn and the rhizomes remain dormant during winter. \nThe inflorescence again becomes visible in spring from the \nrhizome. The peduncle grows to 20-25 cm in height. Foliage \nappears after the emergence of the flowers. A full grown plant \ncan reach upto height of 90 cm.\nA. Macroscopic — Rhizomes of Curcuma Longa Linn, are \novate to oblong or pyrifor"},{"name":"Tests","text":"' \n'\nDisintegration (2,5,1), Not more than 60 seconds;5\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated, under Tablets\nAssay. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Dissolve a quantity of \nthe powder containing about 90 mg of zinc in 15 mi of \nacetic acid and dilute to 200 ml with water. Add I gdfxy/eno/ \norange triturate,mix and neutralize the solution with about \n2 g of hexamine until the solution is a violet-pink colour.Titrate \nwith 0.1 M disodium edetate until the solution is yellow. -\n1 ml of 0.1 M disodium edetate is equivalent to 0.01795 g of \nZnSO4s H2O,\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not \nexceeding 30°. \n.’¿v/\nLabelling. Label the tablets in terms of zinc sulphate \nmonohydrate and in terms of elemental zinc, ( /\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIBwW PHARMACOPOEIA 2022\nPHYTOPHARMACEUTICALS\nGeneral Requirements\nti 1\nMonographs\nAegiemarmelos PPI\nAndro graphis paniculata PPI\nCureuinalongaPPi\nGymnema sylvestre PPI\nLawsonia inermis PPI\nSilybum marianum Dry Extract PPI\ndï Idïii; i\" .■ \nidügïdd '\n;= chuv-\n-idüidd: ■\nûf ;ï; !dr : \ndÎS;:^<\n■sscrd.:\\i ■\n-J ¿d;.-\nS<h-\nadny pi:.\n■;itydpd:: '\n'Wî'^ï;- \nidcuv\nd Wd: ■\nmonographs\n4139\n4141\n4142\n4143\n4145\n4146\n4147\n4149\n4137\nwww.webofpharma.com\n■■ ■■ ■■\n■■■ ¿s”? .i í;,¿?í<;\n■;-;VÜ a qLíí;;::\n': \n,hj; i\n71W»\n*>W\n-^;:4a<dgS-g<>ibr;Á\n■■ z- yÁH7A.'.: : .. . .\n^ról.finuAAHj-\n:;--V7X4'«í&Sfí.A:z-)\n''' : • \ní\n■ -AZC .\n-nfiff; A!ÍzJ/:;7\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIR 202?\njhe concept of science-based phytopharmaceutical drugs was \nfjist introduced through a draft amendment notified to the \nppjgs and Cosmetics Act 1940, and Rules 1945 there under \n¿¿24$ October 2013. This notification introduced a definition\nfor Botanical drugs for scientific evaluation for quality safety\nand efficacy bÿ thé office of Central Drugs Standard and \nControl Organisation (CDSCO) as a requirement for issue of \nmarketing authorization. Botanical drugs were given a legal\nterni as ‘Thytopharrhaceutical” and the notification proposed\nt0 define it as '‘Phytopharmaceutical drug includes processed \norfiinprbeessed standardized materials derived from plants\n01- parts thereof or combination of parts of plants, extracts or \nfractions thereof in a dosage form for internal or external\nuse of human beings or animals and intended to:be used for \ndiagnosis,treatment, mitigation dr prevention of any disease \nor disorder in human beings or animals, but does not include \nadministration by parenteral route”.' Subsequently based\non stakeholder inputs the definition of phytopharmaceutical\nwas ametided in 2015 and subsequently in 2019. Currently the \n¿ntire regulations related to phytopharmaceuticals as drugs\nhas been brought under Chapter 1 of subsection (aa) of New\nDrugs and-ClinicalTrials Rules, 2018 (ref: GS.R; 227 (E), dated\n19th“March 2019). The regulatory requirements for \nphytopharmaceutical drug are under the purview of CDSCO\nas it differs' from Ayurveda, Siddha or Unani drugs which \ninclude all medicines intended for internal or external use for \nor in the'diagnosis, treatment, mitigation or prevention of \n(disease or disorder in human beings or animals, and \nmanufactured) exclusively in accordance with the formulae \ndescribed in, the authoritative books of Ayurveda, Siddha \nand Unani and other systems of medicine specified in the \nFirst Schedule. However, phytopharmaceutical drug are \nfraction of crude extract and are distinctly differentiated by \nbeing purified and standardized. A separate category of \nPhytopharmaceutical Ingredient (PPI) monograph has been \nincluded in IP where, an herbal drug/extract/purified fraction \nhas been characterized with minimum of four marker\ncompounds. However, such PPI monographs may or may not \nsftictly meet the requirements of definition of \nPhytopharmaceutical drugs.\nCurrent regulations define “Phytopharmaceutical drugs \ndeludes a purified and standard fraction with defined\nMinirhum four bio-active or phyto-chemical compounds \n(ffdliiatively and quantitatively assessed), of an extract of\na medicinal plant or its part, for internal or external use on\nbeings or animals, for diagnosis, treatment, mitigation \nOfPPfyention of any disease or disorder but does not include\nadministered through parenteral route”.\nPHYTOPHARMACEUTICALS\nThis general chapter provides, additional, information and \nguidance to researchers, innovators, and industry on \nPhytopharmaceutical.,As per definition:f..\n— One step extracts of botanicals (with clear botanical\nidentity and part /parts of the plants) involving vehicle/ \nsolvents such as: water (maceration, infusion,'decoction, \nconcentrated decoction, soft extracts, and such extracts \ntechnologically converted to powder formats), solvents\nsuch as ethanol, ethanol: water mixtures- of predefined \nratios (liquid extract, technologically converted to powder \nform do not meet the requirement of phytopharmaceutical \nas defined above).\n— Fixed oils extracted from botanicals either by cold pressing\nor by solvent extraction or a mix of both do not meet the \nrequirement of phytopharmaceutical as defined above.\n— Distilled oils of volatile components of botanicals dp not\nmeet the requirement of phytopharmaceutical as defined \nabove. .\n— Single step oleo-resin extracts, gums, dried latexes do not\nmeet the requirement of phytopharmaceutical as defined \nabove.\n— Extracts obtained by traditional process involving, other\n. solvents such as vegetable oil, clarified butter, milk do\nnot meet the (requirement of phytopharmaceutical as \ndefined above.: \n,\n— Steps involving more than brie solvent for fractionation,\npurification adopting suitable technologies, and such \nprocessed purified fractions subject to complying with \nother parts of the definition meet the requirement for the \nphytopharmaceutical viz. at least 2 or more fractionation\nsteps involved. Fractionated fatty acids from vegetable \noils, fractionated distilled oils, would meet the first \nrequirement to be a phytopharmaceutical. The extent of \nfractionation and purification may vary depending on \nthe"}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Sulphate\nZnSO4,7H2O\nMol. Wt. 287.5\nZinc Sulphate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 104.0 per cent of ZnSO4,7H2O. ■\nCategory. Astringent. \n' \n'( \n'\nDescription. Colourless, transparent crystals or a white, \ncrystalline powder; efflorescent \n. ,"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zinc Sulphate\nZnSO4,7H2O\nMol. Wt. 287.5\nZinc Sulphate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 104.0 per cent of ZnSO4,7H2O. ■\nCategory. Astringent. \n' \n'( \n'\nDescription. Colourless, transparent crystals or a white, \ncrystalline powder; efflorescent \n. ,"},{"name":"Identification","text":"; !i?;\nDissolve .2.5. g in. sufficient .carbon dioxide^free. water \nproduce 50 ml (solution A). Solution A gives the reactions pl\nzinc salts and sulphates (2.3.1). \n.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZINC SULPHATE DISPERSIBLE TABLETS\n............. ....................-........ -......... -.......—.\nrests\nnearance of solution. Solution A is clear (2.4.1), and \niM2:4'1’- \n..\ni®» ;4.4to 5.6, determined in solution A.\nijSnic^3-W)- Dissolve 1.0 g in 50 ml of water and add 10 ml\nhydrochloric acid AsT. The resulting solution \n¿^¡i^siwith the limit test for arsenic (10 ppm).\n^0^2^14): Dissolve 0.4 g in 20 ml of water. The resulting \nsoiui^'dbihplies with the limit test for iron (100 ppm).\n^ofides'(2.3; 12). 20 ml of solution A complies with the limit \nKStfbrchlondes (250 ppm).\nA$say>Dissolve 0.5 g in 5 ml of 2 M acetic acid and dilute to\n50 ml with water. To the resulting solution add about 50 mg of \nxy/etial orange triturate and sufficient hexamine to produce\ncolour. Further, add 2 g of hexamine and titrate\nwith (U Mdisodium edetate until the colour changes to yellow.\nXtAofO.l M disodium edetate is equivalent to 0.02875 g of \nZnS04,7H20.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, in non-metallic \ncontainers."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-undecenoate-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Undecenoate Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Undecenoate Ointment\nZinc Undecylenate Ointment\nZinc Undecenoate Ointment contains 20 per cent w/v ofZinc\nUndecenoate in a suitable ointment basis.\nZinc Undecenoate Ointment contains not less than 18.0 per\ncent and not more than 22.0 per cent of zinc undecenoate,\nC22H3S04Zn, and not less than 4.5 per cent and not more than\n5,5 per cent offree undecenoic acid, CIIH2002."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zinc Undecenoate Ointment\nZinc Undecylenate Ointment\nZinc Undecenoate Ointment contains 20 per cent w/v ofZinc\nUndecenoate in a suitable ointment basis.\nZinc Undecenoate Ointment contains not less than 18.0 per\ncent and not more than 22.0 per cent of zinc undecenoate,\nC22H3S04Zn, and not less than 4.5 per cent and not more than\n5,5 per cent offree undecenoic acid, CIIH2002."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Other tests. Complies with the tests stated under Ointments.\nAssay. For zinc undecenoate -\nWeigh accurately about\n2.0 g, add 20 ml ofdilute hydrochloric acid and boil under a\nreflux condenser for at least 20 minutes or until the fatty layer\nis clear. Filter while hot and wash the residue with hot water.\nCool the combined filtrate and washings, neutralise to litmus\npaper with dilute ammonia solution, add 3 ml of dilute\nhydrochloric acid and 5 g of hexamine and titrate with\n0.05 M disodium edetate using xylenol orange solution as\nindicator, until the colour changes to yellow.\n1 mlof0.05 M disodium edetate is equivalent to 0.02160 g of\nC22H3S04Zn.\nFor ji-ee undecenoic acid\nWeigh accurately about 5.0 g,\nadd 100 ml of dilute hydrochloric acid and heat to 70° with\nconstant stirring. Cool and transfer to a separator with the aid\noffour quantities, each of25 ml ofether and add the rinsings\nto the mixture in the separator. Dilute the aqueous phase to\n300 ml, saturate it with sodium chloride and shake the mixture.\nTransfer the aqueous layer to a second separator and extract\nwith another 100 ml ofether. Wash the combined ether extracts\nwith successive quantities, each of 10 ml, of water until the\nwashings are free from chloride. Transfer the ether solution to\na beaker and evaporate on a water-bath to about 5 ml. Add\n20 ml of carbon tetrachloride, mix, transfer the mixture to a\n2339\nZOLEDRONIC ACID\nsmall separator and drain the carbon tetrachloride layer into\na 100-ml volumetric flask. Rinse the beaker with three\nquantities, each of 5 ml, ofcarbon tetrachloride and transfer\nthe rinsings to the volumetric flask, dilute to volume with\ncarbon tetrachloride and mix. Evaporate 50.0 ml ofthe resulting\nsolution to about 5 ml, add 100 ml of ethanol (95per cent),\npreviously neutralised, and 0.15 ml of phenolphthalein\nsolution and titrate the total undecenoic acid with\n0.1 M sodium hydroxide.\n1ml of0.1 M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.01843 g of\nCIIH2002.\nCalculate the content of free undecenoic acid from the\ndifference between the total undecenoic acid and the\nundecenoic acid equivalent to the determined zinc\nundecenoate (the content of zinc undecenoate multiplied by\n0.8533 gives the equivalent ofundecenoic acid).\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nZoledronic acid\nMol. Wt. 272.1\nZoledronicAcid is [1-hydroxy-2-(lH-imidazol-l-\nyl)ethylidene]diphosphonic acid.\nZoledronic Acid contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 102.0 per cent of CsHIQN20 7P2, calculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory. Bone resorption inhibitor.\nDescription. A white to almost white crystalline powder.\npH (2.4.24). 2.0 to 4.0, determinedina 0.02 percentw/v solution.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZOLEDRONIC ACID\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance uJider\nexamination in 50.0 m1 ofthe mobile phase.\nReference solution. Dilute 1 ml ofthe test solution to 100 ml\nwith the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 em x 4 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 /lm) (Such\nas Hypersil BDS),\n-\nmobile phase: mix 1ml oftriethylamine with 500 ml of\nwater, adjust the pH to 3.2 with orthophosphoric acid,\n-\nflow rate. 1ml per minute,\nspectrophotometer set at 215 nm,\ninjection volume. 20 Ill.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and\nthe tailil1g factor is not more than 2.0.\nInject the test solution and the reference solution. Run the\nchromatogram 3 times the retention time ofthe principal peak.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, area of\nany secondary peale is not more than the area ofthe principal\npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution\n(1.0 per cent). The sum ofareas ofall the secondary peaks is\nnot more than twice the area of the principal peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (2.0 per\ncent). Ignore any peak with an area less than 0.1 times the\narea of the peak in the chromatogram obtained with the\nreference solution (0.1 per cent).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19).5.0 to 8.0 per cent, determined on\n1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105° for 6 hours.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under\nexamination in 50.0 ml ofthe mobile phase. Dilute 20 ml ofthe\nsolution to 100 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution. A 0.02 per cent w/v solution ofzoledronic\nacidRS in the mobile phase.\nUse the chromatographic system as described under Related\nsubstances.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more\nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the test solution and the reference solution.\nCalculate-the-content-ofCsHloN2Q7P2.---------------------------\nStorage. Store protected froIIllight a.nd IIloisture.\nLabelling. The label states whether or not the material· is\nintended for use in the manufacture ofparenteral preparations.\nIP 2010"},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectmm with that obtained with zoledronic\nacidRS or with the reference spectrum ofzoledronic acid.\nB. In the assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-undecenoate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Undecenoate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Undecenoate\nCZZH3S04Zn\nMol. Wt. 431.9\nZinc Undecenoate is zinc di(undec-lO-enoate).\nIP 2010\nZinc Undecenoate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nnot more than 102.0 per cent ofCzzH3s04Zn, calculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory. Antifungal (topical).\nDescription. A white or almost white, fine powder."},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zinc Undecenoate\nCZZH3S04Zn\nMol. Wt. 431.9\nZinc Undecenoate is zinc di(undec-lO-enoate).\nIP 2010\nZinc Undecenoate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nnot more than 102.0 per cent ofCzzH3s04Zn, calculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory. Antifungal (topical).\nDescription. A white or almost white, fine powder."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. To 2.5 g add 10 ml ofwater and 10 ml of1 M sulphuric acid\nand extract with two quantities, each of10 ml, ofether. Reserve\nthe aqueous layer for test C. Wash the combined ether extracts\nwith water and evaporate to dryness. To the residue add 2 ml\nof freshly distilled aniline and boil under a reflux condenser\nfor 10 minutes, cool and add 30 ml ofether. Extract with three\nquantities, each of 20 ml, of 2 M hydrochloric acid and then\nwith 20 ml ofwater. Evaporate the ether extract to dryness on\na water-bath. The residue, after recrystallising twice from\nethanol (70 per cent) and drying at a pressure not exceeding\n2 kPa for 3 hours, melts at about 67° (2.4.21).\nB. Dissolve 0.1 g in a mixture of2 mLof1 Msulphuric acidand\n5 ml of glacial acetic acid and add, dropwise, 0.25 ml of\npotassium permanganate solution; the pink colour of\npermanganate is discharged.\nC. A mixture of 1ml ofthe aqueous layer reserved in testA and\n4 ml ofwater gives the reaction ofzinc salts (2.3.1).\nD. Melting range (2.4.21). 115°to 121°."},{"name":"Tests","text":"Alkalinity. Mix 1.0 g with 5 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and\n0.5 ml ofphenol red solution, add 50 ml of carbon dioxide-\nfree water and examine immediately; no reddish colour is\nproduced.\nAllmlis and alkaline earths. Add 1.0 g to a mixture of25 mlof\nwater and 5 ml ofhydrochloric acid, boil, filter immediately\nand wash with 25 ml of hot water. Add dilute ammonia\nsolution to make the filtrate just alkaline and then add\nammonium sulphate solution in excess to precipitate the zinc\nas zinc sulphide completely. Filter, add 0.5 ml of sulphuric\nacidto the filtrate, evaporate to dryness and ignite to constant\nweight; the residue weighs not more than 20 mg.\nSulphates (2.3.17). To 0.25 g add a mixture ofl0 rnl ofdistilled\nwater and 5 ml of 2 M hydrochloric acid. Cool, filter and\ndilute to 20 ml with distilled water. The resulting solution\ncomplies with the limit test for sulphates (600 ppm).\nDegree ofunsaturation. Dissolve 0.1 g in a mixture of5 mlof\n2Mhydrochloricacid and 30 ml of glCic.:ial aceticacid and\ntitrate wTth~0~05Mbro/;ZineusiniO.05ml ofethoXychrysoidine\nhydrochloride solution, added towards the end ClfthetihitiClll,\nas indicator. Not less than 9.1 ml and not more than 9.4 ml of\n0.05 M bromine is required to discharge the red colour.\n2338\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 1.5 percent, determined\non 0.5 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.35 g, add 25 m1 of2 M acetic\nacid, heat to boiling, cool and dilute to 50 ml with water. Add\nabout 50 mg of xylenol orange triturate and sufficient\nhexamine to produce a violet-pink colour. Add a further 2 g of\nhexamine and titrate with 0.1 M disodium edetate until the\ncolour changes to yellow.\n1 ml of 0.1 M disodium edetate is equivalent to 0.04319 g of\nC22H3S04Zn.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zoledronic-acid-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zoledronic Acid Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zoledronic Acid Injection\nZoledronic Acid Injection is prepared by dissolving the\ncontents of a sealed container containing Zoledronic Acid\nwith or without auxiliary substances in the requisite a.mount\nofwater for injection as directed on the label.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for\nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under\nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nUsual strength. 4 mg per ml.\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately\nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nZoledronic Acid Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent\nand notmore than 110.0 per centofstated amount ofzoledronic\nacid, CSHION207P2.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powdersfor Injection) andwith thefollowing requirements:"},"sections":[{"name":"PREAMBLE","text":"Zoledronic Acid Injection\nZoledronic Acid Injection is prepared by dissolving the\ncontents of a sealed container containing Zoledronic Acid\nwith or without auxiliary substances in the requisite a.mount\nofwater for injection as directed on the label.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for\nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under\nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nUsual strength. 4 mg per ml.\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately\nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nZoledronic Acid Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent\nand notmore than 110.0 per centofstated amount ofzoledronic\nacid, CSHION207P2.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powdersfor Injection) andwith thefollowing requirements:"},{"name":"Identification","text":"In theAssay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained\nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."},{"name":"Tests","text":"pH (2.4.24).4.5 to 7.0, determined in a solution constituted as\ndirected on the label, in Water for Injections.\nOther tests. Complies with the tests stated under Parental\nPreparations (Injections).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 17.0 Endotoxin\nUnits per mg ofZoledronic acid.\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution.\nDissolve the contents of sealed container in\nsufficient mobile phase to give a concentration 0.16 mg per m!.\nReferencesolution. A 0.016 per cent w/v solution ofzoledronic\nacidRS in the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 em x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 J.lm) (Such\nas Hypersil BDS),\nrnobilephase:....mix..l..ml_oftr.iethylaminewith..5_0.0mlof\nwater, adjusted to pH: 3.2 with orthophosphoric acid,\nflow rate. 1ml perminute,\nspectrophotometer set at 215 urn,\ninjection volume. 20 Ill.\n2340\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the\nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more\nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the test solution and the reference solution.\nCalculate the content ofCsHIONz07Pz in the Injection.\nStorage. Store the sealed container at a temperature not\nexceeding 30°. Use the prepared solution within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer and stored strictly in\naccordance with the instructions of the manufacturer."}]},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zoledronic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zoledronic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zoledronic Acid\nZoledronic Acid Monohydrate? ;\nP<\n0' 0H\nG,H10N2O7P2,H2O \n■ MoLWt 290.1\nZoledronic Acid is [1 -hydroxy-2-( 1 H-