thixol Acetate Injection is a sterile solution of \nZuclopenthixol Acetate in a suitable vegetable oil.\nZuclopenthixol Acetate. Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of C24H27CIN2O2S."},{"name":"Identification","text":"A. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel F254.\nNOTE —Protect the solutions from light.\nMobile phase.- A mixture of 3 volumes of diethylamine and \n90 volumes cd cyclohexane.\n4011\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZUCLOPENTHIXOL: ACETATE INJECTION\nReference solution (a). A 0.5 per cent w/v solution of \nzuclopenthixol acetate dihydrochloride IPRS in ethanol \n(95 per cent): ' \n’’’ \n• 1 \n■'\nReference solution (b)„A solution containing 0,5 per cent \nw/v, each of, zuclopenthixol acetate dihydrochloride IPRS \nand zuclopenthixol decanoate dihydrochloride IPRS in \nethanol (95 per cent). \n: \n. e\nApply to the plate 5 p.1 of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 15 cm. Dry the plate in air, spray with a 1.0 per \ncent w/v solution of sodium molybdate in sulphuric acid \nheat at 110° for 20 minutes and> examine in daylight. The \nprincipal spot in the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution corresponds to that, in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) shows two \nclearly separated spots. . \n,\ni \nìdiij Ï i\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nB\nTests .\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel.F254.\nNOTE— Protect the solutions from light.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 10 volumes of diethylamine, \n40 volumes of dichloromethane and 50 volumes of \ncyclohexane.\nTest solution:Dissolve a quantity of the injection containing \n0.1 g of Zuclopenthixol Acetate in dichloromethahe and dilute \nto 50.0 ml with dichloromethane.\nReference solution (a). A 0.001 per cent w/v solution of \n2-chlorathioxanthone IPRS in dichloromethane.\nReference solution (b). A 0.002 per cent w/v solution of \nzuclopenthixol hydrochloride IPRS in dichloromethane \n(containing a few drops of diethylamine). .■ ... , . ...\nReference solution (c). A 0.0004 per, cent w/v solution of \nzticlppenthixol hydro chloride IPRS in dichloromethane \n(containing a few drops of diethylamine).\nReference solution (d). A 0.0002 per cent w/v solution of \nzuclopenthixol hydrochloride IPRS in dichloromethane \n(containing a few drops of diethylamine). / T - 1 ;\nApply to the plate 5 pl of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 15 cm. Dry the plate in air, spray with a mixture of \nequal volumes of sulphuric acid and ethanol, heat, at 110°. for \n5 minutes and examine under ultraviolet light at 365 nm. In the\n4012\nZUCLOPENTHIXOL DECANOATE\n........................—.............................. \n-W46?- \n' chromatogram obtained with the test solution,\nTest solution. Dilute a volume of the injection with ethanol \n(95per cent) to obtain a solution containing 0.5 per cent w/v \nofZuclopenthixol Acetate. •.\nReference solution (b).A 0.00025 percentw/vsolutionof \nzuclopenthixol impurity B in acetomtrile^yA\nReference solution (c). Dissolve the contents pf 'a'viai o¥\nDissolve 20 mg ofzuclopenthixol acetate\ncorresponding to 2-chlorothioxanthone is not more \nthan the spot in the chromatogram obtained with refti \nsolution (a) (0.5 per cent). Any spot correspond^ \nzuclopenthixol is not more intense than the spot.^ \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (fj \ncent). Any other secondary spot is not more intense ti^ \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with reference soluS \n(0.2 per cent) and not more than one other secondary^ \nmore, intense than the spot in the chromatogram ofl \nwith reference solution (d) (0.1 per cent). \n||\nin 25 ml of dichloromethane containing \n{ \ndiethylamine, add 5 ml of the internal standard\nzuclopenthixol for system suitability IPRS (zuclopenthixol\njpjytjo^btfdilnte to 50.0 ml with dichloromethane.\n■system as described under test for trans-\ndecanoate containing impurities A, B and C) in lb ml of \nmethanol. \n’-}^-\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed withy\nfS^^.rcference solution and the test solution.\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), ...\n- column temperature: 40°, ■ \n.-.B\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 25 volumes of a buffer solution\nprepared by dissolving 8.9 gm of docusate sodium in\nTmws-Isomer. Determine by liquid chromatography (2|g\n700 ml of water, stirring for about 6-8 hours, and dilute \nto 1000 ml with the same solvent, 75 volumes of ethanol\nNOTE— Protect the solutions from light. ' \n>||\nZiiclöpenthixol Decanoate\nTest solution. Dissolve a quantity of the injection contaMj \n20 mg of Zuclopenthixol Acetate in dichlorometha^ \ndilute to 50.0 ml with dichloromethane.\nand 0.1 volume of orthophosphoric acid,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- ■ spectrophotometer set at 270 nm, \n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nSOB\nI\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 23 mg of trans-clopeh^ \nacetate dihydrochloride IPRS (equivalent to 20 mg \nclopenthixol acetate) in dichloromethane. containing yie? \ndrops of diethylamine and dilute to 50.0 ml with same soiveat: \nDilute 1.0 ml of the solution to i 00.0 ml with dich'loromei&.\nRelative \nretention time\nName\nI\n0.4\nZuclopenthixol decanoate impurity C’\nReference solution (b). A mixture of equal volumes of fetes \nsolution and undiluted reference solution (a). W\nMol. Wt 555.2\n0.5\nZuclopenthixol decanoate impurity B\nC^GINAS\n2-[4-[3-[(9Z)-2-Chloro-9H-\nZuclopenthixol Decanoate 2-[4-[3-L(yz;-z-vuiviv-^.t\nZuclopenthixol decanoate (Retention time.\nChromatographic system - \n. ... , , \n■\n- a .stainless steel, column 25 cm x. 4.6 ram packed 5#\n10\nabout 12 minutes